window.CPP_EN=window.CPP_EN||{};window.CPP_EN["personnel"]={"personnel-1":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-1-e0\">1. Context and industry (chapter introduction)</h3><ul>\n<li>Evolution of the names: watchman (whose function was to protect property) → guard → today security officer or protection officer. In some settings: loss prevention officer.</li>\n<li>Uniformed service is the most visible element of the private sector and a worldwide industry worth billions of dollars. The public still sees it as “guards,” without knowing the variety of services.</li>\n<li>The attacks of September 11, 2001 led to unprecedented public and media scrutiny of airport security personnel. Result: a better appreciation of their functions.</li>\n<li>Today governments regulate both proprietary and contract officers. Some jurisdictions also regulate door supervisors, repossession agents, and locksmiths.</li>\n<li>Better-trained personnel = greater return on investment (ROI). With technology, fewer people cover more tasks, some outside strict security: communications, customer service, and transportation.</li>\n<li>There is additional growth in regions with civil unrest and war. Against armed adversaries, heavily armed security forces have been used.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Figure</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Increase in security personnel in the US after 9/11</td>\n<td>13%; more than 1 million jobs in 2002</td>\n<td>Bureau of Labor Statistics 2007</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security officers in the US (2019)</td>\n<td>1.1 million; about 60% employed by contract agencies</td>\n<td>Bureau of Labor Statistics 2019</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Police and sheriff’s officers in the US</td>\n<td>About 665,000</td>\n<td>Bureau of Labor Statistics 2019</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private security / police ratio in the US</td>\n<td>“Almost two to one”</td>\n<td>Book</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private security worldwide (2017)</td>\n<td>About 20 million workers; an industry of about US$180 billion, forecast at US$240 billion in 2020</td>\n<td>McCarthy 2017</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>India</td>\n<td>About 7 million in private security versus 1.4 million police</td>\n<td>McCarthy 2017</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>China</td>\n<td>About 5 million in private security versus 2.7 million police</td>\n<td>McCarthy 2017</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> with the book’s own figures (1.1 million versus 665,000) the ratio is 1.65 to 1, not “almost two to one.” If asked, the book’s literal phrase is “almost two to one.”</p><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e1\">2. Use of security officers (1.1)</h3><ul>\n<li>They are best used as part of a complete protection plan, not as a stand-alone resource.</li>\n<li>Security management plans are based on understanding the risks that must be controlled. Officers are just one more strategy.</li>\n<li>Their use must be evaluated periodically together with the other protection resources (hardware and electronics).</li>\n<li>Today they work in more settings than ever and sometimes replace the police or the military. They patrol urban areas or military installations.</li>\n<li>They watch heavily frequented areas: stadiums, shopping centers, and large residential complexes. They also transport prisoners or detainees.</li>\n<li>They are increasingly the organization’s “eyes and ears”: they detect violations and also worrisome behavior that may herald a threat.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Number of officers required (1.1.1)</h4>\n<p>Factors that determine how many officers a facility needs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Level of risk.</li>\n<li>Physical complexity and size of the facility.</li>\n<li>Number of employees.</li>\n<li>Nature of the work.</li>\n<li>Number of entrances (and the hours they are open).</li>\n<li>Value and quantity of the assets protected.</li>\n<li>Number of patrols needed.</li>\n<li>Number of escorts and special assignments.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Post covered 24 hours, 7 days (officers working 40 h per week)</th>\n<th>Officers per post</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimum (24 × 7 = 168 h ÷ 40 h)</td>\n<td>4.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Covering sick leave, vacations, and holidays</td>\n<td>4.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>With longer vacations or more training demands</td>\n<td>Up to 5 full-time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The supervisory force is usually completed with support staff: administrative, communications, training, and supplies.</li>\n<li>The amount of support staff depends on the size and complexity of the security force.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e2\">3. Basic functions (1.2)</h3><p>Whether the force is proprietary or contract, the tasks it must perform are identified first. Possible basic functions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Access control and control of pedestrian and vehicle movement.</li>\n<li>Patrol of buildings and perimeters.</li>\n<li>Escort of materials and people.</li>\n<li>Inspection for security and fire risks.</li>\n<li>Monitoring of assets from a control center.</li>\n<li>Emergency response.</li>\n<li>Handling disruptive people.</li>\n<li>Observing and reporting worrisome activities or behavior.</li>\n<li>Occupational safety and accident prevention.</li>\n<li>Public relations.</li>\n<li>Special assignments.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Access control (1.2.1)</h4>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> access control is the regulation of movement into, out of, and within a building or designated area. It applies to people, means of transport, and materials. It is a primary function of the officer.</p>\n<p>Typical instructions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Verify that employees are who they say they are and wear a visible badge, or comply with other security rules.</li>\n<li>Issue special passes to visitors and regulate their access with an established authorization process.</li>\n<li>Identify and report anyone without proper identification. Some may be temporarily detained.</li>\n<li>Report anyone who attempts to bring in contraband or appears to be under the influence of alcohol or controlled substances.</li>\n<li>Identify and question anyone removing property, to confirm that they are authorized.</li>\n<li>Watch the materials coming in and going out: check packages and bags and receive courier or parcel shipments.</li>\n<li>Watch the vehicles coming in and going out: vessels, aircraft, and land vehicles (trains, buses, heavy machinery, cars, motorcycles, and trucks).</li>\n<li>In crises or disturbances: take body temperature, detect inappropriate clothing (e.g., a heavy coat in hot weather), and identify erratic behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Patrol (1.2.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The patrol officer observes people, assets, and places. Reports risks and program deficiencies so they can be corrected.</li>\n<li>Responds to door alarms and other irregularities. The presence of a uniformed officer or an official vehicle deters crime.</li>\n<li>Two categories: foot patrol and vehicular patrol.</li>\n<li>Patrol systematically, but doubling back often and taking unexpected routes. An invariable route lets the aggressor find the best moment to attack.</li>\n<li>Know the area thoroughly: shortcuts, dead ends, construction, and everything that affects response. Also know the area’s risks and legitimate activities.</li>\n<li>Observation is the main task. When something is abnormal, assess the situation and act. Take special care when watching people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical conditions the patrol officer looks for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unsecured doors, windows, or other openings.</li>\n<li>Suspicious persons, vehicles, or circumstances.</li>\n<li>Disorder or unusual activity: people under the influence of alcohol or other substances, unusual gatherings, rowdy individuals, or fights.</li>\n<li>Hazardous conditions: leaks of water or other fluids, fire hazards, and broken equipment.</li>\n<li>Equipment running at odd hours.</li>\n<li>Problems with fire-fighting equipment or other safety equipment.</li>\n<li>Violations of the company’s security or protection policies.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Escort (1.2.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>General rule: security should not escort visitors or customers, because that diverts it from its protection tasks. The person who invites the visitor is responsible for escorting them.</li>\n<li>Exceptions, with trained officers: escorting anyone carrying large sums of money, information, or special property.</li>\n<li>Also: accompanying employees for their safety (e.g., to the parking lot at night) and assisting people who are ill or physically disabled. These tasks require special alertness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Inspection (1.2.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It can be done together with other tasks, such as patrol.</li>\n<li>Special inspections: fire hazards; unclosed doors, safes, and windows; other security matters.</li>\n<li>Because of their 24-hour presence, they are often expected to watch mechanical equipment and inspect fire-extinguishing equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Monitoring (1.2.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Increasingly it is done from centralized centers: security operations centers, monitoring centers, or global operations centers.</li>\n<li>A typical center controls: video surveillance, access control, fire alarms, elevators and other building functions, lighting, duress alarms, environmental alarms, and emergency call stations.</li>\n<li>It can cover a single site or sites across a whole country or several countries.</li>\n<li>Its staff must master electronic systems and computers, and tolerate an environment that is sometimes very stressful.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Emergency response (1.2.6)</h4>\n<p>Officers direct, coordinate, and carry out the responses set by security management. Possible emergencies:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fire alarms, real and nuisance alarms.</li>\n<li>Bomb threats (by telephone, electronic communication, or mail).</li>\n<li>Bombings.</li>\n<li>Suspicious packages.</li>\n<li>Release of hazardous materials (HAZMAT), inside or outside the building.</li>\n<li>Medical emergencies.</li>\n<li>Mechanical and other equipment failures.</li>\n<li>People trapped in elevators.</li>\n<li>Extreme weather: floods, tornadoes, ice storms, wind, and heat.</li>\n<li>Terrorist acts.</li>\n<li>Other criminal acts: hostage-taking, shootings, or bombings.</li>\n<li>Building collapses.</li>\n<li>Building evacuations, partial and total.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Handling disruptive people (1.2.7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Cases: people who appear to have a mental illness, are under the influence of alcohol or illegal drugs, or lack self-control temporarily because of physical or emotional trauma.</li>\n<li>Also: any irrational behavior that may harm the person or others.</li>\n<li>It requires sensitivity. Two objectives: (1) prevent the harm their actions may cause; (2) control the person with a minimum of injury, discomfort, or embarrassment.</li>\n<li>The first objective is the immediate concern. The second is often forgotten during the response.</li>\n<li>Once the danger has passed, witnesses judge the treatment. If it was poor, they will criticize the officer and forget the risks of the moment.</li>\n<li>Complication 1: a gender or age difference between the person and the officer. Reluctance to make physical contact can cause a critical delay.</li>\n<li>Complication 2: misinterpreting or ignoring cultural factors. It damages the ability to build rapport and can unintentionally escalate the situation.</li>\n<li>That is why officers must be trained thoroughly in the employer’s policies, especially on the use of physical force. A written procedure is strongly recommended here.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Most important consideration:</strong> safety and de-escalating or eliminating the immediate danger. Other considerations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Legal liability of the security force and the company if an injury or other harm is caused.</li>\n<li>Legal liability of both if the person is not restrained and later injures others.</li>\n<li>Relations with employees, community, public, and media, which the officers’ conduct can affect.</li>\n<li>Worrying too much about legal liability leads to acting “too little and too late.”</li>\n<li>Adequate insurance protecting the company and security personnel must already be in place, reviewed with the insurance department.</li>\n<li>Even self-insureds or those who retain the risk must not decide the response solely for economic reasons.</li>\n<li>Publishing the policy and training partly reduces liability and image problems. It shows that the issue was given serious thought, although the officer’s conduct remains critical.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to explore crisis intervention training when practical.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Observe and report (1.2.8)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Officers take part in the organization’s intelligence function. They help mitigate targeted incidents by criminals or terrorists.</li>\n<li>Even on patrol or in public relations tasks they can detect unusual surveillance and intelligence gathering, security risks, and suspicious packages.</li>\n<li>It is essential to recognize the signs of surveillance and pre-attack behaviors in order to mitigate the risk in time.</li>\n<li>If it is part of their mission, they must be ready to photograph or record suspicious behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Public relations (1.2.9)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are often the first and last contact that visitors, customers, vendors, or employees have with the organization. Their manner sets the overall impression.</li>\n<li>They help create and maintain good relations between the security department (proprietary or contract) and the rest of the organization.</li>\n<li>Courtesy and efficiency are essential. Courteous, restrained officers with good judgment overcome the resistance of those who reject any authority figure.</li>\n<li>They must enforce the rules, but with fair and understanding treatment. An officious or careless officer damages the program or exposes it to legal liability.</li>\n<li>If an employee refuses to cooperate with an officer who acted correctly, the employee is referred to a supervisor for administrative action.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Special assignments (1.2.10)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Since the force is usually on duty 24 hours, it may be the only personnel available. Examples: messengers, communications coordinators, or drivers.</li>\n<li>Diverting officers to miscellaneous services erodes the protection program.</li>\n<li>Another special task: promoting security awareness, by teaching or serving as a model. This way they encourage reporting risks and complying with the programs.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e3\">4. Officer requirements (1.3)</h3><ul>\n<li>They vary according to the assignment, the size and function of the facility, and the labor market. Regulations change by jurisdiction and sometimes apply only to certain industries.</li>\n<li>Every organization with assets to protect must set the requirements. They are based on the duties in the job description.</li>\n<li>Site characteristics to consider: working alone or in a team; complex or simple; routine or varied; indoor or outdoor; physically demanding or sedentary; technology-assisted or people-oriented.</li>\n<li>Personal traits that predict success: honesty, resourcefulness, knowledge of security principles, interpersonal skills, good presence, and dependability.</li>\n<li>In 2001 ASIS International (formerly the American Society for Industrial Security) recognized the need to raise standards. For that reason the guideline <em>Private Security Officer Selection and Training</em> was developed, updated in 2019.</li>\n<li>The guideline proposes minimum selection and training criteria for all officers, contract and proprietary.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Regulations (1.3.1)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Country or region</th>\n<th>Regulation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>United States</td>\n<td>Each state regulates. You must know the rules of each locality where you operate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canada</td>\n<td>The Canadian General Standards Board published standard CAN/CGSB-133.1–2017, <em>Security Officers and Security Officer Supervisors</em>. It is the minimum criterion for providing guard services to the government of Canada</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>European Union</td>\n<td>Leaves regulation to each member state. It issued voluntary standards on training, ethics, and other topics (Button 2017)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>China</td>\n<td><em>Regulation on the Administration of Security and Guarding Services</em>, January 2010</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>India</td>\n<td><em>Private Security Agencies (Regulation) Act</em> of 2005</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nigeria</td>\n<td>Supervised by the Nigerian Security and Civil Defence Corps (NSCDC)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Before hiring officers in a jurisdiction, review the local and national rules.</li>\n<li>Preemployment background screening is essential.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Personal attributes (1.3.2)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attribute</th>\n<th>What it implies</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Character</td>\n<td>Honest, brave, alert, disciplined, self-reliant, and loyal. This way the officer does not overlook rules or accept bribes. Responds courageously in emergencies, follows orders, reacts well to the unexpected, and looks out for the company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior and communications</td>\n<td>Courtesy, restraint, and interest at all times, especially in uniform. Act without haste or excessive emotion. Avoid abusive language, arguments and, if possible, force. Speak clearly and listen carefully</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coachability</td>\n<td>Open to new ways of working, able to learn and adapt. Works as part of a team</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Appearance</td>\n<td>Neat and clean, in uniform or plainclothes. Generates respect for the officer and the organization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knowledge of the job</td>\n<td>Know the facility and the organization, for the public relations part. Know federal, state, and local laws, company rules, and criminal procedures. Know security, fire protection, emergency plans, and first aid. Requires ongoing training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>The level has improved. There are certificates and degrees in security in the US, Canada, and other countries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ethics</td>\n<td>ASIS defines ethics as a set of accepted principles that govern a group or profession. The International Foundation for Protection Officers (IFPO) created a code of ethics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.1 — Protection Officer Code of Ethics, adapted from IFPO 2020:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Respond to the employer’s professional needs to protect people and assets.</li>\n<li>Show exemplary conduct on and off duty.</li>\n<li>Protect confidential and proprietary information.</li>\n<li>Maintain a safe and secure workplace.</li>\n<li>Be professional in dress and behavior.</li>\n<li>Enforce all legal rules and regulations.</li>\n<li>Establish a working relationship with public officers.</li>\n<li>Develop and show stewardship and serve as a mentor within the profession.</li>\n<li>Strive for professional competence and improve professional knowledge.</li>\n<li>Encourage high standards of ethics among officers.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Site needs (1.3.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose candidates who fit the position they will fill.</li>\n<li>Example: an operations center officer does not need the same mobility as one who patrols the grounds.</li>\n<li>Example: someone who deals with the public needs better oral communication than someone who works without contact with them.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e4\">5. Training (1.4)</h3><ul>\n<li>The officer’s functions determine the training. The officer must be trained in all the equipment and all the procedures they will carry out.</li>\n<li>The topic is developed in Chapter 2.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e5\">6. Weapons (1.5)</h3><ul>\n<li>If an officer fires a firearm, the consequences are serious. The company is likely to be sued.</li>\n<li>At trial it is very rare for judge and jury to consider the use justified. It is almost always perceived as excessive force.</li>\n<li>Firearm claims often exceed policy limits. Whoever employs armed officers must purchase higher liability limits (Brownyard 2016).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Arming an officer is justified if one or both conditions exist:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Without the weapon there is greater danger to life.</li>\n<li>It is reasonable to expect that the officer will have to use fatal force.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Firearms (1.5.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Many jurisdictions require armed officers to be licensed by a government agency. They also set minimum standards for licenses or carry permits.</li>\n<li>Legal liability, the cost of training, and the time to complete it lead many organizations to use unarmed officers.</li>\n<li>Worldwide picture: in Latin America many countries allow armed officers, with highly variable training and qualification standards.</li>\n<li>In Europe and Asia armed officers are less common. Africa and the Middle East may allow them.</li>\n<li>Where no prior training is required, mishandling or misuse of weapons is a concern.</li>\n<li>Issuing lethal weapons is one of an organization’s greatest responsibilities. With proprietary officers the responsibility is obvious.</li>\n<li>Because of the smartphone, every use of force will probably be recorded and circulated on social media.</li>\n<li><strong>Contract provider:</strong> some managers believe a good contract shifts almost all the responsibility to the provider. It does not: lawsuits usually include anyone remotely connected.</li>\n<li>Paying the provider means accepting its style and management practices. That is why the organization that hires it must set the crucial policies and protocols.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>If officers are armed, facility management takes on:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Adequate training of the officers who will carry weapons.</li>\n<li>Selection of appropriate weapons and ammunition.</li>\n<li>Maintenance of the weapons by a qualified gunsmith.</li>\n<li>Records of all of the above.</li>\n<li>Civil liability insurance at an adequate level.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Firearms training:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Complete mastery before carrying them: correct handling and live firing at a range to qualify.</li>\n<li>Qualification shooting is done with the weapon the officer carries. The record includes the weapon’s serial number.</li>\n<li>Each weapon has its own shooting characteristics. The officer must be able to state in court that they know it thoroughly.</li>\n<li>Train in situations that could occur at the facility. Include weapon retention. Provide periodic retraining.</li>\n<li>Private weapons: if permitted on duty, set and enforce standards for the type of weapon and ammunition. The alternative is to prohibit them.</li>\n<li>The employer’s weapons and ammunition are issued at the start of the shift and collected at the end.</li>\n<li>The insurer must be told that officers carry weapons.</li>\n<li>If a contract agency provides armed officers, all of the above must appear in the service contract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When firearms may be appropriate:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>The book’s nuance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A lot of cash is regularly handled and there is a risk of robbery</td>\n<td>Armed officers deter more than unarmed ones, and deterring robberies reduces the overall risk to life. But the risk of a shootout for third parties must be weighed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A single officer at a post or escort, in a place with felonious attacks that have occurred or are anticipated. Examples: remote booths and high-crime areas with exterior patrol</td>\n<td>Alternative: do not use officers and replace them with remote surveillance or intrusion alarms</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strategic value:</strong> aggressors may desist if they know they will meet armed resistance.</li>\n<li>But the quality and quantity of defensive weapons must be a real obstacle. One or two officers with pistols may not deter if the target is very attractive and there are no other countermeasures.</li>\n<li>Stronger deterrence: layers of protection. They combine armed officers with vehicle control, fence and boundary security, and hardened locations.</li>\n<li>Shoulder weapons in hardened or defensible positions covering the attack route can deter more than pistols.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Less than lethal weapons (1.5.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Equipping officers with less than lethal weapons requires the same thorough analysis as firearms.</li>\n<li>A full analysis must be done of whether the benefits outweigh the risks. Depending on the organization, a multidisciplinary team to assess risks and liabilities is advisable.</li>\n<li>Despite their name, they can kill. In 2017 Reuters documented more than 1,000 deaths in US police incidents involving TASERs (Reuters 2017).</li>\n<li>In most of those deaths other forces were also present, such as batons or pepper spray.</li>\n<li>An officer with a less than lethal weapon is better positioned to protect themselves and others from serious injury or death.</li>\n<li>In security they are only self-defense tools, not offensive weapons. They are not used to show force, intimidate, or apprehend.</li>\n<li>Every weapon of the officer has a single purpose: to defend their life and the life of another person.</li>\n<li>They are never deployed without two key elements: (1) a clear and concise use of force policy; (2) sufficient initial and recurring training.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Most common type</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Electroshock weapons</td>\n<td>They use electricity to stun or incapacitate. Three common types: TASER, stun gun, and stun baton</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— TASER (registered trademark)</td>\n<td>Fires two barbed probes toward the subject with compressed gas. Affects the subject’s nervous system and usually causes loss of muscle control (Carli 2018)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Stun gun</td>\n<td>Requires direct contact with the subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Stun baton</td>\n<td>Also requires direct contact, but is longer: the officer does not need to get as close. Stun guns and stun batons can cause pain without incapacitating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Alternative names</td>\n<td>Conducted electrical weapons (CEW) or conducted energy devices (CED). Temporary effects that can stop the aggression. Generally no permanent effects, but the subject may fall and suffer secondary injuries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Batons</td>\n<td>Perhaps the most used in security. The most common is the extendable, collapsible baton. Variants: straight-handled nightstick and side-handled baton. Materials: wood, metal, or plastic-type resin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Use of the baton</td>\n<td>It is an impact weapon: its correct use almost always causes some injury. Only on large muscle masses, such as the thigh and shoulder. Never on joints or the head. Misused it can cause serious injury or death</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aerosol irritants</td>\n<td>Commonly used. The most frequent compounds are CS, CN, and OC (oleoresin capsicum). They are mixed with a propellant in a compact container for quick, safe use. They are known as “tear gas” or “pepper spray”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Effects and use</td>\n<td>They irritate mucous membranes and eyes: tearing, coughing, difficulty breathing, and skin irritation. Today they last less and leave fewer residual symptoms. Their use obliges aftercare and custody of the subject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Risk to third parties</td>\n<td>They have the greatest residual impact on third parties. That is why their use is irresponsible in places like a crowded emergency room (Mesloh 2008)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes “CS (2-chloroacetophenone)” and “CN (o-chlorobenzylidene).” This is reversed: CN is 2-chloroacetophenone and CS is o-chlorobenzylidene malononitrile.</p>\n<h4>Policy considerations (1.5.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>All use of weapons must be set out in the organization’s Use of Force policy.</li>\n<li>Training must be specific, comprehensive, and ongoing, with practice and drills. Since the officer will almost never use a weapon, lessons must be reinforced regularly (Salomon 2021).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Aspects to regulate on lethal weapons, in addition to the officer’s competence:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conditions under which weapons may be issued.</li>\n<li>Specific persons who may be armed.</li>\n<li>Type of weapon and ammunition issued.</li>\n<li>Quality and reliability of the weapon when issued.</li>\n<li>Repair and maintenance by a qualified armorer.</li>\n<li>Control and accountability of each weapon and its ammunition.</li>\n<li>Specific training with that weapon.</li>\n<li>Conditions under which they may be used.</li>\n<li>Safety precautions when issuing and carrying them.</li>\n<li>Return or handing in of the weapon when it is not needed for an authorized use.</li>\n<li>Secure storage when not issued: unloaded, separated from ammunition, and locked in an approved container.</li>\n<li>Accurate records of all the above points.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Additional controls for large numbers of weapons, in areas prone to theft by terrorists or other adversaries, or highly dangerous ones (automatic).</li>\n<li>Measures to consider: reinforced building with intruder detection, armed patrols, and dual access, as part of a defense-in-depth.</li>\n<li>In those cases government standards and insurer requirements must be exceeded.</li>\n<li>Weapons not considered lethal (impact, electronic devices, and aerosols) must also be in the policy.</li>\n<li>Those statements record the company’s position on the use of weapons and are the basis of the training program.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Use-of-force policy, according to the United Nations Office on Drugs and Crime (UNODC) 2020.</strong> A complete policy:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sets the circumstances and limits of the use of force, including:<ul>\n<li>force in individual self-defense;</li>\n<li>force to protect others;</li>\n<li>force to protect property;</li>\n<li>response to serious crimes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Sets the limits of force acceptable to the government and the organization.</li>\n<li>Serves to determine whether a specific use of force was reasonable and necessary.</li>\n<li>It must comply with the law of the jurisdiction. Individuals and companies may be held criminally or civilly liable for excessive or unjustified force, so legal advice is advisable.</li>\n<li>Many regulators require specific training and certification before carrying lethal or less than lethal weapons.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security force continuum, McGoey 2018.</strong> A concept taught at most police academies. The example has six levels. Force can go up and down a level in seconds, as the situation demands.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Officer Presence</td>\n<td>A visible uniformed officer or a marked vehicle is usually enough to deter. Nonthreatening gestures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Verbal Communication</td>\n<td>Together with presence, speech increases deterrence. Begin with a calm but firm message, seeking to de-escalate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Control Holds and Restraints</td>\n<td>Use the hands to guide, hold, or contain. No blows, takedowns, or chokeholds. Handcuffs only if the officer is trained and authorized to carry them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Chemical Agent</td>\n<td>Defensive weapon used only if lesser tactics failed or if there is an imminent physical threat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Temporary Incapacitation</td>\n<td>Extreme, violent, and immediate situation. Nonlethal force from empty hand to an intermediate weapon: pepper spray, TASER, or baton</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Deadly Force</td>\n<td>Only if there is immediate fear of death or serious bodily injury at the hands of an aggressor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> pepper spray appears at level 4 (chemical agent) and again as an example at level 5. It is an internal inconsistency in the book.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e6\">7. Uniforms and equipment (1.6)</h3><h4>Uniforms (1.6.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Most organizations supply them. This way the appearance is truly uniform and each garment fits well.</li>\n<li>Complete seasonal uniforms are usually issued, with clothing for bad weather.</li>\n<li>The number of uniforms depends on the type of service and the laundry or dry-cleaning cycle.</li>\n<li>Trend: recognizable officers without looking authoritarian. This is achieved with casual or plainclothes attire with patches on the shoulder or pocket.</li>\n<li>The policy must say whether the uniform is worn off duty, including the commute to work.</li>\n<li>Allowing it only on duty avoids bad publicity if the officer is involved in an incident in their free time.</li>\n<li>Equipment according to the post: at a lobby desk, minimal. On mobile patrol: flashlight, means of communication, weapon, and notebook.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Body worn cameras (1.6.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Police Executive Research Forum survey (August 2018): more than 80% of US police agencies were using body worn cameras or planned to do so soon.</li>\n<li>More than 85% of the departments already using them would recommend them.</li>\n<li>In favor: more accountability and they provide evidence.</li>\n<li>Against: the cost of storing video and the consequences when they do not record because of failure or misuse (Tarallo 2019).</li>\n<li>Before using them: consult legal counsel and the local police on their legality and on recording, storage, and use protocols.</li>\n<li>Create policies and procedures inspired by police protocols. Decide how long and where recordings are kept and how they will be used.</li>\n<li>Privacy: the officer must notify the public that they are being recorded. All interested parties must be informed so they can act accordingly (Torres 2019).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Handcuffs (1.6.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are not technically weapons, but they help subdue a subject.</li>\n<li>They can cause discomfort and injuries, so they require training.</li>\n<li>Do not leave the detainee handcuffed face down on the floor for long, to avoid positional asphyxiation (Stowell 2016).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Other equipment (1.6.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Communications: cell phones, radios, tour devices, and personal alarms.</li>\n<li>Also metal detectors, body armor, and miscellaneous tools.</li>\n<li>The officer must know exactly how and when to use each item.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e7\">8. Organizational structure (1.7)</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> organizational structure is the formal pattern of interactions and coordination designed by management. It links the tasks of individuals and groups to achieve the organization’s objectives.</li>\n<li>Formerly, many forces copied the military or police model: chief of security, captain, lieutenant, sergeant, and officer.</li>\n<li>Current trend: abandon the military or paramilitary model and use business supervisory lines. Entry-level position: security officer. Next level: supervisor. After that: assistant manager, manager, or director.</li>\n<li>The efficiency of the force depends on the sufficiency and skill of its supervisors.</li>\n<li>The manager may delegate operational authority to certain supervisors to see how they decide and supervise.</li>\n<li>Without adequate training and close supervision, officers may not provide the protection required.</li>\n<li>Supervisors are chosen for their knowledge of the job and their demonstrated administrative and leadership abilities.</li>\n<li>They must have the base skills of the level above and good people skills. They need special training to stay current.</li>\n<li>Some organizations rotate supervisors to avoid cliques and so that they learn all phases of the work.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Vertical model (1.7.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called the hierarchical model. Authority originates with the senior manager and flows down through managers and supervisors to front-line personnel.</li>\n<li>Most effective supervisor style: recognize good performance and be objective when pointing out deficiencies.</li>\n<li>Must apply discipline as a corrective measure, not only as punishment.</li>\n<li>Managers and supervisors maintain high standards of their own to set an example and motivate.</li>\n<li>The art of delegating: give responsibility with the corresponding authority and demand full accountability for its use.</li>\n<li>The number of levels varies. The organization chart is a series of pyramids connected to other pyramids and within them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Network model (1.7.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Also called flattened, horizontal, or open. It is compared to a computer network.</li>\n<li>Each employee connects with their boss and subordinates, and also with many others. The security department connects with other departments.</li>\n<li>The emphasis is not on reporting lines, but on people who come together for a task and then separate or regroup.</li>\n<li>It may not be best for front-line personnel with fixed positions and responsibilities.</li>\n<li>It gives flexibility for one-off needs and allows the manager to contribute their skills to the good of the whole organization.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Vertical model (hierarchical)</th>\n<th>Network model (flattened, horizontal, open)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Flow of authority</td>\n<td>From top to bottom down to the front line</td>\n<td>Multiple connections, also with other departments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emphasis</td>\n<td>Chain of command, delegation, and corrective discipline</td>\n<td>Teams that form and dissolve by task</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What the book says about its fit</td>\n<td>Organization chart of pyramids with varying levels</td>\n<td>Not ideal for front line with fixed positions; flexible for one-off needs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Proprietary versus contract officers (1.7.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Options: proprietary officers, contract officers, or a combination.</li>\n<li>In the 1990s, the trend toward downsizing and outsourcing favored contract. Example: General Motors converted its large proprietary force into a contract operation.</li>\n<li>Historically, this pattern is common when the budget is tight.</li>\n<li><strong>Hybrid force:</strong> proprietary supervisors direct front-line contract officers. That relationship is called principal-agent.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Arrangement</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Burdens or disadvantages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Proprietary</td>\n<td>Closer control of selection, screening, training, and supervision. Loyalty to the protected organization, which gives lower turnover, more knowledge of the job, and better performance</td>\n<td>The company takes on recruiting, application management, personnel screening, benefits, performance management, discipline, and scheduling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contract</td>\n<td>Shifts part of the burden of hiring, training, and supervising to the service company. More flexibility in the number of personnel. A legal review will say whether the contract shifts part of the liability</td>\n<td>Vendors lower prices to sell, which can mislead. Service awarded at the lowest price (low-bid) usually disappoints because the service level drops</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>To avoid the lowest-price problem, the client writes detailed bid specifications: wages, benefits, and performance expectations.</li>\n<li>With performance specifications it is easier to demand quality from the contractor.</li>\n<li>Regulating the activity requires licensing the business and also licensing or registering its security employees.</li>\n<li>Profile sought: the contractor looks for generalists who perform with any client. The manager of a proprietary force may look for special skills, such as detecting suspicious behavior or an affinity for electronics.</li>\n<li>Before opting for a proprietary force, analyze its operational and cost advantages compared with contract.</li>\n<li>The proprietary force would have to take on recruiting, applications, screening, training, benefits, discipline, and open post coverage.</li>\n<li>General civil liability, workers’ compensation, and labor relations would also have to be considered.</li>\n<li><strong>Final decision:</strong> base it on the type of assets protected, the level of risk, and the expectations of the officers, not on cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.2 — Differences between contract and proprietary officers:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Contract officers</th>\n<th>Proprietary / in-house officers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>May be regulated by local licensing authorities</td>\n<td>Regulated by the employer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hiring criteria set by the licensing authority, the contract, and the employer’s policies</td>\n<td>Hiring criteria set by the employer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Often receive less specific training and perhaps less supervision</td>\n<td>Usually receive more training and supervision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Work in varied environments with changing schedules</td>\n<td>Usually have constant schedules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Working conditions set by the contract</td>\n<td>Working conditions set by the employer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High turnover typical</td>\n<td>Generally, better retention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loyalty to the contract provider</td>\n<td>Loyalty to the organization that hires them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tendency toward short-term assignments</td>\n<td>Longer-term assignments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May report to several bosses; less clear chain of command</td>\n<td>Clear chain of command</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Less integrated into the workplace</td>\n<td>More integrated and part of the workplace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>May need to adapt to frequent retraining at different sites or assignments</td>\n<td>More progressive site-specific training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lower hiring and total costs</td>\n<td>Higher hiring and total costs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>More job oriented</td>\n<td>More career oriented</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Hybrid programs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The hybrid force, with proprietary and contract officers, is gaining popularity. In some states and countries sworn police officers and licensed officers even work together.</li>\n<li>Hybridization always worked well between officers and technology. Joining proprietary and contract “under one roof” is an effective compromise: it saves costs and maintains reasonable control.</li>\n<li>Hidden cost: the natural competition between proprietary and contract. It is disruptive if the differences in pay, personality, knowledge, and ability are not resolved beforehand.</li>\n<li>Solution: place the account manager, the site supervisor, and the lead officers, better paid and higher performing, from the proprietary company.</li>\n<li>They select and hire the officers the contractor provides. This way the good relationship is created inside before the external change and improves buy-in on both sides.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Main efforts to make a hybrid program work:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build a unified, standardized, sustainable, positive, and productive work culture that fosters commitment, satisfaction, and retention.</li>\n<li>Establish a defined chain of command and clear communication, to defuse problems and give instructions.</li>\n<li>Create a strong sense of fit, belonging, and loyalty to the work under one roof.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>This may require the contractor to give up some control over its employees, who work very close to the proprietary ones.</li>\n<li>The proprietary side must consciously avoid “us against them” attitudes. An open mind and clear purpose improve the odds of success.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e8\">9. Legal aspects (1.8)</h3><h4>The security officer as a peace officer (1.8.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Security executives are divided on giving officers peace officer status.</li>\n<li>Against: it may give them police authority outside the facility and broaden the employer’s liability.</li>\n<li>Some tasks may require deputization: directing traffic on the public road or enforcing parking rules.</li>\n<li>If delegated, the usual and prudent course is to limit that authority to the officer’s actual functions.</li>\n<li>The manager must know state civil law on labor matters.</li>\n<li>Risk: a local police chief could mobilize all officers, including officers with peace officer status. That would reduce the guard force in the middle of a public emergency.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Insurance (1.8.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Most organizations assume their probable liability for the acts of their officers on duty.</li>\n<li>But they cannot prevent the officer from being sued or criminally charged separately. The officer remains primarily responsible for their own conduct.</li>\n<li>In practice, the plaintiff sees the employer as having more resources and seeks to make it the principal defendant under any theory of liability.</li>\n<li>With officers who have deputized authority there is usually limited additional coverage: the indemnity bond required by the local government.</li>\n<li>That bond only protects the public. If it is collected, the surety can sue the company for what it paid, if the company was civilly liable for the harm.</li>\n<li>Many companies have no special insurance for officers because their commercial general liability policy covers them.</li>\n<li>Many contractor clients now require specific coverage for employee dishonesty and data breaches.</li>\n<li>With a contractor: verify that insurance for the assigned employees exists and is sufficient. Include the client’s insurance requirements in the contract.</li>\n<li>Being an additional named insured depends on the policy wording, which varies by insurer.</li>\n<li><strong>Minimum required:</strong> current insurance certificates issued by the contractor’s insurer. They must show the agreed limits and include the commitment to notify the client of any change or cancellation.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e9\">10. Scheduling and managing the officer force (1.9)</h3><ul>\n<li>Despite technology and staff reductions in some settings, officers remain the largest segment of employees in the sector.</li>\n<li>They are also the largest cost element in most security operating budgets.</li>\n<li>Deployment must be guided by rational, objective business criteria, with a thorough assessment of the asset protection needs.</li>\n<li>Once the need is established, the next step is to schedule and assign officers in a cost-efficient way.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Staffing requirements (1.9.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The security manager determines how many posts and officers are needed.</li>\n<li>Post orders are based on standard operating procedures (SOP), which must be site-specific.</li>\n<li>SOPs guide daily operations and may include emergency response procedures. By setting expectations, they help define the requirements of each post.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> a security officer post is any location or combination of activities that requires a trained human being. It has three key concepts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A location or combination of activities.</li>\n<li>A necessary human being.</li>\n<li>Training and competency to perform the activities.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Location or combination of activities:</strong> it can be a stationary or fixed post or a roving patrol or patrol post. Also a predetermined point: control of employees, visitors, or contractors, or a control center.</p>\n<p><strong>Necessary human being:</strong> the characteristics and reactions of the post require human capabilities. Each post combines cognitive (knowledge), psychomotor (physical), and affective (attitudes) skills. A person is needed if the post requires:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Distinguishing between events, people, circumstances, or objects using complex and changing criteria.</li>\n<li>Conversing rationally face to face and deciding the next step based on that conversation.</li>\n<li>Using physical force, or the threat of it, to restrain one or more people.</li>\n<li>Exercising judgment to choose a logical mental or physical action in circumstances that vary at random.</li>\n<li>Preparing continuous reports of the activities.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>If those capabilities are applied from another location, the post may not need personnel.</li>\n<li>Example: a person-trap with interlocked or revolving doors to control entry or exit.</li>\n<li>Example: fully automated access with video, intercoms, and card readers, with a human response only in case of a problem.</li>\n<li>If personal recognition is required and the queues are low density, a remote operator can control three or four accesses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Training and competency:</strong> possessing the skills and knowledge for the assigned task.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Without post-specific training, the officer’s effectiveness drops and the need for the post is called into question.</li>\n<li>If possible or actual losses require the post, appropriate training must be designed.</li>\n<li>Competency = the person’s ability, skills, and qualifications to perform the duties.</li>\n<li>It requires mental capacity (administrative tasks and judgment) and physical capacity (observing, listening, reporting, and acting in emergencies).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Salaries (1.9.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>There is growing awareness that salary must match the responsibility of the position.</li>\n<li>Officers protect people, physical assets, and information, and the demands have never been higher.</li>\n<li>They deserve salaries that let them concentrate on the work without worrying about their basic needs. Many work long hours, chain shifts without rest, or hold a second job.</li>\n<li>They need education, training, and experience. A fair salary helps obtain them: everyone wins.</li>\n<li>Better salaries lead to lower turnover and, therefore, lower turnover costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Turnover costs:</strong> advertising, interview time, background checks, hiring, onboarding of the new employee, and training. It is usually 25% or more of the employee’s annual salary.</li>\n<li>Additional costs: lost productivity until the new hire performs, covering the departed employee’s hours, and a drop in customer satisfaction.</li>\n<li>High turnover also weakens the performance of the security operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Shift scheduling (1.9.3)</h4>\n<p><strong>Post hours:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Once the need for the post is established, it is decided when it operates. It may cover only business hours, only peak hours, or only when the need arises.</li>\n<li>The manager analyzes the requirements with the main internal clients affected by the post, to understand their operational needs.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to graph the post’s activity over 24 hours and seven days.</li>\n<li>If there is a lot of movement in short periods and little or none in long periods, it is more cost-effective to open only at the peaks.</li>\n<li>Some off-peak activity can be moved to another post. The same applies to vehicle control posts, movement of goods, and other activities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Post schedule:</strong> lists the hours of each shift during which the post is covered. If it opens or closes outside the start or end of the guard shift, the actual time is noted.</p>\n<p><strong>Security officer schedule — two techniques:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>For which force</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First</td>\n<td>Contract service</td>\n<td>Indicates whether each post requires a full-time or part-time officer. Restates the hours required per shift and per post</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second</td>\n<td>Proprietary force</td>\n<td>Typical North American week: 40 straight-time hours over five days. Under fair labor standards and wage and hour laws, going over 40 hours or five days requires premium pay. The same in the usual collective agreement. Each officer is represented by a number in the week</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Unnecessary overtime must be avoided, but vacations and other replacements may require overtime.</li>\n<li>The numeric representation shows the post’s coverage and reveals errors: split days off, more than five consecutive days, and chained shifts.</li>\n<li>It also indicates the minimum number of officers to cover the force properly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Rotating shifts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In many facilities the force rotates through the three traditional shifts. At fixed intervals, each officer moves from day to evening to night, or to an intermediate relief shift.</li>\n<li>At each change, the officer needs sufficient rest: several days off, preferably three or more consecutive (Miller 2010).</li>\n<li>Without adequate rest: lack of attention, falling asleep, accidents, or loss of cognitive capacity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Computer or automated scheduling (1.9.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The software is usually modular. The basic module assigns personnel to posts.</li>\n<li>Additional modules: time and attendance recording or payroll administration. For the contractor: client billing or cost control by service.</li>\n<li>It generates reports, charts, and graphs, and usually exports data to other programs.</li>\n<li>Security: authorization levels, from read-only to permission to change data.</li>\n<li>It analyzes the job requirements and cross-references them with the available personnel base.</li>\n<li>Remote access: employees can see their schedule at any time and managers can consult it outside the office.</li>\n<li>It allows controlling overtime, complying with labor law, and seeing who is available if an officer cannot report.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Work site requirements</strong> — mandatory analysis, manual or with software:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identification of each post by type (fixed, vehicle, or foot) and by number.</li>\n<li>Start and end hours of each shift, by day.</li>\n<li>Number of officers per shift and per day.</li>\n<li>Special staffing requirements per shift, if any.</li>\n<li>Names of personnel not assigned to the post.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Personnel database</strong> — profile of each employee:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Name, address, and telephone numbers.</li>\n<li>Employee number.</li>\n<li>Shift and overtime preferences.</li>\n<li>Maximum hours the employee can work.</li>\n<li>Availability (key for part-time officers).</li>\n<li>Special training, certifications, and qualifications: the skills inventory.</li>\n<li>Vacation entitled and days requested.</li>\n<li>Scheduled training days.</li>\n<li>Posts from which the employee is excluded.</li>\n<li>Other considerations, such as performance limitations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Generated schedules:</strong> the program combines site requirements and personal data to optimize staffing. It avoids overtime. If an officer is missing, it considers travel time from home, scheduled hours, and the overtime policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Proprietary force:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The paid holidays of each officer must be calculated. Some collective agreements allow refusing to work a holiday: pay attention to the text of the agreement.</li>\n<li>Include sick leave. The force’s average over the last two years serves to estimate future leave.</li>\n<li>Look at its actual distribution: more time off is taken in the winter months.</li>\n<li>Other absences: jury duty, personal reasons, or emergencies. In a proprietary force they are usually covered with overtime.</li>\n<li>In small forces that is expensive. Besides the extra pay, it costs fatigue, damaged morale, and less alertness.</li>\n<li>Those costs may not justify the apparent savings of a very tight base schedule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Contract force:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Client and agency agree on the base schedule: the total officer-hours per week. The agency must place officers at the posts to cover them.</li>\n<li>The problem passes from the client to the agency, but resolving schedule conflicts and absences is not necessarily easier.</li>\n<li>In theory, the agency has a broad personnel base and pulls officers from other clients to cover unscheduled absences.</li>\n<li>In practice, the replacement may never have been at that site. They do not learn the post in an hour or two and an inexperienced officer can do more harm than good.</li>\n<li>If relief is required from officers regularly assigned to the site, overtime helps.</li>\n<li>On large accounts, the agency can periodically assign personnel from other clients to occasional relief shifts, so they gain experience at the site.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Maximizing the straight-time schedule:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Overlapping shifts in a planned way covers peaks without overtime.</li>\n<li>If the eight-hour shift includes a 30-minute or one-hour meal, the presence is extended by that same time.</li>\n<li>Example: a 0700 to 1500 shift with a one-hour meal ends at 1600. If the second shift is from 1500 to 2300, the two shifts coincide between 1500 and 1600.</li>\n<li>That overlap covers peaks such as plant shift changes or office breaks. But during the meal the officer leaves their duties.</li>\n<li>The meal may be unpaid, depending on company policy or the collective agreement.</li>\n<li>Planning meals is difficult in small forces or ones with only fixed posts. Solutions: creative use of patrol officers, temporary closing of fixed posts, or a supervisor covering the breaks.</li>\n<li>With a collective agreement, regularly using the supervisor for officer work can lead to a formal grievance.</li>\n<li>Officers must note their meals in the log. In the event of an incident, that makes it possible to know whether they were eating or should have been at the post.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e10\">11. General orders (1.10)</h3><ul>\n<li>General orders have been used by the military police, municipal and state police, and contract security companies.</li>\n<li>They are canons or bodies of principles for protection officers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Common general orders:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Familiarity, fraternization, and unnecessary conversation on duty are prohibited. The professional is objective and favors no one. At a post or on patrol the officer must be vigilant, and hanging out with friends does not help.</li>\n<li>Maintain military or professional bearing and keep the uniform neat. Applies mainly to uniformed personnel.</li>\n<li>Plainclothes officers must also have professional bearing (retail loss prevention, doormen, and executive protection specialists). Friendly with the customer, but projecting knowledge, precision, and commitment.</li>\n<li>Smoking and eating on duty are prohibited. It is done in a private, secluded place: dining room, break area, or ready room.</li>\n<li>Every statement to the media is made by the designated spokesperson or the public information officer (PIO). Officers do not make statements.</li>\n<li>Officers must know the operation and structure of the organization and support it. A poorly made statement can cause panic, ruin reputations, or sabotage investigations.</li>\n<li>Enforce the rules with diplomacy: respect, civility, and authority only when needed. Without abusing the position.</li>\n<li>The officer is both a compliance agent of property management and a representative of management.</li>\n<li>Use the employer’s uniform and equipment only when and where authorized. It is a problem if they work for a second employer or stop at public places on the commute.</li>\n<li>Report any criminal conviction to the employer immediately. It may be a license requirement: a state board may revoke it.</li>\n<li>Remain at the post unless properly relieved or otherwise instructed. No post is left “open,” except perhaps in a serious emergency. Covering certain critical posts without a break is an operational necessity.</li>\n<li>When leaving the post, fully brief the relief and pass on the orders received. This way all the intelligence and the day’s instructions are handed over.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: the numbering above is from this summary; the book presents nine orders in bullets (bearing, media, and diplomacy are broken out here into two points each).</p><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e11\">12. Post orders (1.11)</h3><p>Post orders are the most important written instructions of the security force. Functions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>They express the policies of the protected company.</li>\n<li>They summarize the officer’s required duties.</li>\n<li>They avoid the problems of verbal instructions.</li>\n<li>They provide the basis for site-specific training.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>They are key in serious incidents that call into question an officer’s integrity, competence, or ability.</li>\n<li>Not every scenario can be described. Innovation, flexibility, and improvisation are valuable qualities.</li>\n<li>But it is a sign of poor management when an officer acts with poor judgment in a foreseeable incident for which there were no clear instructions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Criteria for drafting them:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Criterion</th>\n<th>Detail</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One subject per order</td>\n<td>Makes it possible to find a policy or procedure quickly. Makes it easier to revise or cancel it when circumstances change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>As brief as possible</td>\n<td>It is an action document: what must be achieved and when. The why is explained in other parts of the training</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple, easy-to-understand terms</td>\n<td>Basic version of the local language. Reading time is inversely proportional to comprehension. That time depends on the structure of the text and the reader’s skill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Vocabulary</td>\n<td>Plain meaning, without jargon, nuance, or ambiguity. <strong>Three-reader test:</strong> if three people who know the company do not agree on the meaning, rewrite it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detailed index</td>\n<td>Cross-referenced index, with each subject listed in several ways. Example: “Snow removal, emergency procedures” and “Emergency procedures, snow removal”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>They must be at every post, up to date and accessible. They are the vital link between the client’s requirements and the officer’s ability to meet them.</li>\n<li>Fixed post: in a designated place. Vehicle patrol: inside the vehicle.</li>\n<li>Foot patrol: carrying many orders is awkward. The officer can use company electronic devices with access to the orders, reports, and information exchange.</li>\n<li>If on paper: a reduced version, only for that post, pocket size for the uniform.</li>\n<li>Each set of orders is reviewed periodically to keep it current and complete.</li>\n<li>They require a coordinated effort to avoid contradictory orders. The risk grows with complex systems, electronic reports, or many posts.</li>\n<li>Management must always maintain a single coherent set of instructions.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e12\">13. Supervision (1.12)</h3><ul>\n<li>Shift supervisors train and direct the shift’s officers. They are front-line managers and key in mentoring, staff turnover, and satisfaction.</li>\n<li>They lead teams that respond to highly varied situations. They must understand the site requirements, management’s needs, and their own role.</li>\n<li><strong>The quality of asset protection is a direct function of local training and local supervision.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Forms of on-site training:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The supervisor observes the officer and gives feedback when something was not right or the officer hesitated.</li>\n<li>New material for one officer or the whole shift. Common practice: a staff training at the start and end of each shift. In those minutes a topic is explained, such as the essence of a new order, and the officers are asked to read it during the shift.</li>\n<li>New personnel: if there is opportunity and budget, pair them for a long time with the supervisor or an experienced patrol officer. Responsibilities do not decrease when someone new arrives.</li>\n<li><strong>The quality of training is directly proportional to its quantity.</strong></li>\n<li>Training log sheets: they break the site’s responsibilities down into tasks. Trainer and trainee initial each completed task.</li>\n<li>Those sheets can serve as the basis for post order checklists that patrol officers carry with them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>To check that a new order was read and to reinforce it, on the visit to the post the supervisor:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Asks one or more specific questions about the order.</li>\n<li>Observes the officer in a real situation where it applies.</li>\n<li>Poses a hypothetical situation that requires showing practical knowledge of the order.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>It takes a few minutes and should be a routine part of every post visit.</li>\n<li>The supervisor must visit every post. If they stay at the command post for most of the shift, performance at the other posts worsens and problems increase.</li>\n<li>Radios, public address, and cell phones eliminate the need to stay at a fixed post.</li>\n<li>Regular but unscheduled visits to all posts, with instant communication with the force.</li>\n<li>The supervisor observes the condition of the post, the officer, and the environment and the level of activity. By watching the route between posts, the supervisor also performs a complementary patrol.</li>\n<li><strong>Better alternative</strong>, unless the collective agreement prohibits it: the supervisor relieves at the post while the officer is away. It gives the best view of what happens there.</li>\n<li>Supervisors need special training in management, human relations, interpersonal communication, labor and criminal law, emergency response, and other topics.</li>\n<li>That training should be given, where possible, before promotion, and continue while they hold the position.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Evaluations (1.12.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Constant challenge: without an incident that shows very good or very poor performance, a shift’s work may never be evaluated. That complicates salary review.</li>\n<li>Partial solution: the supervisor evaluates and records performance after each post visit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The evaluation must include at a minimum:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Personal appearance and condition of the officer.</li>\n<li>Physical condition of the post.</li>\n<li>Availability and condition of all required personnel and equipment, including the post orders.</li>\n<li>Quality of the officer’s response to questions or training situations.</li>\n<li>Quality of their response to real situations that arise during the visit.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Everything is recorded and passed to a force management program.</li>\n<li>When officers or shifts change, different supervisors make independent observations.</li>\n<li>The manager reviews performance and ratings at regular intervals to decide whether more training or counseling is needed.</li>\n<li>Evaluations must give regular feedback, positive and negative, to officers and supervisors.</li>\n<li><strong>Coaching:</strong> a two-way communication process to influence and develop the employee’s skills, motivation, attitude, judgment, ability to perform, and willingness to contribute.</li>\n<li>Coaching and leadership go hand in hand. Two-way communication gives the employee a voice and raises commitment and retention.</li>\n<li>Conscious, regular coaching shows that the manager cares about the person and builds trust (Tarallo 2020).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e13\">14. Officer reports (1.13)</h3><p>It is said that officers are the “eyes and ears of management,” but their information often does not reach it. Causes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Those who choose security may be action-oriented, not word-oriented.</li>\n<li>What is reported does not seem to be acknowledged or acted on.</li>\n<li>The reporting process does not encourage processing the facts carefully.</li>\n<li>The organization lacks the infrastructure to collect data.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical empty report: “Came on 0700. Nothing unusual. Relieved 1500.”</li>\n<li>Several days like that suggest the post is unnecessary, that the officer is not trained, or that the officer does not report well.</li>\n<li>It is advisable to ask the officer not to write that the area is secure, but what they did to secure it.</li>\n<li>Post orders must require reporting specific incidents: key handovers, escorts, and assistance to contractors.</li>\n<li>Forms that require positive statements are better than those that ask for writing a narrative.</li>\n<li>Reports are filed electronically so they can be searched and sorted. A web platform improves access and makes the program more transparent to management.</li>\n<li>This matters especially with contract security: the client sees exactly what happens. It also motivates the force.</li>\n<li>The manager periodically uses the accumulated information to evaluate and reinforce performance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Security log (1.13.1)</h4>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> the security log is the central reporting document. It is the primary record of events significant to the protection of the facility, made by the officers contemporaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Database log:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is configured in incident management software. The force’s database can be relational to the main incident database.</li>\n<li>It allows retrieving all events for a place, date, and time in an easy-to-understand format.</li>\n<li>Trick: a drop-down menu of incident types, which the manager refines over time according to the incidents that actually occur.</li>\n<li>Automated format: date and time are generated automatically. The officer logs in with a unique identifier and password.</li>\n<li>Each event receives a unique, consecutive number. It is recorded in real time and is immediately available to supervisors and managers, contract and proprietary.</li>\n<li>It is captured from a desktop or laptop computer, terminal, or handheld device.</li>\n<li>The officer can view their entries, but not edit them. Changes or corrections go in as later entries. Editing is reserved for higher-level supervisors.</li>\n<li>Database and reporting functions can be expensive, but they are a great tool. They should be included if possible.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Manual log format:</strong> uncommon, but it still exists. Whether manual or electronic, there are two kinds:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Main or control log</td>\n<td>Cumulative, consecutive history of the significant events of all shifts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post log</td>\n<td>Local record of the events of a specific post</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Example: if the post must count people or vehicles, the counts go in the post log, as a historical fact.</li>\n<li>The main log notes that counts were due that day and refers to the existence of that record.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each entry must include:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Entry number: makes it possible to locate or cite it.</li>\n<li>Day of the week: normally abbreviated.</li>\n<li>Time of day: in military time.</li>\n<li>Category label: one or two words on the nature of the entry, for example “Weather.” Allows scanning the page quickly.</li>\n<li>Description of the incident or event: brief, often with symbols or codes.</li>\n<li>Reference: name, date, number, or other identification of another document with more information, for example a detailed complaint.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Day</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n<th>Ref.</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>36</td>\n<td>Mon.</td>\n<td>0700</td>\n<td>Weather</td>\n<td>75 °F, clear and sunny</td>\n<td>None</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>So that they are not altered or deleted, manual entries are written in ink and in consecutive order.</li>\n<li>Every correction is made with a new event number, as a separate entry.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Use of reports and logs by management (1.13.2)</h4>\n<p>In addition to their historical and audit value, they serve general management as an authoritative source to prove:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Weather conditions on a date.</li>\n<li>Receipt of a telephone call.</li>\n<li>Exact time of an event.</li>\n<li>Presence of specific people at the facility.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Legal admissibility:</strong> they are admissible as records made in the regular course of business. That is why they are exceptions to the rule excluding hearsay testimony. This matters in litigation, arbitration, or administrative matters where the entry is the only evidence. Requirements:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Kept regularly.</li>\n<li>Kept by a person as part of their regular duties.</li>\n<li>Recording a fact that the recorder knew personally, or that was reported by someone with personal knowledge and a duty to report.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Data capture and trend analysis:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They help the manager measure the program’s effectiveness and adjust it: they show what works and what does not.</li>\n<li>They can justify the department’s existence by detailing incidents and staff responses.</li>\n<li>Trend analysis indicates whether events form a trend. If so, the program is modified to address it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Failure to use reports and logs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Management appearing not to read them is a source of officer dissatisfaction.</li>\n<li>The manager must read them on time, recognize what is important, and order the correction.</li>\n<li>Case: an officer reports a broken exit light and it is still not fixed weeks later. The officer will think no one cares and will stop reporting.</li>\n<li>Scheduling and controlling the force is of utmost importance. Allocating human resources well is a fundamental responsibility of the manager.</li>\n<li>As personnel costs keep rising, the maximum value must be obtained from them.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e14\">15. Improving officer performance (1.14)</h3><ul>\n<li>Functions range from the routine to the highly complex. Officers usually work alone and with little direct supervision.</li>\n<li>They take risks that other employees are asked to avoid and deal with the full spectrum of human behavior.</li>\n<li>Their decisions affect the well-being of people and millions of dollars in physical assets.</li>\n<li>Because of the weight of first impressions, an inefficient or indifferent officer harms visitors, coworkers, and staff.</li>\n<li>Good officer performance is critical fiscally and humanly. The consequences of poor personnel development become apparent as soon as a serious incident occurs.</li>\n<li><strong>Excellence requires:</strong> the traditional (pay, supervision, and deployment) plus job analysis, training techniques, the work environment (including corporate and departmental cultures), and ergonomics.</li>\n<li>The manager and supervisor must detect deficiencies and decide what correction to apply and when.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Vigilant performance (1.14.1)</h4>\n<p>The term vigilant performance has a special meaning in security. Vigilance is described in three contexts:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Context</th>\n<th>Definition of vigilance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dictionary</td>\n<td>Keen attention to detect danger; caution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physiology</td>\n<td>Overall responsiveness of the nervous system to external (sensory) and internal (muscles, tendons, and joints) stimuli</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychology</td>\n<td>Unspecified function of the central nervous system. Allows responding effectively to specific, infrequent, uncertain, and often low-intensity stimuli, in a monotonous environment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>In part it is an expectation that needs cultivation and opportunities. But it is the officer who must recognize, assume, and fulfill it. That is why personnel selection is vital.</li>\n<li><strong>Vigilance fatigue:</strong> occurs when the brain becomes saturated with the task. Main theory: cognitive resources are depleted.</li>\n<li>Letting the mind wander, that is, activating the default mode network, helps restore blood flow to the area of the brain that does the task.</li>\n<li>When nothing happens during an entire shift, fatigue can increase (Stowell 2017).</li>\n<li><strong>Stress:</strong> generates behaviors that damage performance and physical and mental health.</li>\n<li>A study for the US National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH) was carried out by the University of Michigan’s Institute for Social Research.</li>\n<li>That study linked stress with: low use of one’s own abilities, low participation, low job complexity, few opportunities for advancement, and the lowest level of social support.</li>\n<li>According to the book, that description fits many security officer positions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Elements that affect vigilant performance:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Design of the work area, especially space, light, heat, and noise. Example: in a booth without good heating or air conditioning, the officer thinks more about temperature than the task. It also requires suitable chairs and desks.</li>\n<li>Human engineering of the tasks and equipment handling. People often perform only as well as their equipment or the design of their task allows.</li>\n<li>Visual and auditory acuity: the physical ability to observe an area by sight and hearing.</li>\n<li>Detection, identification, and recognition abilities. Frequently reviewing expected events improves vigilance and detection.</li>\n<li>Attention and information processing: the ability to detect an event and respond as desired.</li>\n<li>Job analysis: systematic collection and recording of information about the purpose of the job, its main functions, its conditions, the contacts needed, and the knowledge, skills, and abilities required (Bartol 1998). One detailed analysis per post; there must be a fit between officer, post, and company expectations.</li>\n<li>Training: general (report writing, patrol techniques, emergency response) and site- and equipment-specific, with testing. Neglecting it certainly harms vigilance.</li>\n<li>Work environment, including corporate and departmental cultures. Since each department is managed differently, the officer may receive contradictory messages or see unequal treatment. It happens especially in departments with both proprietary and contract officers.</li>\n<li>Quality control: sets the expectations of the department and the organization. Vigilant performance is defined through expectations.</li>\n<li>Morale: personnel must feel good about themselves and their work. Low morale causes complacency, inattention, more turnover, tardiness, absenteeism, and excessive sick leave.</li>\n<li>Management advocacy: management reinforces vigilant behavior by advocating for the officers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>ASIS CRISP (Connecting Research in Security to Practice) report: <em>Fatigue Effects and Countermeasures in 24/7 Security Operations</em> (Miller 2010).</strong> Finding: automation often puts the person in a monitoring role, which is not handled well. Problems of automation and human monitoring:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>More monitoring load for the operator, with associated attention lapses.</li>\n<li>A lot of operator responsibility with little to do.</li>\n<li>Loss of the operator’s manual skill.</li>\n<li>“Out-of-the-loop” operator problems.</li>\n<li>Automation replaces human pattern recognition with less competent sensors.</li>\n<li>“Cry wolf” syndrome: many false alarms make the operator ignore signs of system failure. Failure detection falls to almost zero.</li>\n<li>Inadequate displays that support neither optimal performance nor individual differences in staying alert.</li>\n<li>Automation problems that are misdiagnosed, leading to erroneous corrective measures.</li>\n<li>Not recognizing subtle vigilance problems until there have already been many automation-related accidents.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Miller’s findings on fatigue (police and other shift workers):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Humans are incompatible with night work; schedule changes cause problems; fatigue can be acute or chronic.</li>\n<li>The accident rate rises sharply around 0300. It also rises at 1500, because of circadian rhythms.</li>\n<li>Anyone who has been awake for more than 17 hours probably reaches a dangerous level of mental fatigue.</li>\n<li>Chronic fatigue appears when sleep debt accumulates. Both kinds of fatigue reduce alertness and cognitive ability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Countermeasures:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reassess how officers are viewed as a human asset.</li>\n<li>Review their interaction with technology, so that it improves their performance and does not reduce it.</li>\n<li>Train managers and subordinates on the challenges of fatigue and its countermeasures.</li>\n<li>Schedules with real rest: at least two nights of unrestricted sleep between shift changes, and limits on consecutive days and hours per shift.</li>\n<li>Raising lighting at night and keeping the environment cool increases alertness.</li>\n<li>Consider allowing naps where possible, although it clashes with traditional management in much of the world.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fixed post, environment, and sleeping on post:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The space of a fixed post can reduce vigilance. Often the design and location of the perimeter booth do not help seeing and hearing.</li>\n<li>Poor ventilation causes large changes in indoor temperature: unhealthy at worst and uncomfortable at best.</li>\n<li>Prolonged high temperatures can cause a serious loss of vigilance and drowsiness.</li>\n<li>The officer usually works alone and sometimes speaks only with the supervisor by telephone. Inactivity and isolation induce sleep even in the healthiest person: it is a natural process.</li>\n<li>There may be mitigating factors when an officer is found asleep. If discipline is warranted, the supervisor must act promptly and sensitively.</li>\n<li>In such cases a simple failure analysis checklist helps. The manager adapts it to achieve equitable discipline and to know what to change in the work area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.3 — Checklist for analyzing performance failures: Sleeping on Post:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the officer healthy?</li>\n<li>Did the officer take any medication that causes drowsiness or reduces alertness?</li>\n<li>How many hours did the officer sleep in the 24 hours before taking the post?</li>\n<li>Were there shift schedule changes that affected the officer?</li>\n<li>How many hours had the officer been awake before reporting for work?</li>\n<li>How many hours had the officer been at the post before falling asleep?</li>\n<li>What was the indoor temperature in the area when the officer was found?</li>\n<li>What was the lighting level in the area when the officer was found?</li>\n<li>Did the officer have active duties at the post or was there forced inactivity?</li>\n<li>How was “sleeping on post” detected or reported?</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Even a well-designed system can cause failures if it ignores human behavior.</li>\n<li>Example: if several signals or response functions coincide, the officer gets confused and may make a serious error. Behavioral theory and practice confirm it.</li>\n<li>Passive, constant monitoring of video surveillance quickly reduces vigilance and can prevent detecting events.</li>\n<li>Corrections: simulate the worst case and create a drill response sequence to ensure an adequate response.</li>\n<li>Other measures: periodic patrols, movement or rotation; having the officer call the supervisor to check in; automated calls to confirm status; other strategies that vary the routine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Behavioral theories (1.14.2)</h4>\n<p>Theories useful for motivating officers and keeping them engaged:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Author</th>\n<th>Theory</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Abraham Maslow</td>\n<td>Hierarchy of needs: each person is motivated according to the level they are at, starting with the most basic. A satisfied need no longer motivates, so giving more of the same may improve nothing. Recognition of the employee can be a powerful positive influence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Douglas McGregor</td>\n<td>The supervisor treats subordinates according to contradictory assumptions. Theory X: the worker is lazy by nature and avoids responsibility. Theory Y: work is natural and the worker can be self-directed, motivated, and creative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frederick Herzberg</td>\n<td>Two-dimension model: hygiene or maintenance factors and satisfiers or motivators. Hygiene factors appeal to the person’s avoidance mechanism. Motivators make the work more challenging and lead to personal growth. The manager balances both and respects individual preferences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chris Argyris</td>\n<td>Traditional principles, structures, and procedures are incompatible with the employee’s mental health. They make the employee passive and submissive, with little control over the work. The employee then becomes apathetic, uses self-protective defenses, or fights the system</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warren Bennis</td>\n<td>The organization can be an adaptive, innovative system that solves problems in highly changing environments. Future organizations will be more inclusive and will have five traits (below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Maslow’s levels (from base to top):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Physiological: survival, such as food, shelter, and sleep.</li>\n<li>Safety and security: economic, physical, and mental well-being.</li>\n<li>Social or affiliation: desire to be loved and accepted.</li>\n<li>Ego: knowledge, independence, achievement, status, self-confidence, and the respect of others.</li>\n<li>Self-actualization: inner drive to develop one’s own potential.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Traits of the future organization according to Bennis:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Integration: synthesize the employee’s needs and management’s objectives.</li>\n<li>Social influence: distribute authority and power effectively.</li>\n<li>Collaboration: manage and resolve conflicts.</li>\n<li>Adaptation: respond well to the changes imposed by the environment.</li>\n<li>Revitalization: handle the problems of growth and decay.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The officer’s knowledge, competence, and personality must be matched to the assignment.</li>\n<li>One method is to consider whether the officer is introverted or extroverted. The introvert may adapt better to monotonous tasks; the extrovert likes to move around.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Work environment (1.14.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Officers operate in highly varied and changing situations. They use automated systems: visitor management, geographic information systems (GIS), and communications.</li>\n<li>There are sedentary posts and others with long foot or vehicle patrols.</li>\n<li>All share environmental factors that may or may not be modifiable: space, heat, light, and noise.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Mechanical environment:</strong> control centers can be the core of one or several sites. Many workstations and equipment that monitor security and building systems make them very demanding.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>System</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Video surveillance systems</td>\n<td>Extends the officer’s effectiveness, but watching monitors is passive, almost hypnotic. At the same time, observing 20 monitors can be very stressful. Always consider the ergonomics of the console</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— System design</td>\n<td>Include display and annunciation functions. Showing several cameras in sequence reduces the number of monitors and the hypnotic effect. A video motion detector alerts to changes in the image. Rotate personnel out of this environment: no one stays alert forever</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Artificial intelligence</td>\n<td>Artificial intelligence and machine learning improve video analytics: object detection, image classification, visual tracking, and action recognition. Advanced learning creates behavior probability maps, for example to detect fights and count people (Guan 2018)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access control systems</td>\n<td>Symbol of technological advance. Connects with video surveillance and building management. Its design must favor surveillance: if there is no way to verify door alarms, officers soon learn to ignore them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Life safety systems</td>\n<td>Poorly managed fire alarms quickly reduce vigilance. They raise legitimate and illegitimate alarms to unacceptable levels. Faced with false alarms, people learn to ignore danger. Managing them requires diligence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Other systems</td>\n<td>Because it is on duty 24 hours, security ends up monitoring other departments’ systems by default or serving as after-hours backup. Examples: elevator monitoring, lighting control, inspection of mechanical areas, and heating, ventilation, and air conditioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>— Internet of things</td>\n<td>With the Internet of things (IoT) systems interconnect and generate enormous volumes of data. Many organizations monitor them remotely or in central systems where officers assist</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Without training, testing, and an assessment of whether personnel can take on the extra load, the officer feels overloaded and undervalued.</li>\n<li>Those two factors can lead to performance failures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Physical environment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When analyzing the operation, physical stress must be considered. Standards vary by assignment, but the factors in Figure 1.4 occur in almost all posts.</li>\n<li>The physical capacity of the uniformed officer is assessed with those standards. When hiring, the prior screening is fairer and more efficient with a detailed list of physical requirements.</li>\n<li>Before applying job enrichment, each post is analyzed and those with similar physical demands are grouped.</li>\n<li>Example: several fixed posts may share factors, while foot or vehicle patrols may be very different.</li>\n<li>Then each physical demand factor is analyzed according to the average exposure in an eight-hour shift or in an average week.</li>\n<li>If on one shift there is a lot of standing and on another a lot of walking in the same post, both factors can be noted with the maximum of eight hours.</li>\n<li>A complete analysis documents requirements based on the core content of the job. Without it, they could appear contrary to the anti-discrimination provisions of the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA).</li>\n<li>Stress is reduced by modifying the post. Example: split patrols so the officer spends four hours at a fixed post and the rest patrolling.</li>\n<li>Other times it requires larger changes or improving the environment: reducing noise, heat, cold, wind, or dust.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.4 — Physical Demand Analysis of the officer post:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Physical or sensory factor</th>\n<th>Value in the figure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Total hours on feet</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standing</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Walking on level surfaces</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Climbing stairs or ramps</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stooping or squatting</td>\n<td>&lt;1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Back and hip flexion (30°–90°)</td>\n<td>&lt;1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elbow flexion (both arms)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reaching above shoulders</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reaching below shoulders</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knee flexion</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lifting, carrying, pushing, or pulling 16 to 40 lb (7 to 18 kg) from the floor to four feet high</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fine manipulation (fingering: pinching or manual dexterity)</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gross manipulation, grasping, or hand movement</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Figure note: the officer may have to help lift or move up to 200 lb (90 kg) if an employee becomes ill or injured in an emergency.</li>\n<li>Minimum requirements: binocular vision correctable to 20/30; speech without serious impediment; three-dimensional vision; hearing a voice at 20 feet (6 m) and a whisper at 10 feet (3 m); color vision.</li>\n<li>Common exposures: operating (motorized equipment and vehicles; hand-held power tools); walking (unprepared walking surfaces; obstacles); heights (ladders; elevated test platforms).</li>\n<li>More exposures: temperatures above and below normal, from indoor and outdoor patrols at any hour and in any weather; skin irritants (solvents; cleaning products); respiratory irritants (dust from surfaces and roads).</li>\n<li>Pending: in the extracted text the values column has no legible unit label (its header appears as “Proprietary/In-house security officers,” apparently a layout error). The figures follow the order of the text and appear to be hours per shift (6 on feet = 4 standing + 2 walking).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Fixed or static posts:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Functions that maintain vigilance can be created: alarm test sequences, badge making, key control, and record keeping.</li>\n<li><strong>The most effective way to maintain vigilance is to rotate the officer between fixed post and patrol, on foot and mobile.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Foot patrols:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are physically demanding and are sometimes seen as punishment or an unrewarding assignment.</li>\n<li>Typical failures: the skipped or half-done round. Causes: fatigue, or relying too much on electronic alarms and believing the area does not need a human patrol.</li>\n<li>Another failure: the officer does not know what is expected of them in an incident, or decides not to act according to policy.</li>\n<li>Every organization has many minor functions. The manager can broaden the officer’s role to non-security tasks if that increases interest and improves performance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Vehicular or mobile patrols:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>They involve stopping, starting, and getting in and out of the vehicle frequently. Many injuries occur this way, from falls in the dark.</li>\n<li>Inside the vehicle, observation is very restricted and hearing is almost nullified.</li>\n<li>It is mitigated by using electric golf carts, bicycles, or other special vehicles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Human environment:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>There is no magic selection formula, because of the great variety of functions. Someone who succeeds in one assignment may fail in the next if knowledge, competence, and personality are not matched.</li>\n<li>The face-to-face interview remains the most important. But more and more companies invest in personality inventories, assessments, and tests.</li>\n<li>With those assessments, the officer understands their strengths and weaknesses and learns to be a better learner, employee, and communicator.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Myers-Briggs Type Indicator:</strong> defines four pairs of mental preferences. According to the book:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Preference</th>\n<th>Poles</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>How information is received</td>\n<td>Sensing: clear, tangible information, tied to direct experience. Intuition: abstract, conceptual information, with a big-picture view and future possibilities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>How one judges or decides</td>\n<td>Thinking: objective, logical, and analytical, focused on tasks and results; in a dispute seeks clarity and objective principles. Feeling: global, visceral, and value-based, attentive to the impact on others; in a dispute seeks harmony and collaboration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Energy consciousness</td>\n<td>Extroversion: energy from the outer world of people, things, and action; enjoys large groups; acts before reflecting. Introversion: energy from the inner world of ideas and concepts; prefers quiet activities; reflects before acting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Life management orientation</td>\n<td>The book says “sensing versus intuition”: attending to the five senses and seeing the world as it is (practical, grounded, experience-based) versus interpreting and giving meaning (visionary or idealist, interested in the future or in timeless principles)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book repeats “sensing versus intuition” as the fourth pair. In the real Myers-Briggs indicator, the fourth pair is judging vs. perceiving; sensing versus intuition is only the first.</p>\n<p><strong>Big Five Model of Personality</strong>, Lussier and Achua 2004, p. 30. It classifies traits into five dimensions; each dimension groups several traits:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Surgency</td>\n<td>Leadership and extroversion. Also called dominance: wanting to be in charge and advancing through competition and influence. Someone weak in surgency is a follower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreeableness</td>\n<td>Ability to get along with others. Ranges from warm, calm, compassionate, friendly, and sociable to cold, difficult, uncompassionate, unfriendly, and unsociable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjustment</td>\n<td>Emotional stability, from strong to weak. High: calm under pressure, relaxed, secure, and positive. Low: loss of control, poor handling of pressure, nervous or insecure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conscientiousness</td>\n<td>Achievement traits. Ranges from responsible and dependable to irresponsible and undependable. Also suggests credibility, conformity, organization, and willingness to work hard for goals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Openness to experience</td>\n<td>Willingness to change and try new things</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Personality inventories do not give definitive answers. They only indicate possible strengths and weaknesses.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Personal communication, training, and supervision:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Officers depend on written instructions because they may not see a supervisor every day. Those instructions must be reinforced with personal communication.</li>\n<li>The supervisor organizes his or her schedule to have daily personal contact with the officers.</li>\n<li>If the officer's knowledge and competence are not constantly tested, it is impossible to ensure the protection of the facility.</li>\n<li>The inspecting supervisor asks about the details of the assignment and about the correct responses to incidents.</li>\n<li>People listen more when ideas touch their interests, desires, family, and work.</li>\n<li>Direct communication matters: with more intermediaries, the message is distorted, delayed, or misunderstood.</li>\n<li>What is used is better retained. If the supervisor demonstrates a task and the officer repeats it, recall lasts much longer than with verbal instructions alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recommendations for communication and training:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Base training on needs: the supervisor detects gaps in knowledge and corrects them.</li>\n<li>Recognize individual differences. They influence the pace of learning: intelligence, desire to learn, knowledge, aptitudes, interests, motives, attitudes, emotions, and learning capacity.</li>\n<li>(Continuation of point 2) Set reasonable standards for everyone, knowing that not everyone performs equally. Not everyone learns the same thing at the same age; individualized instruction helps each person give his or her best at the time.</li>\n<li>Maximize motivation: understand drives and desires; set meaningful standards together; show personal interest; give suitable incentives; set a good example; use praise, reward, and recognition; instruct clearly and completely.</li>\n<li>Adopt the learner's point of view: put yourself in the learner's place and adapt the instruction. Asking learners what they feel they need to learn also gives good results.</li>\n<li>Give timely feedback: everyone wants to know how they are doing. Systematic progress reports can greatly speed up learning.</li>\n<li>Expect difficulties: learning is uneven; it rises quickly, plateaus, and rises again. Causes: motivation, effort, physiological conditions, or training method.</li>\n<li>Follow up: repeat and summarize. Give the information and then recap what was said.</li>\n<li>Learn to instruct: knowing how to do a job is not knowing how to teach it. It requires preparation, practice, and patience; knowing the principles of learning; mastering the job; applying the fundamentals of instruction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note: the book gives eight recommendations; here the second one is split into two points (2 and 3).</p>\n<p><strong>Changes in the work environment:</strong> many managers have problems when changing shifts. An officer who performed well for months or years on one shift may not adapt to the same post on another shift. Post transfers, even on the same shift, cause the same thing. The officer usually adapts in a few days.</p>\n<p><strong>Interaction with other employees:</strong> it must be facilitated on an ongoing basis. The officer may be seen as something negative because of being an authority figure. Minor, positive daily interactions reduce hostility and alienation.</p>\n<p><strong>Improving observation capability:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The post must be designed to maximize observation of the protected area. Alarms and video surveillance extend the reach.</li>\n<li>The type of glass matters. Glare from the sun or artificial light is an important factor in fatigue, and a very common one.</li>\n<li>It gets worse at night: glare from artificial light greatly limits the field of observation.</li>\n<li>High-efficiency anti-glare coatings practically eliminate glare as an obstacle.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Testing and post-testing:</strong> competence in a skill is not maintained without random performance tests and feedback. They can be simple or complex.</p>\n<p><strong>Figure 1.5 — Test/Post-Test Routine:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Step</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Test 1</td>\n<td>An external source sends the test signal at random times, but at least once per shift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test 2</td>\n<td>The sender records the exact time at which the signal is transmitted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test 3</td>\n<td>The officer who receives it presses the acknowledge button, which records only the date and time of receipt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test 4</td>\n<td>The officer calls a designated internal number and reports the test to a message recorder, using a drill checklist. Or calls the sender directly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-test 1</td>\n<td>The transmission time is compared with the reception time recorded on the alarm printer tape</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-test 2</td>\n<td>The accuracy and clarity of the message recorded by the officer are reviewed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-test 3</td>\n<td>The accuracy and completeness of the drill checklist are reviewed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-test 4</td>\n<td>The results are discussed with the officer no later than his or her next scheduled shift</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Analyzing job performance:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Performance evaluation is essential. Common criteria: task performance, general job conduct, and positive and negative traits related to performance.</li>\n<li>Recommended minimum: one formal individual evaluation per year and an informal review at six months. This way the employee knows how management sees him or her.</li>\n<li>Afterward: examine each post, identify frequent deficiencies and their causes. Correct first those with the greatest effect on performance.</li>\n<li>Only a well-identified skill deficiency is corrected or modified with additional training.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Serious misconduct</strong> that requires immediate attention:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Misconduct involving weapons.</li>\n<li>Use of unreasonable force. Example from the book: physically restraining a youth because a garden hose is missing.</li>\n<li>Conduct that takes the officer away from his or her post without justification.</li>\n<li>Accusations of criminal conduct: fraud, theft, or searching a person who revoked consent.</li>\n<li>Conduct that significantly interferes with the ability to perform duties.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Among the most serious offenses: alcohol or drugs on duty. They are especially serious if the officer carries weapons.</li>\n<li>Leaving the post without adequate notice and without sufficient reason is serious misconduct. If the officer becomes ill and needs urgent care, he or she must first notify a supervisor.</li>\n<li>All of these offenses can be covered in a single policy statement: failure to comply with any of them will be treated as serious misconduct.</li>\n<li>That statement lays the groundwork for dismissal or other serious sanctions.</li>\n<li>Under common law, deliberate acts of employees, including officers, can be sued over as intentional torts.</li>\n<li>If a union represents the force and the contract has a just-cause discharge clause, a clear statement is needed to support severe sanctions.</li>\n<li>Appendix 1A is a model disciplinary policy (violations and consequences). Appendix 1B is a simplified version to verify that the officer knows the rules.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Offsetting the effects of task simplification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Reducing distractions made many posts too simple and monotonous. Officers need variety.</li>\n<li>The supervisor can assign brief, practical special tasks. Example: inspect all the locks in a building and report faults.</li>\n<li>Even on a fixed post there is room for tasks that keep vigilance up: alarm testing, badge making, and record keeping.</li>\n<li>If the manager covered each post for several shifts a year, he or she would detect and solve many of these problems early.</li>\n<li>Organizations change constantly and that affects the officer's duties. Interesting and rewarding posts must be created.</li>\n<li>Most officers respond well to a manager who communicates the value of their work to them.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e15\">16. Reinforcing Officers with Robotics (1.15)</h3><ul>\n<li>Every technology has pros and cons. The security professional must understand it and evaluate the risks and benefits of incorporating it into the program.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Better for humans</th>\n<th>Better for technology</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tasks that require deciding, perceiving, or changing course on the fly (pivot)</td>\n<td>Monotonous, repetitive, simple tasks, in which humans get bored or become complacent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> robotics is the branch of technology that deals with the design, construction, operation, and application of robots. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Unmanned vehicles:<ul>\n<li>UAV (Unmanned Aerial Vehicle), also called UAS (Unmanned Aerial System) or drone;</li>\n<li>UGV (Unmanned Ground Vehicle);</li>\n<li>USV (Unmanned Surface Vehicle, that is, waterborne).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Robots: essentially a type of UGV that resembles a human (although this is not required).</li>\n<li>All of them can be remotely operated or autonomous (they guide themselves, without a pilot).</li>\n<li>Risk-benefit of the autonomous: the advantage of not requiring human intervention versus the risk of damage or injury if the autonomy is not perfect.</li>\n<li>The decision depends on each organization's risk appetite and on the perception of its stakeholders, for example whether they believe a human pilot raises or lowers the risk of an accident.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>General use cases for robotics:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Patrolling the perimeter or outdoor areas of a campus (university, commercial, or government).</li>\n<li>Static post (e.g., main entrance) that transmits video and monitors chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear, and explosive (CBRNE) materials.</li>\n<li>Video patrols of rights-of-way, pipelines, or national borders.</li>\n<li>Video monitoring of small areas: prison yards, parking lots, or substations.</li>\n<li>Investigation and confirmation of CBRNE materials.</li>\n<li>Mobile support to first responders.</li>\n<li>Investigation and triage of alarms at remote locations.</li>\n<li>Remote inspections of assets.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Functions more common in robots than in other unmanned vehicles</strong> (robots can also be remotely operated or autonomous and carry similar sensors):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>License plate recognition.</li>\n<li>Facial recognition.</li>\n<li>Object detection and recognition.</li>\n<li>Other video analytics (effective when the robot is not moving).</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Audio: it is common in robots, although not always live and two-way with a human, as in unmanned vehicles. They usually have prerecorded messages according to what they detect.</li>\n<li>Examples: if someone tries to lift it, it announces that it is reporting the problem and sends a human response. If it detects a chemical or biological release, it repeats a prerecorded warning.</li>\n<li>Common use: in controlled environments (inside the perimeter, often indoors) to complement officers.</li>\n<li>Their “cool factor” attracts more attention than a traditional officer. They are force multipliers that increase the visibility of security.</li>\n<li>It is so common that some robots are sold or rented by the hour to guard companies, matching their per-officer pricing model.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e16\">17. Conclusion (1.16)</h3><ul>\n<li>Security personnel are a vital component of almost every asset protection program.</li>\n<li>Their selection, training, and management determine the level of protection they can provide.</li>\n<li>Today more committed, better trained, and better qualified people are required than before. Getting the most out of them requires good management and comprehensive policies.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-1-e17\">Key Terms</h3><p>watchman · guard · security officer · protection officer · loss prevention officer · proprietary officer · contract officer · door supervisor · repossession agent · return on investment (ROI) · number of officers required · access control · patrol · foot patrol · vehicular patrol · escort · inspection · monitoring · security operations center · global operations center · duress alarm · emergency response · nuisance alarm · HAZMAT · disruptive people · crisis intervention training · self-insured · observe and report · targeted incidents · pre-attack behaviors · public relations · officious · special assignments · security awareness · officer qualifications · job description · Private Security Officer Selection and Training guideline · CAN/CGSB-133.1–2017 · Private Security Agencies (Regulation) Act · preemployment background screening · character · coachability · ethics · Protection Officer Code of Ethics · International Foundation for Protection Officers (IFPO) · site-specific needs · fatal force · deadly force · qualified gunsmith · qualified armorer · weapon retention · layers of protection · shoulder weapons · felonious attacks · less than lethal weapons · electroshock weapons · TASER · stun gun · stun baton · conducted electrical weapon (CEW) · conducted energy device (CED) · baton · impact weapon · aerosol irritants · CS · CN · OC (oleoresin capsicum) · tear gas · pepper spray · aftercare · use-of-force policy · accountability · dual access · defense-in-depth · security force continuum · officer presence · verbal communication · control holds and restraints · chemical agent · temporary incapacitation · body worn cameras · positional asphyxiation · tour devices · body armor · organizational structure · vertical (hierarchical) model · network (flattened, horizontal, open) model · proprietary versus contract officers · hybrid security force · principal-agent · low-bid · bid specifications · open post coverage · workers’ compensation · job oriented · career oriented · buy-in · peace officer status · deputization · indemnity bond · additional named insured · certificate of insurance · standard operating procedures (SOPs) · post orders · security officer post · fixed post · patrol post · necessary human being · person-trap · training and competency · turnover costs · post hours · post schedule · security officer schedule · straight time · premium pay · fair labor standards · rotating shifts · computerized scheduling · work site requirements · personnel database · skills inventory · maximizing the straight time schedule · grievance · general orders · public information officer (PIO) · ready room · supervision · training log sheets · post visit · evaluations · counseling · coaching · security officer reports · security log · database log · main (control) log · post log · entry number · category label · military time · regular course of business · hearsay · data capture and trend analysis · vigilant performance · vigilance fatigue · default mode network · NIOSH · job analysis · quality control · morale · management advocacy · CRISP (Connecting Research in Security to Practice) · out-of-the-loop · cry wolf syndrome · circadian rhythms · acute fatigue · chronic fatigue · sleep debt · sleeping on post · failure analysis checklist · behavioral theories · hierarchy of needs · self-actualization · Theory X · Theory Y · hygiene (maintenance) factors · motivators (satisfiers) · integration · social influence · collaboration · adaptation · revitalization · introvert · extrovert · work environment · mechanical environment · annunciation · video motion detector · machine learning · life safety systems · Internet of things (IoT) · physical environment · job enrichment · physical demand analysis · Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) · human environment · personality inventories · Myers-Briggs Type Indicator · sensing · intuition · thinking · feeling · energy consciousness · life management orientation · Big Five Model of Personality · surgency · agreeableness · adjustment · conscientiousness · openness to experience · testing and post-testing · test/post-test routine · performance appraisal · serious misconduct · intentional torts · just-cause discharge · task simplification · robotics · unmanned vehicles · UAV · UAS · UGV · USV · autonomous · risk appetite · CBRNE · triage · license plate recognition · force multiplier</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Context and industry"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Use of security officers"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. Basic functions"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Officer requirements"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. Training"},{"id":"e5","t":"6. Weapons"},{"id":"e6","t":"7. Uniforms and equipment"},{"id":"e7","t":"8. Organizational structure"},{"id":"e8","t":"9. Legal aspects"},{"id":"e9","t":"10. Scheduling and managing the officer force"},{"id":"e10","t":"11. General orders"},{"id":"e11","t":"12. Post orders"},{"id":"e12","t":"13. Supervision"},{"id":"e13","t":"14. Officer reports"},{"id":"e14","t":"15. Improving officer performance"},{"id":"e15","t":"16. Reinforcing Officers with Robotics"},{"id":"e16","t":"17. Conclusion"},{"id":"e17","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"1. Context and industry","h":"with the book’s own figures (1.1 million versus 665,000) the ratio is 1.65 to 1, not “almost two to one.” If asked, the book’s literal phrase is “almost two to one.”"},{"s":"6. Weapons","h":"the book writes “CS (2-chloroacetophenone)” and “CN (o-chlorobenzylidene).” This is reversed: CN is 2-chloroacetophenone and CS is o-chlorobenzylidene malononitrile."},{"s":"6. Weapons","h":"pepper spray appears at level 4 (chemical agent) and again as an example at level 5. It is an internal inconsistency in the book."},{"s":"15. Improving officer performance","h":"the book repeats “sensing versus intuition” as the fourth pair. In the real Myers-Briggs indicator, the fourth pair is judging vs. perceiving; sensing versus intuition is only the first."}]},"personnel-2":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-2-e0\">1. Introduction: Training and the Apprenticeship Model</h3><ul>\n<li>An employee cannot be expected to perform duties he or she was not trained for. Demanding professional performance requires ongoing training and development.</li>\n<li>Training and educating is an increasingly important and relatively new function of security departments.</li>\n<li>The complexity of the role demands extensive training in two phases: pre-assignment and on-the-job.</li>\n<li>U.S. police have long used apprenticeship programs: law enforcement explorers, cadets, community service, and internships. They serve for recruiting and as community outreach.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Four parallels between apprenticeship and security training:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Parallel</th>\n<th>Why it resembles apprenticeship</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>On-the-job training (OJT)</td>\n<td>Abbreviated form: easy to structure; it only requires an instructor and the tools or equipment of the job. It must be documented to ensure that critical knowledge is transmitted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Professional security certifications</td>\n<td>Simplified form: in general they do not require a master, but they do require and document a certain level of experience.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Field training officers (FTO)</td>\n<td>Common in the police: they mentor the new officer and assess whether he or she is suitable. Because they evaluate as well as teach, they require extensive planning and preparation by the instructor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Formal training based on the apprenticeship model</td>\n<td>Example: the British Security Industry Association (BSIA) helped create alarm installer apprentices. It is not for officers, but it shows how it could be done. Applicable to highly specialized officers, such as executive protection for celebrities or other VIPs.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e1\">2. Key Training Concepts (2.1)</h3><ul>\n<li>Setting up a training program requires persistence, humility, empathy, and creativity.</li>\n<li>The key to success when designing and implementing training is to understand and apply learning concepts well. Whoever trains must keep studying teaching, training, and adult learning theory.</li>\n<li>A dedicated trainer position allows the security department to provide training to other departments:<ul>\n<li>Hospital: certified training for nursing staff in nonviolent crisis intervention or management of aggressive behavior.</li>\n<li>Hotel: TIPS (Training for Intervention Procedures by Servers of Alcohol) certification for restaurant employees.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Concepts in this section: cognitive, affective, and psychomotor learning; retention and transfer; socialization; and education, training, and development.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.1 Learning (2.1.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> a positive change in a person's knowledge, skill, ability, or outlook.</li>\n<li>Basic principle: there are many learning styles and each person learns something different. There are visual learners, readers, and active learners (who need to participate in order to learn).</li>\n<li>People learn differently depending on life and career stage. A veteran or supervisor sees things differently than a novice; older people “see” differently than younger ones.</li>\n<li>An officer with a graduate degree understands management theory better. One with only work experience adapts ideas to the practical better.</li>\n<li>Instructional design cannot be adjusted to each individual, but it must combine several means of delivering information. It must be applied systematically and continuously throughout the employment relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three domains of learning:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Domain</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Content and how it is achieved</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognitive</td>\n<td>Knowledge-based: the intellectual part; amount of material learned and its theory. Key to solving problems: understanding a theory and applying it to practice.</td>\n<td>Requires a complete explanation of the topic. Classroom or video lectures must be supported with active learning: questions for reflection, pretest, valid and demanding posttest, and instructor or supervisor guidance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Affective</td>\n<td>Attitudinal or perceptual: changes how the person sees a situation.</td>\n<td>Understanding other cultures; dealing with people with disabilities; workplace safety compliance; adversaries' methods for obtaining information. Understanding loss and its effect on the organization; appreciating the adversary. Understanding aggressive behavior, the criminal mindset, and the speed of an assault (whoever does not understand it may become a victim). Essential in the face of terrorism, espionage, workplace safety, and relations with the various publics: customers, employees, vendors, police, emergency medical personnel, and hazardous materials (HAZMAT) teams. It must be integrated into the cognitive and the psychomotor.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Psychomotor</td>\n<td>Physical or practical skills.</td>\n<td>From equipment handling to defensive driving and emergency response. Requires repetition, practice, and refresher courses: regular defensive tactics and firearms, fire extinguishers every year, and first aid recertification. The person responsible must research the recognized refresher frequency for each skill and include it in the calendar.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Retention and transfer</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Retention:</strong> the amount of what was learned that the learner keeps over time. It requires reiteration.</li>\n<li>The greatest retention is achieved when the learner <strong>performs the task while listening to the instructor</strong>.</li>\n<li>Continuous learning favors retention and transfer.</li>\n<li><strong>Transfer:</strong> application on the job of the knowledge, skills, and abilities obtained in training. It is the relevance of what was learned to the work environment: it ensures that what is taught in the classroom is used.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2 Socialization (2.1.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> the process by which the employee learns and adopts the values of the workplace.</li>\n<li><strong>Formal:</strong> training is an important part (occupational or professional socialization); supervision is another key part. Management must convey a single, consistent message.</li>\n<li><strong>Informal:</strong> it always occurs and usually has more influence than the formal. Negative socialization from the peer group must be watched.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.3 Education, Training, and Development (2.1.3)</h4>\n<p>Formula from the book: <strong>Education + Training + Guided Experience = Development</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Supervisors and managers guide the officer's experience so that it is formative. That is why teaching, counseling, and coaching are crucial.</li>\n<li>The three concepts are distinct, related, and reinforce each other. All produce learning: a positive change in work behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>Acquisition of knowledge and information that broadens perspective and appreciation (affective learning).</td>\n<td>Concerned with the “why” more than the “how.” May not apply directly to the post. The more education, the easier it is to learn new things: an educated force trains more easily. It is the foundation of training in the cognitive and affective domains. The educated accept change better, read faster, write better, and solve problems better (the officer's greatest challenge).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Acquisition of knowledge, skills, and abilities directly tied to performance; a formal process that facilitates learning.</td>\n<td>Defined purpose: for example, operating detection equipment, conducting interviews, or monitoring alarms. Specific preparation with practice and repetition: it must be continuous. True training resembles indoctrination: it instills basic principles and changes behavior. Example: always using long pauses and paraverbal notes (“uh-huh”) in interviews makes the officer skilled; once the basics are mastered, one moves on to the advanced.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Development</td>\n<td>Competence and professional growth of someone who already meets expectations: turning a good employee into an excellent one.</td>\n<td>Culmination of education, training, and experience plus a strong dose of professional commitment.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>“Experience is the best teacher” is not necessarily true. Experience must be guided with education and training; without them the employee learns the wrong lessons and repeats mistakes.</li>\n<li>Experience without education or training is <strong>the most expensive teacher</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>What development contributes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Adds new skills or competencies.</li>\n<li>Sharpens or improves existing ones.</li>\n<li>Improves communication.</li>\n<li>Encourages applying knowledge or skills to the job.</li>\n<li>Increases performance: the employee is more efficient and works smarter.</li>\n<li>Prepares for a promotion.</li>\n<li>Prepares the subordinate for new challenges.</li>\n<li>Helps the officer or investigator solve problems.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Possible challenges: new tasks within his or her category (investigations, special events, emergencies) or temporarily covering for a supervisor.</li>\n<li>Flexibility is critical: without cross-training, covering certain posts is a problem. Problem solving is a central part of the protection mission.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e2\">3. Benefits of Training (2.2)</h3><p>Training costs money. Costs and benefits must be weighed to achieve the greatest return on investment. Six main benefits:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Improved job performance (2.2.1)</td>\n<td>True training makes officers competent; without it, competence cannot be expected.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ease of supervision (2.2.2)</td>\n<td>Well-trained officers are supervised more easily; managers devote their time to other matters instead of teaching the basics. Some experts hold that it is more effective to discipline with additional training than with punishment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Procedure review (2.2.3)</td>\n<td>The task analysis done before training is the same one needed to write procedures. McGlaughlin case (2003): after writing and implementing a program for operating a hospital's elevators, all problems with them ceased. That is why training is often located in the planning or research department.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Staff motivation (2.2.4)</td>\n<td>It increases self-esteem and satisfaction. Knowing what is expected improves performance, and employees and visitors see them as professionals. A professional identity leads to more professional performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reduced turnover (2.2.5)</td>\n<td>See below.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal protection (2.2.6)</td>\n<td>See below.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the initial list calls this benefit “revised procedures,” but the section is titled <em>procedure review</em>. It is the same benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>Reduced turnover (2.2.5)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>When properly applied, training reduces turnover: it lowers stress in the face of new challenges and shows management's interest in the officer.</li>\n<li>Knowing the environment, management's expectations, and problem-solving techniques reduces the newcomer's anxiety.</li>\n<li>Steps to retain the employee:<ol>\n<li>Open communication to set realistic expectations (new and current employees).</li>\n<li>Proper orientation to the work environment.</li>\n<li>Training on the organization's products, services, culture, and expectations.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Goodboe case (2002): Wackenhut Corporation program for supervisors to train their subordinates better. Three years later, turnover had dropped substantially.</li>\n<li>Social justice: development opportunities are open to everyone. Training provides recognition, something traditionally lacking in the industry. Pins, certificates, and rank promotions offer many occasions for recognition.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Legal protection (2.2.6)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Failure to train can generate union complaints or negligence lawsuits.</li>\n<li>Documentation measures:<ul>\n<li>Document all training.</li>\n<li>List of attendees and activities of each class.</li>\n<li>Tests signed by the instructor and by the employee evaluated.</li>\n<li>Centralized database of all officer training.</li>\n<li>OJT checklists and evaluations that record the instruction given, because deficiencies usually come from training and supervision failures at the same time.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Documenting each step ensures accountability. Repeating training undermines allegations that there was no frequent or recent training.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e3\">4. Identifying Training Needs (2.3)</h3><p>Before any instruction, needs must be identified. Sources, in the book's order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Legal requirements (first step):</strong> imposed by government or by contract. Examples: government contracts that require standards such as those of the Canadian General Standards Board; client contracts; union agreements; insurer requirements.</li>\n<li><strong>Industry standards, guidelines, and best practices:</strong> they set parameters. Example: the ASIS guideline <em>Private Security Officer Selection and Training</em> suggests topics.</li>\n<li><strong>Community standards:</strong> what training similar facilities in the area provide (hotels, universities). Reviewing their schedules reveals one's own gaps, innovations, and resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance deficiencies:</strong> in after-action reports or in performance evaluations. Training must prepare for routine and emergency tasks.</li>\n<li><strong>The officers themselves:</strong> they identify and rank the topics they need. It is a form of job task analysis and can reveal unknown needs. It validates their self-esteem and encourages their participation, something vital if the training alters schedules or challenges their abilities.</li>\n</ol><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e4\">5. Program Assessment and Design (2.4)</h3><p>Before designing, developing, and evaluating the program, four questions must be answered:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>The book's criterion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Is it needed to solve a current performance problem?</td>\n<td>Only if there is a <strong>documented</strong> deficiency in knowledge, skill, or ability. Using it for problems unrelated to that (poor job design, inadequate equipment, low morale) is inappropriate and wastes funds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How will it be funded?</td>\n<td>Identify and quantify each source. Also calculate the total cost to the company of <strong>not</strong> training: in the long run it can exceed the short-term expense. Saying: “If you think education is expensive, try ignorance.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When must it be completed?</td>\n<td>If immediate: off-the-shelf program or outside trainer. If the program already exists or can be developed within <strong>two years</strong>: in-house instructors. Rushing is problematic: runaway costs and clashes with the routines of trainers and learners. Best is an established training structure, with a person in charge who tracks state and insurer requirements, which change often.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who needs training?</td>\n<td>Distinguish basic pre-assignment training from topics for veterans, and tailor the content. Investing in pre-assignment training can yield more loyal and competent employees. Everyone needs to be updated from time to time.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Forcing people to train in order to keep their jobs should be avoided (Stroberger 2000). Three reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It contradicts the “principles of andragogy” of <strong>Malcolm Knowles</strong>: a theory of adult learning that assumes the adult is self-directed and takes responsibility for his or her learning needs.</li>\n<li>It detracts from the program's credibility with those who receive it; management, as provider, may also lose credibility.</li>\n<li>Requirements, especially governmental ones, only set minimums. Meeting them does not guarantee reasonable professional competence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> internal tension. Here mandatory training to keep a job is discouraged, but in 2.7.1 it is proposed that outside courses be a “condition of employment.” If asked about mandatory training, the book's position is to avoid it (Knowles, Stroberger 2000).</p>\n<h4>5.1 Behavioral Objectives (2.4.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are written based on the need detected.</li>\n<li>Each objective describes three elements: the desired <strong>task</strong>, the <strong>conditions</strong> for performing it, and the <strong>criteria</strong> for acceptable performance.</li>\n<li>They guide the development of instruction and tests; they are more effective if written with the help of a consultant.</li>\n<li>At first they can be simple (“conduct vehicle checks,” “answer the telephone”). In design they become very specific.</li>\n<li>Example: “The learner will identify the contraband items shown in photographs of the facility with 100 percent accuracy.” Task: identify. Conditions: photographs. Criterion: 100 percent accuracy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2 Instructional Design (2.4.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Creating modules takes time and expertise. Keys: knowing how to design the instruction and then preparing tests and documentation.</li>\n<li>Options: appoint an employee to oversee training, hire an outside consultant, buy programs, or subcontract instructors.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Disadvantages or conditions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>In-house staff</td>\n<td>Knows the organization.</td>\n<td>May lack technical expertise to design training. As an instructor may have a narrow perspective and ignore new research or court rulings.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outside consultant</td>\n<td>Experience in training and a network of professionals to turn to.</td>\n<td>Does not know the organization in depth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Off-the-shelf programs</td>\n<td>Sensible if time, expertise, or money are lacking.</td>\n<td>Only if they cover the organization's needs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Subcontracted instructors</td>\n<td>May be necessary when starting a program.</td>\n<td>Careful selection (see below).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Few master all the phases of developing a program: a <strong>training team</strong> is usually needed.</li>\n<li>Selecting instructors (they greatly influence the officer's socialization):<ul>\n<li>Assess their qualifications to teach: education, training, experience, and certifications.</li>\n<li>Explore their view of the officer's role. Those from the police may not understand officer roles, and some are condescending toward learners.</li>\n<li>Beware of <strong>Certified Trainer Syndrome</strong>: the instructor claims to be certified in a subject but has no basis for teaching it.</li>\n<li>Instructors must advocate for their learners and be well prepared for that role.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.3 Evaluating Training (2.4.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Training is expensive and takes time: it must be evaluated closely.</li>\n<li><strong>Learning contact:</strong> the number or proportion of employees exposed to the training. A program in which not all security staff participate has limited value, perhaps doubtful.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Limits or requirements</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Participant evaluations</td>\n<td>Always use them: they measure how the training is perceived and give feedback to the instructor.</td>\n<td>Poor for validation: they do not measure actual learning, but what the learner believes he or she learned. A form that requires describing what was learned and how to apply it is better: it measures transfer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tests</td>\n<td>As objective as possible: the answer is either correct or not.</td>\n<td>Essays and multiple choice give more information, but must be designed carefully and graded objectively. In test design it is wise to consult outside experts. Off-the-shelf programs usually include tests (they save time and money), but you must verify that they are valid and demanding.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rating forms</td>\n<td>The evaluator indicates whether or not the learner performed a specific task. Easy to create.</td>\n<td>The task must be clear to everyone and there must be room for narrative comments.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drills and scenarios</td>\n<td>Final exam for the entire force or for each officer. They motivate in periods of low activity and teach problem solving.</td>\n<td>They must be safe and valid, and evaluate only training already received.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incident review</td>\n<td>After a crisis, how the officers and the organization responded is assessed. Policies and procedures are analyzed and equipment and training are tested.</td>\n<td>If the response was inadequate, revised training may be required.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e5\">6. Training Methodology (2.5)</h3><ul>\n<li>The choice of method depends on: schedules, available technology, the instructor's comfort, and the number of people to be trained.</li>\n<li>Options listed by the book: lectures, job aids, distance learning, on-the-job training, and mentoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the initial list in 2.5 gives five methods, but the section develops six: it adds the case study (2.5.2).</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>What it is and when it works</th>\n<th>Advantages</th>\n<th>Limits and tips</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lecture (2.5.1)</td>\n<td>Traditional method for a group to receive a common body of knowledge. Works to introduce a topic and set direction; follow-up is done with role-playing, video, and physical skills.</td>\n<td>Ensures common knowledge.</td>\n<td>Not suited to individualized instruction. Ineffective unless short and dynamic. After lunch: have them drink water before sitting down, slightly brighter light, and get them moving. Use visual aids (flip charts, overhead projector, white and black boards, digital slides, videos) and change the lighting when projecting or using a flip chart. Fill-in-the-blank handout: key words are removed from the lesson plan and the learner writes them in (on paper or online).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Case study (2.5.2)</td>\n<td>For topics that require discretionary judgment: crisis management, use of force, and ethics.</td>\n<td>Brings the class to life: the class presents the facts and then discusses them; video shortens the presentation of the facts.</td>\n<td>Works best in groups with active discussion. It also works for individual tutoring, one-on-one instruction, and online courses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job aids (2.5.3)</td>\n<td>Reminders of how to do a task; also called takeaways. They can be detailed (an equipment operator's manual) or simple (laminated cards with key points for answering the telephone).</td>\n<td>Ensure that the procedures taught are followed.</td>\n<td>They are a complement, <strong>not a substitute</strong>, for training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance learning or distance education (2.5.4)</td>\n<td>Learner and teacher in different places but in communication. Formats: from the correspondence course to computer-based instruction.</td>\n<td>If individualized instruction is needed, it is probably the <strong>only</strong> means. Certifications use it (individual study and exam); even a university research paper counts. Security service providers use it to deliver uniform training at many sites.</td>\n<td>Some trainers reject it. But whoever does not use all techniques, including this one, almost certainly will not train all subordinates well.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On-the-job training (OJT) (2.5.5)</td>\n<td>Easy to develop. Very useful to ensure transfer from instructor to learner.</td>\n<td>Works with specified procedures and a competent coach. The written part can be a list of key topics with room for comments.</td>\n<td>If not well structured, its effectiveness is questionable.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mentoring (2.5.6)</td>\n<td>See next table.</td>\n<td>Reinforces socialization.</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Mentoring versus the field training officer (2.5.6)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many current officers are management representatives. They must know management's philosophy and the corporate culture: they must be highly socialized.</li>\n<li>Mentoring and FTO programs reinforce that socialization.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Mentoring</th>\n<th>FTO (field training officer) program</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How it works</td>\n<td>After pre-assignment training, the new officer works with a mentor who coaches him or her on the post. It joins the classroom with the reality of the job.</td>\n<td>The mentor is also a supervisor and evaluates the rookie's performance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Does it evaluate?</td>\n<td><strong>No:</strong> it teaches but does not evaluate.</td>\n<td><strong>Yes:</strong> at the end the FTO recommends retaining or dismissing the employee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complexity</td>\n<td>Lower.</td>\n<td>Complex role: requires extensive planning and training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where it works best</td>\n<td>In most security environments. Mentors can receive a raise and a title such as lead protection officer or senior security officer.</td>\n<td>Common in the police.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e6\">7. The Training Process (2.6)</h3><ul>\n<li>Training is a process of structured sessions that support each other; each is based on the previous one.</li>\n<li>Ideal: lifelong learning, constant throughout the employment relationship.</li>\n<li>Design a comprehensive process, not isolated episodes that get cut from the budget or that the next security director replaces.</li>\n<li>Method: divide the process into segments. Each topic has an <strong>introduction, body, and closing (summary and test)</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Segment</th>\n<th>Example from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Introduction</td>\n<td>At orientation the topics are presented: patrol duties, fixed posts, executive protection, vehicle security, and fire prevention.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Body</td>\n<td>OJT, study of the Certified Protection Officer (CPO) program of the International Foundation for Protection Officers (IFPO), web programs, or in-house courses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td>Supervisor sign-off on an OJT form, commercial exam (such as those for the CPO), or a course's own exams.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>There must be some redundancy and a learning sequence.</li>\n<li>Review whether the current training for each topic has sufficient introduction, body, and closing.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e7\">8. Obstacles to Training (2.7)</h3><p>Launching a program blindly usually derails it, and officers are left without training. The five most common obstacles: <strong>budget limitations, schedules, management's lack of training expertise, prejudice, and ego</strong>.</p>\n<h4>8.1 Budget Limitations (2.7.1)</h4>\n<p>Costs that must be identified: pay of those being trained, overtime for replacements, instructor fees, teaching aids and equipment, purchase of materials, and administrative development.</p>\n<p>Seven ways to stretch the budget:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Buy off-the-shelf programs:</strong> much cheaper than custom development. They must be relevant, not perfect, especially if the alternative is no training or slow development. They can be supplemented with in-house material.</li>\n<li><strong>Subcontract to a community college or technical institute:</strong> can be cheaper than training in-house. It can be a condition of employment or part of a lateral promotion program.</li>\n<li><strong>Seek grants:</strong> state and federal programs fund job training.</li>\n<li><strong>Join professional organizations:</strong> International Law Enforcement Educators and Trainers Association (ILEETA), ASIS International, and IFPO give discounts to their members. They provide access to instructors and materials that are difficult and expensive to replicate.</li>\n<li><strong>Share materials:</strong> with other sites or neighboring facilities. Borrow a video from a school or university; buying one and lending it to a school brings good publicity and goodwill.</li>\n<li><strong>Use distance learning:</strong> reduces classroom and instructor time and cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Turn to in-house trainers:</strong> only if they are truly qualified and experienced.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>8.2 Schedules (2.7.2)</h4>\n<p>In 24/7 operations each class must be repeated many times. The most useful are distance education or self-study. Options:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Advantages and notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pass-along media</td>\n<td>CDs, DVDs, videotapes, audiotapes, or other audio. They are viewed or listened to whenever convenient and then questions or exercises are answered.</td>\n<td>Easy and cheap, especially for small organizations or groups preparing for a certification.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Online learning</td>\n<td>Synchronous (instructor and learner in real time) or asynchronous (without immediate feedback). Formal (company, institute, association) or informal (articles, short awareness videos, social media).</td>\n<td>Deployed at many sites with a uniform experience. Can be commercial or in-house (if there is a lot of staff). An organizational learning needs assessment helps decide.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Written procedures</td>\n<td>They are handed out and their content is then tested.</td>\n<td>Cheap, tailored to the facility, and easy to administer. Requires some human interaction to ensure correct interpretation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pass-along logs</td>\n<td>Procedures, instructions, or articles that pass from one person to another: by mail, web, or a document with a routing sheet.</td>\n<td>Cheap; keeps learning alive; reminds and reiterates important information according to current needs.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distance learning</td>\n<td>Online programs from ASIS International or universities.</td>\n<td>Used mainly to train supervisors or managers; they also serve investigators.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certifications</td>\n<td>See list below.</td>\n<td>Based on distance or home study, which minimizes scheduling problems.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Certifications cited:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Associate Protection Professional (APP) and Physical Security Professional (PSP®), from ASIS International.</li>\n<li>Certified Protection Officer (CPO), from IFPO.</li>\n<li>Certified Institutional Protection Specialist (CIPS), from the International Foundation for Cultural Property Protection.</li>\n<li>Tiered specialties: International Association for Healthcare Security and Safety (IAHSS) and the American Hotel and Lodging Association.</li>\n<li>For the employee: a great development opportunity. For the employer: a recognized educational standard, a benchmark, and a promotional tool. They add an international character in a global economy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.3 Management's Lack of Training Expertise (2.7.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Most managers can teach some topics, but without preparation in design and delivery they struggle.</li>\n<li>Often outside their expertise: assessing needs, writing instructional objectives, developing materials, and designing tests.</li>\n<li>They must learn the whole process or hire someone who knows it. Even knowing it, they often lack time. It is crucial that they self-assess their strengths and limits.</li>\n<li>Instructor certification: <strong>Certified Protection Officer Instructor (CPOI)</strong>, from IFPO. Those who are already CPOs and have teaching experience can apply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Designing a program is a group effort with at least <strong>three prongs</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Protection managers:</strong> guide and initiate the process; they probably also teach.</li>\n<li><strong>Designated staff</strong> as training director, coordinator, or officer: handles the details and links management, supervision, line officers, and outside resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Outside resource</strong> (almost certainly needed): off-the-shelf program, contract with a nearby institution, or consultant. The consultant brings an objective perspective, resources, and current research that can save money.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Consultants can also teach highly specialized topics, such as violence or crisis management.</li>\n<li>It may be worth paying more on high-risk topics with liability exposure. The contracted instructor must be accredited, have real experience, and be able to testify effectively if needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Stereotypes (prejudice)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Society tends to view the officer negatively; the media show him or her as lazy and ignorant. Managers may believe it.</li>\n<li>The slightest sign of prejudice when teaching can derail a program. The manager must review his or her own feelings toward subordinates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ego</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frog syndrome</strong> (IFPO 2015): the manager decides to personally train everyone, jumps into the project, and, on seeing the real workload, jumps out. The training is left incomplete, stops, and organizational development stalls.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the list in 2.7 says “prejudice,” but the section is titled <em>Stereotypes</em>. In addition, stereotypes and ego appear unnumbered, within 2.7.3.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e8\">9. Training Strategies (2.8)</h3><p>Cost-effective strategies listed by the book (12): off-duty training, tuition reimbursement, recognition programs, integrated training, videos, selling security services, supervisory training, internships, previous experience, officer selection, job rotation, and professional memberships.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the list names 12 strategies, but the text develops only nine sections (2.8.1–2.8.9). “Previous experience,” “officer selection,” “job rotation,” and “professional memberships” are not developed. Instead, “formal and informal efforts” (2.8.9) appears, which was not in the list.</p>\n<h4>9.1 Off-Duty Training (2.8.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Voluntary</strong> off-hours training has great individual and organizational potential: it motivates learning new topics.</li>\n<li>Mandatory training inspires less and seems repetitive if it covers what is already mastered.</li>\n<li>It allows the most motivated to stand out and generates positive peer pressure from peers who see the benefits.</li>\n<li>Example: Saturday seminars on executive protection, counterterrorism, and defense against multiple attackers. Providing food and a certificate creates interest and eliminates the payment of overtime.</li>\n<li>Reduced or no fees if associated with high school protective services programs. In return, the company offers: guest speaker, job shadowing for students, or visits to the facility.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.2 Tuition Reimbursement (2.8.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Programs such as emergency medical technician, state firearms qualification, and CPO are paid for from that budget (not from the security training budget).</li>\n<li>Small cost compared with great professional growth. If the employee trains on regular pay and without a replacement on overtime, the main cost is avoided.</li>\n<li>These milestones can be a criterion for promotion to supervisor.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.3 Recognition Programs (2.8.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They reinforce the officer's value. They must be conceived to project the department's right image: customer service, enforcement of rules, or emergency response.</li>\n<li>Examples: uniform patch for advanced first aid; CPO pin; plaques with photo and achievements in visible places; online certificates or plaques; company newsletter; dinners or lunches for training milestones.</li>\n<li><strong>Lateral promotion or “promotion-in-place”:</strong> same level, but with a raise, change of rank or title, or change of duties. It creates specialties: workplace safety specialists, investigators, crime prevention coordinators, and FTOs. It recognizes achievements, gives a goal, and structures the career.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonuses:</strong> one-time reward that controls salary costs and reinforces behaviors. Examples: for advanced first aid, CPO, certification to carry weapons, or another relevant certification. It creates another level of expertise in the force.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4 Integrated Training (2.8.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Include officers in the training of the parent company or client: customer service, communication, writing, and other topics. It is cost-effective and takes advantage of the computer-assisted instruction of companies and governments.</li>\n<li>It helps socialization: it broadens perspectives and focuses the department on what services to provide and how to provide them.</li>\n<li>It builds trust, gives the department visibility, and increases employees' security awareness.</li>\n<li>Someone who sees something unusual trusts an officer he or she knows more. It is an example of community policing that helps in investigation and intelligence.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.5 Video Collaborations (2.8.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>In-house videos, if production time can be included in the calendar. Possible help: a university's theater department or a community theater group.</li>\n<li>Collaborating with police, fire, and other crisis management partners allows sharing costs and resources.</li>\n<li>Printing, computing resources, and offices of the parent company or client are also shared. Sharing creatively reduces costs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.6 Selling Security Services (2.8.6)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Some departments sell their training to other entities and fund their own with the proceeds.</li>\n<li>York College of Pennsylvania offered short seminars (for example, report writing) and reserved seats for its own protection staff.</li>\n<li>Some contract security companies created institutes that train clients and their staff.</li>\n<li>Cautions: attend to legal liability on certain topics; contractors must not end up training their competition.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.7 Supervisory Training (2.8.7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It reinforces the officer's role as a management representative.</li>\n<li>They are not supervisors, but they lead in routine tasks and emergencies. It is advisable to train them as adjunct members of the management team.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.8 Internships (2.8.8)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They expose the student to line and management functions. Interns can recruit among their peers and research to create instruction.</li>\n<li>They are a recruiting tool: they make it possible to hire an officer with a college education, trained and familiar with the environment. They will grow, because universities emphasize them.</li>\n<li>They also attract older workers changing careers. Postsecondary-level ones should have a management focus.</li>\n<li>To recruit entry-level officers: externships from high school protective services programs. They are shorter: the student accompanies a professional for a day or so.</li>\n<li>They should be seen as mentoring and educating the newcomer in the work environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.9 Formal and Informal Efforts (2.8.9)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Professional alliances of officers with a common employer or area: from informal meetings to international organizations such as IFPO, which brings together line officers.</li>\n<li>Guest-expert lectures, for example at monthly training meetings: police (suicide bombers, hostages, how to testify), lawyers (legal topics), and professors (interpersonal communication and group behavior).</li>\n<li><strong>Wackenhut case:</strong> founded in <strong>1954</strong>; it created the Wackenhut Institute, which became the G4S North American Training Institute. It trains in-house staff and the proprietary forces of other organizations; some courses give college credit. It teaches treating training as a core corporate function, not an expense: it improved efficiency, generated revenue, and commercialized services.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e9\">10. Roles of the Security Officer (2.9)</h3><ul>\n<li>How the company views the protection of people, property, and information (fundamental security responsibility) defines how the department is trained. Examples: hospital versus manufacturing plant; nuclear plant versus shopping mall.</li>\n<li>The officer's functions are in chapter 1. Two officers can have the same functions and a different role.</li>\n<li>Defining the role at each site allows the training schedule to cover all phases of the job.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Five roles:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>What it involves</th>\n<th>Training needed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Management representative (2.9.1)</td>\n<td>Management's agent in public relations with visitors, customers, tenants, and vendors.</td>\n<td>Know the expectations and corporate culture; that way they become adjunct members of the management team.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intelligence agent (2.9.2)</td>\n<td>Gathers information for management about criminal or adversary activities. Discovers and reports rule violations by employees or tenants. Patrols look for conditions that cause losses. Assesses fire and safety risks. Grows in the face of organized crime (narcotics, shoplifting rings, prostitution, gambling, gangs) and terrorism (prior surveillance by attackers).</td>\n<td>Investigation fundamentals, interview techniques, and investigative report writing. Recording and reporting observations. Evaluating conditions to decide whether to correct immediately or report.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enforcement/compliance agent (2.9.3)</td>\n<td>Ensures that management's rules are followed. Resembles the police, but within the legal and cultural framework of the environment. Sometimes enforces laws that are also internal rules, especially if a commissioned police officer.</td>\n<td>Know the rules and their procedure; human interaction to achieve efficient compliance; reduce and resolve conflicts. Investigative aspect: document and testify about violations (combines intelligence and compliance).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal consultant (2.9.4)</td>\n<td>Understand legal and quasi-legal (disciplinary) matters of the environment.</td>\n<td>Torts, negligence, and contracts. Notions of criminal procedure: it may not bind the officer, but it does bind the police, and improves the police-security relationship. Special rules for minors. Administrative or regulatory laws. Industry standards (different from law, same compliance principle). Audits by government, insurers, and professional organizations. Labor laws: privacy, disciplinary processes, and union contracts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical security or crime prevention specialist (2.9.5)</td>\n<td>Physical security is the heart of the discipline. The role grows because of technological complexity.</td>\n<td>Technical training on new equipment, which can last a lifetime. The ASIS PSP® certification helps define physical security and marks out a possible career. Central function: improve policies, procedures, and functions, not just follow them; bring perspective to management.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e10\">11. Future Trends in Training (2.10)</h3><ul>\n<li>Attacks in Oklahoma City, New York, Madrid, Beslan, Mumbai, London, Paris, and other places reoriented the agenda: counterterrorism training is the rule, not the exception.</li>\n<li>New topics: response to protests, active assailant incidents, red flags of potential attackers, and social media monitoring.</li>\n<li>Training must evolve with the job: if the officer uses an access control system or operates a robot, he or she must understand how it works.</li>\n<li>Problem: training is usually reactive, although the officer must be proactive. Three factors will shape the future: government oversight, self-regulation, and new ways of delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.1 Changing Roles (2.10.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The greater the social complexity, the greater the officer's importance and the more training. Human service skills will gain weight.</li>\n<li>Examples: community protection in high-crime housing; traditional police functions because of the police's budget limits.</li>\n<li>Public-private partnerships: crime prevention, supply chain, critical infrastructure, and borders.</li>\n<li>Demographic changes and suspicions about certain groups create tension: interpersonal and multicultural skills are needed. This is aggravated by long, complex supply chains and the search for oil and natural gas.</li>\n<li>Better communication, legal knowledge, and mastery of technological devices and weapons will be demanded. For this, the traditional classroom and role-playing will be needed.</li>\n<li>A professional career improves recruitment, motivation, and retention. It is advisable to train in supervision, management, and business principles. The ASIS APP certification is another route.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.2 Government Oversight (2.10.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Few governments legislate officer training. They often regulate only contracted providers, and a large part of the industry remains unregulated.</li>\n<li>Government standards often cover only licensing and screening, not training. Even so, they are hard to enforce for operational, economic, and political reasons.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>11.3 Self-Regulation (2.10.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Standards and guidelines of professional organizations will gain weight.</li>\n<li>Europe: the Confederation of European Security Suppliers and the BSIA push for better training.</li>\n<li>North America: the International Association of Security and Investigative Regulators (IASIR) facilitates the logistics of regulation. It collaborates with ASIS and with NASCO (National Association of Security Companies) on officer training.</li>\n<li>The ASIS guideline <em>Private Security Officer Selection and Training</em> will probably be adopted by companies, providers, and professional organizations.</li>\n<li>Outside the sector: the Joint Commission on the Accreditation of Healthcare Organizations (JCAHO) expects security staff to participate in continuing education and development. The IAHSS supports this with training briefs for short sessions.</li>\n<li>Second problem: quality control. Service companies must lead; some already exceed government standards.</li>\n<li>Unions: sometimes they ask for more training. The Service Employees International Union (SEIU) seeks to improve training, raise standards, and create career opportunities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the correct name is Confederation of European Security <strong>Services</strong> (CoESS), not “Suppliers.” And the JCAHO has been called The Joint Commission since 2007; it recognizes both names.</p>\n<h4>11.4 Delivery of Training (2.10.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Training is more complex and specialized as security becomes international. More automated external defibrillators, computers, detection equipment, robots, surveillance systems, and integrated access controls are used. Evacuation and terrorism complicate the guidelines.</li>\n<li>Common problem: only one or two people need a topic. In-house classes are not cost-effective, except in large organizations with their own instructors; sending them outside is best.</li>\n<li>Organizations that train and certify individual officers will gain weight: handcuffs, pepper spray, aggression management, or interviews. The officer is sent outside or a trainer comes in.</li>\n<li>A credible outside certification gives high quality assurance and avoids developing courses. Outside experts will always be used in some way.</li>\n<li>Contractors with their own programs will open their classes to outsiders: they make money, train their staff, and show their capabilities (marketing).</li>\n<li>Collaborations: active threat training with tactical intervention brings together all the entities that would interact. It controls costs, improves relationships, tests the response, and gives tactical training to security staff, who are on site and in command of the environment.</li>\n<li>Security academies with highly interactive learning, also at a distance. In their favor: small security staffs (a small organization, or a large one with few officers or few per site). There will be a niche for whoever enrolls one or two people quickly.</li>\n<li>E-learning is growing: it is offered by high schools, universities, institutes, and the public sector. The learner, his or her manager, or his or her mentor choose courses (asset protection in retail, executive protection, physical security).</li>\n<li>Conclusion: quality training, internal and external, will remain at the center of all effective security management.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-2-e11\">Key Terms</h3><p>pre-assignment training · on-the-job training (OJT) · apprenticeship · field training officer (FTO) · learning · cognitive learning · affective learning · psychomotor learning · retention · transfer · socialization · education · training · development · guided experience · cross-training · improved job performance · ease of supervision · procedure review · staff motivation · reduced turnover · legal protection · after-action report · job task analysis · principles of andragogy (Malcolm Knowles) · behavioral objectives · off-the-shelf program · Certified Trainer Syndrome · learning contact · participant evaluations · rating forms · drills and scenarios · incident review · lecture · case study · job aids (takeaways) · distance learning · mentoring · lifelong learning · introduction, body and closure · pass-along media · pass-along logs · synchronous delivery · asynchronous delivery · three-pronged effort · stereotypes · frog syndrome · off-duty training · tuition reimbursement · recognition programs · promotion-in-place · bonuses · integrated training · internship · externship · management representative · intelligence agent · enforcement/compliance agent · legal consultant · physical security or crime prevention specialist · active assailant · red flags · Certified Protection Officer (CPO) · Certified Protection Officer Instructor (CPOI) · Associate Protection Professional (APP) · Physical Security Professional (PSP) · Certified Institutional Protection Specialist (CIPS) · TIPS</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Introduction: Training and the Apprenticeship Model"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Key Training Concepts"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. Benefits of Training"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Identifying Training Needs"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. Program Assessment and Design"},{"id":"e5","t":"6. Training Methodology"},{"id":"e6","t":"7. The Training Process"},{"id":"e7","t":"8. Obstacles to Training"},{"id":"e8","t":"9. Training Strategies"},{"id":"e9","t":"10. Roles of the Security Officer"},{"id":"e10","t":"11. Future Trends in Training"},{"id":"e11","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"3. Benefits of Training","h":"the initial list calls this benefit “revised procedures,” but the section is titled <em>procedure review</em>. It is the same benefit."},{"s":"5. Program Assessment and Design","h":"internal tension. Here mandatory training to keep a job is discouraged, but in 2.7.1 it is proposed that outside courses be a “condition of employment.” If asked about mandatory training, the book's position is to avoid it (Knowles, Stroberger 2000)."},{"s":"6. Training Methodology","h":"the initial list in 2.5 gives five methods, but the section develops six: it adds the case study (2.5.2)."},{"s":"8. Obstacles to Training","h":"the list in 2.7 says “prejudice,” but the section is titled <em>Stereotypes</em>. In addition, stereotypes and ego appear unnumbered, within 2.7.3."},{"s":"9. Training Strategies","h":"the list names 12 strategies, but the text develops only nine sections (2.8.1–2.8.9). “Previous experience,” “officer selection,” “job rotation,” and “professional memberships” are not developed. Instead, “formal and informal efforts” (2.8.9) appears, which was not in the list."},{"s":"11. Future Trends in Training","h":"the correct name is Confederation of European Security <strong>Services</strong> (CoESS), not “Suppliers.” And the JCAHO has been called The Joint Commission since 2007; it recognizes both names."}]},"personnel-3":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-3-e0\">1. Context: Types of Force and Service Environments (introduction)</h3><ul>\n<li>Three force models: proprietary staff only, mixed, or contract officers only.</li>\n<li>Besides protecting facilities, contract companies can help with recovery after crises, personnel recruitment, and transport of patients or employees. The book says they offer many more, without listing them.</li>\n<li>Officers are also deployed in areas with civil unrest and terrorism.</li>\n<li>Typical clients: governments, corporations, small businesses, residential communities, and individuals.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Environment</th>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Examples from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Public areas</td>\n<td>Unrestricted access</td>\n<td>Residential areas; parades and street festivals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Semi-public areas</td>\n<td>Some access restriction or admission charge</td>\n<td>Stores, banks, hospitals, sporting and entertainment events, museums</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private facilities and property</td>\n<td>Entry limited to certain people</td>\n<td>Manufacturing plants, warehouses, offices</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The client must specify the environment and requirements in an express contract with defined terms and conditions.</li>\n<li>An implied contract, inferred from the conduct of the parties, is not advisable.</li>\n<li>Contract versus warranty: the contract is between specific parties.</li>\n<li>The contractor's experience and the skills of its staff are not interchangeable from one application to another.</li>\n<li>Choosing the right contractor takes a lot of time; it should not be repeated unnecessarily.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-3-e1\">2. Client Responsibilities (3.1)</h3><p><strong>Definition — security services contract:</strong> a legally binding agreement between at least two organizations. It details the expectations, responsibilities, and obligations of both parties.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Small, informal, simple organizations have small, informal, simple processes. Large, formal, complex ones have large, formal, complex processes. That is why drafting must be done carefully.</li>\n<li>First step for the security manager: learn the contracting rules of his or her own organization.</li>\n<li>Purpura (2018) guidelines for hiring officers:<ul>\n<li>The buyer must beware.</li>\n<li>Assess well the needs of the organization to be protected.</li>\n<li>Get informed and know the state of the art.</li>\n<li>Analyze advantages and disadvantages of each service.</li>\n<li>Avoid decisions made out of panic.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.1 Request for Proposal (RFP) (3.1.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Personnel costs are usually the largest item in a security department's operating budget.</li>\n<li>Before hiring, needs must be analyzed, especially the relationship between staff and electronic security solutions.</li>\n<li>That analysis is key to preparing the RFP, negotiating the contract, and administering it afterward.</li>\n<li>The needs must clearly justify the use of security officers.</li>\n<li>Three broad criteria for choosing a contractor:<ul>\n<li>Consistent performance.</li>\n<li>Quick, efficient, and positive response to the client's concerns.</li>\n<li>Competitive pricing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Then the scope of work is defined based on: site examination, facility layout, building design, and internal business processes.</li>\n<li>Factors that are also considered:<ul>\n<li>Corporate culture: how much it tolerates reinforced security measures and to what degree.</li>\n<li>Security objective: maintain an image of security or create an effective asset protection force? They are different things.</li>\n<li>Contract duration.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>From this analysis come the scope of work and a personnel schedule. The schedule translates into the total hours of the contract.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Solicitation summary</strong> (McCrie 2015). It is the next useful step. It gives providers the maximum information so they can put together complete proposals. It contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Statement of purpose with the tasks the security staff will perform.</li>\n<li>Client contact person.</li>\n<li>Details of the bidders' conference.</li>\n<li>Letter of intent for providers.</li>\n<li>Proposal submission requirements.</li>\n<li>Modification or withdrawal of proposals.</li>\n<li>Post-opening withdrawals.</li>\n<li>Late proposals, withdrawals, or modifications.</li>\n<li>Right of appeal of bidders.</li>\n<li>Payment policies.</li>\n<li>Amendments to the RFP.</li>\n<li>Details of any planned discussion between provider and client.</li>\n<li>Procurement rules.</li>\n<li>Challenges based on unfairness.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The RFP</strong> is distributed after the summary and details the requested services in depth. Suggested headings:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nature of the security services required.</li>\n<li>Contract duration.</li>\n<li>Client background.</li>\n<li>Specific facilities requiring protection.</li>\n<li>Security problems.</li>\n<li>Services needed at the facility.</li>\n<li>Scope of services.</li>\n<li>Specific tasks and minimum requirements.</li>\n<li>Guidelines for officers.</li>\n<li>Incident report requirements.</li>\n<li>Provider responsibilities:<ul>\n<li>Continuity of service.</li>\n<li>Possible provision of male and female guards.</li>\n<li>Uniforms and equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Proposal content and how certain tasks will be handled.</li>\n<li>General information:<ul>\n<li>Conflicts.</li>\n<li>Inspectors and tests.</li>\n<li>Supervision by the contractor.</li>\n<li>Adequate and competent supervision.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Additional requirements:<ul>\n<li>Policies.</li>\n<li>Tour limitation rules.</li>\n<li>Overtime payment policies.</li>\n<li>Training agreement.</li>\n<li>Right to audit.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Staff standards:<ul>\n<li>Education and background requirements.</li>\n<li>Hiring and replacement of personnel.</li>\n<li>Information about the security service provider.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>General guidelines for submitting proposals:<ul>\n<li>Letter of intent.</li>\n<li>Deadline for requests.</li>\n<li>Questions.</li>\n<li>Proposals or declinations.</li>\n<li>Restriction on contact with client employees.</li>\n<li>Press releases.</li>\n<li>Proprietary information.</li>\n<li>Contract award.</li>\n<li>Reservation.</li>\n<li>Site visit.</li>\n<li>Oral presentations.</li>\n<li>Incurring costs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Provider selection:<ul>\n<li>Selection method:</li>\n<li>Organizational capability and quality.</li>\n<li>Understanding of the client's need.</li>\n<li>Recruitment strategy and planning.</li>\n<li>Qualifications and experience of the proposer's personnel.</li>\n<li>Training: plans, curriculum, and capabilities.</li>\n<li>Supervisory control.</li>\n<li>Rating system.</li>\n<li>Payment.</li>\n<li>Liquidated damages/adjustment of compensation.</li>\n<li>General provisions.</li>\n<li>Incomplete shift penalty.</li>\n<li>Improper assignment penalty.</li>\n<li>Return of a previously dismissed worker.</li>\n<li>On-the-job negligence.</li>\n<li>Notification requirements.</li>\n<li>General provisions for the contractor.</li>\n<li>Delivery of the federal or other government employer identification number.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Insurance.</li>\n<li>Reports.</li>\n<li>Responsibilities of the prime contractor.</li>\n<li>Subcontracting.</li>\n<li>Reservations.</li>\n<li>Contract duration.</li>\n<li>Contract termination.</li>\n<li>Equal employment opportunity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Making an RFP takes a lot of work, but it is necessary. If the manager does not detail what he or she needs, the agency chosen will probably not meet his or her expectations.</p>\n<h4>2.2 Scope of Work (3.1.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The client defines the scope and performance standards completely and precisely. Without that precision, disputes soon arise over what performance is acceptable.</li>\n<li>Calling contractors at random can produce enthusiastic answers that do not reflect the actual quality of service.</li>\n<li>The client must be realistic about the risks, based on a recent risk assessment.</li>\n<li>The more specific the information, the more likely a quality service at a competitive price.</li>\n<li>The scope states: security tasks, days and hours of service, and total number of hours. It is given to all potential contractors. (Appendix 3A: example.)</li>\n<li>With the scope, the general and special orders, and the proposed operating agreement, agencies should be able to deduce all typical and routine requirements and foresee the administrative burden.</li>\n<li>Much more information is conveyed in formal and informal conferences. Contractors should be encouraged to ask for all the detail they need.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.3 General and Special Orders (3.1.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is crucial to prepare them in advance and in detail. Special instructions are also needed for each assignment or post.</li>\n<li>Providing them is the client's responsibility; in practice, the agency often writes them.</li>\n<li>Whoever prepares them, the client always reviews and approves them.</li>\n<li>Risk of the agency's orders: they may be designed for many different clients. They may not reflect specific needs, organizational culture, or local factors.</li>\n<li>Topics they must cover:<ul>\n<li>Personal conduct.</li>\n<li>Duties of each post.</li>\n<li>Disciplinary actions.</li>\n<li>Reporting procedures.</li>\n<li>Company rules and regulations.</li>\n<li>The officer's authority on behalf of the client company.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Appendix 3B: examples of orders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.4 Bidders' List (3.1.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The quality of service depends directly on the quality of local supervision and local training.</li>\n<li>Depending on the size of the agency:<ul>\n<li>Large national or regional firms operating at the edge of their usual area: they find it hard to customize service as much as a local provider.</li>\n<li>Small firms: they may lack the infrastructure, resources, and capital of a large one.</li>\n<li>Firms of sufficient size: more flexibility in scheduling and staffing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Pay to officers:<ul>\n<li>The client must set the hourly wage rates, not leave it to the agency.</li>\n<li>It is especially important in hybrid organizations (contract plus proprietary). Large differences lower morale and satisfaction, raise turnover, and erode service.</li>\n<li>General rule: the contract officer's lowest wage must be at least equal to the entry-level wage of the proprietary posts.</li>\n<li>If the purchasing department is bureaucratic, the contract's compensation policy is set by the security manager, not purchasing.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-3-e2\">3. Agency Responsibilities (3.2)</h3><h4>3.1 Operating Agreement (3.2.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Based on the RFP, the agency delivers a detailed operating agreement with each party's responsibilities.</li>\n<li>Agencies prefer a standard agreement, which can be vague and written to limit their liability.</li>\n<li>The agreement must reflect the client's unique requirements according to a risk assessment. Moving away from the generic takes effort, but yields a tailored service, of higher quality and satisfaction.</li>\n<li>Alternative: a detailed specification document that describes the performance standards required of both parties.</li>\n<li>The agency's willingness to adapt its agreement to the client's specifications should be a key selection criterion. It indicates its service philosophy and even its future performance. (Appendix 3C: example.)</li>\n<li>Topics the agreement may cover:<ul>\n<li>Liability.</li>\n<li>Insurance.</li>\n<li>Physical standards for officers.</li>\n<li>Pre-placement and in-service training.</li>\n<li>Continuity of service.</li>\n<li>Type and scope of supervision.</li>\n<li>Legal limitations.</li>\n<li>Scheduling.</li>\n<li>Equipment.</li>\n<li>Compensation.</li>\n<li>Adjustments to compensation.</li>\n<li>Short-term supplementary service needs.</li>\n<li>Workplace.</li>\n<li>Fees to be charged.</li>\n<li>Hours of coverage.</li>\n<li>Staffing levels.</li>\n<li>Services provided.</li>\n<li>Audit and payment.</li>\n<li>Period of performance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>A general statement of the client's philosophy about its security program conveys intentions and nuances that formal language does not capture.</li>\n<li>The detailed agreement makes client and contractor decide together the policy and procedures on contract performance and officer performance.</li>\n<li>The client receives only what is detailed in the agreement.</li>\n<li>If the client sets unrealistic requirements, agencies eager for the work accept them and failure is set up.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Controls over the agency:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Look for agencies that demonstrate below-average turnover.</li>\n<li>Require itemized billing: officer's hourly wage, overhead, and profit, separately. If needed, also break down the overhead.</li>\n<li>Periodically auditing invoices at that level of detail is revealing.</li>\n<li>The agency must guarantee that its advertising about officer qualification, screening, induction, and training is true.</li>\n<li>It must teach new officers about the client company and its line of business, and give them additional site-specific training.</li>\n<li>Good practice: a fixed number of pre-assignment training hours paid by the agency. It can reduce turnover, because the agency bears the cost of training replacements.</li>\n<li>Financial penalties for nonperformance:<ul>\n<li>The areas of nonperformance are clearly defined.</li>\n<li>The fine must be enough to stimulate performance without making the agency abandon the account.</li>\n<li>The agency takes part in negotiating them, to ensure that it understands them and is willing to comply.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Other factors that affect the agency's performance:<ul>\n<li>Poor scheduling (highly variable and inconsistent hours for officers).</li>\n<li>Insufficient supervision.</li>\n<li>Few opportunities for advancement.</li>\n<li>Little or no recognition.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>For adequate supervision: a single point of contact between agency and client.</li>\n<li>To verify that both parties comply: regular meetings, perhaps weekly or monthly. Any disagreement, large or small, requires immediate attention.</li>\n<li>Most important: a real commitment to partnership.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2 Bidders' Conference (3.2.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Carefully assemble the list of qualified agencies. Include several to ensure healthy competition.</li>\n<li>Invite them to explain their capabilities and to provide client lists as references.</li>\n<li>Verify the references thoroughly. Useful technique: ask the references in the proposal for names of other clients of the contractor. These give different observations than the references chosen by the contractor.</li>\n<li>Compare experiences (benchmarking) with nearby companies about different agencies.</li>\n<li>Visit one or more sites to see the agency's officers at work.</li>\n<li>At the conference, qualified bidders discuss the statement of work, the general and special orders, and the proposed operating agreement. Sequence:<ol>\n<li>Walking tour of the facility with the agencies' representatives.</li>\n<li>Detailed explanation of the officer's main duties.</li>\n<li>Final question-and-answer meeting.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>That way everyone prepares their proposal with the same information.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-3-e3\">4. Bid Evaluation (3.3)</h3><p>An adequate, standardized methodology is needed. Its criteria must allow competing agencies to be compared fairly.</p>\n<h4>4.1 Criteria (3.3.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>First criterion: the strict deadline for submitting the proposal.</li>\n<li>Criteria on the content of the proposal:<ul>\n<li>Spelling, grammar, and accuracy in responding to each element of the RFP.</li>\n<li>Whether it is customized or comes from a template.</li>\n<li>Whether it includes all the details requested.</li>\n<li>Whether the quality of the package suggests that the agency has real interest in the job.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Additional criteria: speed in obtaining officers' licenses, training offered, and staff experience in the client's sector.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Supervision of contract personnel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Agencies usually provide their own supervisors. They may be assigned to the site or make periodic unscheduled visits.</li>\n<li>The supervisor assigned to the site can cover a scheduled shift or take on a full-time supervisory role.</li>\n<li>The supervisory role generally depends on the number of people on site (contract hours required). That role should influence the pay rate.</li>\n<li>Unless the client hires someone with solid security supervision experience, it is better to leave supervision to the agency.</li>\n<li>It is recommended that the client give the agency's supervisors a broad induction.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> if contract personnel work more than <strong>400 hours per week</strong>, the client should hire its own security supervisor.</li>\n<li>Risk: if the client's supervisor watches contract personnel too closely, employment issues can arise.</li>\n<li>Solution: pass the client's instructions through the agency's supervisor or the lead officer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 3.1 — Measures of Contract Security Agency Suitability</strong> (Perry 2000). Agencies are judged on at least seven measures:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>What to evaluate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Personnel</td>\n<td>Complete verification of employment and references; psychological tests where the law permits; polygraph where the law permits; minimum qualifications that meet the state or provincial standard; review and verification of the qualifications of upper and middle management; selection process; average length of employment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Pre-hire testing and training in accordance with the state or provincial standard; review of training manuals, films, and tests; review of the contractor's facilities; annual training; state, provincial, or university certification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervision</td>\n<td>Supervisor selection process; training and evaluation procedures; on-site supervision; field supervision (quality and quantity of visits); visit schedule and support from the provider's management; employee evaluation reports; agency contact who handles problems and concerns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wages and benefits</td>\n<td>Competitive wage scale to attract qualified candidates; merit pay to motivate; life and health insurance for contract personnel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insurance</td>\n<td>General and excess liability insurance (and its amount); whether any part is self-insured; automobile liability (if applicable); policy exclusions; policy availability; notice of cancellation; workers' compensation insurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost of service</td>\n<td>Charge-out rate and how much the officer receives; overtime rates and how much the officer receives; holiday rates and how much the employee receives; effective rate (overhead plus administrative costs); equipment charges; billing periods and procedures; cost determination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational issues</td>\n<td>Availability of the agency's management 24 hours a day; emergency response capability; standard operating procedures (examine them); service agreement expectations; who provides and pays for uniforms and equipment; officer performance standards</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>4.2 Sample Analysis (3.3.2)</h4>\n<p><strong>Case data:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The client needs about 16,000 hours a year of contract service (308 hours per week).</li>\n<li>It expects about 850 overtime hours.</li>\n<li>It wants a five-step wage scale that includes a supervisor category.</li>\n<li>Special rates are noted for short-term supplemental service and for in-service training requested by the client.</li>\n<li>The contractor provides, on a 24-month lease: an electronic security tour management system, two mobile radios, and a bicycle for extended patrol.</li>\n<li>Seven agencies responded to the RFP with detailed proposals.</li>\n<li>The rates are examples only; they do not reflect current wages in any area.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Step 1 — Base pay and billing rates (Figure 3.2):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Column A: the competing agencies, in no particular order.</li>\n<li>Column B: rate each agency bills at step 1. The officer's hourly wage is the base pay; each agency adds administrative expenses, overhead, and profit to it.</li>\n<li>Billing percentage = billed rate ÷ step base wage. Example: Company 1, $12.72 ÷ $7.35 = 173%.</li>\n<li>Columns C through F: the same calculation for steps 2 through 5. Values are rounded to the whole number.</li>\n<li>Column G: sum of the percentages in B through F. It is a weighted total (score) that indicates cost better than the rates alone. It then serves as an objective comparison criterion.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"formula katex-block\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">Percentage of billing</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:2.2519em;vertical-align:-0.8804em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mopen nulldelimiter\"></span><span class=\"mfrac\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:1.3714em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.314em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">step base salary</span></span></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.23em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"frac-line\" style=\"border-bottom-width:0.04em;\"></span></span><span style=\"top:-3.677em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">billed rate</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8804em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mclose nulldelimiter\"></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span></span></span></span></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Figure 3.2 — Straight-time and billing rates by wage step</strong> (billed rate and, in parentheses, billing %):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Company</th>\n<th>Step 1 (base $7.35)</th>\n<th>Step 2 (base $7.80)</th>\n<th>Step 3 (base $8.40)</th>\n<th>Step 4 (base $9.00)</th>\n<th>Step 5 (base $9.00 per the figure)</th>\n<th>Points (G)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>$12.72 (173)</td>\n<td>$13.20 (169)</td>\n<td>$13.89 (165)</td>\n<td>$14.58 (162)</td>\n<td>$15.30 (159)</td>\n<td>828</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>$11.16 (152)</td>\n<td>$11.85 (152)</td>\n<td>$12.57 (150)</td>\n<td>$13.29 (148)</td>\n<td>$14.01 (146)</td>\n<td>748</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$11.76 (160)</td>\n<td>$12.09 (155)</td>\n<td>$13.02 (155)</td>\n<td>$13.96 (155)</td>\n<td>$14.40 (150)</td>\n<td>775</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$11.55 (157)</td>\n<td>$12.09 (155)</td>\n<td>$12.81 (153)</td>\n<td>$13.50 (150)</td>\n<td>$14.22 (148)</td>\n<td>763</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>$11.31 (154)</td>\n<td>$11.94 (153)</td>\n<td>$12.66 (151)</td>\n<td>$13.38 (149)</td>\n<td>$14.16 (147)</td>\n<td>755</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>$13.29 (181)</td>\n<td>$13.47 (173)</td>\n<td>$14.01 (167)</td>\n<td>$14.79 (163)</td>\n<td>$14.79 (154)</td>\n<td>838</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>$11.67 (159)</td>\n<td>$12.33 (158)</td>\n<td>$13.23 (157)</td>\n<td>$15.00 (157)</td>\n<td>$15.00 (156)</td>\n<td>788</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 3.2 gives $9.00 as the step 5 base wage, the same as step 4. But the step 5 percentages only work out with $9.60 (for example, $15.30 ÷ $9.60 = 159%). The overtime rates in Figure 3.3 ($12.65 and $13.50) also work out with $9.60.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> other figures in Figure 3.2 do not add up. Company 7 at step 4 ($15.00) does not give 157%; Figure 3.6 implies $14.13. Company 6 at step 4 ($14.79) would give 164%, not 163%. The sums for Companies 5 and 7 give 754 and 787, not 755 and 788.</p>\n<p><strong>Step 2 — Supplemental service costs (Figure 3.3):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evaluates the rates for overtime, training, and short-term supplemental personnel. Controlling them is critical to staying within budget.</li>\n<li>Two base overtime rates:<ul>\n<li>B1 = $12.65: 150% of the average of all the wage steps in Figure 3.2.</li>\n<li>B2 = $13.50: 150% of the average of only the three highest steps.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Both are used because, when analyzing the proposals, it is not known how the officers' wages will be distributed.</li>\n<li>The weighted total gives a clearer picture of the actual supplemental rates.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 3.3 — Overtime, training, and short-term supplemental service rates</strong> (billed rate and, in parentheses, %):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Company</th>\n<th>Overtime B1 (base $12.65)</th>\n<th>Overtime B2 (base $13.50)</th>\n<th>Training C (base $7.35)</th>\n<th>Short-term supplemental D (base $13.50)</th>\n<th>Points (E)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>$18.82 (148)</td>\n<td>$19.80 (147)</td>\n<td>$10.98 (149)</td>\n<td>$24.00 (178)</td>\n<td>622</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>$17.55 (139)</td>\n<td>$18.51 (137)</td>\n<td>$11.16 (152)</td>\n<td>$18.51 (137)</td>\n<td>565</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$19.56 (155)</td>\n<td>$20.94 (155)</td>\n<td>$11.76 (160)</td>\n<td>$21.60 (160)</td>\n<td>630</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$17.55 (139)</td>\n<td>$18.51 (137)</td>\n<td>$11.55 (157)</td>\n<td>$17.40 (129)</td>\n<td>562</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>$17.55 (139)</td>\n<td>$18.48 (137)</td>\n<td>$11.31 (154)</td>\n<td>$17.40 (129)</td>\n<td>559</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>$18.66 (148)</td>\n<td>$19.71 (146)</td>\n<td>$13.20 (180)</td>\n<td>$19.44 (144)</td>\n<td>618</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>$17.55 (139)</td>\n<td>$18.60 (138)</td>\n<td>$10.95 (149)</td>\n<td>$19.95 (148)</td>\n<td>574</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Step 3 — Equipment costs:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The annualized cost of the required equipment, if separate from the company's overhead, is added to the hourly labor rate. That way the bids are analyzed completely.</li>\n<li>In the example, the contractor provides the tour system, bicycle, and radios.</li>\n<li>The equipment points appear in Figure 3.5. The book does not explain how they were assigned: pending.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Step 4 — Fringe benefit costs (Figure 3.4):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benefits considered: paid holidays, vacation, sick leave, health insurance, and life insurance.</li>\n<li>An agency with few benefits has a price advantage over a more generous one. But benefits matter for officers' morale.</li>\n<li>Method to equalize: 5 points for each of the five benefits, 25 points possible. 5 points are subtracted for each benefit the agency does provide.</li>\n<li>The agency with the fewest benefits gets the most points. The low score wins. That way the cost advantage of providing few benefits disappears.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figure 3.4 — Fringe benefit costs of contract security:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Company</th>\n<th>Paid holidays</th>\n<th>Vacation days (1–3 years)</th>\n<th>Sick leave</th>\n<th>Group health insurance</th>\n<th>Life insurance</th>\n<th>Points</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>$300 ded. 85/15</td>\n<td>N/S</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>$300 ded. 80/20</td>\n<td>$2,000</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>$300 ded. 80/20</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>$300 ded. 80/20</td>\n<td>$2,000</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>$300 ded. 80/20</td>\n<td>$1,000</td>\n<td>5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>$300 ded. 80/20</td>\n<td>$2,000</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>N/S</td>\n<td>$2,000</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>“ded.” = deductible. The book does not explain the abbreviation N/S or the 85/15 and 80/20 splits: pending. In the scoring, N/S counts as a benefit not provided.</li>\n<li>The position of the two N/S cells was reconstructed from the points, because the text of the figure comes out scrambled.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Results — Figure 3.5, Contract Security Bid Analysis:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area of competition</th>\n<th>C1</th>\n<th>C2</th>\n<th>C3</th>\n<th>C4</th>\n<th>C5</th>\n<th>C6</th>\n<th>C7</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Straight-time rates (Fig. 3.2)</td>\n<td>828</td>\n<td>748</td>\n<td>775</td>\n<td>763</td>\n<td>755</td>\n<td>838</td>\n<td>788</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Overtime, training, and short-term supplemental (Fig. 3.3)</td>\n<td>622</td>\n<td>565</td>\n<td>630</td>\n<td>562</td>\n<td>559</td>\n<td>618</td>\n<td>574</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Equipment costs</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Fringe benefits (Fig. 3.4)</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Current contracts in the country? (10 if “no”)</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td>1,473</td>\n<td>1,333</td>\n<td>1,445</td>\n<td>1,345</td>\n<td>1,329</td>\n<td>1,476</td>\n<td>1,390</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rank</strong></td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Bidder 5 wins, with the lowest score: 1,329 points.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> row 5 of the figure asks about contracts “in the country.” The text, on the other hand, speaks of having accounts “in the same area.”</p>\n<p><strong>Validation — Figure 3.6, Adjusted Base Rates (over 16,000 hours):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The estimated annual labor cost of each bid is calculated to validate the evaluation.</li>\n<li>Average straight-time (S/T) rate: average of the step 3 and step 4 rates in Figure 3.2 (base wage $8.40 and $9.00).</li>\n<li>Average overtime (O/T) rate: average of the two overtime rates in Figure 3.3.</li>\n<li>Base schedule for the calculation: 16,150 S/T hours and 850 O/T hours.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Company</th>\n<th>Average S/T rate</th>\n<th>Average O/T rate</th>\n<th>Estimated S/T $</th>\n<th>Estimated O/T $</th>\n<th>Estimated annual labor cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>$14.24</td>\n<td>$19.26</td>\n<td>$229,976</td>\n<td>$16,371</td>\n<td>$246,347</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>$12.93</td>\n<td>$18.03</td>\n<td>$208,820</td>\n<td>$15,326</td>\n<td>$224,146</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>$13.40</td>\n<td>$20.25</td>\n<td>$216,410</td>\n<td>$17,213</td>\n<td>$233,623</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>$13.16</td>\n<td>$18.03</td>\n<td>$212,534</td>\n<td>$15,326</td>\n<td>$227,860</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>$13.08</td>\n<td>$18.02</td>\n<td>$211,242</td>\n<td>$15,317</td>\n<td>$226,559</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>$14.34</td>\n<td>$19.19</td>\n<td>$231,519</td>\n<td>$16,312</td>\n<td>$247,903</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>$13.68</td>\n<td>$18.08</td>\n<td>$220,932</td>\n<td>$15,386</td>\n<td>$236,318</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The winner (5) cost $2,413 more than the cheapest (2): $226,559 versus $224,146.</li>\n<li>It still won: 5 already had other accounts in the same area and 2 did not. Local presence weighed more than the small cost difference.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Figure 3.6 has arithmetic errors. Company 6: 16,150 × $14.34 = $231,591, not $231,519 (its total does use $231,591). Company 7: 850 × $18.08 = $15,368, not $15,386. Several average rates (Companies 1, 3, 5, 6, and 7) do not match Figures 3.2 and 3.3 exactly.</p>\n<h4>4.3 Incentive Pricing (3.3.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Growing trend: negotiate wages directly and base the agency's profit on the base payroll plus some incentive.</li>\n<li>The client must ensure that the wage actually paid is sufficient to attract qualified officers.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>High direct wage + recruiting paid by the client</td>\n<td>A direct wage higher than normal is agreed; the client pays the direct recruiting expenses</td>\n<td>The contractor attracts and retains staff without charging extra compensation; the client can demand tougher specifications and better performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maximum officer turnover level</td>\n<td>Cap based on local turnover in jobs with equivalent pay; measured each month</td>\n<td>Turnover at the agreed level → bills at the agreed rates. Lower → bonus, as a % of the normal bill. Higher → penalty, as a % discount on the bill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The first approach is different from just raising the hourly rate: of that increase, only a part reaches the officer.</li>\n<li>Even if the client pays a low-turnover bonus every month, it costs less than the expense of training constant replacements.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-3-e4\">5. Contract Award (3.4)</h3><ul>\n<li>After deciding the award, formal negotiations between the parties begin.</li>\n<li>Objective: that the general and special orders and the terms and conditions of the operating agreement are fully understood.</li>\n<li>Both parties review and agree on the risks and responsibilities relating to the officers.</li>\n<li>Silently accepting terms that are unsatisfactory to either party is a bad start.</li>\n<li>From the beginning it must be clear that the terms of the agreement apply strictly to both parties.</li>\n<li>Professional, serious contractors welcome this kind of agreement. It eliminates many frustrations they experience with distant, unprepared clients.</li>\n<li>A professional approach generates a professional response.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1 Legal Considerations (3.4.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Premises liability is a broad, complex, and changing area of law.</li>\n<li>The client must determine how much liability it can transfer to the contractor through the contract.</li>\n<li>Example: the notion of agency, that is, whether someone acts on behalf of another person, is key when administering the contract.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-3-e5\">6. Administering the Operating Agreement (3.5)</h3><ul>\n<li>Day-to-day administration is not simple. A solid, satisfactory relationship requires continuous communication.</li>\n<li>Checks and balances needed:<ul>\n<li>Regular scheduled meetings to review operations.</li>\n<li>Surprise site inspections by representatives of the client and the contractor, together and separately.</li>\n<li>Regular operational and procedural audits.</li>\n<li>Invoice review.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If inadequate performance, by the officers or the agency, is tolerated, it soon becomes the new norm.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-3-e6\">Key Terms</h3><p>proprietary security force · contract security officers · hybrid (contract and proprietary) security organization · public areas · semi-public areas · private facilities and property · express contract · implied contract · warranty · security services contract · buyer beware · request for proposal (RFP) · solicitation summary · bidders' conference · letter of intent · scope of work · statement of work · personnel schedule · general orders · special orders · post · bidders' list · operating agreement · specification document · right to audit · tour limitation rules · liquidated damages · incomplete shift penalty · improper assignment penalty · prime contractor · subcontracting · turnover · overhead · financial penalties for nonperformance · single point of contact · benchmarking · bid evaluation · lead officer · charge-out rate · effective rate · workers' compensation · five-step wage scale · billing rate percentage · weighted total · straight time (S/T) · overtime (O/T) · short-term supplemental service · fringe benefits · incentive pricing · maximum officer turnover level · contract award · premises liability · agency · checks and balances</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Context: Types of Force and Service Environments"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Client Responsibilities"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. Agency Responsibilities"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Bid Evaluation"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. Contract Award"},{"id":"e5","t":"6. Administering the Operating Agreement"},{"id":"e6","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"4. Bid Evaluation","h":"Figure 3.2 gives $9.00 as the step 5 base wage, the same as step 4. But the step 5 percentages only work out with $9.60 (for example, $15.30 ÷ $9.60 = 159%). The overtime rates in Figure 3.3 ($12.65 and $13.50) also work out with $9.60."},{"s":"4. Bid Evaluation","h":"other figures in Figure 3.2 do not add up. Company 7 at step 4 ($15.00) does not give 157%; Figure 3.6 implies $14.13. Company 6 at step 4 ($14.79) would give 164%, not 163%. The sums for Companies 5 and 7 give 754 and 787, not 755 and 788."},{"s":"4. Bid Evaluation","h":"row 5 of the figure asks about contracts “in the country.” The text, on the other hand, speaks of having accounts “in the same area.”"},{"s":"4. Bid Evaluation","h":"Figure 3.6 has arithmetic errors. Company 6: 16,150 × $14.34 = $231,591, not $231,519 (its total does use $231,591). Company 7: 850 × $18.08 = $15,368, not $15,386. Several average rates (Companies 1, 3, 5, 6, and 7) do not match Figures 3.2 and 3.3 exactly."}]},"personnel-4":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-4-e0\">1. Why Consultants Are Used (introduction)</h3><p>Reasons for seeking outside help:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>lack of time;</li>\n<li>lack of in-house specialized knowledge;</li>\n<li>desire for an independent, objective assessment;</li>\n<li>fresh ideas;</li>\n<li>flexibility to hire personnel only when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Professional security consultants are the main resource. Sometimes expert people from within the company itself are used.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e1\">2. The Value of Consultants (4.1)</h3><p>Advantages of the independent consultant:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Does not promote or sell products: assesses real needs and recommends a mix of solutions to reduce threats.</li>\n<li>Facing liability risks, an objective study by a third party is preferred to an internal analysis. It is an advantage in liability and due diligence.</li>\n<li>Brings creative, “out-of-the-box” thinking.</li>\n<li>Is less susceptible to internal politics and bureaucracy.</li>\n<li>Usually costs less than hiring staff: no capital outlay or payroll burden. Especially if the work is periodic and does not justify a full-time position.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Resistance: middle managers and line employees may fear for their jobs. The fear is mostly unfounded, but consultant and management must address it. Typical concerns:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Asking for outside help suggests that the security staff is incompetent.</li>\n<li>A negative report from an outsider makes the security program and the organization look bad.</li>\n<li>An outsider who knows the company in depth could compromise the organization and its policies and procedures.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Even so, using consultants allows the security executive to add temporary talent, reduce costs, and improve the status of the department and its staff.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e2\">3. Types of Security Consultants (4.2)</h3><p>Three broad external categories. In addition, an internal security consultant or an advisory committee can serve as an internal resource to detect problems before calling an outsider.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Key features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Security management consultant</td>\n<td>Helps the client manage its protection strategies</td>\n<td>Largest group; specialized by sector; many are generalists; does not get into detailed technical design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Technical security consultant</td>\n<td>Converts concepts and functionality into detailed plans and equipment specifications</td>\n<td>Requires years of technical training and experience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forensic security consultant</td>\n<td>Investigation, evidence, vulnerabilities, mitigation, and litigation</td>\n<td>Works only on security issues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security advisory committee</td>\n<td>Internal resource that monitors whether current measures are adequate</td>\n<td>Decides whether a problem is solved in-house or referred to an outside consultant</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>3.1 Security Management Consultants (4.2.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are the largest group in this niche profession.</li>\n<li>Their specialty is the basis of their expertise and reputation. The most frequent: healthcare, manufacturing, transportation, banking, finance, and retail.</li>\n<li>Knowing the consultant's specialty is a key selection criterion.</li>\n<li>Example 1: a retailer assessing its distribution system should look for expertise in retail security, loss prevention, or supply chain.</li>\n<li>Example 2: a theme park rewriting its policy and procedure manual should look for experience in theme parks and in policy writing.</li>\n<li>Both examples show the importance of the scope of work.</li>\n<li>Other specialties could also serve. For the retail case: experts in warehouses, trucking, delivery services, or shipping and receiving. For the park: a retail or loss prevention consultant.</li>\n<li>Many are generalists. Example: a banking and finance expert generally knows investigations, physical and electronic security, and preemployment screening.</li>\n<li>Limit: they can give the functional concepts of a system, but are not specialists in its detailed design.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2 Technical Security Consultants (4.2.2)</h4>\n<p>They have specific technical or subject-matter expertise. Typical applications:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>physical security and systems integration;</li>\n<li>information technology security (IT security);</li>\n<li>personnel security;</li>\n<li>convergence (combining groups, procedures, or technologies);</li>\n<li>legal matters and other regulations;</li>\n<li>engineering;</li>\n<li>personal or proprietary information;</li>\n<li>liability and due diligence;</li>\n<li>management of security personnel and the protection force.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Function and use:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They translate the management consultant's concepts into plans and equipment specifications.</li>\n<li>Some also write policies and procedures, or subcontract that part to a management consultant.</li>\n<li>Typical use: new construction or renovation. They work with architects and design engineers.</li>\n<li>They integrate access control, video surveillance, and alarm monitoring into the initial design.</li>\n<li>They detect security problems in the plans before they are finalized.</li>\n<li>They recommend hardware and software compatible with the building's other systems.</li>\n<li>Advantage: they keep security at the forefront of planning and save money, because they avoid retrofitting security after construction.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.3 Forensic Security Consultants (4.2.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Forensic consulting covers: investigation; identification and collection of evidence; identification of vulnerabilities; mitigation strategies; and litigation.</li>\n<li>They may be called an expert or expert witness. That term is broad and implies expertise in any subject; the forensic security expert works only on security issues.</li>\n<li>Management and technical consultants may also accept forensic assignments in civil security lawsuits.</li>\n<li>Use-of-force case: a nightclub security employee ejects a drunk customer, who suffers serious injuries. The expert gives an opinion on whether the force was reasonable or excessive.</li>\n<li>False arrest case: a customer is detained for theft and it is proven that there was no crime. The expert gives an opinion on whether there was probable cause for the detention.</li>\n<li>Risk managers use them to assess whether a negligence claim has merit or whether a claim against the insurance has merit.</li>\n<li>Some security departments, with their insurer, require certain forensic consultants in their defense because of prior successes in litigation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.4 Security Advisory Committee (4.2.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Internal resource that helps executives and the chief security officer (CSO) verify that current measures are adequate.</li>\n<li>If changes are needed, it helps decide whether they are corrected with internal resources or referred to an outside consultant.</li>\n<li>Purpose, according to the book's sample policy statement: critically examine the program to protect all assets; maintain overall oversight of the program; and help meet corporate and government requirements.</li>\n<li>It is chaired by a project coordinator. It reviews the program <strong>at least quarterly</strong>.</li>\n<li>Functions: see whether additional measures are needed; advise on changes to policies or procedures; evaluate proposals according to their effect on the company, on specific units, and on employees; receive suggestions from supervisors and employees; and consider corrective actions.</li>\n<li>Members: represent key functions, have internal stature and credibility, and know the operation well.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e3\">4. How to Use a Consultant (4.3)</h3><p>Hiring arises from a specific problem, need, challenge, or goal.</p>\n<p>Case: a supermarket chain suffers many violent crimes at one store. If the scope is limited to that store, the consultant is asked to measure the crime level and recommend changes. In practice, the consultant will do a complete crime analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Crime analysis</strong> (Vellani 2001): a logical examination of the crimes that have overcome preventive measures. It includes the frequency of specific crimes, the timing details of each incident (hour and day), and the risk to occupants. It also includes applying revised standards and preventive measures that, if met and monitored, can solve the problem.</p>\n<p>Consultant's steps in the case:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Determine what crimes occurred at the store and in its parking lot.</li>\n<li>Assess the existing security measures where they occurred.</li>\n<li>Note the additional measures needed to block future opportunities.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Effective combination of solutions that may be recommended:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED): architectural design plus prevention methods;</li>\n<li>changes in environmental design;</li>\n<li>updating of policies and procedures;</li>\n<li>more security personnel;</li>\n<li>improvement of physical security requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Warning sign: a non-independent consultant who promotes a single product or a limited range of measures. A good program uses a varied, well-considered set of measures.</li>\n<li>Security-in-depth: series of barriers in a protection plan. If management rejects the proposal because of competition for resources, the independent consultant rules on whether the barriers are efficient and cost-effective.</li>\n<li>The consultant must also detect whether the barriers create new hazards or problems and how to solve them.</li>\n<li>“Fresh eyes”: the outsider sees what the store staff do not see every day.</li>\n<li>Management usually accepts the consultant's ideas because he or she brings experience from other companies and knows industry standards.</li>\n<li>Sometimes the consultant organizes meetings among clients from several companies to discuss best practices.</li>\n<li>The consultant must stay above internal politics: getting into it distracts and costs time and money.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e4\">5. How to Find a Consultant (4.4)</h3><ul>\n<li>Logical starting point: the consultants the executive already knows.</li>\n<li>The more specialized, the more selective the consultant is in accepting assignments.</li>\n<li>Distrust the independent consultant who claims to master all aspects of security.</li>\n<li>Consulting firms bring together varied experts to broaden the assignments they accept.</li>\n<li>Best source: a colleague's recommendation, ideally from a similar business.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Associations to search (companies without security contacts):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of source</th>\n<th>Organizations cited by the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Security associations</td>\n<td>International Association of Professional Security Consultants (IAPSC); ASIS International</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-sector consulting</td>\n<td>Institute of Management Consultants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sector associations</td>\n<td>Building Owners and Managers Association; Institute for Real Estate Management; International Association of Chiefs of Police; or local equivalents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Others found on the Internet</td>\n<td>International Professional Security Association; Professional Information Security Association (lists consultants by service and location); Information Systems Security Association</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book writes “Institute for Real Estate Management”; the real name is Institute <strong>of</strong> Real Estate Management.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e5\">6. Selecting the Consultant (4.5)</h3><p>It requires reviewing credentials and interviewing. Depending on the size of the project, a request for proposal (RFP) can be used with the steps from chapter 3. Alternative method, <strong>after defining the scope of work</strong>, in five steps:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What is done</th>\n<th>Details</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Identify candidates</td>\n<td>Ask peers and colleagues for names</td>\n<td>Industry associations (the IAPSC website lists consultants by location, services, and industry); ads on industry websites</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Invite to submit an application</td>\n<td>Tailored application with comparable data (model in Appendix 4A)</td>\n<td>Alternative: letter of services, using 4A as a checklist; attach résumé; proof of license or registration where required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Evaluate the applications</td>\n<td>Compare quality of documents and credentials</td>\n<td>Contact several previous clients; with the best, background investigation through references and structured interview</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Interview the top two to three</td>\n<td>In person, with at least two company representatives</td>\n<td>Redacted work samples; same questions for everyone; probe their security philosophy; prior walk-through if there is physical security; ask them to explain how they would approach a problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Negotiate an agreement and finalize</td>\n<td>Negotiate with the first candidate</td>\n<td>Scope, deliverable, methodology, deadlines, and expenses; if it fails, move quickly to the next; then present the contract</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Details of step 2:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The sample résumé (Appendix 4B) includes a section on knowledge, skills, and abilities (KSAs). There the consultant adapts it to the project.</li>\n<li>Application plus résumé allow a uniform comparison.</li>\n<li>Asking for both discourages weak candidates: their own documents rule them out.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Evaluation criteria according to the ASIS Risk Assessment guideline (2015):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>verify background, education, and experience;</li>\n<li>test knowledge and skills;</li>\n<li>review written work samples;</li>\n<li>interview to evaluate knowledge, communication, and personal behavior;</li>\n<li>confirm relevant credentials and certifications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Non-local candidate: negotiate the cost of bringing him or her in, or have an employee travel to see him or her. Teleconferencing is an increasingly useful option.</li>\n<li>Contract: sample professional services agreement in Appendix 4C.</li>\n<li>4C includes supplementary agreements on handling proprietary or government-classified information and on conflicts of interest.</li>\n<li>All are discussed and signed <strong>before</strong> starting. Always consult legal counsel when drafting the contract.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e6\">7. Fees and Expenses (4.6)</h3><p>Factors that set fees: the subject matter, the level of expertise, and the geographic region.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Consider time and quality: a low fee can turn out more expensive, because a less skilled consultant takes longer.</li>\n<li>The industry has a long history of awarding to the lowest bidder.</li>\n<li>Some product or service companies offer very cheap consulting. Their objectivity is doubtful if the solution leads to buying their equipment or services.</li>\n<li>Faced with an apparent bargain: <em>caveat emptor</em> (“let the buyer beware”).</li>\n<li>Medium or large firms charge more than the individual consultant or small firm. It may be due to higher overhead, but also to analysts, editors, and specialists who work as a team.</li>\n<li>All bill direct charges (time and travel) and overhead: office rent, administrative help, proposal expenses, publications, and professional taxes and licenses.</li>\n<li>That is why the daily rate does not equal an annual income: they do not work every day, but their expenses continue.</li>\n<li>Time tracking: time and expense software, project diary, spreadsheets, or billing and scheduling software.</li>\n<li>The client chooses the form of payment that fits its accounting and the type of assignment.</li>\n<li>There are <strong>five options</strong>, not mutually exclusive (e.g., a retainer plus additional hours). Also set how expenses are billed and approved.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Arrangement</th>\n<th>When it applies</th>\n<th>How it works, advantages, and risks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hourly fees</td>\n<td>Assignment of less than a day with uncertain duration; forensic consulting</td>\n<td>“Let the meter run.” E.g.: interviewing an employee who is being reassigned, about two hours. In forensic work it is charged by the hour and fraction (avoiding litigation, assessing a case, negotiating a settlement)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily fees</td>\n<td>Assignments of one or several days</td>\n<td>Hourly rate × 8. It usually benefits the client, because the workday can run long, unless the contract fixes the hours. A number of days at a fixed rate and a somewhat lower rate afterward can be agreed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed fees or flat fee</td>\n<td>Days that can be estimated precisely and are controlled by the consultant; non-complex study</td>\n<td>Total amount, often with expenses included. Calculated with the consultant's hourly rate plus that of office staff. Advantage: compare offers and budget exactly. Not suitable for implementing a program: the consultant depends on client employees and absorbs the extra time; in that case the scope must be defined very carefully to protect both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not-to-exceed fees</td>\n<td>When a guaranteed limit is wanted</td>\n<td>Costs of contingencies or delays are not passed on to the client, unless the client agrees. Difference from fixed: it allows paying less than estimated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retainers</td>\n<td>Regular use of the consultant</td>\n<td>The consultant commits to a certain number of days a year and the client has guaranteed access. Substantially discounted rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Not-to-exceed example: 5 days are estimated with a cap of 7. If it is finished in 5 or 6, 5 or 6 are paid. If 8 are needed, 7 are paid.</p>\n<p>Retainer details:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Example: 2 days a month at a fixed rate per day, or 24 days a year at a fixed annual price.</li>\n<li>The client secures the minimum days; the consultant secures an income.</li>\n<li>It is negotiable: the client can use all the days in the first half of the year or less than half.</li>\n<li>The consultant keeps the payment even if the minimum days are not used.</li>\n<li>Disadvantage for the consultant: scheduling problems if an emergency arises from a retainer client while he or she is working on another project.</li>\n<li>Recommended solution: set specific days in specific months. If not possible, start on the first available day after notice.</li>\n<li>Extra days: paid at the discounted rate or the normal rate, as agreed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Miscellaneous arrangements (4.6.6):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>agencies that bill for individual consultants, especially experts and specialized services;</li>\n<li>full or partial payment with equity in the client;</li>\n<li>percentage of the savings achieved (uncommon in security management consulting).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>With innovative formulas, both parties seek legal advice. A clear, binding agreement avoids litigation or interruptions.</p>\n<p>Expenses (4.6.7):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They cover transportation, subsistence, administrative services, and other project costs.</li>\n<li>They are reduced if the consultant uses the client's services: administrative assistance, office, and copies.</li>\n<li>Reimbursement at actual cost, supported with expense reports. Alternative: daily per diem for subsistence plus actual cost of transportation and others.</li>\n<li>Define what is reimbursable and the limits (e.g., daily cap on hotel and meals), and communicate it during selection.</li>\n<li>Common practice: give the consultant the same travel allowances as the client's senior management.</li>\n<li>Airfare in coach class, unless first or business class is expressly approved. Do not assume everyone knows that policy.</li>\n<li>International travel: almost always at least 2 days of travel (round trip). It requires special compensation.</li>\n<li>Goal: agree on the expenses at the start. Appendix 4C has expense forms and guidelines.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e7\">8. Working with Consultants (4.7)</h3><ul>\n<li>After signing and before starting, inform affected managers and employees. The announcement, ideally from the chief executive officer (CEO), asks for cooperation.</li>\n<li>Assign a consulting project coordinator who informs, helps, and monitors progress.</li>\n<li>The scope of work must include a work plan, progress reports, and a final report.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.1 Coordinating the Project (4.7.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Option A: a single representative as coordinator, normally the CSO or the vice president (VP) of security.</li>\n<li>Option B: a temporary project committee, often drawn from the security advisory committee.</li>\n<li>There must be a designated alternate who responds in time if the coordinator is absent.</li>\n<li>An internal project sponsor is advisable. Good candidate: whoever proposed the original idea. He or she is an ally of the consultant and the project manager.</li>\n<li>Mission of the coordinator and the committee: to be the liaison between consultant and company. It is a critical task.</li>\n<li>Members: represent the areas involved, know the organization well, and have the credibility and influence to obtain data and schedule interviews.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.2 Organizational Orientation (4.7.2)</h4>\n<p>The coordinator organizes an orientation that includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>background and responsibilities of key personnel, before meeting them, and their role in the assignment;</li>\n<li>organization chart;</li>\n<li>operating environment;</li>\n<li>key assets and functions;</li>\n<li>relevant internal and external relationships;</li>\n<li>specific legislative or regulatory controls;</li>\n<li>company history;</li>\n<li>senior management's philosophy;</li>\n<li>competitive position;</li>\n<li>if publicly traded: the latest annual shareholder report and mandatory regulatory reports.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In addition:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>discuss results of similar previous projects;</li>\n<li>reveal unique or unusual situations;</li>\n<li>warn of cultural idiosyncrasies: a minor action by the consultant could cause an incident.</li>\n<li>Even so, the consultant is an objective, independent observer, with the freedom and the obligation to state the facts.</li>\n<li>Work in another country: he or she must know the culture and customs, working conditions, local legislation, and visa requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.3 Levels of Assistance (4.7.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The consultant's time costs money: the coordinator ensures timely support.</li>\n<li>Support departments: legal, labor relations, public relations, and finance; qualified internal technical help.</li>\n<li>Internal staff can prepare letters, memoranda, and reports; the contract's reports are prepared by the consultant.</li>\n<li>Nondisclosure agreements: necessary, because the consultant has access to sensitive information.</li>\n<li>Method for handling sensitive information, including conclusions and recommendations: few people, and trusted ones.</li>\n<li>Key: written reports are subject to discovery by the opposing party in a trial. Define the custodian and location in case of a subpoena.</li>\n<li>Visits to other companies or sites: the coordinator handles security clearances, flights or corporate aircraft, rental cars, and hotels.</li>\n<li>The methodology must be sound and accepted. The most used: the ASIS Risk Assessment guideline; IAPSC's <em>Forensic Methodology Best Practice</em>; and ISO 31000 <em>Risk Management—Guidelines</em> (International Organization for Standardization).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.4 Scope of Work (4.7.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It must be in the request for quote and in the written contract. Those few paragraphs are not enough to start.</li>\n<li>Before starting, client and consultant review objectives, goals, scope, and deliverables, together with the coordinator, committee, and those affected.</li>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> the scope of work is the central objective or clear focus of the assignment.</li>\n<li>Example: “reassess the distribution system to detect procedural deficiencies that cause cargo losses.” Every inspection, interview, and document is guided and limited by that objective.</li>\n<li>The initial review defines strategies, brings up to date anyone who did not take part in the selection, and allows questions. The consultant is already part of the team.</li>\n<li><strong>Scope creep:</strong> the scope grows after signing and starting. Either party can raise it, often out of necessity.</li>\n<li>It is of most concern if payment is hourly and the budget can be exceeded.</li>\n<li>It is expanded only with the agreement of both parties, in writing, as an addendum to the contract.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.5 Work Plans (4.7.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are prepared with the coordinator after agreeing on the scope: tasks, priorities, assignments, and schedules.</li>\n<li>Deadlines are turned into milestone and goal charts to review progress.</li>\n<li>The frequency of reviews and the information the client will deliver are set, with dates.</li>\n<li>In long projects: frequent progress meetings.</li>\n<li>The consultant is ultimately responsible for staying on course despite internal politics or culture.</li>\n<li>Measuring progress: earned value analysis, done by consultant and coordinator at those meetings.</li>\n<li>If support deadlines are not met, the work slows or stops. The coordinator is responsible for seeing that they are met.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.6 Progress Reports (4.7.6)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Periodic meetings and written reports, with the qualifier “if deemed necessary” in the work plan.</li>\n<li>All project staff and interested managers attend.</li>\n<li>The coordinator, or an assigned member, takes the minutes: decisions, progress, tasks, and deadlines.</li>\n<li>Frequency depends on size and complexity. Too many meetings hinder the work; too few put control at risk.</li>\n<li>If the meetings are enough, interim reports can be omitted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.7 Final Report (4.7.7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every project requires a written final report. In management consulting, with recommendations for the future, it must be detailed. Even a technical assignment with a system up and running requires one.</li>\n<li>Structure: 1) executive summary; 2) results achieved; 3) recommendations. It is advisable to follow the order of the scope.</li>\n<li>Results: whether the goals were met, which parts of the plan were not achieved and why.</li>\n<li>Recommendations: additional work needed and how to do it.</li>\n<li>Final briefing to senior management: given by the consultant, with the coordinator's support.</li>\n<li>Number each recommendation. Example from the book: “Recommendation #23”: stop using part-time police officers in the cash offices. Replace them with modern procedures and physical barriers, with a two-door man trap and remote electronic access control. Its implementation would be “Project #23.”</li>\n<li>The report must have enough detail for the client's staff to carry out the tasks.</li>\n<li>If the client needs the consultant to implement: additional contract or amendment, with a new scope.</li>\n<li>Example: after a vulnerability study, the consultant searches for and prequalifies a security executive. Usual fee: <strong>25% to 30% of the first-year salary</strong>, plus search expenses.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e8\">9. The Future of Consulting (4.8)</h3><ul>\n<li>The use of consultants will tend to grow.</li>\n<li>When downsizing, companies lose internal specialists and then hire consultants. That is why many consultants focus on a niche they offer to many clients.</li>\n<li>Niche example: crime analysis. The employees who did it were promoted or moved to general functions.</li>\n<li>Consultant alliances, increasingly common:<ul>\n<li>management consultant who recommends improving access control + technical consultant who specifies, bids, and supervises the installation;</li>\n<li>forensic consultant who testifies about a security deficiency + management consultant who recommends how to correct it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Some organizations have specialized consultants on staff and turn to vendors and other consultants when needed.</li>\n<li>The key to success for both parties is knowing how to work effectively with the consultant.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-4-e9\">Key Terms</h3><p>security management consultant · technical security consultant · forensic security consultant · expert witness · security advisory committee · scope of work · scope creep · addendum · crime analysis · crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED) · security-in-depth · request for proposal (RFP) · request for quote · knowledge, skills, and abilities (KSAs) · hourly fees · daily fees · fixed fees · not-to-exceed fees · retainer · per diem · caveat emptor · overhead · project coordinator · project sponsor · liaison · nondisclosure agreement · discovery · subpoena · earned value analysis · work plan · progress reports · final report · executive summary · final briefing · man trap · liability · due diligence · probable cause · false arrest · IAPSC · ISO 31000</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Why Consultants Are Used"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. The Value of Consultants"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. Types of Security Consultants"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. How to Use a Consultant"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. How to Find a Consultant"},{"id":"e5","t":"6. Selecting the Consultant"},{"id":"e6","t":"7. Fees and Expenses"},{"id":"e7","t":"8. Working with Consultants"},{"id":"e8","t":"9. The Future of Consulting"},{"id":"e9","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"5. How to Find a Consultant","h":"the book writes “Institute for Real Estate Management”; the real name is Institute <strong>of</strong> Real Estate Management."}]},"personnel-5":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-5-e0\">1. Purpose and Collateral Value of Executive Protection (5.1)</h3><p><strong>Definition.</strong> Executive protection (EP), also called close protection: security and risk mitigation measures for people with elevated personal risk. That risk can come from their employment, high public profile, great wealth (high net worth), or affiliations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Many programs are loosely based on the standards and procedures of highly visible government programs.</li>\n<li>The main references are the U.S. Secret Service and the Diplomatic Security Service.</li>\n<li>The field evolved greatly in the early 21st century. Today a dynamic professional with multiple skills is demanded, one who integrates into changing environments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key distinction from a traditional police officer or guard:</strong> the EP agent's main objective is to keep the principal out of harm's way. The agent does not confront the conflict head-on. The agent quickly gets the client out of the situation and, above all, avoids it through advance planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Purpose of the program.</strong> It varies, and the professional must know exactly which risks his or her program mitigates.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Specific, credible threat: requires a particular style of close protection for a period.</li>\n<li>Most common case: a sustainable, programmatic, and flexible approach.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>People who may require protection:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>People recognizable by position or prominence, sought out by the public and with public personal data. E.g.: A-List celebrity or CEO (chief executive officer) of a large company.</li>\n<li>People with a highly controversial job or position. E.g.: leader of a political party or group.</li>\n<li>People of great wealth, with higher risk of extortion or kidnapping.</li>\n<li>People who have received threats against their safety.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Also business leaders. The board of directors often imposes protection on the CEO and/or the COO (chief operating officer). Reason: the harm the company would suffer if something happened to them.</p>\n<p><strong>First step to build a program:</strong> assess the risk to the individual, his or her family, and the organization. The current risk portfolio must be understood and the future one anticipated.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nothing detracts more from credibility than a plan that does not respond to the client's real risk.</li>\n<li>Example: demanding four agents for a CEO at a safe event, when his or her peers have one. The client will not forget it and it will be hard to persuade him or her later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Hard vs. soft skills:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hard skills</td>\n<td>Shooting, martial arts, driving</td>\n<td>It is the image of the bodyguard in popular culture. They are important, but the client almost never sees them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Soft skills</td>\n<td>Clear and efficient communication, logistical planning, critical thinking and problem solving, verbal conflict resolution</td>\n<td>They are what make the agent successful and give the client the most benefit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What the client sees, remembers, and values:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>how the agent represents him or her and his or her organization;</li>\n<li>how the agent integrates into different professional environments;</li>\n<li>how the agent supports his or her family and staff;</li>\n<li>how the agent effectively solves all kinds of problems.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Collateral benefits that every agent must know how to quantify and deliver:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>What it consists of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Security drivers</td>\n<td>They reduce risks. They plan primary and alternate motorcade routes and study traffic. They receive alerts and know the best drop-off and pickup points. They save the client time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time management</td>\n<td>Plan schedule and trips at events, conferences, and engagements. Reduces unnecessary time among crowds and speeds the move from one event to another</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Travel logistics</td>\n<td>Advance planning is the most critical thing the agent does to mitigate risk, and the most useful for the schedule. It is coordinated with the client's staff and contingencies are prepared</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Normalcy</td>\n<td>For ultra-high-profile clients, routine tasks (taking a child to the zoo, getting a coffee) are complicated by the public. That creates risk and annoyance. The agent, discreetly, manages those approaches</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Return on investment (ROI).</strong> EP is costly, so its ROI must be quantified. It is usually tied to the collateral benefits; each program can contribute others of its own. Clients and civilian staff do not see the hours of training. They judge success by how contingencies are resolved, how the agent communicates, and how the agent represents them.</p>\n<p><strong>Intelligence.</strong> Successful protection is tied to a robust, continuous intelligence program. It gives the agent a complete picture of the risk in order to adapt his or her practices.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-5-e1\">2. Domains of Protection (5.2)</h3><h4>2.1 Corporate Executive Protection (5.2.1)</h4>\n<p>If the CEO or another prominent figure is kidnapped or incapacitated, the company can lose focus in the short term. In the long term, it can suffer reputational damage. Business continuity is disrupted: operations, culture, stock price, revenue, and position.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>EP teams have been an integral part of mature security organizations in large corporations for about 20 years.</li>\n<li>Risks are increasingly global and compound, from cyber to privacy.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Corporate program structure models:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n<th>When it is used</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Full-time employees</td>\n<td>EP staff are in-house personnel</td>\n<td>When the board imposes the program and/or several executives use it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outsourced</td>\n<td>Vendors provide the protection</td>\n<td>The book does not specify</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hybrid</td>\n<td>In-house staff set strategy, policies, and procedures; vendors supplement</td>\n<td>It is common to outsource specialties: technical security countermeasures or travel support in a specific region</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Why a corporate program is started:</strong> (1) risk to the executive; (2) business need; (3) need for structure and efficiency to support senior management. The corporate agent must understand the business of EP as much as the tactical and operational.</p>\n<p><strong>Questions that EP agents and managers must know how to answer and explain:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Why did the corporation start the program?</li>\n<li>Is there a board mandate to protect certain people or positions?</li>\n<li>Is there a specific or ongoing risk, and how is it measured?</li>\n<li>Are there key performance indicators (KPIs) for the team?</li>\n<li>Is there a one-, three-, or five-year roadmap to expand the EP team and threat management?</li>\n<li>Is the program corporate only, or does it also provide personal and residential coverage? If so, how are those tasks divided and recorded?</li>\n<li>Does it include the executive's family, and how is that recorded?</li>\n<li>Has the executive had protection before?</li>\n<li>What access will EP managers have to discuss strategy, expectations, and mitigation with executives, staff, and families?</li>\n<li>What other key stakeholders will the team work with? The book cites, without closing the list:<ul>\n<li>legal team;</li>\n<li>chief financial officer (CFO) and tax and executive perk team;</li>\n<li>human resources;</li>\n<li>policy team;</li>\n<li>communications and public relations;</li>\n<li>events staff;</li>\n<li>corporate support staff, such as executive assistants.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Knowing how to explain the need for protection to the board, the CFO, and others makes the program look like a valued professional unit.</p>\n<p><strong>Case: “car brand A”:</strong> if the executive visits a client who manufactures brand A, he or she must arrive in a car of that brand. It teaches that an eye for detail avoids embarrassment and business problems, and demonstrates value.</p>\n<p><strong>U.S. tax regulation.</strong> Corporate EP is usually covered by Internal Revenue Service (IRS) rules on executive perks. Notable requirements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>genuine (bona fide) 24/7 security program, provided by trained professionals;</li>\n<li>a verifiable risk;</li>\n<li>an annual audit demonstrating that a genuine risk exists.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2 Celebrity Protection (5.2.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Historically associated with the term bodyguards. In the 21st century it became professionalized.</li>\n<li>It can overlap with other domains: many corporate leaders and some religious leaders are iconic.</li>\n<li>Fame is achieved through media attention. It includes entertainers, athletes, media personalities, actors, and artists. They depend heavily on their public brand.</li>\n<li>Typical challenges: meet-and-greet encounters with fans, concerts or performances, and press events.</li>\n<li>Those events raise the risk to privacy and the risk of being targeted by persons of concern or stalkers.</li>\n<li>According to studies (Meloy 2016), athletes have greater situational vulnerability. They appear in nightclub altercations, robberies, drive-by shootings, and other violent encounters.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.3 Government Protection (5.2.3)</h4>\n<p>All levels of government have units that protect prominent figures.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>U.S. Secret Service</strong>, a division of the Department of Homeland Security. By federal mandate it protects:<ul>\n<li>the president and the vice president;</li>\n<li>their respective families;</li>\n<li>many visiting heads of state;</li>\n<li>presidential candidates;</li>\n<li>national special security events (NSSE).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>United Kingdom:</strong> the prime minister is protected by SO1, a special unit of the Metropolitan Police.</li>\n<li><strong>FBI</strong> (Federal Bureau of Investigation): cross-trained agents protect its director. The model is repeated in other specialized agencies.</li>\n<li><strong>State governors:</strong> the state police provide the “dignitary protection details.”</li>\n<li><strong>Mayors:</strong> specialized units of their municipal police.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Protection agent vs. law enforcement agent.</strong> Although many are sworn police officers, the protection agent's duty is to get the principal out of danger if there is an incident. And, above all, to avoid exposing the principal through planning. The agent does not run toward the problem: the agent flees the danger with the principal. This attitude is hard to retrain in police officers.</p>\n<p><strong>Differences between government and private protection:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Government</th>\n<th>Private</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Authority</td>\n<td>Derives from specific laws and regulations</td>\n<td>Often no formal authority to act</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control of the environment</td>\n<td>The authority may allow control of the principal's area. E.g.: the Secret Service's federal law is 18 U.S.C. Section 3056 (Title 18 of the U.S. Code)</td>\n<td>No such power</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Status</td>\n<td>Sworn officials or law enforcement agents: they have authority and go through demanding training and vetting</td>\n<td>Not necessarily</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weapons</td>\n<td>Often legally armed</td>\n<td>Not always permitted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Mandatory regular cycles of training and tactical and physical evaluation</td>\n<td>Not always required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Plans</td>\n<td>Strict protocols: advance report with primary and contingency plans, reviewable after an incident</td>\n<td>The book does not require it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incidents</td>\n<td>There may be a formal investigation by bodies such as the U.S. Congress</td>\n<td>The book does not mention it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Essential characteristics of the effective agent, common to all levels:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Excellent critical thinking.</li>\n<li>Effective communication.</li>\n<li>Ability to de-escalate situations.</li>\n<li>Ability to plan and advance all of the principal's movements.</li>\n<li>Anticipate challenges and have primary and backup plans for every scenario.</li>\n<li>Access to information and intelligence.</li>\n<li>Assess risk constantly and apply it to the protection plan.</li>\n<li>Focus on keeping the principal out of danger, not on getting into an altercation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Many move from government to the private sector and their training is valuable. But they must understand the weight of soft skills. Also the limits of their authority and the licenses required in the private sector.</p>\n<h4>2.4 Faith-Based Protection (5.2.4)</h4>\n<p>Aimed at leaders of faith-based entities. Two extremes of risk:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>people fixated on their preaching, who believe they are “one step away” from a higher power and cling to them in a dangerous way;</li>\n<li>people who vehemently oppose their preaching and want to harm them.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because they are in the public eye and in contact with their community, their risks resemble those of celebrities.</p>\n<h4>2.5 Hybrid Protection Models (5.2.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They connect several domains. E.g.: iconic corporate figures with celebrity status, or politicians with a celebrity presence.</li>\n<li>Global trust barometers indicate that people increasingly depend on corporate leaders and celebrities for opinions on social issues.</li>\n<li>Deloitte, <em>Global Human Capital Trends</em> 2018 (Agarwal 2018): organizations are no longer judged only by financial results or product quality. They are judged by their relationship with workers, customers, and communities, and by their social impact.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse:</strong> “hybrid” has two senses in the chapter. In 5.2.1 it is a structure model (in-house staff plus vendors). In 5.2.5 it is the mixing of domains (corporate, celebrity, government).</p><h3 id=\"personnel-5-e2\">3. Managing the Executive Protection Program (5.3)</h3><h4>3.1 Third-Party Operations (5.3.1)</h4>\n<p>A corporation can outsource part or all of its protection operation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Supplementary services:</strong> local security drivers; advance staff who know the destination city; additional protection staff authorized to carry weapons in that city.</li>\n<li><strong>Auxiliary elements:</strong> protective intelligence analysts and technical surveillance countermeasures (TSCM) technicians.</li>\n<li><strong>Full outsourcing:</strong> including management and strategic direction of the program.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The approach depends on many factors. A key one: whether the corporate security program has the depth to run it. That includes recruiting and retaining staff in this specialized career and handling their logistics.</p>\n<p>The process of hiring consultants is in Chapter 4. <strong>Considerations specific to hiring EP consulting or services:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Types of services the provider offers.</li>\n<li>Whether the staff are the provider's own or subcontracted.</li>\n<li>Where it has offices and staff.</li>\n<li>Insurance.</li>\n<li>Licenses.</li>\n<li>Reputation. The team is the principal's immediate circle. If the provider carries ethical problems, litigation, or criminal matters, it can seriously damage the executive's brand.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Other considerations when evaluating providers (West 2016):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>strategic planning support;</li>\n<li>residential security services;</li>\n<li>protective surveillance or countersurveillance;</li>\n<li>ability to add intelligence services;</li>\n<li>event security support;</li>\n<li>possible investigative support.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2 Threat, Vulnerability, and Risk Assessments (5.3.2)</h4>\n<p>They are similar in concept to traditional risk assessments. They are aligned with the strategic approach of enterprise security risk management (ESRM).</p>\n<p>Unlike static facilities, they measure the principal's own risk according to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>career;</li>\n<li>public image;</li>\n<li>lifestyle;</li>\n<li>current position and status;</li>\n<li>family history.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The scope can include the impact of the entity he or she represents (religious group, corporation) on his or her risk. Also his or her social environment: family members as soft targets to reach him or her. E.g.: the kidnapping of a child can prevent him or her from functioning.</p>\n<h4>Threat Assessment</h4>\n<p>In EP it has two meanings:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>What it assesses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Proximity to criminal elements</td>\n<td>Terrorism and random crimes. The risk of random crime comes from the proximity of residence, office, or frequented places to areas with more crime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Behavior-based</td>\n<td>Persons or groups of interest detected by the protective intelligence function. The risk of targeted acts depends on public presence: conference, religious event, concert</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The company must consider both. In addition, violence is not enough: threats to the personal brand and to personal or corporate assets must be included.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Frauds against high-net-worth people: fraudulent unemployment claims, fraudulent bank or credit accounts, threats to their online presence.</li>\n<li>Cybercrimes: social media hacking, smishing (text message fraud), SIM swapping, and others.</li>\n<li>They can attack accounts of family members and staff to reach the principal's.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Threat vectors of a comprehensive assessment:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>technical (mobile and cyber systems);</li>\n<li>reputational;</li>\n<li>physical;</li>\n<li>health and well-being (lifestyle, medical, natural).</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Vulnerability Assessment</h4>\n<p>It considers the environments the principal passes through:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>static locations: home and office;</li>\n<li>vessels and aircraft;</li>\n<li>fixed base operations (FBO), that is, private aviation terminals;</li>\n<li>other frequent destinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Vulnerabilities are not limited to security systems. They can be psychological, administrative, technical, and physical. There are also external factors: the capacity of local government and police to respond to crime.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Understaffed police, fire, or emergency medical services create a vulnerability that may require reinforced mitigation.</li>\n<li>If the average police response time exceeds a tolerable window, shelter-in-place or evacuation strategies are reinforced.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Risk Assessment</h4>\n<p>It is appropriate to use ASIS risk models. But in EP there are two aspects for setting the executive's risk profile:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Attractiveness of the target.</strong> It influences the probability of attack. It looks at whether the principal is iconic, highly recognizable, or associated with very attractive assets or people. Also whether he or she is an important social, political, or economic figure.</li>\n<li><strong>Probability that a threat will succeed.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Threats are analyzed according to: position in the company; access and exposure to potential adversaries; access to wealth or other lifestyle traits; publicity; travel habits.</p>\n<p><strong>Questions an EP risk analysis answers:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Who would want to harm the executive?</li>\n<li>How do adversaries obtain information about him or her?</li>\n<li>What is the current probability of each identified threat?</li>\n<li>Does the executive want, need, and accept protection during the workday? Only on trips? 24 hours?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The specialist must assess the probability that threats will be carried out successfully. The most worrying are those known to occur but that arrive by surprise. <strong>Sample of real threats to executives (11):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Assassination.</li>\n<li>Kidnapping.</li>\n<li>Extortion.</li>\n<li>Street violence.</li>\n<li>Targeted attacks.</li>\n<li>Workplace violence.</li>\n<li>Online account hacking.</li>\n<li>Danger to family members.</li>\n<li>Public embarrassment (deliberate or accidental).</li>\n<li>Injury.</li>\n<li>Illness or medical emergency.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Graduated levels of protection.</strong> Not all of a company's executives have the same risk. Some are public and represent controversial aspects; others work behind the scenes. That is why a holistic approach is advisable.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>E.g.: the full weight of the program for the CEO; transportation and protective intelligence for the entire C-suite.</li>\n<li>The gradation is based on the individual's risk and on his or her value to the corporation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to set an executive's threat level:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify all threat elements: protesters, criminals, extremists, terrorists, workplace violence, and hazards from travel or other activities.</li>\n<li>Analyze whether each element threatens the executive.</li>\n<li>Determine how an event could develop.</li>\n<li>Identify anyone with the capability and intent to harm, a history of threatening the executive or others, or who has already targeted him or her.</li>\n<li>Assign the principal a risk category and give him or her the appropriate protection.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Risk assessments do not last: risk changes often. That is why they are repeated periodically.</p>\n<h4>3.3 Operations (5.3.3)</h4>\n<p>Once threats and vulnerabilities are defined, in-house employees or third parties operate. <strong>Functions of the EP team:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Contribute to security policies, including the team's use-of-force protocols.</li>\n<li>Develop training plans in personal protection, travel, and security awareness for executives and employees.</li>\n<li>Manage event security and implement protection protocols for staff and VIPs (very important persons).</li>\n<li>Communicate and liaise with local police and federal agencies where appropriate, especially on trips abroad.</li>\n<li>Develop, implement, and supervise security measures at the executives' residences.</li>\n<li>Develop and implement processes, procedures, and best practices in: fire safety and life protection; executive data privacy; crisis communication; event security.</li>\n<li>Manage travel security:\n   - advance destinations: best routes, locate venues, detect risks, assess hotels, and refine plans;\n   - brief senior management on the logistics and risks of trips;\n   - communicate risks to the executive's support team before the trip;\n   - be ready to inform the executive of new risks during the trip.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>3.4 Metrics and Return on Investment (5.3.4)</h4>\n<p><strong>Possible metrics (Oatman 2015):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Number of threats or other communications.</li>\n<li>Number of public appearances by the principal with protesters present.</li>\n<li>Executive time saved.</li>\n<li>Number of events with in-person EP service.</li>\n<li>Number of home drive-bys when the executive is away.</li>\n<li>Number of risk assessments and advances performed.</li>\n<li>Number of trips a year to medium- or high-risk destinations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Key performance indicators (KPIs).</strong> They keep the program focused on what matters. Requirements of a good EP KPI:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>align with the program's goals and their importance;</li>\n<li>be understandable to all parties;</li>\n<li>be measurable and consistent;</li>\n<li>be timely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Possible KPI areas (West 2016):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>What it includes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous risk assessments</td>\n<td>Principal's prominence, destination risk, intelligence (with social media monitoring), persons of concern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stakeholder satisfaction</td>\n<td>Opinion of the principal or traveler, his or her productivity, opinion of key partners, of program management and vendors, and of event managers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Responsiveness</td>\n<td>Availability and adequacy of EP resources, issues addressed, performance on urgent requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quality of services</td>\n<td>Program management, deliverables, field support, administrative tasks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Team workload</td>\n<td>Travel days, domestic trips, international trips, compensatory time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Readiness</td>\n<td>Training, drills, physical assessments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quality of communications</td>\n<td>Appropriate, accurate, clear, and concise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational transparency</td>\n<td>Execution as expected; stakeholders understand the program well</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Financial performance</td>\n<td>Budget set and met; response to problems detected</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>3.5 Essential Competencies of Protection Personnel (5.3.5)</h4>\n<p>Success depends on choosing service-minded candidates. <strong>Behavioral attributes that are not acquired through training:</strong> integrity, professional behavior, good judgment, team mindset, and self-awareness.</p>\n<p><strong>History of the competency framework:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Before 2019:</strong> the industry relied on publications without peer review and curricula written by schools in the 1980s. Without defined competencies, recruitment, selection, and training in knowledge, skills, and abilities (KSA) suffered.</li>\n<li><strong>2019:</strong> the ASIS Executive Protection Council, Committee for Standards &amp; Guidelines, began drafting competencies with the international community.</li>\n<li><strong>2020:</strong> an international team of experts drafted the competencies with support from the University of Nebraska Public Policy Center. Working groups broke them down into KSAs. The university validated them with an international survey (University of Nebraska 2020).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Essential competencies (6 categories):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Components</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Personal characteristics</td>\n<td>Physical fitness; mental fitness; traits and attributes (communication, integrity, resilience, diligence, professionalism, judgment, critical thinking, problem solving)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Legal and ethical considerations</td>\n<td>Confidentiality; rules and laws of the jurisdiction (duty of care, certification and licensing, legal liability; open list in the book); use-of-force guidelines (organization policies, force continuum, reports; open list); industry ethical standards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Information security and management</td>\n<td>Basic technology risks; familiarity with technology security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Communication</td>\n<td>Effective communication; listening practices; elicitation of information</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Protective operations: planning</td>\n<td>Advance work; management of oneself and small teams; threat, risk, and vulnerability assessment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Protective operations: tactical</td>\n<td>Principles of practice (defensive tactics and de-escalation, weapons, surveillance and countersurveillance, equipment, alertness; open list); transportation; medical first response (first aid, patient assessment; open list); movement to maintain safety; incident management (situational assessment, evacuation plan, crisis response)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>3.6 Training and Education of Protection Personnel (5.3.6)</h4>\n<p><strong>Three factors when designing the training of a private program:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>A standardized training and education program, so that agents know how to mitigate their clients' risks.</li>\n<li>Governance over those programs: quality standards that withstand critical scrutiny after an incident.</li>\n<li>An audit function that confirms up-to-date regulatory compliance. E.g.: private licenses to operate as an agent in the jurisdiction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is advisable to define what training they receive, how often, and how it is audited. That way the training strategy is aligned with the long-term protection strategy and roadmap.</p>\n<p><strong>Hard skills training.</strong> Two reference federal programs in the U.S.:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the Protective Service Operations Training Program of the Federal Law Enforcement Training Center (FLETC);</li>\n<li>the Secret Service's James J. Rowley Training Center, with its <em>Protective Operations and Protective Detail Training</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They can go beyond the basics, but serve as a guide. Courses must be certified or recognized by local bodies or regulators. List (not exhaustive, according to the book):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Basic protective service operations (PSO):\n   - protection formations;\n   - PSO advances;\n   - case studies and attacks on principals;\n   - cover and evacuation of principals.</li>\n<li>Defensive tactics and appropriate use of force.</li>\n<li>Motorcades:\n   - route surveys;\n   - motorcade formations and the importance of arrivals and departures;\n   - motorcade security.</li>\n<li>Advanced vehicle handling:\n   - offensive and defensive driving techniques;\n   - specialized or technical driving, such as winter driving;\n   - recognition of vehicle ambushes and countermeasures.</li>\n<li>Medical training according to the program's needs:\n   - basic first responder;\n   - ongoing field medical training (scenarios);\n   - emergency medical technician (EMT) or advanced medical training if needed.</li>\n<li>Detection of protective surveillance and countermeasures.</li>\n<li>Protective intelligence and its use in advance planning and operations.</li>\n<li>Firearms:\n   - weapons safety rules and standards;\n   - armed confrontation in a protective services environment.</li>\n<li>Physical fitness standards:\n   - set them together with the human resources (HR) department;\n   - set regular training and evaluation dates.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Then a schedule of ongoing training is set. It must consider the renewal of certifications, the minimums of local regulations, and how to exceed them. Ongoing scenarios, tabletop exercises, and problem solving improve the agent and the team.</p>\n<p><strong>Continuing education and soft skills.</strong> Few agents will experience a large-scale attack like in the movies. Almost always they deal with diverse people to give the principal a smooth, safe experience. They also solve problems, plan, and de-escalate. What makes an agent successful: communicating well, treating people with respect, negotiating, and mediating.</p>\n<p>There are not always courses for this; it is worked on through continuing education. <strong>Opportunities and competencies to consider:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Education in de-escalation. It can be delivered by psychologists specializing in dangerous behaviors, or done together with government agencies:\n   - managing words and body language with an enraged citizen;\n   - calming someone under the influence of substances and/or with altered mental capacity;\n   - training with role-playing to practice the techniques.</li>\n<li>Written and verbal skills:\n   - effective report writing;\n   - how to write an incident report;\n   - how to write a PSO advance report;\n   - how to communicate with the principal and his or her staff.</li>\n<li>Case studies and trends in protective operations:\n   - follow industry magazines, articles, and cases;\n   - join peer and benchmarking groups;\n   - communicate what is learned from cases and apply it to proposals for greater protection.</li>\n<li>Self-improvement:\n   - resourcefulness and resilience;\n   - self-awareness and emotional intelligence.</li>\n<li>Continuous study of the principal's business, clients, and organization. It serves to understand risk and communicate with stakeholders.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The agent must always balance hard and soft skills for long-term success.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-5-e3\">4. Executive Protection Operations (5.4)</h3><h4>4.1 Residential Security Services (5.4.1)</h4>\n<p>Protecting the principal at the main residence and the others has become an important niche. It combines technology (cameras, motion detectors) with close protection agents. It usually operates 24/7, especially at the main residence.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it is necessary:</strong> some corporate programs require it by board mandate. In addition, the data and addresses of wealthy and prominent people are easy to find. That attracts unwanted attention and crowds to their homes.</p>\n<p><strong>Multiple layers with redundancy</strong>, as at large events or monuments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED);</li>\n<li>redundant layers of technology with back-up and rollover if one part fails;</li>\n<li>several layers of staff posts to maximize response;</li>\n<li>engagement and participation of principals, staff, and residents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Principals and team must agree on what to do and how to react in an incident. Staff are trained and evaluated on it. Ideally: residents review it with the team at least once a year.</p>\n<p><strong>Functions:</strong> physical presence, almost always continuous; access control to the house; readiness to respond to incidents; managing unwanted visitors; redirecting the press; patrols and monitoring of anti-intruder systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Complexity.</strong> Unlike the office or an event, the agent is always present in the home. That can generate tension even with very security-conscious principals. Clear communication, clarified expectations, and maximum discretion with principal, family, and neighborhood are required. Trust takes years to build and is lost in moments if protocol is broken.</p>\n<p><strong>Eminem case (Matthews 2020):</strong> a young man broke a window and entered the rapper's house. According to reports, the property's guards were asleep. It is an example of residential security failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Good practice when starting a program or adding agents:</strong> property-specific training, with a period of shadowing experienced agents. <strong>What the new agent must know:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Security systems and technology: cameras, motion sensors, panic alarms, and access control.</li>\n<li>Who updates, maintains, and accesses the data of those systems.</li>\n<li>How the team monitors them: what training is needed and what privacy protocols apply.</li>\n<li>How the principal notifies security if a problem arises.</li>\n<li>How security contacts the principal if an incident is developing on the property.</li>\n<li>The property's emergency response protocols.</li>\n<li>Response time of fire, police, and emergency services, and how they are let in.</li>\n<li>Access control management:\n   - who can enter the house;\n   - with what level of access;\n   - how permanent and temporary staff, such as chefs, are verified;\n   - who has authority to authorize access;\n   - how the access control list is updated and managed;\n   - escalation procedures when an access is questioned or denied.</li>\n<li>Agent posts on the property.</li>\n<li>Post rotation: fixed or roving?</li>\n<li>When and how often they may train on the property.</li>\n<li>Service expectations of the family and staff.</li>\n<li>Training additional to basic EP:<ul>\n<li>intelligence picture of the area and the property;</li>\n<li>metrics and cases of incidents that have occurred on the property;</li>\n<li>all emergency protocols and responses;</li>\n<li>face page of critical staff;</li>\n<li>access control practices.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Privacy protection on the property:<ul>\n<li>when and how often to conduct technical countermeasures sweeps;</li>\n<li>protocol for accepting gifts, furniture, and other objects.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The list is not exhaustive. The residential agent works at a fixed location, often known to the public. Paradoxically, that place is also the principal's safe haven. It is a crucial post and a great force multiplier for earning his or her trust.</p>\n<h4>4.2 Close Protection (5.4.2)</h4>\n<p><strong>Security advances.</strong> Planning the principal's movements in detail is key to the agent's success and the principal's safety. That logistical ability distinguishes the professional from someone who thinks EP is just escorting someone through a crowd. It also demonstrates collateral benefits and ROI, because it maximizes the principal's time and efficiency.</p>\n<p>The agent controls risks proactively. Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>choose hotels in safe areas with a good security record;</li>\n<li>choose primary and alternate motorcade routes that minimize danger;</li>\n<li>plan the walking paths for entering and leaving events, with hold rooms identified;</li>\n<li>plan evacuation routes at each venue;</li>\n<li>secure medical support and any special needs in advance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>International Travel Advances</h4>\n<p>Trips abroad can be riskier if the destination is unfamiliar or dangerous. The team must research from home and also travel to the destination beforehand. First: determine whether the trip is really necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>“Know before you go” steps:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Research current data on the destination online.</li>\n<li>Obtain a professional country report: history, current affairs, and attitude toward the executive's country. There are government and commercial sources.</li>\n<li>Learn the climate, health conditions, public services, time zone(s), and exchange rate.</li>\n<li>Learn the essentials of social customs.</li>\n<li>Clarify why he or she is traveling, how he or she wants to travel, and who will attend the meetings. If there are other prominent figures, see whether the principal will absorb part of their risk.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Steps before departure:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conduct an advance mission to the destination.</li>\n<li>Contact local security or police and the embassy or consulate of the executive's country.</li>\n<li>Conduct a risk assessment of the destination.</li>\n<li>Book strategically (safest lodging and transportation) and discreetly (without announcing the trip to potential adversaries).</li>\n<li>Manage travel documents: visas, passports, and itineraries.</li>\n<li>Rehearse, at least mentally, security on every possible means: commercial and private aircraft, cars, boats, ships, and trains.</li>\n<li>Review personal safety tips with the executive.</li>\n<li>Review health: bring health supplies and information; prepare or update the medical emergency plan; have hospitals, trauma centers, medical transport, and suitable doctors located.</li>\n<li>Research local travel and vaccination rules, especially if there is a pandemic or other health emergency.</li>\n<li>Assess network and cybersecurity vulnerabilities, and whether clean devices (laptops, tablets, smartphones) are needed.</li>\n<li>Download travel security apps from the organization's provider and/or from reliable sources, such as the U.S. Department of State or the Red Cross.</li>\n<li>Practice good cyber hygiene: avoid public Wi-Fi and consider a virtual private network (VPN) to access work systems.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>During the trip, three-part security concept:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>keep a low profile;</li>\n<li>stay away from problem areas and situations;</li>\n<li>know what to do if a problem arises.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In addition, the specialist must communicate often with the security base.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk regions</strong> (designated by the U.S. Department of State or another authority). Places where Westerners gather must be avoided. Terrorists look for sites with a secure concentration of Americans or Westerners at a specific time.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>E.g.: religious services at churches frequented by Westerners. An international church is full every Sunday morning.</li>\n<li>The same goes for nightclubs and other places for Americans and Western Europeans.</li>\n<li>If the Department of State advises avoiding them, it is best to heed it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key terms of a security advance:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Advance team</td>\n<td>Person or group that goes ahead of the principal and the protection team. Plans and verifies the advance plan according to the vulnerability and risk assessments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vulnerability assessments</td>\n<td>Also called intelligence reports or risk, threat, and vulnerability assessments (RTVA). Information on the risks and vulnerabilities the plan must mitigate. E.g.: without modern medicine on site, agents bring medical support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Site surveys</td>\n<td>Complete site information: room layout, the principal's path, and emergency services (generators, emergency exits, response times)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remain overnight/hotel security plan</td>\n<td>Extra protocols if he or she stays overnight: overnight security plan; general assessment of the lodging; emergency evacuation or relocation plans; communication plan between principal and overnight team</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Motorcade routes</td>\n<td>Primary and alternate routes according to local traffic. They are rehearsed one or two days before, at the same time of the transfer. Includes a manifest of who is going or may go and the general seating assignment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrival/departure areas</td>\n<td>Primary and alternate points at each venue. Contingency plans say what to do if the primary is not available and when to switch to the alternate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Path of travel</td>\n<td>Walking route at each venue. E.g.: exit the vehicle, hallway to the hold room, left side, stage, exit left, press room, hallway back, motorcade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access control list</td>\n<td>People authorized to enter the site, the backstage area, or another area where the principal will be</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run of show</td>\n<td>Schedule or itinerary of any event the principal attends or participates in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trip itinerary</td>\n<td>Complete itinerary: flight times, manifests, arrival and departure times for events, guests, or people in the motorcades</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in this box the book equates “vulnerability assessments” with risk, threat, and vulnerability assessments (RTVA). In 5.3.2 it treats them as three distinct assessments.</p>\n<p>Many large organizations use a standard format or checklist, with rules for when and how to present it. The formal advance plan becomes the playbook for the event, appearance, or trip. That way, large teams at several events and locations follow the same rules. The principal receives the same level of service everywhere, which gives consistency and confidence.</p>\n<p>Site advances are essential on prominent trips or events, especially if the principal is a keynote speaker or the event is highly publicized. They are also useful on personal trips, such as a family vacation. At lower-risk private events they can be greatly reduced, but their basic principles are kept:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>know where the clients will be;</li>\n<li>how to communicate with them;</li>\n<li>what emergency capabilities there are at each location;</li>\n<li>how to evacuate and/or activate emergency protocols.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Close Protection Teams</h4>\n<p>They are the final layer of physical protection for the principal. They are the ones who walk in front of and around prominent figures in the news. In the U.S., the typical example is Secret Service agents next to the president or vice president, in suits, sunglasses, and earpieces.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Several agents are not always needed. In corporate or non-government settings a low-profile team that goes unnoticed is preferred.</li>\n<li>The layers of security must be anticipated in advance planning.</li>\n<li>Facing a prominent event, the team leader: (1) reviews the vulnerability and risk assessments of the event, the location, and the principal; (2) reviews the advance plan; (3) decides how many agents are needed that day or event.</li>\n<li>That decision combines the assessments with the organization's close protection standards.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key terms of close protection:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Team leader</td>\n<td>Ultimately responsible for protection on a day or event; directs the agents' movements. A large team can have several, who take turns. Directs the choreography: moving closer, giving space, getting ahead to the next event</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detail leader</td>\n<td>Responsible for the entire detail, including rules and procedures. Team leaders usually report to him or her; in small teams one person can fill both roles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formations</td>\n<td>They depend on the venue, the size of the event, and other factors that set the distance to the principal (see next table)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Loose” protection</td>\n<td>Discreet style for small private events or vacations where the principal does not want a visible team. Agents set apart, plenty of space, or nontraditional formations. The principal is clearly informed of the risk assumed and it is agreed when to return to closer protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective surveillance</td>\n<td>Also called covert protection; it has gained ground in recent years. It is an additional layer or a substitute for close protection, and distinct from it, even from “loose.” Teams held back or on the outer perimeter that warn of out-of-the-ordinary activity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security driver vs. protection agent</td>\n<td>Many agents are cross-trained, but one is designated as driver that day or assignment. He or she stays at the wheel while the principal is at the event, for a quick evacuation. If he or she does double duty inside the event, the team will not be ready for exits or evacuations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Formations:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Formation</th>\n<th>Agents</th>\n<th>Description and use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Box formation</td>\n<td>At least four</td>\n<td>Traditional box around the principal. For moving through large crowds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diamond formation</td>\n<td>Generally four</td>\n<td>Variant of the box, with agents in front of and behind the principal. For large crowds, narrow spaces, and when an agent is needed at the “point” in front</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V formation</td>\n<td>Small team</td>\n<td>One agent in front of the principal and one or two at the sides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple formations</td>\n<td>Variable</td>\n<td>They are combined according to the environment. The team or detail leader decides in advance or on the fly. Each agent must know the formations, how to take his or her position, and his or her function in each</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Keys for a close protection team to work well:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>clear, constant communication;</li>\n<li>a standardized level of training;</li>\n<li>train together as much as possible;</li>\n<li>clear, consistent security plans, reviewed before each day, event, or trip.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Mental preparation and resources:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Specialist and executive must prepare mentally to anticipate threats.</li>\n<li>The program's strengths and resources must be cataloged so they can be used when needed.</li>\n<li>The program's vulnerabilities must be identified to better predict the adversary's likely approach.</li>\n<li>Strengths are combined. E.g.: if a brave, intelligent, strong agent does not master defensive driving, a professional driver is hired or he or she is trained.</li>\n<li>Successful EP is above all an intellectual labor. That is why anyone who is intelligent, trained, and in good physical shape can protect anyone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Practicing reactions.</strong> A threat or attack breaks out suddenly, with no time for a considered reaction. That is why one must practice constantly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>physically: protective movements, quick escapes, driving, or shooting;</li>\n<li>mentally: always ask “what if?” and think through the reaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>That way the agent reacts immediately, because the reasoning is already done. Assaults and assassination attempts begin and end with enormous speed. Being mentally prepared is worth more than any other precaution.</p>\n<p><strong>“Working the principal”:</strong> the choreography with which the specialist moves physically alongside the principal.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>EP specialists are not fighters, bodyguards, or soldiers. Their job is not to take down, arrest, or kill the aggressors.</li>\n<li>Their main job is to avoid dangerous people or conditions: fire, street crime, or public embarrassment.</li>\n<li>Faced with a possible assassin: (1) move the principal away from the danger; (2) cover him or her with the body (shield); (3) get him or her out of the area as soon as possible.</li>\n<li>Do not stay to fight unless there is no alternative.</li>\n<li>To blend in, the specialist can use a title such as “CEO's assistant” and stay to one side doing countersurveillance.</li>\n<li>Rule of thumb: be close enough to protect, but not so close that he or she has to be introduced.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Example from the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Getting the client out of trouble</td>\n<td>On seeing a disturbance that may turn violent, the agent puts the principal in the car and moves away quickly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keeping an executive out of trouble</td>\n<td>Agent and principal alert each other with a neutral phrase or a visual signal that it is time to leave</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Security–convenience continuum.</strong> Security and convenience are at opposite ends. Moving toward one end moves away from the other to the same degree. More security requires sacrificing convenience; more freedom implies less security.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neither extreme, total security or total convenience, is practical.</li>\n<li>Principal and agent decide where to position themselves on the continuum and what concessions to make.</li>\n<li>Past a certain point, more security hampers the executive and makes him or her less effective. He or she becomes a “victim of protection” instead of a victim of an attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says that at the maximum security end there is also “the greatest degree of risk.” That contradicts its own explanation: at that end the risk is minimal. Remember: maximum security = minimum convenience.</p>\n<p><strong>Protective equipment is not enough.</strong> Firearms, alarms, armored cars, cameras, and two-way radios are useful. But neither one nor all together guarantee protection, for two reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Relying too much on technology locks the principal in a “vault.” The executive needs to move; isolated, he or she is no longer an executive but a prisoner.</li>\n<li>Adversaries usually know how to defeat the equipment: bypass alarms, disable armored cars, or listen to the radios.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Equipment can buy defensive time. But when the adversary attacks, success depends on the agent's conditioned responses to get the principal out of danger.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Almost no shootout in the EP field has lasted more than a few seconds.</li>\n<li>In all presidential assassination attempts in the U.S., the Secret Service followed its “cover and evacuate” model. It did not choose to return fire.</li>\n<li>In a crisis, using your head matters more than weapons or equipment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> that the Secret Service never returned fire is inaccurate. In the attack on Truman at Blair House (1950), White House agents and police officers did fire. For the exam, keep the book's idea: “cover and evacuate.”</p>\n<h4>Protective Details in High-Threat Environments</h4>\n<p>Protective security details (PSD) are also called personal security detachments, personal security details, or personal protection details. They are teams that protect high-risk personnel, normally in high-threat environments.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The term has existed for decades. It originated with the protection of generals and dignitaries abroad.</li>\n<li>It spread in the early 21st century with private teams in conflict zones such as Iraq and Afghanistan.</li>\n<li>Those early deployments were barely regulated. There were public incidents, abuses, and other misconduct, later tried in foreign courts.</li>\n<li>Audits by the governments that hired PSDs gave rise to international guidelines.</li>\n<li>Among them, those cited in the ASIS/ANSI (American National Standards Institute) standard <em>Management System for Quality of Private Security Company Operations – Requirements with Guidance</em>, revised in 2017.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.3 Transportation (5.4.3)</h4>\n<p><strong>Flight operations.</strong> High-profile people usually fly on private aircraft. Advantages over commercial flights:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>manage their own flight itinerary;</li>\n<li>control the passenger manifest;</li>\n<li>express or expedited arrival and departure;</li>\n<li>greater privacy;</li>\n<li>ability to work effectively in transit.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The productive time gained alone usually justifies the investment. Some see it as frivolous. A good EP program defends it on grounds of more productivity and less risk, which translate into more profitability for shareholders.</p>\n<p>Ways to operate: own the aircraft, lease them, share ownership with other organizations, or charter them.</p>\n<p><strong>Fixed base operations (FBO).</strong> Executive flight operations facility where aircraft are housed between flights.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are usually operated by third parties. Some companies with large flight departments run their own home-base FBO.</li>\n<li>Third-party FBOs support moving aircraft to other FBOs away from the base, called “foreign.”</li>\n<li>A full-service FBO may offer: on-site security, tarmac support, mechanics, repair, catering, and cleaning.</li>\n<li>Many are inside larger hubs, such as municipal airports. Others are independent of government facilities.</li>\n<li>Independent ones may have less scrutiny than a public airport on perimeter and access control.</li>\n<li>They make it easy to go from car to plane, sometimes by vehicle to the aircraft. They also speed up customs and border control.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Driver services, two forms:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Origin</th>\n<th>Focus</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chauffeur</td>\n<td>In-house drivers or outside livery companies</td>\n<td>Customer service and efficient, safe transfer. More attention to professional image and pride in the work than to security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security driver</td>\n<td>The book does not specify</td>\n<td>Specific training in defensive driving and in protective operations, to integrate into the program</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Perception of “unnecessary luxury.”</strong> It worsened in the 2008 recession. Several executives even testified before the U.S. Congress after traveling by private plane and private transport. That view ignores the benefits of the driver:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>better security and better time management;</li>\n<li>the principal works in transit and arrives on time, without hunting for the address or parking;</li>\n<li>less risk for the organization, by taking the high-net-worth person out of the driver's seat;</li>\n<li>less personal risk: the principal is dropped off at the most efficient point, such as the main entrance, instead of parking and walking.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ideally: a security driver and, in addition, an EP agent.</strong> If the agent also drives, he cannot watch the route. He may also have to drop off the principal and then park. That leaves the principal alone at arrivals and departures, the critical moments.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Denis Voronenkov case (March 2017):</strong> his bodyguard dropped the former Russian lawmaker off in front of the Premier Palace hotel and went to park. Meanwhile, an assassin shot him dead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The executive's vehicle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>roomy interior, for the executive, the specialist, and the necessary equipment;</li>\n<li>large protective mass, for ramming;</li>\n<li>powerful engine, to escape the attackers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The risk assessment decides whether armoring is needed. Almost any car can be armored after it is manufactured. Some makes offer factory-armored vehicles. Their advantage: they look like other vehicles and do not draw attention to the principal.</p>\n<p><strong>Features of an armored vehicle:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>metal panels and bullet-resistant glass;</li>\n<li>run-flat tires;</li>\n<li>explosion-proof fuel tank;</li>\n<li>front bumper reinforced with steel for ramming;</li>\n<li>electric dead bolt locks;</li>\n<li>dual battery system;</li>\n<li>interior/exterior intercom;</li>\n<li>remote start.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Other features of an EP car, armored or not:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>trunk release from the inside, useful for escaping (already standard in many new cars);</li>\n<li>global positioning system (GPS), to avoid getting lost;</li>\n<li>locking gas cap;</li>\n<li>mobile phone;</li>\n<li>protected exhaust pipe;</li>\n<li>electronic assistance system, such as On-Star;</li>\n<li>alarm.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key practices during transport:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Call the central security office at the start of the trip, with the route and time. E.g.: \"We are leaving the plant for the office; it is 3:15 p.m.; we are taking I-67 toward U.S. 20.\"</li>\n<li>That call lets security or the police reconstruct the journey if the car disappears.</li>\n<li>Use discreet or coded language to say who is traveling and where, in case someone is listening.</li>\n<li>Thoroughly inspect the car whenever it has been out of sight and unattended.</li>\n<li>Because inspection takes time, keep it in a locked, alarmed garage whenever possible.</li>\n<li>An inspected car stays \"sterile\" only if it is kept locked or watched.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The route (based on the advance):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>fast, avoiding dangerous areas, with the minimum number of stops;</li>\n<li>several known alternate routes;</li>\n<li>safe havens for stops along the way;</li>\n<li>locations of hospitals, police stations, and other vital resources along the route;</li>\n<li>times to each leg, likely traffic, road conditions, construction or detours, drawbridge openings, and other temporary conditions;</li>\n<li>do the advance at the same time of day as the trip, to see actual traffic;</li>\n<li>additional precaution: drive the advance route in a different vehicle from the one that will carry the principal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in this section the book writes \"<em>EPS specialist</em>\" without defining the acronym. It refers to the same executive protection specialist (<em>EP specialist</em>) as in the rest of the chapter.</p>\n<h4>4.4 Special Equipment and Technical EP (5.4.4)</h4>\n<p>Close protection teams can rely on highly specialized equipment and resources. The professional must know when to use them, how to vet providers, and how to integrate them into operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Digital executive protection.</strong> Just as there are layers of physical protection, the principal needs a team that looks after his digital security.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital risk causes stress, financial loss, loss of private information, and reputational damage. It can also create physical risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Doxing:</strong> posting personal information online with malicious intent. It can expose the principal to physical danger.</li>\n<li>Principals and protection leaders must hire experts. They must also have contingency plans if digital security is compromised, just like evacuation plans for a site.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Possible measures:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>ongoing training of the principal, staff, and family in online security and privacy, including how to avoid <em>phishing</em> and <em>spear phishing</em> (targeted impersonation);</li>\n<li>ongoing audits of home and company networks;</li>\n<li>ongoing audits and reviews with family and staff on good social media practices: not revealing too much and keeping privacy high;</li>\n<li>a protocol for taking devices to places with high cyber risk. E.g.: using a separate device, with no intellectual property, when traveling to those places.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Technical security countermeasures (TSCM).</strong> The practice of \"sweeping\" places for listening devices. It derives from government practices to protect classified information.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>More and more companies and individuals offer it, alone or as part of a service package.</li>\n<li>It can take several hours and be highly technical.</li>\n<li>The close protection leader decides whether it is needed together with other stakeholders.</li>\n<li>Good practices go beyond the technical: training principals, staff, and family in protecting private information.</li>\n<li>E.g.: not discussing sensitive topics in hotel lobbies, restaurants, or other public spaces where it is not known who is listening.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When TSCM may be required:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>monitoring corporate spaces where sensitive intellectual property or other matters harmful to the company or the principal are recorded, overheard, or leaked;</li>\n<li>monitoring private spaces to ensure the privacy of the principal and family;</li>\n<li>ensuring that certain objects, such as gifts from strangers, do not carry audio or video devices;</li>\n<li>ensuring the privacy of hotel rooms and event spaces where the principal is known to be;</li>\n<li>ensuring the privacy of vehicles and/or aircraft where the principal handles sensitive information.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book develops TSCM in two ways. In 5.3.1 it writes <em>technical surveillance countermeasures</em>; in 5.2.1 and 5.4.4, <em>technical security countermeasures</em>. The standard form of the acronym is <em>technical surveillance countermeasures</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>K-9 sweeps.</strong> In close protection, dogs are often used to detect explosives, as a layer of physical protection. When to consider them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>at times of high risk;</li>\n<li>before events with large crowds, such as major sporting events or concerts;</li>\n<li>at high-risk locations;</li>\n<li>as an ongoing measure at places recognized as high-value targets.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Other services.</strong> Covert surveillance, specialized driving techniques, or even training in a sport the principal practices. Before hiring them, it is advisable to have the agreement of stakeholders in the organization: legal, finance, and vendor management.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-5-e4\">Key Terms</h3><p>executive protection (EP) · close protection · principal · out of harm's way · advance planning · hard skills · soft skills · collateral benefits · security drivers · time management · travel logistics · normalcy · return on investment (ROI) · risk portfolio · board mandate · full-time employees / outsourced / hybrid model · key performance indicators (KPIs) · bona fide 24/7 security program · executive perks · bodyguards · persons of concern · stalkers · U.S. Secret Service · Diplomatic Security Service · national special security events (NSSEs) · SO1 · dignitary protection details · 18 U.S.C. Section 3056 · advance report · faith-based executive protection · hybrid executive protection models · third-party operations · protective intelligence · technical surveillance countermeasures (TSCM) · enterprise security risk management (ESRM) · threat assessment · behavior-based threat assessment · soft targets · smishing · SIM swapping · vulnerability assessment · fixed base operations (FBOs) · shelter-in-place · risk assessment · attractiveness of the target · C-suite · home drive-bys · readiness · service-minded · knowledge, skills, and abilities (KSAs) · ASIS Executive Protection Council · duty of care · force continuum · advance work · Federal Law Enforcement Training Center (FLETC) · James J. Rowley Training Center · protective service operations (PSO) · route surveys · vehicle ambush recognition · emergency medical technician (EMT) · tabletop exercises · de-escalation · residential security · crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED) · back-up and rollover · shadowing · roving posts · face page · force multiplier · security advances · hold rooms · know before you go · clean devices · virtual private network (VPN) · advance team · risk, threat, and vulnerability assessments (RTVAs) · site surveys · remain overnight/hotel security plan · motorcade routes · arrival/departure areas · path of travel · access control list · run of show · trip itinerary · playbook · close protection teams · team leader · detail leader · box formation · diamond formation · V formation · point · \"loose\" protection · protective surveillance · covert protection · security driver vs. protection agent · working the principal · what if? · security–convenience continuum · cover and evacuate · protective security details (PSDs) · personal security detachments · Management System for Quality of Private Security Company Operations · flight operations · chartering · foreign FBO · chauffeur · livery · security driver · ramming · factory-armored vehicles · run-flat tires · sterile vehicle · safe havens · digital executive protection · doxing · spear phishing · technical security countermeasures · sweeping · K-9 sweeps</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Purpose and Collateral Value of Executive Protection"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Domains of Protection"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. Managing the Executive Protection Program"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Executive Protection Operations"},{"id":"e4","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"4. Executive Protection Operations","h":"in this box the book equates “vulnerability assessments” with risk, threat, and vulnerability assessments (RTVA). In 5.3.2 it treats them as three distinct assessments."},{"s":"4. Executive Protection Operations","h":"the book says that at the maximum security end there is also “the greatest degree of risk.” That contradicts its own explanation: at that end the risk is minimal. Remember: maximum security = minimum convenience."},{"s":"4. Executive Protection Operations","h":"that the Secret Service never returned fire is inaccurate. In the attack on Truman at Blair House (1950), White House agents and police officers did fire. For the exam, keep the book's idea: “cover and evacuate.”"},{"s":"4. Executive Protection Operations","h":"in this section the book writes \"<em>EPS specialist</em>\" without defining the acronym. It refers to the same executive protection specialist (<em>EP specialist</em>) as in the rest of the chapter."},{"s":"4. Executive Protection Operations","h":"the book develops TSCM in two ways. In 5.3.1 it writes <em>technical surveillance countermeasures</em>; in 5.2.1 and 5.4.4, <em>technical security countermeasures</em>. The standard form of the acronym is <em>technical surveillance countermeasures</em>."}]},"personnel-6":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-6-e0\">1. Definition and Basic Concepts (introduction)</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> awareness that a security program exists, of its relevance, and of the effect of one's own conduct on reducing risks.</li>\n<li>It is a <strong>continuing attitude</strong> that can move people to act in support of the organization's security.</li>\n<li>Two factors increase its success: linking the security strategy with the organization's strategy (showing how it supports the mission) and involving asset owners.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Education</td>\n<td>Conveys general knowledge.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training</td>\n<td>Develops specific skills.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Awareness</td>\n<td>Seeks conscious attention.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Aware employees and nonemployees act as a <strong>force multiplier</strong> for the program.</li>\n<li>A <strong>security culture</strong> makes each person actively protect their own safety and the organization's assets (ASIS International 2020).</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-6-e1\">2. Levels of Awareness (6.1)</h3><ul>\n<li>Each category of employees and visitors needs a different level.</li>\n<li>Under enterprise security risk management (ESRM), the levels are decided by the <strong>stakeholders</strong>, with guidance from security professionals.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>How it views security</th>\n<th>What to focus awareness on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Executive management (6.1.1)</td>\n<td>CEOs, chief operating officers, and other senior officers decide on risk and resources. If they see the program as a cost with no return, they may cut or eliminate it.</td>\n<td>Whether the program is worth it: (1) its benefits and (2) how reasonable its costs are against those benefits. For them, awareness = knowing the program's <strong>financial contribution</strong> to the bottom line.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Middle management (6.1.2)</td>\n<td>They answer for their department's success and value security for its contribution to that goal. If they believe it does not support their unit's goals, they do not support it; the result is mismatches and tensions that cause failures elsewhere in the company.</td>\n<td>Show that it supports their goals. They must demonstrate commitment and set an example, just like executive management.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First-line supervision (6.1.3)</td>\n<td>They handle specific processes or activities: they see whether security helps or hinders their objectives. They receive most security complaints first; if there are many, they feel it consumes too much of their time.</td>\n<td>Show that the time spent on compliance is worth it: it supports their core tasks and protects employees and the business. Remind them that they are the closest link to employees.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Individual employees (6.1.4)</td>\n<td>Crucial to facility security every day.</td>\n<td>Detect intruders who piggyback on their credentials or who use social engineering. In information technology (IT) they can leave the network vulnerable. Security is everyone's responsibility.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nonemployees (6.1.5)</td>\n<td>Vendors, suppliers, customers, service personnel, government representatives, and the public. They have less opportunity to learn the requirements.</td>\n<td>At least some requirements. They comply more if the reason why is explained, even briefly or in advance.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Executive management (6.1.1), additional points</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Their support and priority are crucial to the program's success.</li>\n<li>They must reinforce a culture in which everyone understands their security role and takes ownership of their own security. They must set an example of compliance and commitment.</li>\n<li>Without that commitment, a <strong>culture of complacency</strong> can arise, with unethical conduct and violation of security policies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Middle management (6.1.2): the laboratory case</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The head of a sensitive research laboratory believes the requirements are unnecessary and allows the free exchange of information.</li>\n<li>Over time, the internal spread of sensitive data can end in unauthorized disclosure and loss of competitive advantage.</li>\n<li>Lesson: that possibility alone should lead him to support security, as long as the requirements do not harm the research.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>First-line supervision (6.1.3): cases and legal liability</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Case of the head teller at a retail bank: may refuse to use bait money for silent holdup alarms at the teller windows, because it lengthens the end-of-day count.</li>\n<li>If an employee complains to the supervisor about an unsafe condition, conduct, or practice (for example, someone threatens violence or the parking lot is very dark at night), the situation becomes <strong>foreseeable</strong> and the company is <strong>on notice</strong>, even if the supervisor does not escalate it.</li>\n<li>The company must have training and procedures so that the supervisor recognizes the complaint and mitigates or escalates it.</li>\n<li>If an incident occurs, these questions will be asked:<ul>\n<li>What was done to mitigate the problem?</li>\n<li>Did the supervisor address the reported vulnerability? If not, why not?</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>If an injury or death was foreseeable and preventable, and the employer did not act despite being on notice, the company is exposed to a lawsuit.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Individual employees (6.1.4), additional points</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Most modern approaches to motivation assume the employee is willing and interested: he needs information and instruction, not coercion or pressure.</li>\n<li>In many companies, the only formal contact with the program is a brief mention on the first day. That conveys that security does not matter.</li>\n<li>If supervisors and managers disapprove of the program or show no interest, employees do not support it. If they support it, employees view it favorably and comply.</li>\n<li>Strategy: link the program with the employee's personal priorities and goals. Example: good cyber hygiene at work teaches them to protect their personal information at home and elsewhere.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Nonemployees (6.1.5), additional points</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Example: a vendor who will access sensitive proprietary information must know the procedures that protect and control it.</li>\n<li>Sometimes awareness is formalized with a <strong>confidentiality or nondisclosure agreement</strong>.</li>\n<li>Example: a visitor may not see the point of wearing the guest badge. He changes his mind if it is explained that the badge lets employees recognize him and help him right away.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-6-e2\">3. Purposes of Awareness (6.2)</h3><p>A person who receives awareness is better prepared for these twelve purposes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Protect the company's assets:</strong> this is the <strong>main purpose</strong>; it teaches people to help protect assets and reduce losses. Everything else derives from it. It involves everyone in protecting people, property, and other assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Understand the relationship between security and successful operations:</strong> professionals must devote time and talent to demonstrating the program's value and profitability. A crucial point for <strong>executive management</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Identify their obligations within the program:</strong> for employees and nonemployees, presented as reasonable and necessary. They are taught through initial orientation and periodic refreshers.</li>\n<li><strong>Recognize the connection between the program's objectives and the chosen measures:</strong> important for <strong>middle management</strong>. Unit heads must take part in the measures and recognize (preferably accept) that they are appropriate and necessary.</li>\n<li><strong>Know the sources of help:</strong> the materials must cover how to apply the requirements. Example: if a rule requires locking certain spaces or containers, the employee must know how to get a padlock and key or access to the lock. If he does not know whom to turn to, he improvises or consults the wrong person and is delayed.</li>\n<li><strong>Meet legal notice requirements</strong> (by statute or <em>common law</em>), for employees and nonemployees:\n   - Civil trespass to land: in general, unauthorized entry or presence on real property. To recover damages, the owner or responsible party must prove that the intruder intended to trespass. Physical, verbal, and symbolic indicators must make clear the boundary that may not be crossed.\n   - Trade secret: the owner must take positive action to prevent its disclosure. One such action is telling the employees who know it that the information is secret and valuable.\n   - Creating programs to give those notices and documenting them is part of awareness.</li>\n<li><strong>Meet regulatory requirements:</strong> in the U.S., employee orientation is required by the <strong>Bank Protection Act of 1968</strong>, Federal Reserve System regulations, and the requirements of the “Controller of the Currency.” Other agencies that require training and awareness: Drug Enforcement Administration, Department of Transportation, and Nuclear Regulatory Commission.</li>\n<li><strong>Meet contractual obligations:</strong>\n   - U.S.: the <strong>National Industrial Security Program Operating Manual</strong> (contractor obligations regarding classified defense information) requires briefings, education and training, including awareness.\n   - Collective bargaining agreements: often require that dismissal be for just cause and that employees be notified of the rules, including security rules.\n   - Insurance (for example, kidnapping): requires adopting specific procedures and communicating them to designated officials.\n   - Contract for use of another company's proprietary information: may require awareness training for the employees who use it.</li>\n<li><strong>Comply with company policies and procedures:</strong> for example, access control or “clean desk” and “locked workspace” initiatives to protect proprietary information.</li>\n<li><strong>Prepare the organization for emergencies and business continuity:</strong> it responds better to emergencies and nonroutine matters, such as reports of suspicious persons or fire alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduce the organization's legal liability:</strong><ul>\n<li>In a lawsuit it helps to show that the company is security-conscious and strives for a safe environment (Ahrens 2006).</li>\n<li>Effective programs hold employees accountable for their actions (Whitman 2004).</li>\n<li>A criminal charge against an employee is more likely to succeed if the company proves an effectively communicated program (Kovacich 2017).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Communicate the value of the security department:</strong> without trying to scare management or employees; showing the value of security to the mission.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the correct title is <strong>Comptroller</strong> of the Currency (Office of the Comptroller of the Currency), not “Controller.”</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in U.S. tort law, intent to enter is enough for trespass; knowing that one is trespassing is not required. The idea the book wants to convey is different: the boundary must be clearly marked (notice).</p><h3 id=\"personnel-6-e3\">4. Developing and Delivering an Awareness Program (6.3)</h3><p>The program is designed according to the organization's needs. Departments and levels may need different content, but <strong>all</strong> employees must understand:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How security supports the organization's mission.</li>\n<li>Why the organization needs protection strategies.</li>\n<li>What value those strategies provide.</li>\n<li>The organization's code of conduct.</li>\n<li>What actions the protection of specific assets requires.</li>\n<li>How to identify indicators of risk or danger, how to react, and whom to notify.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Possible specific topics (ASIS 2020; the book says the list is not exhaustive):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Client or customer engagement requirements.</li>\n<li>Confidential reporting.</li>\n<li>Personnel security policies and procedures.</li>\n<li>Bullying and harassment.</li>\n<li>Workplace violence prevention.</li>\n<li>Travel security procedures.</li>\n<li>Personnel management.</li>\n<li>Physical security policies and procedures.</li>\n<li>Access control, visitor management, and other physical protection systems.</li>\n<li>Reporting of suspicious behavior or conditions.</li>\n<li>Emergency reporting or response.</li>\n<li>Security incident reporting and response management.</li>\n<li>Information security policies and procedures.</li>\n<li>Credential and password management.</li>\n<li>Records retention and data management.</li>\n<li>Social engineering, scams, <em>phishing</em>, and other online threats.</li>\n<li>Mobile device security techniques.</li>\n<li>Safe browsing.</li>\n<li>Incident management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Difference from detailed training:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Awareness material may not include task-specific information. It can refer to content available elsewhere and focuses on building support for the program.</li>\n<li>It must be engaging and interesting: the best tools involve staff and entertain them (Gips 2006).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.1 Techniques, Materials, and Resources (6.3.1)</h4>\n<p>Five common techniques and materials:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Content and examples</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Written material</td>\n<td>Instructional or advisory material, agreements and acknowledgments of receipt; written policies and procedures, posters, and informal reminders. Distributed as manuals, brochures, and department guides, or within other departments' materials. Formats: pocket cards, desk reference material, calendars, fact sheets, notepads, bookmarks, letter openers, mugs, pencils, rulers, key chains, newsletters, posters, refrigerator magnets, and stickers.</td>\n<td>Can be integrated into the materials of professional organizations. Morris case (2002): a department added its material to the <em>Annual Patient Nursing Assessment for Private Duty Patients</em>, which deals with violence prevention.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Audiovisual material</td>\n<td>Audio and video files, interactive CD-ROMs, films, software presentations, e-mail, and one's own and others' websites.</td>\n<td>Do not post sensitive information where it is publicly accessible.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formal security briefings</td>\n<td>Before and after hiring, at orientation to a new assignment, and at promotions or transfers.</td>\n<td>Individual or group.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integration into line operations</td>\n<td>Review awareness in performance evaluations, consider it when setting bonuses, and reinforce it at supervisor and manager meetings. Include security tasks in job descriptions, manuals, and employee standards.</td>\n<td>It is advisable to collaborate with other departments.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drills and exercises</td>\n<td>Life safety drills, simulations, role-playing, and exercises: they give the employee confidence.</td>\n<td>In cybersecurity, <em>phishing</em> and other attacks are simulated to see whether employees are vulnerable.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Guides and checklists can cover 14 topics: organization assets, personal security, travel security, protection of information assets, terrorist threat, protection of classified material, counterintelligence, cybercrime, personnel security, foreign intelligence threats, operational security, responsible use of company equipment, access control procedures, and possible penalties for violating security rules.</p>\n<p>Support for creating the program:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Internal: the company's training and communications staff.</li>\n<li>External experts in communications, advertising, and public relations: they provide knowledge, experience, and credibility.</li>\n<li>External partners as guest speakers: police (incident response), security system vendors (system operation), and behavior experts (workplace violence prevention).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Delivery methods:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Variety is needed: some people learn well on their own with computer-based instruction; others, in class.</li>\n<li>Review each method for suitability, relevance, and compliance with legal and contractual obligations.</li>\n<li>COVID-19 case (2020): with much of the workforce at home, programs moved to online platforms. Topics changed too: business travel required less support and home-office cybersecurity became vital. Lesson: be flexible and adapt the program.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2 Obstacles to an Effective Program (6.3.2)</h4>\n<p>Employee and management support is not automatic. Six obstacles (Roper 2006):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Obstacle</th>\n<th>Causes or manifestations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Low credibility of the security department</td>\n<td>Staff's prior performance; new department with no track record; prejudices brought from other organizations; lack of professionalism; security staff who do not understand the company's functions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organizational culture</td>\n<td>Negative “we've never done it that way” culture or mindset. Understanding how security contributes to success is key to a positive culture; if executive management does not understand it, implementing the program will be difficult.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naiveté</td>\n<td>Believing nothing bad will happen to them, especially if they were never victims. Believing employees will always protect assets and never knowingly harm the organization. Mistaken conclusion: the program is unnecessary.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perception of a minimal threat</td>\n<td>The employee believes the threat is insignificant or unlikely; that their assets are not a credible target and nobody would want to steal them.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Departmental or employee indifference</td>\n<td>They do not see security as their responsibility: they are overloaded, have another agenda (for example, they steal from the company), or see it as unwanted extra work. Some believe protecting assets is only the security department's job.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lack of reporting capability</td>\n<td>An effective reporting system is essential. Its mere existence increases awareness and the number of reports (Kellogg 2006). With it, security can measure its effectiveness and report to executive management.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>4.3 Measuring the Program (6.3.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Awareness costs time and money and can briefly interrupt work. For that reason it may require management approval to start and continue.</li>\n<li>The organization must set metrics to measure the program and document its value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Eight possible metrics:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Company losses before and after implementing the program.</li>\n<li>Number of people briefed and sessions held in specific periods.</li>\n<li>Topics covered, planned or actual completion dates, and delivery methods.</li>\n<li>Cost of the session per employee.</li>\n<li>Analysis of opinion surveys after the activities or individual study.</li>\n<li>Suggestions for improvement from attendees.</li>\n<li>Analysis of the response to emergencies and incidents.</li>\n<li>Evaluation of regulatory and contractual compliance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>New program with no effectiveness data:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Start with a limited budget and gain momentum over time.</li>\n<li>Create inexpensive material with desktop publishing.</li>\n<li>Partner with other departments, such as human resources, to inform employees during their regular training.</li>\n<li>Gather and evaluate data until the time comes to expand the program. Chapter 2 covers low-budget training options.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-6-e4\">5. Involving Employees in Loss Prevention (6.4)</h3><ul>\n<li>Employees are among the most important stakeholders. Everyone must help protect assets, because security cannot be everywhere or see everything.</li>\n<li>Working with other departments and leading protection strategies increases cooperation. An involved employee is less likely to circumvent rules and measures.</li>\n<li>Knowing that a program exists is <strong>not the same</strong> as having an active role in loss prevention. Every department and employee helps identify, prevent, and reduce losses.</li>\n<li>Before designing the program, the security manager must know all elements of the business: which assets and processes to protect and from what risks.</li>\n<li>Losses that employees help reduce:<ul>\n<li>Traditional physical: theft of money or merchandise, misuse of equipment or facilities.</li>\n<li>Others: contractual, legal, regulatory, insurance, and similar.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Small losses can be costly. Example: stealing a laptop with a customer's personal data can cost a great deal, especially under the new privacy laws.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1 Positive Security Contacts (6.4.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Success depends on employee cooperation. To strengthen it, the department must maximize positive (helpful) contacts without neglecting its main mission.</li>\n<li>One avenue: promoting the personal safety of employees and their families at work, at home, and elsewhere. Eight examples:<ol>\n<li>Home protection workshops.</li>\n<li>Lending of property-marking devices.</li>\n<li>Group purchases of anti-theft and fire devices.</li>\n<li>Personal protection programs.</li>\n<li>Cybersecurity awareness programs.</li>\n<li>Children's fire prevention poster contests, with prizes.</li>\n<li>Child identification kits for employees.</li>\n<li>Hazard preparedness programs, with kits handed out.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Employees themselves can suggest other programs.</li>\n<li>Communication must be <strong>two-way</strong>: management guides and employees ask questions.</li>\n<li>To encourage it, security education must be light, nonpunitive, and appealing. That tone also mitigates <strong>security fatigue</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2 Understanding Policies and Procedures (6.4.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>One of the most important missions of awareness is to make policies and procedures known.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy</td>\n<td>Sets the rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Procedure</td>\n<td>Explains how to comply with those rules.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Awareness helps spread them and helps the employee know what to do and what not to do in many situations. Not knowing them or not following them has serious and costly consequences.</li>\n<li><strong>Supermarket case (CBC News 2004):</strong> two clerks and a uniformed guard detained an alleged thief. He died of accidental restraint asphyxia. The employees did not know the store's policy prohibiting the use of force on thieves. Lesson: a good policy is not enough; training, refreshers, and exercises are needed. Store managers, loss prevention professionals, security managers, and human resources must monitor incidents and retrain anyone who does not follow policies.</li>\n<li>Some fail to comply because they do not understand what they must do; others, because they decide not to cooperate.</li>\n<li>Two categories of users who violate IT policies (Mallery 2007):<ol>\n<li><strong>Uneducated users:</strong> they understand little about computers and the consequences of ignoring policies.</li>\n<li><strong>Arrogant users:</strong> they believe other people's rules do not apply to them; they feel more powerful, intelligent, and sophisticated than everyone else.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Anyone who refuses to comply, even after awareness, <strong>must be disciplined</strong>. Otherwise the company is needlessly exposed to losses and liabilities.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the text cites “Mallery 2007,” but the chapter's reference list gives Mallery 2008. Also, Kellogg 2006, Gips 2006, and Morris 2002 are cited in the text but do not appear in that list.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-6-e5\">Key Terms</h3><p>security awareness · continuing attitude · force multiplier · security culture · asset owners · enterprise security risk management (ESRM) · stakeholders · executive management · bottom line · culture of complacency · middle management · first-line supervision · bait money · silent hold-up alarm · foreseeable and preventable · individual employees · piggyback · social engineering · cyber hygiene · nonemployees · confidentiality or nondisclosure agreement · guest badge · civil trespass to land · trade secret · Bank Protection Act of 1968 · National Industrial Security Program Operating Manual · just cause · clean desk · locked workspace · business continuity · liability reduction · code of conduct · written material · audiovisual material · formal security briefings · integration into line operations · drills and exercises · phishing simulation · low credibility of security department · organizational culture · naiveté · perception of a minimal threat · departmental or employee indifference · lack of reporting capability · metrics · cost of briefing per employee · desktop publishing · positive security contacts · security fatigue · policies vs. procedures · restraint asphyxia · uneducated users · arrogant users</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Definition and Basic Concepts"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Levels of Awareness"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. Purposes of Awareness"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Developing and Delivering an Awareness Program"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. Involving Employees in Loss Prevention"},{"id":"e5","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"3. Purposes of Awareness","h":"the correct title is <strong>Comptroller</strong> of the Currency (Office of the Comptroller of the Currency), not “Controller.”"},{"s":"3. Purposes of Awareness","h":"in U.S. tort law, intent to enter is enough for trespass; knowing that one is trespassing is not required. The idea the book wants to convey is different: the boundary must be clearly marked (notice)."},{"s":"5. Involving Employees in Loss Prevention","h":"the text cites “Mallery 2007,” but the chapter's reference list gives Mallery 2008. Also, Kellogg 2006, Gips 2006, and Morris 2002 are cited in the text but do not appear in that list."}]},"personnel-7":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-7-e0\">1. Basic Concepts and Cost to the Employer (introduction)</h3><ul>\n<li><strong>Drug:</strong> a chemical substance that alters the physical, behavioral, psychological, or emotional state of the person who uses it.</li>\n<li><strong>Drugs of abuse:</strong> psychoactive (mind-altering) substances that act on the central nervous system. They impair the ability to think and to process stimuli, and distort the perception of reality.</li>\n<li>They include legal and illegal substances, often used socially. In this chapter <strong>alcohol is considered a drug</strong>.</li>\n<li>Abuse harms physically, mentally, or emotionally. It usually leads to greater tolerance and then to addiction or chemical dependency. It creates personal, family, and financial problems.</li>\n<li>A country's prosperity depends on a healthy workforce, more than on technology, industry, or natural resources.</li>\n<li>Social consequences: birth defects, industrial accidents, company bankruptcies, and highway deaths.</li>\n<li>Worldwide drug trafficking is a multibillion-dollar, cross-border industry, which operates almost entirely in cash and enforces its rules with violence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generally accepted effects of employee substance abuse (nine):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>decreases productivity and morale;</li>\n<li>increases turnover and absenteeism;</li>\n<li>increases accidents;</li>\n<li>increases insurance costs;</li>\n<li>increases theft and dishonesty;</li>\n<li>increases unnecessary use of benefits;</li>\n<li>decreases profits;</li>\n<li>increases the company's potential legal liability;</li>\n<li>increases the risk of negative public exposure and image.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It also robs talent, vitality, and enthusiasm, destroys teamwork, and reduces competitiveness.</p>\n<p><strong>Security's role:</strong> in large organizations it is usually handled by human resources. Even so, managers and security staff must know the problem in order to detect signs and protect the organization.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e1\">2. Human Cost of Abuse (7.1)</h3><p>Compared with those who do not abuse, employees who abuse:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>miss more work;</li>\n<li>file more sick-benefit claims;</li>\n<li>file more workers' compensation claims;</li>\n<li>are more likely to be laid off or fired.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Effects on the family:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Nonalcoholic relatives of an alcoholic use more sick leave.</li>\n<li>They are more prone to prolonged illness, accidents, and divorce.</li>\n<li>Children of alcoholics are more likely to become alcoholics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dysfunctional relationships: they have trouble getting along with others, withdraw from their friends, and become secretive. They spend less time at home and at work, and avoid socializing with those who do not use.</p>\n<p>At work: they perform and create less, and are often disciplinary problems. They deny and blame others. They may be <strong>the 20% who consume 80% of management's time</strong>.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e2\">3. The Employer&#x27;s Role (7.2)</h3><p>Abuse, once seen as a police or government problem, is also a workplace problem. To create a drug-free workplace, leaders must do nine things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>make a commitment;</li>\n<li>set goals and objectives;</li>\n<li>assign responsibilities;</li>\n<li>formulate a comprehensive policy, with input from executive management and legal counsel;</li>\n<li>communicate the policy;</li>\n<li>apply it equitably;</li>\n<li>provide education and training;</li>\n<li>help anyone who asks, including psychological support;</li>\n<li>audit the process regularly.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e3\">4. Drug Use in the Workplace (7.3)</h3><p>Two reasons drugs are used at work:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Rationalization:</strong> they believe they can use without affecting their productivity, and even present it as something noble.</li>\n<li><strong>Opportunity:</strong> they spend several hours a day at work, where opportunities arise to use, share, and buy illegal substances.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>4.1 Rationalization (7.3.1)</h4>\n<p><strong>Definition:</strong> use of superficial and seemingly plausible explanations or excuses to justify one's own conduct.</p>\n<p>Typical rationalizations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>using is a constitutional right;</li>\n<li>addiction and dependency only happen to others;</li>\n<li>the drug improves their ability to perform, produce, and create;</li>\n<li>they can quit whenever they want;</li>\n<li>using at work is acceptable because it is common;</li>\n<li>selling to coworkers is a gesture of camaraderie;</li>\n<li>what they do in their free time does not affect their performance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Effect: they abandon their values, evade responsibilities, and lose respect for others and for their property. They may lie to family, steal from friends and employers, and use without guilt.</p>\n<h4>4.2 Opportunity (7.3.2)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reason</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Users know one another</td>\n<td>Seller and buyer have regular contact that does not arouse suspicion. The workplace is often private property, outside the direct watch of the police: a relatively safe haven.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Better quality</td>\n<td>The seller wants repeat customers, and quality brings them back.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fairer quantity</td>\n<td>Drugs are expensive and sold in minimal quantities, so exact weight matters. The seller gives the correct weight to keep customers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Low risk</td>\n<td>They see bosses as uninformed, untrained, and reluctant to confront. The barriers, fences, and locked doors that protect assets also hide them from surveillance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High return on investment</td>\n<td>An ounce of high-quality cocaine, cut (diluted with an impurity) and repackaged into small doses, leaves thousands of dollars in profit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Buy and sell on credit</td>\n<td>Drugs are “fronted” (<em>fronting</em>) and paid for later, with terms and consequences for nonpayment. The seller takes a small portion of the drug for himself, called a <em>pinch</em>; the practice is called <em>pinching</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e4\">5. The Path of Abuse at Work (7.4)</h3><p>Nobody aspires to be an addict. Typical progression:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Experimentation:</strong> a friend or relative introduces the drug.</li>\n<li><strong>Periodic use</strong>, often social, if the effect is pleasing.</li>\n<li><strong>New relationships</strong> with friends or coworkers who use the same drug. The old ones weaken and may end.</li>\n<li><strong>Personal changes</strong> in appearance, behavior, interests, and relationships. They become secretive, suspicious, and paranoid.</li>\n<li><strong>Change of attitude toward the drug:</strong> they defend its benefits, its social value, and their right to use it. They think, study, and talk about it.</li>\n<li><strong>Job deterioration:</strong> attendance problems with recognizable patterns, less concentration, and more “personal problems.” More frequent and irresponsible use: drinking and driving, smoking marijuana while handling firearms, using in public with strangers.</li>\n<li><strong>Use at work:</strong> at first discreet, then often brazen. They enjoy testing the limits.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Examples of use at work: drinking in the parking lot at lunch and breaks; smoking marijuana in restrooms and locker rooms; cocaine or methamphetamine at the desk; drugs in company vehicles or on business trips; even with customers and vendors.</p>\n<p>Where they keep or move the drug:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>in desks, lockers, or toolboxes;</li>\n<li>they use the mailroom or the shipping department to distribute and receive drugs and money;</li>\n<li>they hide it in safes, furniture, trash cans, hazardous materials containers, beverage containers, lunch boxes, briefcases, handbags, shoes, coats, raw materials, and finished product.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They are ingenious, shrewd, and deceptive.</p>\n<p>Profile of the employee who sells (dealer):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>may sell in secret in front of nonusers, supervisors, and managers;</li>\n<li>socializes more than others and makes contacts nonstop, avoiding detection;</li>\n<li>is often absent or away from his workstation;</li>\n<li>avoids management;</li>\n<li>does not usually cause problems, but becomes belligerent if accused;</li>\n<li>supports employee causes and enjoys creating conflict between management and workers;</li>\n<li>resists teamwork, has hidden agendas, and despises authority.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Financial escalation: available income runs out → buys on credit → credit runs out → sells drugs (especially to colleagues) or steals. <strong>The main victim of the theft is the employer.</strong></p>\n<p>Progression of theft:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>coworkers' food;</li>\n<li>petty cash, cash receipts, office equipment, and coworkers' valuables;</li>\n<li>unchecked, he steals “as far as the organization will allow”: scrap, raw materials, finished product, and intellectual property (customer lists, confidential information, and trade secrets).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Theft from customers and vendors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>shipping short to an important customer and selling the difference;</li>\n<li>accepting kickbacks for miscounting, allowing overages, double shipping, approving improper credits, or diverting a vendor's delivery.</li>\n<li>Impact: destroyed relationships; vendors who withhold service or materials; customers who cancel, do not pay, or sue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other costs: more accidents and injuries; more health claims and benefit use; more expensive insurance; absences that force recruiting, hiring, and training replacements; more false claims and feigned workplace injuries.</p>\n<p>Final cycle:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When performance falls, discipline begins. The job may be the only stable thing in their life, and staying employed “proves” to them that they live normally.</li>\n<li>In extreme cases they give up family, children, home, and car, but fight to keep the job.</li>\n<li>Facing the risk of losing it, some quit the drug. The most common response is to <strong>feign a workplace injury</strong> to escape discipline.</li>\n<li>Benefits they expect from a long absence: relief from responsibilities; getting away from the environment with the most exposure and temptation; and a chance to quit and start over. These are rarely realized.</li>\n<li>Without the structure of work, their life falls apart: more use and more debt. When they return months later they are more dependent, perform less, and tend to file another claim.</li>\n<li>The cycle can repeat several times before dismissal. In the end everyone loses: employer, spouse, family, friends, and the user himself.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e5\">6. Drugs of Abuse (7.5)</h3><p>Legal basis of the U.S. federal strategy: the <strong>Comprehensive Substance Abuse Prevention and Control Act of 1970, Title II (CSA)</strong>. It has four fundamental parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>mechanisms to reduce the availability of controlled substances;</li>\n<li>procedures for placing a substance under control;</li>\n<li>criteria for setting control requirements;</li>\n<li>obligations arising from international treaties.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It regulates the manufacture, purchase, and distribution of drugs according to their abuse potential. The Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) enforces it and oversees the classification into schedules.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the law's actual name is the <em>Comprehensive </em><em>Drug</em><em> Abuse Prevention and Control Act of 1970</em>; its Title II is the <em>Controlled Substances Act</em> (CSA).</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Schedule</th>\n<th>Abuse potential</th>\n<th>Accepted medical use in the U.S.</th>\n<th>Dependence it can cause</th>\n<th>Book examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Not indicated</td>\n<td>marijuana, heroin, methaqualone, lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Yes, with severe restrictions</td>\n<td>Severe psychological or physical</td>\n<td>cocaine, morphine, amphetamine, opium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Lower than I and II</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Moderate or low physical, or high psychological</td>\n<td>codeine, Tylenol with codeine, benzphetamine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Lower than III</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Limited physical or psychological</td>\n<td>Darvon, Darvocet, phenobarbital, Valium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>V</td>\n<td>Lower than IV</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Lower than that of Schedule IV</td>\n<td>low-potency prescription cold and pain medications, common in households</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> codeine alone is in Schedule II. Schedule III covers combination products with little codeine, such as Tylenol with codeine.</p>\n<h4>6.1 Depressants (7.5.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small doses: calm. Medical uses: inducing sleep, relieving anxiety and muscle spasms, and preventing seizures.</li>\n<li>Larger doses: altered reflexes, slurred speech, loss of motor coordination, and uncontrollable drowsiness.</li>\n<li>They are often combined with other depressants or with stimulants.</li>\n<li>Risks: birth defects, overdose, and death.</li>\n<li>They include benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and sedatives. Examples: Valium, Quaalude, Xanax, Lunesta, Nembutal, and Seconal.</li>\n<li><strong>Alcohol is classified as a depressant.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Alcohol:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A fast-acting central nervous system depressant. It acts as an analgesic with sedative effects.</li>\n<li>A little: well-being and slightly altered reflexes. A lot: disorientation, disinhibition, loss of coordination, and irrationality.</li>\n<li>It is addictive. Prolonged abuse damages the brain, liver, and heart.</li>\n<li>It also causes sexual dysfunction, gastritis, ulcers, malnutrition, hypertension, cirrhosis, pancreatitis, cancer, and death.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Alcohol dependence or alcoholism: four symptoms.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Craving</td>\n<td>A strong need or compulsion to drink</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loss of control</td>\n<td>Inability to limit how much one drinks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical dependence</td>\n<td>Withdrawal symptoms (nausea, sweating, shaking, anxiety) when stopping drinking after a period of heavy use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tolerance</td>\n<td>Need to drink more to achieve the desired effect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The craving can be as strong as the need for food or water.</li>\n<li><strong>Key difference between a social drinker and an alcoholic:</strong> the alcoholic loses control over when, where, and how much he drinks.</li>\n<li>Some recover on their own, but most need help.</li>\n<li>Apparent causes: genetic and environmental components.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2 Narcotics (7.5.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Small doses: effects like those of depressants. Larger doses: sleep, unconsciousness, and vomiting.</li>\n<li>A large dose can cause severe respiratory depression and death.</li>\n<li>Intravenous use increases the risk of hepatitis and AIDS.</li>\n<li><strong>Tolerance:</strong> the analgesia, euphoria, and sedation last less time and are less intense, so larger doses are needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependence:</strong> alteration of the body's normal functions; the drug is needed to avoid withdrawal.</li>\n<li>Short-acting narcotics: shorter, more intense withdrawal. Long-acting: prolonged but less severe withdrawal.</li>\n<li>In the medical sense, narcotics = opiates (also opioids): opium, its derivatives, and its synthetic substitutes. Examples: morphine, heroin, and codeine.</li>\n<li>They are indispensable against pain, but highly addictive. They are taken orally or intravenously, and also smoked.</li>\n<li><strong>Uncommon at work:</strong> they are expensive and their physical effects are usually evident.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.3 Stimulants (7.5.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They make the employee seem more alert, enthusiastic, and productive. In reality it is wasted effort that causes errors.</li>\n<li>The user believes they improve his creativity and endurance, but they rob him of energy and rationality.</li>\n<li>They cause frequent, severe mood swings; the user becomes hard to direct and clashes with others.</li>\n<li>He tries to control his mood with another drug, almost always alcohol.</li>\n<li>Prolonged abuse: weight loss, induced psychosis, and addiction to several drugs.</li>\n<li>Stimulants used at work: cocaine, amphetamines, methamphetamine, methcathinone, methylphenidate (Ritalin), and anorectics (appetite suppressants).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cocaine (cocaine hydrochloride):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A white, crystalline substance extracted from the coca plant. It has some medical use as a topical anesthetic. It is considered highly addictive.</li>\n<li>Most commonly snorted; it is also injected or smoked.</li>\n<li>Immediate effects: dilated pupils, high blood pressure, elevated heart rate, and euphoria.</li>\n<li><em>Crack</em> or <em>rock</em>: powder cocaine, baking soda, and water; usually smoked. The effect lasts minutes and leaves a craving for more.</li>\n<li>The effects appear almost immediately and disappear in minutes or hours.</li>\n<li>Small doses: euphoria, energy, talkativeness, and alertness; less need to eat and sleep. Some do simple tasks faster; others, the opposite.</li>\n<li>Large doses: strange, erratic, or violent behavior; tremors, vertigo, muscle spasms, paranoia, or toxic reaction. Also restlessness, irritability, and anxiety.</li>\n<li>Deaths are usually due to cardiac arrest or to seizures followed by respiratory arrest.</li>\n<li>Some develop tolerance. Others become more sensitive and can die from low doses.</li>\n<li>Bingeing: repeated and increasing doses. It causes irritability, restlessness, and paranoia, and in the end paranoid psychosis with auditory hallucinations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Methamphetamine:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A synthetic drug easy to make from common materials and simple equipment. Names: <em>crank</em>, <em>meth</em>, <em>crystal meth</em>, or <em>speed</em>.</li>\n<li>In many workplaces it <strong>has replaced cocaine</strong> as the preferred stimulant.</li>\n<li>It is smoked, snorted, swallowed, or injected.</li>\n<li>Smoked or injected: an intense, very pleasurable “flash” lasting a few minutes. Snorted or swallowed: euphoria without that intense flash.</li>\n<li>Time to effect: snorted, <strong>3 to 5 minutes</strong>; swallowed, <strong>15 to 20 minutes</strong>.</li>\n<li>High doses: dangerous or lethal body temperature, and seizures.</li>\n<li>Chronic abuse: addiction, violence, anxiety, severe mood swings, weight loss, irritability, confusion, insomnia, and general deterioration.</li>\n<li>Psychotic effects: paranoia, auditory hallucinations, mood disturbances, and delusions. Example: feeling insects walking on the skin (<em>formication</em>).</li>\n<li>The paranoia can cause homicidal and suicidal ideas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.4 Hallucinogens (7.5.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They drastically alter mood, sensory perception, and reasoning.</li>\n<li>Those from plants and mushrooms have been used for centuries in shamanic practices. Today even more potent synthetic ones exist.</li>\n<li>The most abused: LSD (<em>acid</em>); MDA (methylenedioxyamphetamine); MDMA (methylenedioxymethamphetamine, ecstasy); PCP (phencyclidine, <em>angel dust</em>); mescaline, from the peyote cactus; and certain mushrooms.</li>\n<li>Physiological effects: elevated heart rate and blood pressure, dilated pupils.</li>\n<li>Sensory effects: perceptual distortions. Psychic effects: thought disturbances about time and space.</li>\n<li>Time seems to stop and shapes and colors change. The experience can be pleasant or terrifying.</li>\n<li>Vivid hallucinations, panic attacks, and synaesthesia or sensory crossover: believing one “sees sounds” or “smells colors.”</li>\n<li>Their effects are unpredictable with each use.</li>\n<li><em>Flashbacks</em>: partial recurrences of the experience weeks or months later, without taking the drug.</li>\n<li>Some are neurotoxic. The most common danger is impaired judgment: rash decisions, accidents, injuries, and death.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.5 Marijuana (cannabis) (7.5.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It is the second most abused drug at work, after alcohol.</strong></li>\n<li>Small doses: effects similar to alcohol; recovering alcoholics use it as a substitute. Large doses: hallucinations, memory loss, and lethargy.</li>\n<li>Data from the U.S. National Safety Council (NSC) (Gates 2019): employees who tested positive for cannabis had <strong>55% more industrial accidents, 85% more injuries, and 75% more absenteeism</strong> than those who tested negative.</li>\n<li>For the NSC, <strong>no level of use is safe or acceptable</strong> in safety-sensitive positions, because of the risk to the operator and to others.</li>\n<li>The amount of detectable THC <strong>does not correlate directly</strong> with the degree of impairment.</li>\n<li>Marijuana, hashish, and hashish oil derive from hemp, <em>cannabis sativa</em>.</li>\n<li>Main psychoactive component: tetrahydrocannabinol (THC). It is retained in body fat and accumulates because it is not easily eliminated.</li>\n<li><strong>Reverse tolerance:</strong> because of that accumulation, the user needs less and less of the drug for the same effect. He smokes a smaller amount, but more often.</li>\n<li>At work it may come mixed with other drugs, sometimes an opiate or PCP, to gain potency and sales. Doses are never consistent and the user can become addicted to what is mixed in as well.</li>\n<li>Forms: traditional dried flower, vaporizer pens, edibles and beverages, concentrates, sprays, topicals, and capsules.</li>\n<li>Hashish: a THC-rich resin that is collected, dried, and pressed into balls, cakes, or cookie-like sheets.</li>\n<li>Hemp flower: little or no THC; it can be legal where marijuana is not (Klein 2021).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Legal or illegal? (U.S.)</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Challenge in states that have legalized medical or recreational use: federal law still classifies it in Schedule I (according to the book, 2021). It can be legal in the state and illegal in the country.</li>\n<li>The employer must clarify its policy and educate the workforce about what it expects.</li>\n<li>Zero-tolerance: seems easier, but can bring complications. Some components remain in the blood up to a month later.</li>\n<li>Alternative: an under-the-influence policy, which treats marijuana like alcohol. One may not arrive intoxicated or use on the premises or during work hours.</li>\n<li>Zero tolerance is common in the <strong>safety and health sectors</strong>. It must be clear that the job demands a clear mind.</li>\n<li>Essential: a specific written marijuana policy, covering drugs that are illegal under federal and state law.</li>\n<li>If one does not want to get into measuring impairment, prohibit any detectable illegal drug (Chapa 2015).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.6 Analogue or Designer Drugs (7.5.6)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A synthetic preparation with effects and characteristics similar to those of a natural substance.</li>\n<li>They are made in a laboratory. Because their formula differs from the one they imitate, at first they are not classified as controlled, even if the imitated one is.</li>\n<li>Many are much more potent than the original; some have caused overdose deaths.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.7 Prescription Drugs (7.5.7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They are frequently abused at work, especially stimulants and sedatives.</li>\n<li>A doctor prescribes them, but they are overused or kept being taken without need. Physical or psychological dependence can arise.</li>\n<li>Without legal access, the user turns to illegal sources or to another drug.</li>\n<li>Those who sell them at work often believe they are doing a favor for a friend or coworker.</li>\n<li>The best sellers at work: <strong>benzodiazepines</strong>, depressants for anxiety, tension, and spasms. Examples: Librium, Xanax, and Valium.</li>\n<li>Their abuse is associated with teenagers and young adults seeking to get high: less inhibition and impaired judgment.</li>\n<li>Mixing them with alcohol or another depressant potentiates the effect and can be fatal.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e6\">7. Addiction and Chemical Dependency (7.6)</h3><h4>7.1 Addiction (7.6.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> the disease of obsession and compulsion. One can be addicted to anything; it is usually understood as the repeated, uncontrollable use of a substance or a behavior.</li>\n<li>The addict also becomes hooked on the social behaviors surrounding the drug: the rituals for obtaining, preparing, and using it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Signs of addiction or abuse (Mayo Clinic 2021):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sign</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Problems at work</td>\n<td>Frequent absences, disinterest, and performance failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical health</td>\n<td>Lack of energy and motivation, weight gain or loss, red eyes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neglected appearance</td>\n<td>Disinterest in grooming, clothing, or appearance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Behavior changes</td>\n<td>Secrecy and changes in relationships with family, friends, and coworkers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Money problems</td>\n<td>Borrowing, or stealing money or objects to sell them</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Consequences as addiction advances:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>arrests and convictions for driving while intoxicated;</li>\n<li>hangovers and blackouts;</li>\n<li>dysfunctional relationships;</li>\n<li>confrontations with family, friends, and employers;</li>\n<li>disciplinary actions;</li>\n<li>demotions;</li>\n<li>dismissals;</li>\n<li>loss of freedom (prison);</li>\n<li>danger to oneself and to others.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.2 Chemical Dependency (7.6.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> an integral component of addiction. It is the physiological craving caused by chemical changes in the body, which are mental and physical.</li>\n<li>The craving is relieved only by using. The dependent person can lose all rationality and do anything for the drug.</li>\n<li><strong>Tolerance:</strong> the body gets used to it and asks for ever larger doses for the same effect.</li>\n<li><strong>Withdrawal:</strong> painful physiological responses on stopping the drug. It is the body's attempt to adapt chemically without it and can be very violent.</li>\n<li>Withdrawal symptoms: irritability, vomiting, tremors, sweating, insomnia, and seizures.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.3 High-Functioning Abusers (7.6.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They seem to manage their dependence. But if the use is obsessive, they need the drug just to function “normally.”</li>\n<li>They look like everyone else: stable job, regular schedule, family, and apparent happiness.</li>\n<li>They lead two lives, one visible and one secret. They usually use <strong>daily</strong>.</li>\n<li>At work they seem productive; without the drug they are completely different people.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>7.4 Denial (7.6.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> a mental state in which the person refuses to believe or acknowledge that his behavior harms him and those around him.</li>\n<li>He rationalizes his behavior and minimizes its impact. He denies that the drug affects his health, work, and family, and denies his relationship with the drug and its growing cost.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical phrases of the user in denial:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"I can quit whenever I want.\"</li>\n<li>\"It doesn't affect me like it does others.\"</li>\n<li>\"What I do in my free time is my business.\"</li>\n<li>\"I've never hurt anyone.\"</li>\n<li>\"I don't use enough to become an addict.\"</li>\n<li>\"It doesn't affect my work.\"</li>\n<li>\"My wife (or husband) doesn't mind.\"</li>\n<li>\"I can control it.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Denial by friends and coworkers: they deny the problem or justify it as temporary or deserved. That way they present it as normal or expected and encourage continued use. Naive friends may even discourage therapy, treatment, or abstinence.</p>\n<p>Denial by bosses and organizations: bosses deny the problem even in the face of obvious evidence. Organizations in denial do not create sound policies, do not enforce the ones they have, and do not respond to incidents.</p>\n<p>Typical phrases of managers in denial:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"We don't have a drug problem here.\"</li>\n<li>\"If we had one, we would see it.\"</li>\n<li>\"We're only concerned about the sellers, not the users.\"</li>\n<li>\"We know some people smoke marijuana at lunch, but what harm does it do?\"</li>\n<li>\"If we enforced our policies, we wouldn't find anyone to work here.\"</li>\n<li>\"This industry doesn't have that kind of problem.\"</li>\n<li>\"That kind of people doesn't work here.\"</li>\n<li>\"We don't need psychological support for our employees.\"</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Out of fear of facing the truth, the organization denies help and takes part in the advance of the disease.</p>\n<h4>7.5 Enabling (7.6.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> allowing or encouraging, consciously or unconsciously, another person's destructive behavior. It usually stems from denial.</li>\n<li>The enabler protects the user from the consequences and maintains the illusion that he is fine.</li>\n<li>The family enables when it calls in sick for him, makes excuses to the boss, or lies to spare him discipline. Also when it forgives broken promises again and again.</li>\n<li>Bosses enable when they cover for him and accept excuses for attendance or poor performance. Also when they believe rationalizations such as:<ul>\n<li>\"I have a lot of problems at home.\"</li>\n<li>\"It won't happen again.\"</li>\n<li>\"I can control it. Just give me more time.\"</li>\n<li>\"I'm not the only one with problems here.\"</li>\n<li>\"I promise you that…\" (incomplete phrase in the book).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>It is easier to enable than to face reality. Dealing with these employees is unpleasant and even frightening.</li>\n<li>Bosses doubt their judgment and fear for their careers, and the user takes advantage of it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To avoid enabling, supervisors and managers must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>know the substance abuse policy and how it is applied;</li>\n<li>know the symptoms of abuse and when to ask for help;</li>\n<li>consider open or anonymous channels that encourage reporting;</li>\n<li>document performance accurately;</li>\n<li>recognize enabling behaviors and stop them;</li>\n<li>communicate expectations and demand accountability;</li>\n<li>document efforts and results;</li>\n<li>communicate with executive management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Breaking the cycle requires confronting the problem honestly.</p>\n<h4>7.6 Codependency (7.6.6)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> letting another person's behavior eclipse one's own values and judgment; not defending what one knows is right. It almost guarantees the destruction of the relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The codependent typically:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>believes he must do more work than his share to maintain the relationship;</li>\n<li>lives worried and absorbed by the dependence of his partner or coworker;</li>\n<li>fears expressing how he feels about the user;</li>\n<li>accepts that the user does not keep promises or commitments;</li>\n<li>fears disciplining an employee for fear that he will leave and have to be replaced.</li>\n<li>Associated feelings: anger, isolation, guilt, fear, shame, despair, and loss of control.</li>\n<li>By not “rocking the boat,” they give the user support to keep using.</li>\n<li>Roles they adopt: rescuers, caretakers, complainers, and adjusters.</li>\n<li>Sometimes they become outstanding performers to set an example; other times, they join in the use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To avoid codependency, supervisors and managers must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>focus on performance, without letting manipulation eclipse what is right;</li>\n<li>set limits and tolerate only what is acceptable;</li>\n<li>ask for help from internal resources: human resources and the employee assistance program (EAP);</li>\n<li>refer the employee to resources that can help him;</li>\n<li>document efforts and results;</li>\n<li>communicate with executive management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Bosses must understand addiction and dependence and know how to recognize the three destructive behaviors. Not confronting them is a lack of compassion and cruelty.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Destructive behavior</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Who shows it according to the book</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Denial</td>\n<td>Refusing to acknowledge the harm the behavior causes</td>\n<td>User, friends, coworkers, bosses, and the organization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enabling</td>\n<td>Allowing or encouraging the destructive behavior, protecting the user from the consequences</td>\n<td>Family, supervisors, and managers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Codependency</td>\n<td>Letting the other person's behavior override one's own values and judgment</td>\n<td>Partner, coworkers, and bosses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e7\">8. Role of Supervisors and Managers (7.7)</h3><h4>8.1 Drug-Free Workplace Policy (7.7.1)</h4>\n<p>A policy is absolutely necessary. It must be practical, functional, and enforceable. It must be written, well communicated, <strong>signed for receipt by every employee</strong>, and applied equitably.</p>\n<p>An effective policy must:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>State the goal of a drug-free workplace and why it matters to everyone.</li>\n<li>Declare drug and alcohol abuse at work unacceptable. Prohibit the use, sale, possession, and offer of sale of controlled substances at work or during work hours.</li>\n<li>Define on-the-job impairment.</li>\n<li>Describe how and when testing will be done, what counts as a positive, and what happens if a sample is not provided.</li>\n<li>Define what an infraction is and its consequences.</li>\n<li>Recognize that abuse is treatable and explain the treatment and rehabilitation options.</li>\n<li>Define the role of the EAP and how to access it.</li>\n<li>Answer any question about abuse, the policy, or its enforcement.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Other points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Avoid the term “under the influence.”</strong> Only alcohol has a legal definition of that state. For other drugs there is no standard, so it cannot be proven.</li>\n<li>After creating the policy, allow a waiting period to educate employees.</li>\n<li>On the effective date, bosses declare their willingness to enforce it. It is one of the most important and least recognized deterrents.</li>\n<li>Bosses are not expected to “hunt” for those who use or sell. They must <strong>watch performance</strong> and correct it when it is not adequate.</li>\n<li>The U.S. Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA) offers guides for creating a drug-free workplace (SAMHSA 2020).</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> internal contradiction. In 7.5.5 the book proposes an <em>under-the-influence</em> policy for marijuana; in 7.7.1 it says the policy should avoid that term.</p>\n<h4>8.2 Investigation and Documentation (7.7.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>When a policy violation occurs, management responds quickly and well, often with an investigation.</li>\n<li><strong>Workplace investigation:</strong> a fact-finding process, ideally separate from decision making. If it cannot be separated, the investigator must not let prior biases influence it.</li>\n<li>It can be simple and informal (confront the suspect and ask) or complex and formal.</li>\n<li>Minimum requirements: fair and impartial; fact-based and objective; thorough and well documented; and protective of the rights of suspects and witnesses.</li>\n<li>It must also not violate the law, policy, labor agreements, or privacy. It must be confidential: evidence, notes, reports, and conclusions only for those who need to know.</li>\n<li>Executive management, the legal department, and human resources must always take part.</li>\n<li>Discipline comes only after a detailed review of the findings by qualified management.</li>\n<li>If the findings do not justify discipline, the appropriate response is <strong>intervention</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.3 Employee Hotlines (7.7.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>After the corporate scandals of the late 1990s and early 2000s, organizations sought new reporting channels.</li>\n<li>Fear of retaliation holds back reports, even anonymous ones. Modern anonymous systems have reduced it in part.</li>\n<li>“Open door” policies are not enough to prevent and detect abuse.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Legal mandate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 requires every publicly traded company to provide a confidential means of anonymously reporting questionable accounting or auditing practices. According to the book, it serves employees, customers, and vendors.</li>\n<li>The company must ensure the “receipt, retention, and treatment” of complaints.</li>\n<li>Those same tools serve to obtain information about substance abuse.</li>\n<li>Multinational challenge: hotlines required in some countries are illegal in others. It reflects cultural attitudes toward whistleblowers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> Section 301 of Sarbanes-Oxley requires anonymous, confidential submission for <strong>employees</strong>. Third-party complaints must be received, retained, and treated, but the law does not require anonymity for them.</p>\n<p>Early warning systems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Anonymous hotlines detect all kinds of misconduct sooner, before major losses and reputational damage.</li>\n<li>Human resources and <strong>security managers</strong> have quickly seen their value for receiving reports of misconduct and substance abuse.</li>\n<li>Their cost is minimal compared with the losses they prevent.</li>\n<li>They show that the organization takes its employees and their safety seriously, and encourage action in the face of inappropriate conduct.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Advantages of outsourcing the hotline:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>regulation may limit strictly internal mechanisms;</li>\n<li>vendors have better technology;</li>\n<li>they employ operators better trained to collect the key data.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>8.4 Intervention (7.7.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Definition:</strong> a calculated interruption of the destructive behaviors of the user and of those around him.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not discipline.</strong> It is a caring behavior in which one plans, prepares, and acts.</li>\n<li>It aims to make the user see the consequences, rescue him, and return him to work as a productive contributor.</li>\n<li>Requirements: documented performance; getting out of denial; not assuming he will improve on his own; not rationalizing or accepting poor performance or misconduct.</li>\n<li>It is often the user's only hope before discipline.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Observe and document performance</td>\n<td>Make sure the employee knows what is expected. Document inappropriate behaviors. Ask another boss for an opinion if in doubt. Act immediately if needed to prevent an accident or serious error.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Confront the employee</td>\n<td>Take him out of his area and confront him in private. Do not do or say anything that embarrasses him.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Interview and talk</td>\n<td>See the detail below.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Document results</td>\n<td>What each party said, the employee's attitude, and his response to the demand for improvement. Schedule the follow-up and make sure, in writing if necessary, that he knows the date.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Communicate to executive management</td>\n<td>Brief executive management and human resources thoroughly. Give them copies of notes and documents. Suggest, if appropriate, that human resources attend the next meeting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. Follow up</td>\n<td>Short, direct meeting, with the same attendees. Review progress. If he complied, thank and congratulate him. If not, apply the progressive action announced, set goals and a new date.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Detail of step 3 (interview):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Have a witness if possible, and a union representative if appropriate or required.</li>\n<li>If there was a prior investigation, share the appropriate information.</li>\n<li>Only concrete facts, never generalities. Show documentary evidence of the poor performance, such as attendance records or time cards.</li>\n<li>Describe in detail what is expected, citing the written policy whenever possible.</li>\n<li>Do not identify the investigation's witnesses unless essential.</li>\n<li>Do not accuse the employee or try to diagnose or rationalize his behavior.</li>\n<li>Give him the chance to explain himself, with an open mind but knowing he may be in denial. Document his responses.</li>\n<li>Ask him what the organization could reasonably do to help him. Empathize, but without committing: just listen.</li>\n<li>Suggest professional help, such as the EAP or community resources. Have phone numbers or brochures on hand.</li>\n<li>Reiterate the failures and the expectations. Define limits and concrete consequences. Example: \"Your next failure will bring a final written reprimand.\"</li>\n<li>Indicate that he will continue to be monitored and when it will be reviewed. Example: \"In 30 days we'll meet here to review your progress.\"</li>\n<li>Close on a positive note: he is expected to improve, he has the organization's support, and his success will be a “win-win.” Then return him to work.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Benefit: intervention corrects and shows concern, not punishment. In the long run it avoids unnecessary discipline, reduces turnover, and can save a life.</p>\n<p><strong>When intervention fails:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sometimes the addiction is too strong. The user may be resentful, indifferent, or angry. If he has “surrendered to the disease,” progressive discipline may be the only response.</li>\n<li><strong>Documented progressive discipline:</strong> gradual escalation in the face of continued failures. Verbal reprimand → written reprimand → suspension → dismissal.</li>\n<li>Message to the user: his relationship with the drug has a cost.</li>\n<li>The job may be the last thing he has left, his “last bastion of normalcy.” That is why he clings to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Security risk:</strong> he may lie, cheat, and steal to keep the job, and even resort to violence if he sees it threatened.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.5 Employee Assistance Programs (EAP) (7.7.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They began in the <strong>1940s</strong> as occupational alcoholism programs, focused on alcohol.</li>\n<li>Today they cover alcohol and other substances, family problems, mental health, and other personal matters.</li>\n<li>They are provided by the employer and are free to the employee; they usually include family members. Both are called clients.</li>\n<li>Strict confidentiality: <strong>not even the employer knows the clients' names</strong>.</li>\n<li>How it works: community referral network; several telephone consultations (sometimes in person) to assess; names of several resources are given; the client decides whether to continue.</li>\n<li>The EAP can track progress as support, but treatment is given by outside, independent professionals. Everything is confidential.</li>\n<li>Health insurance may cover treatment, and leaves of absence are granted.</li>\n<li>In the U.S., the Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) requires reasonable accommodation for employees and applicants <strong>in recovery</strong>. <strong>Current users are not protected.</strong></li>\n<li>The EAP is a clearinghouse. It <strong>does not investigate or do drug testing</strong>: it only connects people with quality professional help.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Access route</th>\n<th>Characteristics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Voluntary</td>\n<td>The employee seeks help on his own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management referral</td>\n<td>Gradual approach, like intervention and progressive discipline. Mandatory participation. Professional treatment. Management receives progress reports.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In a management referral, after treatment (or during it):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>performance and conduct goals are negotiated;</li>\n<li>as a condition of employment, the employee agrees to be monitored and knows the consequences of noncompliance;</li>\n<li>monitoring usually includes periodic drug tests, and may include ongoing therapy;</li>\n<li>over time the monitoring and the condition of conditional employment are withdrawn;</li>\n<li>it must include a plan for reintegration into work.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.6 Role Modeling (7.7.6)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It consists of setting an example with one's own behavior, as parents and teachers do.</li>\n<li>If enough people take part, the culture of the entire organization changes.</li>\n<li>Users prefer environments with others like them and resent the limits of a healthy culture. Above all they resent not being able to rationalize their use.</li>\n<li>Positive peer pressure forces them to confront their behavior: they can no longer lie or deceive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.7 Reintegration of the Recovering Employee (7.7.7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Recovery is long and painful. Many return to the same environment where the abuse began.</li>\n<li>If the environment is controlled by users, staying clean will be difficult or impossible.</li>\n<li>In a healthy environment, the odds of recovery and lasting sobriety are good.</li>\n<li>Coworkers give encouragement, positive role models, and an environment free of temptation.</li>\n<li>Bosses demand accountability, set reasonable expectations, and reinforce achievements.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>8.8 Employee Education and Supervisor Training (7.7.8)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Training at all levels as part of the program: accurate information on the dangers and on policies and expectations.</li>\n<li>Human resources must know how to identify user candidates in order to screen them out, as part of preemployment screening.</li>\n<li>Human resources must know all the policies, because it usually applies correction and discipline.</li>\n<li>Training of supervisors and managers is critical. <strong>Only a trained health professional can diagnose</strong> an abuse problem. Training gives bosses tools to enforce rules and policies.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e8\">9. Drug Testing (7.8)</h3><ul>\n<li>A prevention and detection tool. Preemployment testing detects possible users before hiring them.</li>\n<li>For active employees: for reasonable suspicion; after an accident or injury; randomly; on return after a violation; and as follow-up to treatment.</li>\n<li>It also deters, because it creates fear of being discovered.</li>\n<li>The employer has the <strong>right and the duty</strong> to promote a drug-free workplace.</li>\n<li><strong>Drug-Free Workplace Act of 1988 (U.S.):</strong> companies that contract with the federal government and receive grants of more than $25,000 must certify that they have policies for a drug-free workplace.</li>\n<li>Other laws and regulations require periodic testing of certain transportation and public service workers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Approach by country (Anderson 2015):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Country or region</th>\n<th>Situation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Canada</td>\n<td>In 2013 the Supreme Court ruled that random alcohol testing of employees in safety-sensitive positions invaded their privacy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>European Union</td>\n<td>In some countries all testing of employees is illegal; in others it is permitted depending on the position</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>China, India, and many Latin American countries</td>\n<td>Testing is not the norm, because fewer substances are considered illegal than in the U.S.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>9.1 Methods (7.8.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Drug test:</strong> scientific examination of a biological sample to detect a specific drug or its metabolite.</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolite:</strong> chemical byproduct left when the body metabolizes the substance.</li>\n<li>Most-used sample: <strong>urine</strong>. Blood, hair, and saliva are also analyzed.</li>\n<li>Urine is preferred because its collection is not intrusive: the body does not have to be pricked, as when drawing blood.</li>\n<li>Collection follows protocols that protect privacy. The sample is sealed, labeled, and sent to the laboratory.</li>\n<li>If the preliminary test detects a drug or metabolite, a <strong>confirmatory test</strong> is done with more precise technology. It identifies the type of drug and its concentration.</li>\n<li>The confirmed positive is communicated confidentially to the employer or its representative, such as a medical review officer (MRO).</li>\n<li>Samples are labeled with a number, not a name: not even the laboratory knows whose they are.</li>\n<li>The employer's response to a positive depends on the circumstances and the policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.2 Accuracy (7.8.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Tests are extremely accurate. A very small percentage give false positives, but that is what the confirmatory test is for.</li>\n<li>Laboratories are regulated and have rigorous requirements and quality assurance.</li>\n<li>Certification by the U.S. National Institute on Drug Abuse (NIDA) is difficult and expensive. Every sample, procedure, and test is documented, and control samples are analyzed frequently.</li>\n<li>NIDA data: of about <strong>16 million</strong> tests a year in its laboratories, <strong>fewer than 16</strong> come back positive with no drug present.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book calls NIDA the \"National Institute on Substance Abuse\"; its actual name is the <em>National Institute on Drug Abuse</em>.</p>\n<h4>9.3 Strategy (7.8.3)</h4>\n<p>Many states regulate drug and alcohol testing, so differences by jurisdiction must be addressed. Questions the strategy must resolve:</p>\n<p><strong>For which substances?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Laws, collective bargaining agreements, and contracts may limit it.</li>\n<li>Under U.S. federal regulations, one can test for alcohol and <strong>five controlled substances: marijuana, cocaine, amphetamines, opiates, and PCP</strong>.</li>\n<li>Some states require following the federal rules, whether or not the employer is federally regulated. Others permit more substances, or give financial incentives for including them.</li>\n<li>For additional substances, consider the ADA, which limits the employer's medical inquiries. It is advisable to test <strong>only after a job offer</strong>.</li>\n<li>Risk: an employee in a non-safety-sensitive position, disciplined for a substance outside the five, could file a claim under the ADA.</li>\n<li>Outside the U.S., consult legal counsel about what is lawful.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>First review the applicable laws. The two most common times are <strong>preemployment and random</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Reasonable suspicion, also called for-cause:</strong> when the supervisor has evidence or reasonable cause to suspect use.</li>\n<li>Triggers: physical evidence of substances, patterns of erratic or abnormal behavior, disorientation, or inability to perform routine tasks.</li>\n<li>Because it is discretionary, supervisors must be trained to apply it consistently across the entire workforce.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What type of sample?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Urine, saliva, blood, hair, or breath. Laws vary on which tests are permitted or prohibited.</li>\n<li><strong>Split specimen:</strong> some laws require dividing each sample in two. The first is analyzed; if it comes back positive, the second is offered to the employee for analysis at an independent laboratory.</li>\n<li>Impact: if it is not split where required, <strong>the employee must be reinstated even if he tests positive</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Whom to test?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The employer does not always decide alone. With a collective bargaining agreement, it is decided bilaterally.</li>\n<li>In the U.S., the Federal Highway Administration requires commercial carriers to have a drug and alcohol testing program for all their commercial drivers.</li>\n<li>Anyone doing business with certain government agencies must follow their rules on testing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> since 2000, testing of commercial drivers has been regulated by the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration (FMCSA), created out of the Federal Highway Administration.</p>\n<p>General testing guide (always comply with applicable regulations):</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Whom</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>All workers</td>\n<td>Preemployment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All workers</td>\n<td>Reasonable suspicion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All workers</td>\n<td>Post-accident/injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All workers</td>\n<td>Return to duty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All workers</td>\n<td>Follow up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Only workers in safety-sensitive positions</td>\n<td>Randomly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Who collects the samples?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Some jurisdictions require licensed medical professionals. Others, a “qualified” person.</li>\n<li>U.S. federal rules: only breath alcohol technicians perform alcohol tests. Only screen test technicians perform alcohol screening.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Where?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>At the workplace, or the employee goes to a medical facility.</li>\n<li>Instant test kits are not permitted in many places.</li>\n<li>Some laws permit only certain laboratories to analyze urine.</li>\n<li>U.S. federal employers: only laboratories certified by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) or by the College of American Pathology (CAP).</li>\n<li>Some collective bargaining agreements and contracts also require a facility certified by HHS or approved by CAP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the actual name of the CAP is the <em>College of American Pathologists</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Who receives the results?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>In the U.S., federal and state laws require a confidential medical review before reporting to the employer.</li>\n<li>The <strong>MRO</strong> is a licensed physician. In a confidential interview, by telephone or in person, with the donor, he determines whether the result has a medical justification.</li>\n<li>That is, whether something other than an illegally used controlled substance caused the positive. If there is none, he reports the positive to the employer.</li>\n<li>The prudent course for any organization: treat results as confidential medical information. It protects privacy and avoids legal liability.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.4 Incentives for the Employer (7.8.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Take advantage of state incentives, which are usually in workers' compensation laws.</li>\n<li>Forms: reduced annual premiums, or presumptive denial of benefits if the worker had used prohibited substances when injured.</li>\n<li>With few exceptions, the employer and insurer do not pay compensation under two conditions: that the worker violated a known safety rule and that his intoxication caused the injury.</li>\n<li>At trial, the employer often must prove that the alcohol or drug was <strong>the cause</strong> of the injury, not just a contributing factor.</li>\n<li>There may be immunity in certain employment lawsuits if the employee tested positive or refused to give a sample.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>9.5 Liability (7.8.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A poorly managed program can end in costly lawsuits.</li>\n<li>If the company's own employees collect the urine, it must be done reasonably and according to proper procedures.</li>\n<li><strong>Chain of custody:</strong> establishes who handled the sample from the time it was submitted until there was a result. If it is broken, the result is invalid.</li>\n<li>Poor record keeping allows the chain of custody to be challenged and puts the validity of the result at risk.</li>\n<li><strong>The greatest risk is borne by employers who collect their own samples.</strong> Without strict procedures and well-controlled records, they cannot defend against broken-chain claims.</li>\n<li>Consequence: they lose the result and can end up in court.</li>\n<li>Benefits of testing: more safety and fewer injuries. Requirements: sound written policies and keeping up with changes in rules and regulations.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-7-e9\">Key Terms</h3><p>drug · drugs of abuse · psychoactive · chemical dependency · drug-free workplace · rationalization · opportunity · cut · fronting · pinch · pinching · Controlled Substances Act (CSA) · Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) · Schedule I · Schedule II · Schedule III · Schedule IV · Schedule V · depressants · benzodiazepines · barbiturates · alcoholism · craving · loss of control · physical dependence · tolerance · narcotics · opiates · opioids · withdrawal · stimulants · crack · bingeing · methamphetamine · formication · hallucinogens · synaesthesia · flashbacks · THC · reverse tolerance · hashish · hemp flower · safety-sensitive positions · zero-tolerance policy · under-the-influence policy · analogue · designer drugs · prescription drugs · addiction · high-functioning abusers · denial · enabling · codependency · on-the-job impairment · employee hotlines · Sarbanes-Oxley Act · whistleblowers · intervention · documented progressive discipline · employee assistance program (EAP) · occupational alcoholism programs · management referral · Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) · reasonable accommodation · clearinghouse · role modeling · Drug-Free Workplace Act of 1988 · metabolite · confirmatory test · medical review officer (MRO) · NIDA · reasonable suspicion · for-cause testing · random testing · return to duty · split specimen · breath alcohol technicians · HHS · CAP · chain of custody · workers' compensation</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. Basic Concepts and Cost to the Employer"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Human Cost of Abuse"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. The Employer's Role"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Drug Use in the Workplace"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. The Path of Abuse at Work"},{"id":"e5","t":"6. Drugs of Abuse"},{"id":"e6","t":"7. Addiction and Chemical Dependency"},{"id":"e7","t":"8. Role of Supervisors and Managers"},{"id":"e8","t":"9. Drug Testing"},{"id":"e9","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"6. Drugs of Abuse","h":"the law's actual name is the <em>Comprehensive </em><em>Drug</em><em> Abuse Prevention and Control Act of 1970</em>; its Title II is the <em>Controlled Substances Act</em> (CSA)."},{"s":"6. Drugs of Abuse","h":"codeine alone is in Schedule II. Schedule III covers combination products with little codeine, such as Tylenol with codeine."},{"s":"8. Role of Supervisors and Managers","h":"internal contradiction. In 7.5.5 the book proposes an <em>under-the-influence</em> policy for marijuana; in 7.7.1 it says the policy should avoid that term."},{"s":"8. Role of Supervisors and Managers","h":"Section 301 of Sarbanes-Oxley requires anonymous, confidential submission for <strong>employees</strong>. Third-party complaints must be received, retained, and treated, but the law does not require anonymity for them."},{"s":"9. Drug Testing","h":"the book calls NIDA the \"National Institute on Substance Abuse\"; its actual name is the <em>National Institute on Drug Abuse</em>."},{"s":"9. Drug Testing","h":"since 2000, testing of commercial drivers has been regulated by the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration (FMCSA), created out of the Federal Highway Administration."},{"s":"9. Drug Testing","h":"the actual name of the CAP is the <em>College of American Pathologists</em>."}]},"personnel-8":{"html":"<h3 id=\"personnel-8-e0\">1. History (8.1)</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Milestone</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1970</td>\n<td>General Duty Clause of the OSHA law, Section 5(a): each employer must provide its employees with employment and a place of employment free from recognized hazards that cause or are likely to cause death or serious physical harm. It is the basis of the 1998 document.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1986</td>\n<td>Interest grows in the U.S. after the murder of 14 U.S. Postal Service employees by another employee, who then killed himself.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1998</td>\n<td>The Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) publishes <em>Recommendations for Workplace Violence Programs in Late-Night Retail Establishments</em>. It is one of the first concrete federal efforts.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2004</td>\n<td>OSHA publishes another guideline, for health care and social service workers, with similar safety recommendations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2009</td>\n<td>The 1998 document is updated with an expanded study and new recommendations for late-night retail.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2011</td>\n<td>The ASIS standard on workplace violence prevention is created and approved.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>The standard is revised and renamed <em>Workplace Violence and Active Assailant—Prevention, Intervention, and Response</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the actual title of the 1998 OSHA document is <em>Recommendations for Workplace Violence </em><em>Prevention</em><em> Programs in Late-Night Retail Establishments</em>. The book omits the word <em>Prevention</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>The 1998 OSHA document:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Used data from the Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS).</li>\n<li>Also used WPV tracking from the National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH).</li>\n<li>Considered WPV a serious threat, risk, and hazard to employee health and safety. Especially in occupations that operate at night and in the early morning hours.</li>\n<li>Identified high-risk industries and occupations.</li>\n<li>Presented a detailed safety analysis for developing a mitigation and prevention program, with recommendations for security measures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Figures (OSHA and BLS):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every year, almost 2 million workers are victims of WPV.</li>\n<li>Every year there are about 450 workplace homicides in the U.S.</li>\n<li>In 2018 there were 453 workplace homicides (Bureau of Labor Statistics 2019):</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Perpetrator of the homicide (2018)</th>\n<th>Number</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Robbers</td>\n<td>106</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coworkers or work associates</td>\n<td>77</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customers</td>\n<td>39</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relatives or domestic partners</td>\n<td>33</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acquaintances</td>\n<td>18</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>These categories add up to 273; the book does not break down the remaining 180.</p>\n<p><strong>Role of the security professional:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Their leadership and experience have brought WPV to the center of organizations' thinking.</li>\n<li>They have gotten other departments (human resources, legal) to cooperate on solutions integrated into the overall security program.</li>\n<li>Security is the focal point for obtaining and documenting information about possible WPV incidents.</li>\n<li>In most cases, security will be the first responder to an incident.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The ASIS standard (2020):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Gives guidance and best practices for creating or improving prevention programs.</li>\n<li>Failing to meet those best practices can create legal liability for the organization, with costly, slow, and reputation-damaging outcomes.</li>\n<li>State and local laws, government regulations, and contractual obligations may also apply.</li>\n<li>Even with a reasonable program of deterrence, prevention, and mitigation, injuries and deaths can occur. No organization is exempt or can entirely prevent WPV.</li>\n<li>It calls for a holistic, strategic approach to four phases: prevention, intervention, response, and aftermath.</li>\n<li>That approach makes use of internal resources and information, above all supervisors and concerned employees.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e1\">2. Sources and Types of WPV (8.2)</h3><ul>\n<li>WPV covers any threatening or disruptive behavior:<ul>\n<li>Overt acts of violence (homicide, robbery, physical assault).</li>\n<li>Threats of those acts.</li>\n<li>Other physical or verbal conduct that creates a reasonable concern of violence against employees (harassment, intimidation, bullying).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Definition (ASIS standard):</strong> a spectrum of behaviors—overt acts of violence, threats, and other conduct—that generates a reasonable concern for safety from violence. There must be a nexus between the behavior and the physical safety of employees, from any internal or external relationship.</li>\n<li>Threats and their risk can come from internal or external sources.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Employer's obligation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>It must provide a safe workplace for employees, contractors, visitors, and guests on its premises.</li>\n<li>In general, that obligation does not distinguish between internal and external sources of danger.</li>\n<li>If the employer or its representative has reasonable cause to believe someone may commit violence, it must protect the potential victim.</li>\n<li>It applies to acts on its premises and against an employee acting within the scope of his duties elsewhere.</li>\n<li>It does not matter who the aggressor is: employee, spouse or partner of an employee, shareholder, contractor, supplier, vendor, customer, guest, or other third party.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Consequences:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most serious: loss of life and injuries, physical and mental.</li>\n<li>For the business: legal liability, productivity, morale, reputation, and associated costs.</li>\n<li>The business's main concern may be the cost of being found liable for negligence.</li>\n<li>Hundreds of thousands of dollars per incident can be lost in work group productivity. Causes: absenteeism, sick leave, slower work, distraction of executives and workers, and general disorder.</li>\n<li>Other costs: critical incident care, higher workers' compensation and health insurance premiums, turnover expenses, and shareholder value (Sheely 2019).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.1 Risk Assessment (8.2.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every organization has a unique profile.</li>\n<li>The foundation of every WPV program is the security risk assessment, also called a needs assessment.</li>\n<li>It examines and evaluates the types of threats, the vulnerabilities, and the consequences.</li>\n<li>It also reviews whether the organization is prepared to respond to concerning behaviors and incidents.</li>\n<li>Internal and external threat sources (ASIS International 2020):<ul>\n<li>Current and former employees.</li>\n<li>Customers.</li>\n<li>Vendors.</li>\n<li>Contractors and others who work with the organization or on its behalf.</li>\n<li>Friends, relatives, and intimate partners.</li>\n<li>Visitors and guests.</li>\n<li>People opposed to the organization's mission, goals, or objectives.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Types of offenders</strong> (Penfield 2017). They are classified by their relationship to the workplace, in order to design measures according to that status.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Relationship to the organization</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Type 1</td>\n<td>No legitimate relationship; criminal intent</td>\n<td>Robbers, trespassers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type 2</td>\n<td>Legitimate relationship with the organization</td>\n<td>Customer, patient, visitor, service provider (delivery person)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type 3</td>\n<td>Current or former employee, including contractors</td>\n<td>Employee or former employee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type 4</td>\n<td>May or may not have a relationship with the organization, but has a personal relationship, real or perceived, with an employee</td>\n<td>Domestic partner, ex-spouse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type 5</td>\n<td>Ideological, political, or religious view opposed to the organization</td>\n<td>Extremists, terrorists</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The elements of the workplace violence prevention and intervention (WVPI) program are an integral part of the overall security program.</li>\n<li>The assessment must consider each type of offender when planning, developing, designing, implementing, overseeing, maintaining, and evaluating the solutions.</li>\n<li>The vulnerabilities detected are addressed effectively and reasonably.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Workplace culture.</strong> A negative culture contributes to WPV through:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Policies and procedures that are unfair to employees.</li>\n<li>Lack of adequate staff, including trained and motivated security personnel.</li>\n<li>Poor management practices.</li>\n<li>Reorganizations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These conditions generate frustration, overwork, complaints, and stress. If executive management does not recognize and address them, the risk of an incident rises.</p>\n<p><strong>Preparedness for response.</strong> The assessment reviews current prevention and intervention practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Existing policies and procedures.</li>\n<li>Behavior expectations and their consequences.</li>\n<li>WPV prevention training.</li>\n<li>Reporting protocols.</li>\n<li>Partnerships with outside organizations, such as the police and mental health groups.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Security vulnerabilities.</strong> A general threat assessment may reveal other factors:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lax access control: any offender enters easily and threatens workers.</li>\n<li>Alcohol consumption at a bar or restaurant: encourages aggressive behavior by customers.</li>\n<li>Facilities in high-crime areas: exposed to random or targeted crimes.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e2\">3. WPV Warning Signs (8.3)</h3><ul>\n<li>There is no exact profile of the offender.</li>\n<li>Recognizing the warning signs, called “red flags,” is key to preventing and mitigating. They are problem behaviors that precede violence.</li>\n<li>Most WPV events are preceded by stressors, precursors, and indicators.</li>\n<li>Sixteen examples of concerning behaviors in employees or other related persons:<ul>\n<li>Sudden and severe mood swings.</li>\n<li>Signs of depression.</li>\n<li>More anger or tantrums.</li>\n<li>Increased alcohol and drug use.</li>\n<li>Social isolation.</li>\n<li>Threats, implied or overt.</li>\n<li>Change in appearance or neglect of hygiene.</li>\n<li>Growing, unexplained absenteeism.</li>\n<li>Decreased work output.</li>\n<li>Arguments with coworkers and supervisors.</li>\n<li>Destruction, theft, or sabotage of property.</li>\n<li>Suicidal ideas.</li>\n<li>Increased interest in weapons, firearms, or violence.</li>\n<li>Bullying or workplace harassment.</li>\n<li>Changes in domestic relationships.</li>\n<li>Changes in life insurance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Employees must know these signs and know exactly how to report them.</li>\n<li>They must report immediately: early identification greatly improves prevention.</li>\n<li>Prevention depends on prompt reporting, whether the employee is the offender or the victim.</li>\n<li>Channels: directly to security, to human resources, to a supervisor, or to another employee. Also anonymous reporting procedures or confidential hotlines.</li>\n<li>Security and human resources have the most impact and involvement in the entire WVPI program.</li>\n<li>Even so, all employees must be involved. They are a vital source of information, above all of warning signs, and can prevent an incident.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e3\">4. Developing a WVPI Program (8.4)</h3><ul>\n<li>The WVPI program reflects the components of the organization's security program.</li>\n<li>Its risk assessment considers the overall threat landscape and prior incidents in the organization and in similar organizations.</li>\n<li>It also considers general information on current WPV trends and cases.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.1 Organizational Commitment and Policy (8.4.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Most important step at the start: create a policy backed by executive management.</li>\n<li>That backing spreads throughout the organization, so that no one doubts it is a serious priority.</li>\n<li>The WPV policy must be issued directly from the office of the chief executive officer (CEO).</li>\n<li>Executive management must provide the necessary resources, funds, and authorizations.</li>\n<li>The policy is communicated at hiring and throughout employment.</li>\n<li>It states the employer's commitment to a safe workplace. It describes a code of conduct that prohibits all violence, threats, and conduct interpretable as intent to harm (ASIS International 2020).</li>\n<li>The policy must:<ul>\n<li>Define the unacceptable conduct it prohibits.</li>\n<li>Regulate or prohibit weapons, to the extent the law permits.</li>\n<li>Require prompt reporting of any conduct or circumstance that raises a safety concern or violates the policy.</li>\n<li>Offer several reporting channels.</li>\n<li>Ensure that reports will be treated seriously and with discretion.</li>\n<li>Commit to no retaliation against anyone who reports in good faith.</li>\n<li>Require employees to report protective or restraining orders that are sought, requested, or issued to protect them.</li>\n<li>Impose disciplinary sanctions for violating the policy, including false or misleading reports.</li>\n<li>Avoid the term “zero tolerance,” because it reduces reporting and lowers safety.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Employee participation from the start is as important as that of executive management. That way the program considers their needs and gains their support, cooperation, and commitment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Evolution of the teams</strong> (the key players vary by industry):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>WVPI policy working group (WG): multidisciplinary and broad. It brings together the security, human resources, legal, health and safety, bargaining, employee assistance, crisis management, risk management, and public relations units.</li>\n<li>Stakeholders working group (SWG): smaller.</li>\n<li>Threat management team (TMT): permanent and long-term. It assesses and responds to violent incidents or to reports of behaviors covered by the policy. It also oversees the WVPI program.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>The TMT is also called the threat assessment team (TAT) or incident management team (IMT). The three acronyms are interchangeable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Role of security personnel</strong> (fundamental in the program). Tasks the organization may delegate to them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Conduct surveys, audits, and security risk assessments.</li>\n<li>Recommend additional security measures.</li>\n<li>Develop policies and procedures.</li>\n<li>Train employees in the WVPI.</li>\n<li>Respond to, investigate, and report WPV complaints or incidents.</li>\n<li>Coordinate with local police and emergency services.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2 WVPI Planning (8.4.2)</h4>\n<p>Before formulating the plan, these steps must be done:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify threats and risks.</li>\n<li>Obtain initial authorization from executive management.</li>\n<li>Receive employee input.</li>\n<li>Disseminate the WPV policy.</li>\n<li>Form the stakeholders working group (SWG).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With the risk assessment as the basis, the WVPI allocates four security resources to prevent or mitigate an event:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>What is reviewed and how</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Physical, electronic, and design security</td>\n<td>Thorough survey, review, and audit of the measures at all locations. Done by security and/or an outside consultant. The consultant must have the education, training, experience, knowledge, skills, competencies, and industry-recognized certifications.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security policies, procedures, and protocols</td>\n<td>Current WPV practices, with further input from the SWG, above all from human resources and legal. Changes take operations into account and avoid delaying operational goals. Everything is written in detail, clearly defined, and widely communicated. Employee roles are delimited and explained, with initial and ongoing training.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security personnel</td>\n<td>Competence to deter, detect, delay, deny, and respond to an incident and its aftermath. Recruitment, selection, screening, training, equipment, supervision, evaluation, staffing, and deployment are reviewed. If training or staff is lacking, the organization must provide it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security and crime prevention awareness, education, and training</td>\n<td>Covers the entire organization, from contract staff to executive management, at the start and on an ongoing basis. That for security personnel is more specialized and complete. Includes periodic drills and exercises with police and other emergency services (Appendix 8B: sample scenario).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>The SWG evaluates the threats and their risk against current practices. In this way it decides on improvements and closes the vulnerability gaps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book says the physical security audit is done by security or an outside consultant “as mentioned earlier.” But the chapter had not mentioned it earlier.</p>\n<h4>4.3 Threat Management Team (TMT) (8.4.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>After disseminating the policy and completing the mitigation strategies, the SWG's experience is used to form the TMT.</li>\n<li>The TMT can rely on outside experts to complement its knowledge.</li>\n<li>It provides ongoing assessment and support to meet the policy's goals. It helps implement the prevention and response components.</li>\n<li>Areas the TMT handles:<ul>\n<li>Create incident management protocols to assess, investigate, handle, and resolve reports.</li>\n<li>Create emergency protocols for threats of imminent violence.</li>\n<li>Train all employees and levels of management and supervision, with emphasis on warning signs and immediate reporting.</li>\n<li>Initiate anonymous reporting methods.</li>\n<li>Create a confidential records system that tracks the reports received and the actions taken.</li>\n<li>Create a thorough protocol for preemployment screening and background investigation for all employees and contractors. It includes references, criminal records, drug and alcohol use, and social media searches, within legal limits.</li>\n<li>Use physical, electronic, and design measures: access, entry, and visitor control; barriers; signage; lighting; locks; intrusion and duress alarms; video surveillance.</li>\n<li>Create a conflict resolution and mediation process for workplace issues. It includes training to prevent, nonviolently de-escalate, defuse, and resolve volatile situations and aggressive behaviors.</li>\n<li>Provide information and assistance to employees involved in WPV incidents.</li>\n<li>Create a code of conduct with clear expectations of ethical behavior.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED) concepts that guide the selection, installation, and use of the measures:<ul>\n<li>Natural surveillance and access control.</li>\n<li>Territorial reinforcement.</li>\n<li>Maintenance.</li>\n<li>Target hardening.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e4\">5. WVPI Protocols (8.5)</h3><ul>\n<li>Specific procedures are needed to treat all parties fairly and to comply with the policy and external regulation.</li>\n<li>It is important to consult the legal department when developing them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1 Notification (8.5.1)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Notification can come from inside or outside the company.</li>\n<li>The company needs policy, procedures, and training that direct reports to a specific person or group.</li>\n<li>That recipient does the initial intake and the first assessment of immediate risk.</li>\n<li>However it arrives (for example: observation, e-mail, postal mail, phone call, text message, or fax), everyone must pass it immediately to the person responsible.</li>\n<li>“Everyone” includes operators, receptionists, managers, supervisors, customer service representatives, and other employees.</li>\n<li>Recipients may need to be available 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, and be trained.</li>\n<li>Violence can escalate if management does not respond to the first warning signs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.2 Assessment (8.5.2)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Goal of every threat assessment: identify the threat and the mitigation avenues, for example judicial or mental health.</li>\n<li>Several levels are possible. They depend on whether the offender is known or unknown, and on the quantity and quality of the initial information.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Offender</th>\n<th>What can be done</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown (e.g., anonymous communications)</td>\n<td>A valid violence risk assessment cannot be made. A preliminary behavioral analysis, of low validity, is possible. An investigation is conducted to identify him; the organization's response is governed by other policies or procedures.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Known, at least by name</td>\n<td>Can proceed with a three-level assessment.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Three levels of assessment of the known subject.</strong> Each level is done by one or more TMT members. They seek to decide which resources to use and to what extent.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>What follows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Initial assessment</td>\n<td>Is there immediate risk of harm?</td>\n<td>If there is a significant possibility of immediate harm: emergency procedures until the situation stabilizes. If not: move to the threshold level.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Threshold assessment</td>\n<td>Is further assessment needed, according to the risk thresholds set by the company, or is monitoring enough?</td>\n<td>If the predetermined threshold is reached: comprehensive assessment. If not: monitoring only.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Comprehensive assessment</td>\n<td>Gathers additional information from internal and external sources</td>\n<td>Serves as the basis for designing and applying a nonimmediate emergency resolution plan.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the book announces that the three levels are “explained in more detail below.” But the chapter does not develop them again.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Threat intelligence: investigation, protection, social media monitoring, and tracking and analysis of trends in reports and suspicious activity.</li>\n<li>It gives better results when working with community partners (Breslin 2019).</li>\n<li>More liaison with police, mental health, and education, locally and over a wider geographic area, lets the TMT take a holistic approach to the threat.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.3 Intervention and Nonemergency Situational Resolution (8.5.3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Main goal: the safety of the identified target or targets, in the short and, ideally, long term.</li>\n<li>It seeks to divert the offender from aggression toward socially acceptable behaviors that resolve his perceived need for control.</li>\n<li>Primary consideration: first, do no harm to either the target or the offender.</li>\n<li>It is achieved when the offender voluntarily chooses to abandon the concerning behaviors.</li>\n<li>An involuntary resolution will probably be temporary, unless the offender dies.</li>\n<li>That is why restraining or protective orders, arrest, and criminal or psychiatric confinement are only short-term stabilizing interventions.</li>\n<li>If the offender's attitude toward the target does not change, those measures will eventually fail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Direct stabilizing intervention options</strong> (Cawood 2020):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interviews by the investigator, both to assess and to intervene.</li>\n<li>Administrative or disciplinary actions, including dismissal.</li>\n<li>Cease-and-desist requests.</li>\n<li>No-trespass, restraining, or protective orders.</li>\n<li>Voluntary or involuntary mental health evaluations.</li>\n<li>Contact with the police, with or without arrest.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>How to choose and apply:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The choice depends on the offender's likely reaction and on whether the intervention can correct his perception of the target.</li>\n<li>Any communication or interaction with the offender, direct or indirect (through third parties), counts as intervention.</li>\n<li>The interview with the offender informs about his perception, emotional and cognitive level, impulsivity, and limits.</li>\n<li>It also lets one reframe his goals, raise alternative behaviors, discuss cause and effect and consequences, and look for other solutions.</li>\n<li>Restraining and protective orders create a boundary. They are only worth anything if the target reports violations and they are enforced promptly.</li>\n<li>One often starts with one technique while the TMT prepares others.</li>\n<li>Example: before interviewing the offender, the text and affidavits of a restraining order are drafted.</li>\n<li>A disciplinary reprimand or a dismissal package is also prepared.</li>\n<li>And the police are contacted to find out what crimes the conduct could constitute, how to report, and what response they might give.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.4 Monitoring (8.5.4)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Monitoring new behavior is a critical part of violence risk assessment.</li>\n<li>It creates a behavioral feedback loop. It allows the assessment to be updated, the value of interventions to be tested, and the final resolution to be determined.</li>\n<li>The TMT can set up passive or active monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Passive monitoring</th>\n<th>Active monitoring</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How it works</td>\n<td>The target and other possible witnesses report new behavior to the TMT in a timely way</td>\n<td>The assessor actively seeks new behavioral information; the higher the risk, the more frequent the contacts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When it applies</td>\n<td>Only in low-risk cases, where a delay in reporting would not create a significant risk of harm</td>\n<td>Moderate to high risk, or when the target or witnesses cannot be relied on to report</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Book case</td>\n<td>The victim receives a single anonymous e-mail or voice message of the “I hate you and you'll pay for this” type. If the investigation finds no further grounds for concern, one “waits and sees” and the victim is asked to report new contacts.</td>\n<td>Domestic violence risk: the offender calls the target at work, threatens to make her pay and to come confront her. The target says there is no danger. But the offender has a history of domestic violence, including a confrontation at a former partner's workplace.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Reasons the target or witnesses do not report well:<ul>\n<li>Shock, denial, rationalization, minimization, or other psychological defense mechanisms.</li>\n<li>Fear of retaliation or revenge.</li>\n<li>Mistakenly believing the target can handle it without help.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>In the domestic case, the target may hide or minimize contacts. Reasons: shame, fear of losing the job, or believing herself safe.</li>\n<li>What the TMT does in that case:<ul>\n<li>Identifies coworkers who might witness new contacts and who can be trusted to report them.</li>\n<li>Consults them several times a day.</li>\n<li>If they report contacts, asks the target for an update.</li>\n<li>If the target denies the contact, tries to reduce her resistance to reporting.</li>\n<li>Raises or lowers the frequency of monitoring according to the current risk of imminent violence.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5 Review and Debriefing (8.5.5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Incident review is continuous, as new behavioral information arrives from any source.</li>\n<li>Cycle that repeats until the TMT, according to its standards, considers the situation resolved:<ol>\n<li>Reassessment.</li>\n<li>Review of intervention options.</li>\n<li>Application of the interventions.</li>\n<li>Monitoring for new behavioral signs.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>The review continually refines operational and tactical processes to provide the greatest safety.</li>\n<li>Debriefing and lessons learned are critical to incident management and process improvement. They give a strategic view of how an incident can improve the process on a larger scale.</li>\n<li>Some companies hold short debriefings after the first round of assessment and intervention.</li>\n<li>Afterward they hold monthly, quarterly, semiannual, or annual debriefings to update cases and discuss improvements.</li>\n<li>Reviews, debriefings, or a mix of both allow continuous improvement of the case and of the overall process.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e5\">6. Implementing the WVPI Program (8.6)</h3><ul>\n<li>It is planned carefully and involves all TMT members.</li>\n<li>It proceeds step by step; the steps can happen at the same time or separately.</li>\n</ul>\n<ol>\n<li>Designate a specific group, with particular TMT members, to develop and implement the program.</li>\n<li>Define an implementation plan guided by legal requirements, standards, and the practices of similar organizations. In this step the requirements and recommendations of the ASIS standard are consulted.</li>\n<li>Establish the incident management protocols.</li>\n<li>Disseminate the policy to all employees and contractors by various means: onboarding, posters, mailings, e-mails, orientations, organization meetings, and training programs.</li>\n<li>Provide documented, initial and ongoing training to all employees and groups that work with the organization. Topics:\n   - Basic information on workplace violence.\n   - Specific areas of the WVPI policy.\n   - Warning signs of potential violent behavior.\n   - Responses to the signs and related emergency actions.\n   - Critical importance of reporting concerning behaviors immediately.\n   - Thorough training for all TMT members in all aspects of workplace violence, delivered by qualified experts.\n   - Specific training for managers and supervisors in identifying and reporting observed behaviors or information received.</li>\n<li>Continuously monitor, evaluate, review, and improve the policy and the program, in addition to a formal annual review.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Once under way, the program is maintained with open communication, training, and partnerships.</li>\n<li>Upon receiving a report, the TMT immediately activates the incident management process, because the potential for violence can grow quickly.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e6\">7. Special Cases (8.7)</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Case</th>\n<th>What to do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Intimate partner violence (8.7.1)</td>\n<td>If an employee is in an abusive relationship, the violence can carry over to the workplace and become the organization's problem. The TMT makes it easier for her to report and responds to her concerns. Possible responses: limit the alleged offender's access to the workplace; escort the employee to her car; change her work e-mail and phone; move her to a secure workplace; refer her to outside personal safety resources.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Employee with behavioral or mental disorders (8.7.2)</td>\n<td>May appear to be a risk to others. Consult an attorney about the employer's obligations: certain regulations protect employees with some disabilities, and there are privacy issues. TMT options: encourage him to seek medical care; perhaps grant medical leave; conduct a fitness-for-duty examination, if the organization requires it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Separation of employment (8.7.3)</td>\n<td>When ending the employment or business relationship with a person of concern, the separation must be respectful and safe. It must follow established practices and policies on risk of violence and have legal counsel. Short- and long-term security measures for the facility and employees may be advisable.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e7\">8. Response to Violence (8.8)</h3><ul>\n<li>When violence occurs there is no time to assess risks or to plan.</li>\n<li>The crisis management plan is developed and practiced in advance, so it can be executed smoothly.</li>\n<li>The TMT can create it with input from executive management, human resources, legal, and other key stakeholders.</li>\n<li>Areas to prepare for WPV or an active assailant. More detail in Annex A of the ASIS standard:<ul>\n<li>Roles, responsibilities, procedures, and training of key response personnel.</li>\n<li>Emergency alert, warning, and notification systems through multiple means of communication.</li>\n<li>Employee awareness, education, and training, with special attention to: warning signs, reporting procedures, and “Run. Hide. Fight.” protocols.</li>\n<li>Emergency equipment and materials for key personnel: portable radios, flashlights, keys and access cards, building and floor plans, and first aid supplies.</li>\n<li>Liaison with public safety agencies: police, fire, and ambulance.</li>\n<li>Choosing an emergency operations center (EOC) and an incident command center (ICC). They serve to direct, coordinate, and communicate with internal and external responders.</li>\n<li>Preserving the crime scene, when possible.</li>\n<li>Planning communication during and after the event with employees, families, and the media:</li>\n<li>A system to locate employees who may be in danger.</li>\n<li>Watching employees and others for signs of psychological trauma.</li>\n<li>Being able to contact the families of all employees for reunification and other assistance.</li>\n<li>A media area and a family notification and reunification area, safe and away from ongoing operations.</li>\n<li>Choosing and training a spokesperson to be the voice of the organization during and after the event.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Depending on the nature and severity of the incident, the TMT activates the crisis management plan.</li>\n<li>In life-threatening emergencies, such as an active assailant attack, the plan must include these actions:<ul>\n<li>Alert others in danger.</li>\n<li>Call the police immediately.</li>\n<li>Cooperate with and assist the police.</li>\n<li>Evacuate or shelter in place.</li>\n<li>Keep others from entering the danger zone.</li>\n<li>Defend against the assailant, if possible.</li>\n<li>Account for employees and others present to know their status and whether they need help.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e8\">9. Post-Incident Recovery and Business Continuity (8.9)</h3><ul>\n<li>Once the crisis and response phase is over, the business recovery and business continuity plans are activated.</li>\n<li>They cover immediate, short-, and long-term timeframes.</li>\n<li>Depending on the nature and extent of the incident (especially with major property damage, injuries, or deaths), the recovery plan considers:<ul>\n<li>Priority: preserve life, protect essential assets (including reputation and image), and preserve evidence. Life safety is always the highest priority.</li>\n<li>As soon as possible and safe: gather the facts, secure the area and the evidence, separate the witnesses, and keep watching to preserve lives and prevent another incident.</li>\n<li>Coordinate the TMT with the crisis, recovery, and continuity teams.</li>\n<li>Notify managers, employees, family members, regulatory agencies, and the media.</li>\n<li>Maintain communication with everyone affected by as many means as is fitting: telephone, text, e-mail, social media, public address, digital displays, TV and radio, and press conferences. It is the main role of public relations.</li>\n<li>Coordinate recovery assistance, mental health services, police, legal counsel, families, and other critical needs.</li>\n<li>Resume operations when it is safe and appropriate.</li>\n<li>Rebuild relationships with customers, vendors, and the public, as the incident requires.</li>\n<li>Keep evaluating plans and responses to improve and draw lessons learned.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e9\">10. Chapter Closing Thoughts (8.10)</h3><ul>\n<li>The organization must provide a safe environment, free of reasonably foreseeable threats. The entire security program revolves around this principle, and the WVPI is a vital part of it.</li>\n<li>Most WPV affects one or a few people.</li>\n<li>Incidents with no death or serious injury are probably underreported.</li>\n<li>Not even the best program can prevent all crimes or WPV events.</li>\n<li>Serious incidents are rare. But a single one can cause deaths, disabling injuries, and trauma for the company and the community, with effects lasting years.</li>\n</ul><h3 id=\"personnel-8-e10\">Key Terms</h3><p>workplace violence (WPV) · General Duty Clause · Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA) · Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) · National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health (NIOSH) · Workplace Violence and Active Assailant—Prevention, Intervention, and Response standard · prevention, intervention, response, aftermath · first responder · security risk assessment · needs assessment · internal and external sources · Type 1 to Type 5 offender · workplace violence prevention and intervention (WVPI) program · workplace culture · preparedness for response · warning signs · red flags · stressors, precursors and indicators · anonymous reporting · hotline · zero tolerance · nonretaliation · good faith report · protective order · restraining order · policy working group (WG) · stakeholders working group (SWG) · threat management team (TMT) · threat assessment team (TAT) · incident management team (IMT) · deter, detect, delay, deny, respond · crime prevention through environmental design (CPTED) · natural surveillance and access control · territorial reinforcement · maintenance · target hardening · duress alarm · de-escalation · notification · threat assessment · violence risk assessment · initial assessment · threshold assessment · comprehensive assessment · nonimmediate emergency resolution plan · threat intelligence · intervention and nonemergency situational resolution · cease-and-desist request · no-trespass order · fitness-for-duty examination · passive monitoring · active monitoring · behavioral feedback loop · review and debriefing · intimate partner violence · separation of employment · crisis management plan · active assailant · Run. Hide. Fight. · emergency operations center (EOC) · incident command center (ICC) · shelter in place · business recovery · business continuity · life safety</p>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"1. History"},{"id":"e1","t":"2. Sources and Types of WPV"},{"id":"e2","t":"3. WPV Warning Signs"},{"id":"e3","t":"4. Developing a WVPI Program"},{"id":"e4","t":"5. WVPI Protocols"},{"id":"e5","t":"6. Implementing the WVPI Program"},{"id":"e6","t":"7. Special Cases"},{"id":"e7","t":"8. Response to Violence"},{"id":"e8","t":"9. Post-Incident Recovery and Business Continuity"},{"id":"e9","t":"10. Chapter Closing Thoughts"},{"id":"e10","t":"Key Terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"1. History","h":"the actual title of the 1998 OSHA document is <em>Recommendations for Workplace Violence </em><em>Prevention</em><em> Programs in Late-Night Retail Establishments</em>. The book omits the word <em>Prevention</em>."},{"s":"4. Developing a WVPI Program","h":"the book says the physical security audit is done by security or an outside consultant “as mentioned earlier.” But the chapter had not mentioned it earlier."},{"s":"5. WVPI Protocols","h":"the book announces that the three levels are “explained in more detail below.” But the chapter does not develop them again."}]},"personnel-A":{"html":"<p>The appendices to chapters 1, 3, and 4 are practical models: disciplinary policy, scope of service, orders, contract with the security company, and documents for hiring consultants. Their complete lists, deadlines, figures, and clauses are collected here.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e0\">Appendix 1A — Sample Disciplinary Policy (pp. 62–67)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a model disciplinary policy for a fictitious security company (“XYZ Company”). It lists the infractions, their consequences, and the criteria for sanctioning fairly.</p>\n<h4>I. Policy (approach)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The company demands high performance and good conduct: reliability, honesty, and trustworthiness.</li>\n<li>Discipline <strong>is not punishment</strong>. It is a means of improving performance through corrective counseling.</li>\n<li>That corrective approach is maintained until the conduct reaches the level of statutory misconduct.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>II.A. Criteria for Deciding the Sanction (7)</h4>\n<p>The approach is “strict but fair and reasonable.” Sanctions, up to discharge for willful misconduct, are based on:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The seriousness of the situation.</li>\n<li>The consequences of the act to the employer's detriment.</li>\n<li>Mitigating or aggravating circumstances.</li>\n<li>The employee's attitude.</li>\n<li>His disciplinary history and prior warnings.</li>\n<li>Similar prior cases.</li>\n<li>The welfare of the organization and of coworkers.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Forms of discipline (8):</strong> oral reprimand; written warning; unfavorable performance evaluation; probation; suspension without pay; demotion; reassignment; discharge for willful misconduct.</p>\n<p>The seriousness and the measure are decided, at its discretion, by the <strong>management disciplinary committee</strong>.</p>\n<h4>II.B. Who Applies Discipline and the Supervisor's Role</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Every hearing and disciplinary action is conducted by <strong>two or more</strong> members of executive management, always in accordance with this policy.</li>\n<li>Supervisor's role (5):<ol>\n<li>Know and understand all company policies and guidelines.</li>\n<li>Report every infraction to management using the <em>Employee Warning Notice</em> form. It must include all details, the interim measures already taken, and the necessary follow-up.</li>\n<li>Investigate the facts of the observed or reported infraction.</li>\n<li>Recommend the seriousness of the infraction and the appropriate corrective measure.</li>\n<li>Apply minor corrective measures and emergency measures in serious situations that call for immediate remedy. Examples: sending home an officer under the influence of alcohol; reprimanding an officer who is not wearing the uniform.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>II.C. Legal Definitions (aligned with state regulation)</h4>\n<p>The text refers to the employee as the “claimant.”</p>\n<p><strong>(1) Misconduct</strong> includes, but is not limited to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Willful or wanton disregard of the rights, title, and interests of the employer or a coworker.</li>\n<li>b) Deliberate violation or disregard of the standards of behavior that the employer has a right to expect.</li>\n<li>c) Carelessness or negligence that causes, or would probably cause, serious bodily harm to the employer or a coworker.</li>\n<li>d) Carelessness or negligence of such degree or recurrence that it shows intentional or substantial disregard of the employer's interest.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(2) Acts considered misconduct</strong>, because they involve willful or wanton disregard:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Insubordination: deliberate refusal to follow the employer's reasonable instructions.</li>\n<li>b) Repeated and unjustifiable tardiness after warnings.</li>\n<li>c) Job dishonesty: falsifying company records, theft, deliberate deception or lying.</li>\n<li>d) Repeated and unjustifiable absences, including those that could have been announced in advance and were not.</li>\n<li>e) Deliberate illegal acts, which provoke violence or violations of law, or which violate the collective bargaining agreement. Exception: lawful union activity cannot disqualify anyone for misconduct.</li>\n<li>f) Violating a company rule, if the rule is reasonable and the employee knew or should have known it.</li>\n<li>g) Violations of law committed within the scope of employment that substantially affect performance or substantially damage the employer's ability to do business.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(3) NOT misconduct:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>a) Inefficiency, unsatisfactory conduct, or poor performance due to inability or lack of aptitude.</li>\n<li>b) Ordinary carelessness or negligence in isolated cases.</li>\n<li>c) Errors of judgment or discretion made in good faith.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>(4) Gross misconduct</strong> is either of these two situations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A work-related criminal act for which the person was <strong>convicted</strong> in a criminal court, or which he <strong>admitted</strong> to having committed.</li>\n<li>Work-related conduct that shows a <strong>flagrant and wanton</strong> disregard of the rights, title, or interests of the employer or a coworker.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>II.D. The 37 Grounds for Sanction, up to Discharge</h4>\n<p>Breaking any of these rules is considered <strong>willful misconduct</strong>. Reason: it damages the relationship with clients, the reputation in the industry and in the community, the interests of the business, and the welfare of other officers.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Excessive tardiness or absences.</li>\n<li>Sleeping on duty.</li>\n<li>Any dishonesty, including falsifying company or client records, and the employment application.</li>\n<li>Watching television, using any multimedia device, or reading material unrelated to work, including use of a personal cell phone.</li>\n<li>Social media activity or political activism, if it violates company policy, during work hours and with company equipment.</li>\n<li>Accepting money, gifts, or other gratuities from the client's employees.</li>\n<li>Discourtesy, aggressiveness, vulgarity, or rudeness toward the client's representatives, employees, visitors, or customers, or toward the public. Above all, verbal or physical altercations on duty.</li>\n<li>Bringing any animal to the workplace.</li>\n<li>Returning to the post or visiting a site while off duty.</li>\n<li>Improper, incomplete, or dirty uniform.</li>\n<li>Bringing or allowing visits from friends, guests, or family members at the site.</li>\n<li>Taking a patrol vehicle home without written permission.</li>\n<li>Raising personal or company problems with the client, or trying to get the client to hire him.</li>\n<li>Reporting under the influence of drugs or alcohol; using or selling them on duty.</li>\n<li>Insubordination: refusing to follow lawful or legitimate instructions from a supervisor or manager.</li>\n<li>Failing to perform duties diligently, conscientiously, and to the best of his ability; or attending to personal matters on duty.</li>\n<li>Failing to carry the security officer license whenever on duty, and failing to maintain the ability to obtain it.</li>\n<li>Any damage to company or client property, by act or omission.</li>\n<li>Appropriating company or client property for personal use (conversion).</li>\n<li>Failing to abide by and enforce the client's rules firmly, fairly, and consistently.</li>\n<li>Unauthorized use of company or client equipment or supplies, including telephones, computers, and information.</li>\n<li>Smoking or eating in areas not designated for it.</li>\n<li>Violating the Post Instructions or the client's orders.</li>\n<li>Giving false testimony.</li>\n<li>Failing to cooperate in a criminal or company investigation.</li>\n<li>Carrying any unauthorized weapon (on the person, in a bag, or in a vehicle) on company or client property. Personal defense “weapons” require approval of the Company Chairman.</li>\n<li>Conspiring to commit a crime or allowing another to commit one; any criminal act, including downloading copyrighted material onto any device.</li>\n<li>Disclosing confidential information without authorization.</li>\n<li>Misuse or theft of company or client data.</li>\n<li>Unsafe practices or violation of safety rules.</li>\n<li>Failing to submit correct and timely reports, payroll time sheets, logs, alarm reports, or other required written documentation.</li>\n<li>Any unprofessional conduct that endangers the company's reputation, integrity, or interests, or the welfare of others.</li>\n<li>Excessive use of force or projecting a threatening image.</li>\n<li>Leaving the assigned post without relief or without properly authorized instruction.</li>\n<li>Repeated minor infractions after warnings.</li>\n<li>Violating laws or company rules necessary to operate safely or effectively.</li>\n<li>Working for another security company under a contract that competes with the company.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>II.E. The 17 Fairness Questions Before Sanctioning</h4>\n<p>The company seeks <strong>fundamental fairness</strong>. In every disciplinary case, these questions are asked:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the rule reasonable and necessary? Does it contribute to an orderly and efficient operation? Does it help meet goals? Does its violation harm the business, the relationship with clients, or the reputation?</li>\n<li>Does it relate to the performance the company can reasonably expect of an employee?</li>\n<li>Was it well communicated and distributed? Do employees know it and understand it?</li>\n<li>Has there been significant and legitimate disagreement from more than one employee?</li>\n<li>Has it been applied consistently?</li>\n<li>Have other employees been sanctioned for the same offense across the company?</li>\n<li>Was there a thorough and impartial investigation that proves the offense by a <strong>preponderance of the evidence</strong>?</li>\n<li>Were there relevant mitigating or exculpatory circumstances?</li>\n<li>How deliberate was the offense? Could the employee have believed the rule did not apply to that situation?</li>\n<li>What is the employee's attitude toward the offense?</li>\n<li>Did the company actively seek witnesses and evidence?</li>\n<li>Are the planned measures appropriate to the offense?</li>\n<li>Was it a crime under local, state, or federal law?</li>\n<li>Does the employee have prior offenses?</li>\n<li>What is his seniority?</li>\n<li>Will the sanction adversely affect the company's operation?</li>\n<li>How likely is it that the employee will make the necessary positive change?</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> section D declares any violation of the 37 rules <em>willful</em> misconduct. But C(3) excludes ordinary isolated negligence and good-faith errors. For example, rule 18 (“any damage… by act or omission”) clashes with that exclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> chapter 1 says that, with a union and a “just cause” dismissal clause, a clear statement is needed to sustain severe sanctions. This model provides it: corrective discipline, legal definitions of <em>misconduct</em> and <em>gross misconduct</em>, and a fairness test.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e1\">Appendix 1B — Company Fact Sheet on Employee Discipline (p. 68)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a simplified version of policy 1A, which the officer signs. It serves to verify that he knows the rules.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every organization (companies, sports teams, government) needs rules and policies. A security company even more so.</li>\n<li>Keys to success in security: organizational order, public safety, employee welfare, and respect for the employer's interests and rules.</li>\n<li>Goal: a fully professional, safe, productive, and satisfying workplace.</li>\n<li>Main focus: <strong>employee development</strong> (giving him the tools and removing obstacles).</li>\n<li>Discipline comes in only when the employee had a real opportunity to succeed and <strong>intentionally chose</strong> to violate rules, harming the employer or its relationship with clients.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Progressive discipline</strong> for most infractions, according to seriousness and mitigating factors. Possible measures:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Verbal counseling.</li>\n<li>Remedial training.</li>\n<li>Written note of corrective counseling.</li>\n<li>Suspension without pay.</li>\n<li>Demotion.</li>\n<li>Last and final warning.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Nonnegotiable infractions, which can cause immediate dismissal</strong> because the harm is irreparable (9):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Violations of the law.</li>\n<li>Dishonesty of any kind.</li>\n<li>Extreme negligence that causes physical harm to others.</li>\n<li>Post abandonment.</li>\n<li>Sleeping on duty.</li>\n<li>No-call no-shows.</li>\n<li>Repeated absences or tardiness.</li>\n<li>Insubordination.</li>\n<li>Intentional violation of any reasonable, necessary, and known work rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Closing:</strong> the officer is asked for full attention and his best effort; when in doubt, ask. Employee's signature with date and a witness's initials, as acknowledgment of understanding and agreement.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> chapter 1 presents it as the short version of 1A for checking that the officer knows the rules. The signature with a witness documents that the rule was communicated (question 3 of the 1A fairness test).</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e2\">Appendix 3A — Sample Scope of Work (p. 121)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a sample scope of work for a guard services contract. It defines tasks, posts, schedules, and total hours.</p>\n<p><strong>Tasks:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Routine fixed posts and foot patrols, according to the general and special orders.</li>\n<li><strong>Security control center</strong> officers: monitor fire and intrusion alarms, receive calls, keep key control, and perform administrative tasks.</li>\n<li>Roving or mobile patrol officers: may carry an electronic patrol recorder and a two-way radio.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Posts and weekly hours:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Post</th>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Schedule</th>\n<th>Days</th>\n<th>Hours/week</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 1 — Control center</td>\n<td>Supervisor</td>\n<td>0730–1600</td>\n<td>Monday through Friday</td>\n<td>42.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 1</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>0800–1600</td>\n<td>Saturday and Sunday</td>\n<td>16.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 1</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>1600–2400</td>\n<td>Monday through Sunday</td>\n<td>56.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 1</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>2400–0800</td>\n<td>Monday through Sunday</td>\n<td>56.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 2 — Roving/mobile patrol</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>0800–1600</td>\n<td>Saturday and Sunday</td>\n<td>16.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 2</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>1600–2400</td>\n<td>Saturday and Sunday</td>\n<td>16.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 2</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>1500–2400</td>\n<td>Monday through Friday</td>\n<td>45.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No. 2</td>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>2400–0800</td>\n<td>“Saturday through Sunday”</td>\n<td>56.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>303.5</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Subtotals (own calculation): post 1 = 170.5 h; post 2 = 133.0 h.</p>\n<p><strong>Officer complement:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Personnel</th>\n<th>Shifts/week</th>\n<th>Straight time (ST) hours</th>\n<th>Overtime (OT) hours</th>\n<th>Total</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 supervisor</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>40.0</td>\n<td>2.5</td>\n<td>42.5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6.5 officers</td>\n<td>32</td>\n<td>256.0</td>\n<td>5.0</td>\n<td>261.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td><strong>303.5</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Check: the supervisor's 8.5-hour shifts give 0.5 h of overtime daily (5 × 0.5 = 2.5). The 5 nine-hour shifts (1500–2400) give 5 h of overtime. And 32 shifts × 8 h = 256 h.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the last shift of post 2 says “Saturday through Sunday” but adds up to 56.0 h. Two nights would give 16 h; 56 h is 7 nights. The totals (303.5 h and 32 shifts) only add up with 7 nights: it seems to mean “Monday through Sunday.”</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the chapter 3 analysis example speaks of about 308 hours per week (about 16,000 per year). This appendix adds up to 303.5. The book does not say they are the same example: do not mix figures.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> according to chapter 3, the scope must state the tasks, the days and hours of service, and the total hours. It is given to all prospective contractors along with the orders and the proposed operating agreement.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e3\">Appendix 3B — Sample General and Special Orders (pp. 122–128)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a model set of orders for the contract security force assigned to a company. It summarizes the required conduct, duties, disciplinary grounds, and special orders.</p>\n<h4>1. Three Categories of Orders</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1.1 General orders</td>\n<td>General performance standards for <strong>all</strong> officers.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1.2 Special orders</td>\n<td>Duties of each specific assignment: routine tasks and prescribed courses of action.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1.3 Special instructions</td>\n<td>For special events (inspections, guided tours). Issued when needed, in the form of a memorandum.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>2. Personal Conduct</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>2.1 Responsibilities:</strong> honesty, alertness, loyalty, and intelligence.</li>\n<li><strong>2.2 Attitude:</strong> total interest in protecting all property. It is reinforced by thinking and acting in terms of security, protection, and prevention.</li>\n<li><strong>2.3 Habits:</strong> dignified conduct at all times. <strong>Smoking is prohibited</strong> on duty.</li>\n<li><strong>2.4 Discipline:</strong> prompt and willing obedience to the orders of the designated representative.</li>\n<li><strong>2.5 Appearance:</strong> personal cleanliness and neatness. Well-cut and groomed hair, clean nails and hands, presentable uniform.</li>\n<li><strong>2.6 Uniform and equipment:</strong> provided by the employer; each officer cares for and maintains them.<ul>\n<li>2.6.1 Uniform clean, in good condition, and pressed.</li>\n<li>2.6.2 Shoes well shined.</li>\n<li>2.6.3 Cap (if issued) worn at all times on duty and “straight”: neither tilted nor backward.</li>\n<li>2.6.4 Complete uniform and equipment on duty.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>2.7 Post cleanliness:</strong> no clothing, equipment, or personal items are stored. Loitering there is prohibited. Always clean and orderly.</li>\n<li><strong>2.8 Public relations:</strong> the officer is often the visitor's first contact. His impression influences the opinion of the company and must be very favorable.<ul>\n<li>Firm and decisive, but courteous with employees and visitors.</li>\n<li>Friendly greeting (“good morning,” “good evening”) to visitors, executives, and employees.</li>\n<li>Courtesy and tact with employees. <strong>Arguing</strong> with them <strong>is not tolerated</strong>.</li>\n<li>Every rule violation is reported immediately.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>2.9 Telephones:</strong> only for official business, never personal calls. When answering, identify himself by name and assignment. If the caller does not identify himself, ask for his name.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Duties</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>3.1 General duties:</strong> protect property and personnel, control traffic, and report rule violations.</li>\n<li><strong>3.2 Performance requires knowing (4):</strong> the general procedures; the special orders and instructions of the assignment; the physical layout of the facility; all the rules he must enforce.</li>\n<li><strong>3.3 Familiarization with the patrol area.</strong> Before the assignment he receives a general orientation. He must know (10):<ol>\n<li>Where key personnel work.</li>\n<li>Where the telephones are, including emergency ones.</li>\n<li>Where the fire alarm pull boxes are.</li>\n<li>Where all the stairways and doors lead.</li>\n<li>Where materials are stored.</li>\n<li>Where all the fire-fighting equipment is.</li>\n<li>Where the light switches are.</li>\n<li>The control rooms and the shutoff switches for air conditioning and ventilation.</li>\n<li>All the valves that control water to the sprinklers.</li>\n<li>Fuse boxes, power switches, steam valves, hydrants, and other machinery controls.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>3.4 Routine duties (5):</strong><ol>\n<li>Patrol the exterior of the buildings and the boundaries of the premises.</li>\n<li>Patrol the interior.</li>\n<li>Control the entry and movement of pedestrians and vehicles.</li>\n<li>Prevent theft and damage to company and employee property.</li>\n<li>Report all matters of security interest: theft and pilferage, fire and its prevention, espionage and sabotage, and rule violations.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>3.5 Patrolling:</strong> all critical areas are checked. Marking the electronic checkpoints is <strong>not</strong> the main purpose. The main purpose is to report maintenance deficiencies and security or fire hazards observed on the round.</li>\n<li><strong>3.6 Prevention of theft, fire, and accidents:</strong> protection is <strong>primarily preventive</strong>. Report any situation that could cause loss, damage, or injury, among them (12):<ol>\n<li>Careless or suspicious activity where easily pilfered items are kept or handled.</li>\n<li>Tools or other property unattended at the end of the shift, or poorly stored.</li>\n<li>Employees in areas where they normally should not be, above all finished-goods warehouses or other property.</li>\n<li>People loitering near fences or windows.</li>\n<li>Company property in places where it is not normally found.</li>\n<li>Any deficiency in building security.</li>\n<li>Suggestions for improving security equipment or services.</li>\n<li>Defective or failing locks.</li>\n<li>Misuse of locks or keys, and their destruction or abuse.</li>\n<li>Doors that should be locked and are open.</li>\n<li>Opening of locks and doors to give access: report the person's name, date, time, and other details.</li>\n<li>Keys found unattended and recovered.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>3.7 Attention to duty:</strong> reading newspapers, books, or any other distraction on duty is prohibited.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Disciplinary Action: Grounds for Transfer or Dismissal (11)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Leaving the post without adequate relief, for any reason.</li>\n<li>Failing to notify the designated shift supervisor in time that he will not be able to report.</li>\n<li>Theft or pilferage by the officer.</li>\n<li>Accepting fees, gifts, or gratuities in the service.</li>\n<li>Conduct unbecoming an officer or prejudicial to discipline.</li>\n<li>Consuming alcohol, illegally using controlled substances, or being under their influence on duty.</li>\n<li>Making false reports.</li>\n<li>Sleeping on duty.</li>\n<li>Intentional disobedience of orders or neglect of duty.</li>\n<li>Unnecessary harshness, violence, or foul language.</li>\n<li>Allowing, intentionally or through carelessness, violations of company or facility rules.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Special Orders (9)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Emergency not provided for</strong> in the general or post orders: notify the company representative or his designated alternate.</li>\n<li><strong>Suspected crime or death:</strong>\n   - Do not destroy or diminish the value of evidence on bodies, belongings, and surroundings.\n   - Keep unauthorized persons from entering until an authority arrives.\n   - Meanwhile, immediately and thoroughly investigate the circumstances. If possible, note names and addresses of witnesses and every important detail.</li>\n<li>Report every violation he sees or learns of.</li>\n<li>Official matters or personal complaints against another officer: present them immediately to the company's designated representative.</li>\n<li><strong>Firearms:</strong> only in defense of himself or another person against the use or threat of <strong>deadly force</strong>, or against actual or threatened serious bodily harm.</li>\n<li>Do not let unauthorized persons or items enter or leave.</li>\n<li><strong>Items that may not enter without authorization (8):</strong> firearms, ammunition, or other weapons; photographic equipment; explosives or incendiaries of any kind; telescopes or binoculars; recorders; electronic transmitting or receiving devices; controlled substances or alcoholic beverages; poisonous or corrosive solids, liquids, or gases.</li>\n<li><strong>Items that may not leave (2):</strong> company property without a signed and authorized <strong>property pass</strong>; plans, photographs, drawings, tracings, or any classified or sensitive document.</li>\n<li>Security personnel treat as confidential the official matters of the department, its officers, and the company.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> chapter 3 insists on preparing detailed general and special orders <strong>before</strong> bidding. With them, the scope and the proposed agreement, vendors can calculate costs and requirements.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e4\">Appendix 3C — Sample Operating Agreement for Security Service (pp. 129–142)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a model operating agreement between the client company (“the Company”) and the security company (“the Contractor”). It takes effect at 12:01 a.m. It has three schedules: insurance, compensation, and billing rates.</p>\n<h4>A. General Provisions</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clause</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A.1</td>\n<td>The Contractor protects all real and personal property of the complex (owned, leased, or in possession). Risks: vandalism, theft, trespass, fire, and other damaging events. Number of officers, assignments, and schedules: according to the attached <em>Statement of Work</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A.2</td>\n<td>Officers arrive fully equipped with uniforms and necessary equipment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A.3</td>\n<td>They are employees of the Contractor, which pays their wages, expenses, and taxes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A.4</td>\n<td>Insurance per Schedule 1. Policies not cancelable except with <strong>15 days'</strong> written notice to the Company. Certificates before a set date and at each renewal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A.5</td>\n<td>Officers' conduct is governed by agreed standard rules and by special written instructions from the Company, with a copy to the Contractor. This does not reduce the Contractor's responsibility.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>B. Physical and Training Requirements</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Experience:</strong> officers, at least <strong>1 year</strong> of satisfactory prior employment in a similar function. Supervisors, at least <strong>2 years</strong> as an officer.</li>\n<li><strong>Before accepting him:</strong> background check (as the law permits) and interview with an authorized representative of the Company.</li>\n<li>This is in addition to the Contractor's screening and investigation, the results of which are reported to the Company. A copy of the employment application is provided. No one is proposed who was previously removed from another assignment for disciplinary reasons or improper conduct.</li>\n<li><strong>Health and physical condition:</strong> good general health, with no defects that prevent normal duties. Standards:\n   - a. Adequate proportion between height and weight.\n   - b. Binocular vision correctable to <strong>20/30 (Snellen)</strong>.\n   - c. Distinguish the standard colors.\n   - d. Hear a normal conversation at <strong>20 feet</strong> and a whisper at <strong>10 feet</strong>, with each ear.\n   - e. Moderate to strenuous physical exertion: (1) standing or walking the entire shift; (2) climbing stairs and ladders; (3) lifting or carrying up to <strong>50 pounds</strong>; (4) running short distances; (5) self-defense.\n   - <strong>Medical examination</strong> within the <strong>90 days</strong> before entering service. <strong>Annual</strong> examination within <strong>30 days</strong> of the anniversary of the previous one.\n   - Copy of the medical certificate to the authorized representative before the assignment, with the employee's <strong>written consent</strong> to the examination.\n   - The final decision on acceptance, and the right to waive requirements, belong to the Company's authorized representative.</li>\n<li><strong>Labor relations and training:</strong>\n   - The Contractor handles its own relations with any union and resolves disputes. It may freely sign collective bargaining agreements.\n   - No collective bargaining agreement binds the Company to the Contractor's employees or its union, at the end of the agreement or ever.\n   - In the event of an actual or potential labor dispute that threatens the service: immediate notice to the representative, with all the information.\n   - <strong>Prior training:</strong> at least <strong>8 h</strong> in the classroom and <strong>16 h</strong> of supervised practice on the post (on-the-job training). The representative approves the training shifts.\n   - <strong>Supervisors:</strong> at least <strong>24 h</strong> of supervised training in operating the control center, and full competence for all its functions.\n   - All of this training, and any refresher the Contractor proposes, is <strong>at the Contractor's expense</strong>.\n   - <strong>Classroom topics (4):</strong> a. legal limits of arrest, search, and seizure; b. fire control, detection, and reporting, including portable extinguishers and control of automatic sprinklers; c. how to report a violation of law to the authority (normally through the representative; in an emergency, direct contact; the procedures by type of emergency are in the special orders manual); d. appearance, attitude, conduct, and chains of command according to the officer's manual.</li>\n<li><strong>Specialized training:</strong> paid at a special base training rate, the same for all personnel. The Contractor pays whatever exceeds that rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuity of service:</strong> the Contractor protects plant, property, equipment, materials, and personnel during strikes, strike threats, work stoppages, or other interference. Officers continue to report, do not leave the post without relief, and do the extra tasks the representative sets. If it fails, the Company may hire another vendor during the contingency and <strong>charge</strong> the Contractor the additional costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Quality and removal of personnel:</strong> service by qualified, careful, and efficient employees, in accordance with best practices. On request, <strong>with or without cause</strong>, the Contractor removes any employee who may be guilty of improper conduct, is unqualified, or is not needed (the text says “in the Contractor's sole discretion”). If so instructed, it replaces him immediately.</li>\n<li><strong>Control center equipment:</strong> provided and maintained by the Company. Damage beyond normal wear is paid by the Contractor, as a credit on the next invoice.</li>\n<li><strong>Inspections:</strong> unannounced, daily, at least <strong>once between 1800 and 0600 h</strong>, by a Contractor supervisor not assigned to the site (non-resident). At the end an <em>Officer’s Daily Report</em> is completed and left in a sealed envelope for the representative. Supervisors do not go past the control center without authorization, except in a bona fide emergency.</li>\n<li><strong>Arrests:</strong> no detention or arrest without the representative's express consent or written instruction, unless the written orders provide for it. Officers <strong>do not sign complaints</strong> on behalf of the Company; if the police request it, they are referred to the representative.</li>\n<li><strong>Schedules:</strong> delivered at least <strong>5 business days</strong> before they take effect. No changes or substitutions without prior notice, except in a bona fide emergency.</li>\n<li><strong>Rotation:</strong> officers rotate shifts at a frequency <strong>no greater than 6 weeks</strong>. Permanent fixed shifts only with the representative's prior approval.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>C. Equipment</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>The Contractor provides and maintains, under a <strong>24-month lease-purchase</strong>:\n   - a. An electronic security tour management system or another acceptable one, with enough equipment for all personnel on duty at the same time.\n   - b. A bicycle with its lock system.\n   - c. Two two-way radios for the patrol.</li>\n<li>The Company provides <strong>one set of master keys</strong>. They are not duplicated and never leave the premises. If they are lost or proven to have been duplicated, the Contractor pays for <strong>rekeying</strong> all exterior locks, doors, and gates.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>D. Compensation</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Pay to personnel per Schedule 2, plus benefits. Work-hour limits:\n   - a. Full-time personnel do not work at another site served by the Contractor while assigned here.\n   - b. Maximum <strong>12 continuous hours</strong> in a shift.\n   - c. Maximum <strong>48 hours</strong> in a normal week (from <strong>Saturday through the following Friday</strong>).\n   - d. Maximum <strong>2 different shifts</strong> in a normal week, unless a need is justified to the Company.\n   - In an emergency, the designated representative may approve exceptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjustment of compensation (penalties):</strong>\n   - For the listed failures, the Contractor's charge is equitably reduced, by mutual agreement.\n   - While this is being agreed, the Company may deduct <strong>50 dollars per occurrence, per day, and per incident</strong>.\n   - If there is no agreement within <strong>2 months</strong> of the deduction, the amount deducted is deemed accepted.\n   - Written advance notice of the intent to deduct is always given.\n   - This does not limit legal action if the actual damage exceeds what was withheld. Not demanding compliance once does not prevent demanding it later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Failures that trigger the deduction (11):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>a. Failing to fill the number of officers for each assignment and shift of the <em>Statement of Work</em>.</li>\n<li>b. Failing to assign trained substitutes.</li>\n<li>c. Allowing more than <strong>12 h in 24 h</strong> or more than <strong>48 h per week</strong> without the representative's written consent.</li>\n<li>d. Failing to keep a complete record of each employee's hours.</li>\n<li>e. Failing to pay each employee the agreed minimum.</li>\n<li>f. Raising or lowering an employee's pay without prior notice to the representative.</li>\n<li>g. Failing to provide the documentation of the medical examination and investigation prior to assignment.</li>\n<li>h. Failing to assign an officer with the correct uniform and equipment.</li>\n<li>i. Failing to replace an employee within <strong>8 hours</strong> of the request.</li>\n<li>j. Failing to provide the training required before assignment (the text refers to a “Training” section; it is in section B).</li>\n<li>k. Failing to provide all necessary equipment and material.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>E. Short-Term Supplemental Service</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>In emergencies: up to <strong>5 additional officers within 4 hours</strong> of notice, and more reasonable personnel within the following <strong>8 hours</strong>. If the Contractor does not respond and the Company covers with others, the Contractor pays the extra cost.</li>\n<li>If <strong>4 hours'</strong> notice cannot be given, the “Short-Notice Supplemental Service” rate from Schedule 3 is paid. With sufficient notice, standard rates apply.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>F. Audit and Payment</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Weekly</strong> summary of hours (regular and special). <strong>Monthly or biweekly</strong> invoice. Payment within <strong>30 days</strong>.</li>\n<li>The Company may periodically audit the hiring and training records.</li>\n<li>Total payment: the bid rate corresponding to the wage of the employee who actually performs the service, for each hour of scheduled coverage (Schedule 3).</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>G. Term</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at <strong>12:01 a.m.</strong> and expires at <strong>12:00 midnight</strong> on the dates set.</li>\n<li>Early termination: <strong>30 days'</strong> written notice by either party.</li>\n<li>Extension: in writing before expiration, on the same terms.</li>\n<li>Notices: by <strong>prepaid, registered mail</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Schedule 1 — Insurance (figures illustrative only)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Coverage</th>\n<th>Amount</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Standard Workers’ Compensation and Employee Liability, self-insured</td>\n<td>$100,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B.1 General liability: bodily injury</td>\n<td>$5,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B.2 General liability: property damage</td>\n<td>$5,000,000</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Schedule 2 — Compensation (figures illustrative only)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Seniority</th>\n<th>Hourly pay</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>0 to 6 months</td>\n<td>$10.25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>6 to 12 months</td>\n<td>$11.15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>13 to 18 months</td>\n<td>$12.05</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>19 to 24 months</td>\n<td>$12.60</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Officer</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>More than 24 months</td>\n<td>$13.20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervisor or senior officer</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Up to 24 months</td>\n<td>$12.10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supervisor or senior officer</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>More than 24 months</td>\n<td>$14.20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Training rate</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>$10.25</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>These are rates for discussion, not suggested salaries. The local wage market must be analyzed thoroughly.</li>\n<li><strong>Benefits (at a minimum):</strong><ul>\n<li>A. Work on a Contractor holiday: <strong>time and a half</strong> for everyone.</li>\n<li>B. Vacation: <strong>1 week (40 h)</strong> at the end of each year, up to 5 years of continuous service. <strong>2 weeks (80 h)</strong> at the end of each year after <strong>5 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>C. The employee chooses to take the vacation or be paid for it. It is <strong>not carried over</strong> to the following year.</li>\n<li>D. Uniforms and equipment at no cost to the employee.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> steps 1 (“0 to 6 months”) and 2 (“6 to 12 months”) overlap at month 6.</p>\n<h4>Schedule 3 — Billing Rates (figures illustrative only)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Personnel.</strong> The “straight time” and “overtime” columns are blank. Printed rates:<ul>\n<li>A. Officer: $15.10, $15.90, $13.35, $14.40, and $15.20 (five, like the five steps of Schedule 2).</li>\n<li>B. Supervisor: $17.20 and $16.90.</li>\n<li>C. Training: $14.85.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>II. Lease-purchase equipment (12 months):</strong> electronic tour system, one bicycle, and two portable radios. Each with full maintenance and monthly fee left blank.</li>\n<li><strong>III. Short-notice supplemental service:</strong> hourly rate (blank) if there is no 4-hour notice. If there is, the standard rates in item I apply.</li>\n<li><strong>IV. Benefits (to be filled in):</strong> A. life insurance; B. medical and health; C. long-term disability. For each: categories covered, amount or coverage, and employee contribution.</li>\n<li><strong>V. Number of officers in the company</strong>, name of the bidding company, and signature of its authorized representative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> clause C.1 and the chapter 3 example set the lease-purchase at <strong>24 months</strong>. Schedule 3 says <strong>12 months</strong>.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> if the rates are read in the order of the steps, they do not rise with seniority: they drop from step 2 to 3, and the supervisor rate drops from 1 to 2. Step 3 would bill $13.35 with a wage of $12.05. The book does not explain the correspondence and states that the figures are illustrative.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> chapter 3 asks that the agreement reflect the client's needs according to its risk assessment, not the vendor's generic contract. The vendor's willingness to adapt it is a key selection criterion. Key figures: 50 dollars per failure, limits of 12 h and 48 h, 5 officers in 4 h, 30 days' notice, and payment at 30 days.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e5\">Appendix 4A — Application for Consulting Assignment (pp. 163–164)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a form the company asks each consultant candidate to complete. It allows them to be compared using the same data.</p>\n<p><strong>Consultant data (fields):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Consultant's name; name and address of his firm.</li>\n<li>Telephone, e-mail, and website.</li>\n<li>Main specialty.</li>\n<li>Last position before becoming a consultant; last employer; date he left.</li>\n<li>Start date as a consultant; total years practicing.</li>\n<li>Years of study; university.</li>\n<li>Professional or academic designations.</li>\n<li>Professional affiliations and memberships, and their length.</li>\n<li>Awards or recognitions in the security industry.</li>\n<li>Published works, if any.</li>\n<li>Basic consulting fee.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Two references, with the same fields:</strong> client name; contact person, with phone and e-mail; main purpose of the project; duration of the project.</p>\n<p><strong>Attachments:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Certificates of <strong>professional indemnity</strong> insurance or equivalent.</li>\n<li>Certificates of <strong>liability</strong> insurance or equivalent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> it is step 2 of the five consultant selection steps in chapter 4 (a tailored application). Alternative: request letters of service and use the form as a checklist. The résumé is attached and, where required, proof of license or registration.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e6\">Appendix 4B — Résumé (pp. 165–166)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a sample résumé of a fictitious consultant, “John Doe, CPP” (Certified Protection Professional). Its distinctive feature is the knowledge, skills, and abilities (KSAs) section.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Work history:</strong> U.S. Army military police, 3 years; deputy sheriff, 26 years; university assistant professor, 13 years.</li>\n<li><strong>Education:</strong> bachelor's degree (B.S., Bachelor of Science) in Police Science and Administration.</li>\n<li><strong>Publications (3):</strong> the book <em>Training the Security Officer</em> (2021); the article “Why Security Counts” (August 2020); the chapter “Consultants as Trainers” in <em>Security Consulting</em> (2018).</li>\n<li><strong>Affiliations and achievements:</strong> CPP designation; member of ASIS International; member of the Institute of Management Consultants; 2018 <em>Distinguished Service Accolade</em> from the IAPSC (International Association of Professional Security Consultants).</li>\n<li><strong>KSAs</strong>, each with concrete evidence:<ul>\n<li>Analyzing security systems and improving processes: designed the security system and procedures for a new cannabis dispensary. In total, more than <strong>40 systems</strong> designed across several industries.</li>\n<li>Accomplished trainer: created the program and trained officers in general security and <strong>use of force</strong> at <strong>28 sites</strong> of a company. Other organizations replicated his programs.</li>\n<li>Knowledge of current design tools: courses from leading electronic security vendors (fictitious examples: ABC Corporation, Acme Camera Corporation, Loud Alarms). Certified in CAD/CAM (computer-aided design and manufacturing) and other software.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> chapter 4 notes that there are many types of résumé and that this one includes a KSA section. There the consultant demonstrates his ability with concrete, measurable achievements.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e7\">Appendix 4C — Professional Consulting Services Agreement (pp. 167–180)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a model contract between an individual consultant (“Consultant”) and the client company (“Company”). It includes supplementary agreements, time and expense forms, and the expense and travel policies.</p>\n<h4>Main Contract (10 clauses)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.</th>\n<th>Clause</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Retainer-Term</td>\n<td>The consultant is an <strong>independent contractor</strong>, not an employee. He provides services from the contract date until a set end date.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Statement of Work</td>\n<td>He does the work in the “Scope of Work” schedule when the company requests it, at places mutually agreed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Payment</td>\n<td>(a) Rate per unit of time, with a cap of days or hours (blank). Exceeding it requires a written amendment. Travel time requested by the company is paid at the same rate, whether during normal hours or not. (b) Travel and other expenses, per the “Consultant Expense” schedule.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Patents</td>\n<td>Promptly disclose ideas, inventions, discoveries, and improvements (<em>Subject Inventions</em>), patentable or not. Applies to what is conceived or reduced to practice in the work and based on nonpublic company information. Keep a written record of technical activity. Records and inventions are the <strong>exclusive property of the company</strong>. During and after the contract, sign whatever is needed for the company to obtain patents.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Copyrights</td>\n<td>All writings are the exclusive property of the company, with the exclusive right to assign the <em>copyright</em> in any country. The company will make every effort to give the consultant a <strong>nonexclusive</strong> right to publish them, if circumstances (including security regulations) permit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Professional standards</td>\n<td>Best effort and the highest professional quality.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Security (classified information)</td>\n<td>The company notifies which information is <em>Classified</em> or <em>Restricted Data</em>. The consultant complies with the requirements of the U.S. Government or of the company. If he keeps classified material outside the company: he needs a <strong>facility clearance</strong>, signs a security agreement with the Department of Defense, and applies its industrial security manual (<em>Department of Defense Industrial Security Manual for Safeguarding Classified Security Information</em>). He promptly returns the classified material when the security agreement or this contract ends.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Risk of loss</td>\n<td>The consultant assumes the risk of his personal injuries and of damage to or loss of his property. If he employs others at company facilities, he proves general commercial liability insurance for a minimum amount (blank).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Privileged or proprietary information</td>\n<td>Do not disclose nonpublic information obtained from any source (the company, its customers, associates, or other contractors) without prior written consent. Exception: what the work itself requires.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Termination</td>\n<td>Either party may terminate, in whole or in part, <strong>at any time</strong>, with written notice.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Useful comparison:</strong> here termination requires no notice period. The operating agreement in Appendix 3C requires 30 days.</p>\n<h4>Consulting Security Agreement—Joint Certification</h4>\n<p>Consultant and company certify (3):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Classified information does not physically leave the company's premises.</li>\n<li>The contract is performed on the company's premises.</li>\n<li>The consultant and the certifying employees do not disclose classified information to unauthorized persons.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> in this form the company is called “Contractor”; in the main contract it is “Company.”</p>\n<h4>Conflict of Interest Statement</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The consultant warrants, to the best of his knowledge, that there are no facts that could create an <strong>organizational conflict of interest</strong> and that he has disclosed everything relevant.</li>\n<li>If one is discovered, he discloses it immediately and in writing to the <strong>Contracting Officer</strong>. It includes the measures taken or proposed to avoid or mitigate it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Professional Services Log</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Header: consultant, contract number, period, number of days, and Requestor/Monitor.</li>\n<li>Instructions (4):<ol>\n<li>Record on the same day the work is done.</li>\n<li>Cross-check against the consultant's Statement of Professional Services, for accuracy and completeness.</li>\n<li>Explain every charge for off-site work and every approval of a rented car. The invoice must match.</li>\n<li>If space runs out, use the back.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Columns: date; time; work order; project or task with brief description and evaluation of the work (citing the consultant's documents).</li>\n<li>Signature of the requestor/monitor. <strong>Retain at least 3 years.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Statement of Professional Services</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Header: consultant, week ending, address.</li>\n<li>Instructions (4): 1. complete it fully (one per trip, except local consultants); 2. attach vouchers, receipts, and tickets; 3. consultant's signature; 4. keep a copy and send the original to the corporation's supervisor.</li>\n<li>Services: project, contracts, hours per day from Monday through Sunday, total hours or days, and amount.</li>\n<li>Transportation: origin, destination, dates, and cost (attach receipts).</li>\n<li>Other expenses: meals; lodging; car rental (1)(2); local taxi or bus (2); telephone (2); mileage in own car (3); parking (1); other (2)(4).<ul>\n<li>(1) Attach all receipts.</li>\n<li>(2) Attach receipt if over <strong>$5.00</strong>.</li>\n<li>(3) Mileage at the current rate.</li>\n<li>(4) Explain.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Dated signatures: consultant, “approved by,” and “audited by.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Policy on Consultant’s Expenses</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Documented and approved expense report <strong>within 30 days</strong> after each trip.</li>\n<li>Original receipts for <strong>everything except meals</strong>. No receipt is needed where one is not normally given, such as taxi or bus <strong>under $10.00 per trip</strong>.</li>\n<li>Keep an <strong>expense diary</strong> and retain it <strong>permanently</strong> as a personal record, unless requested with the report.</li>\n<li>The requirements follow the documentation rules of the IRS (Internal Revenue Service, the U.S. tax authority). Complying with the policy equals complying with the IRS.</li>\n<li><strong>Travel expenses:</strong> actual and necessary expenses for lodging, subsistence, incidentals, and transportation in <strong>coach class</strong> are reimbursed. Or mileage, if use of a personal car is authorized in place of air travel. Outside the local area, coach class is not exceeded, except for lack of schedules or space, or a different agreement.</li>\n<li><strong>Local consultants:</strong> meals and mileage are not reimbursed. Authorized trips between work sites are. Daily commuting distance = the vicinity or a <strong>50-mile radius</strong> of the assigned work location.</li>\n<li><strong>Rental car</strong> (out-of-town consultant, authorized on arrival): the company pays the charges necessary for the work. Mileage for personal use is paid by the consultant.</li>\n<li><strong>Telephone and telecommunications:</strong> reasonable and necessary ones are reimbursed.</li>\n<li><strong>Other expenses:</strong> reimbursed with written approval and the consultant's certification that they were necessary. Examples: use of computers and rental of test equipment.</li>\n<li><strong>Substantiation to the IRS:</strong> adequate records and documentary evidence of 5 elements: (a) amount, (b) time, (c) place, (d) business purpose, (e) business relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Consultant Travel Policy</h4>\n<p>The travel policy is for employees, but it is extended to consultants to judge whether expenses are reasonable and necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Mode of travel:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The most direct transportation available. By jet, normally a class below first.</li>\n<li>The consultant organizes the trip and reports it afterward in the Statement of Services. <strong>No advances</strong> of cash or tickets.</li>\n<li>a. Fares of more than <strong>$10.00 per trip</strong>: with receipt attached.</li>\n<li>b. <strong>First class</strong> or other luxury: requires documentation and only in 4 cases:<ol>\n<li>Schedules or availability do not permit another class.</li>\n<li>Overnight departures between <strong>9:00 p.m. and 6:00 a.m.</strong> (local time) with a flight of <strong>4 hours or more</strong>.</li>\n<li>Physical disability that requires it, with a medical certificate or specific management approval.</li>\n<li>When line supervision judges that useful work can be done en route only in first class. Requires management approval.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Personal vehicle:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Local consultant: no meals or mileage between home or office and the assigned site. Authorized trips between work sites are paid. Commuting distance: <strong>50 miles</strong> from the company facility.</li>\n<li>Use on domestic trips: mileage at the current rate, not exceeding the air fare in a class below first. The time recognized is limited to normal flight time; the excess is not paid.</li>\n<li>Mandatory minimum insurance:<ul>\n<li>a. Cars: bodily injury <strong>$250,000 per person</strong> and <strong>$500,000 per accident</strong>; property damage <strong>$50,000 per accident</strong>. The deductible is not reimbursed.</li>\n<li>b. <strong>Personal aircraft</strong> (owned or rented): <strong>prohibited</strong> for company business.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Car rental:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Permitted if it will be used at least <strong>20 miles per day</strong>. Normally not for local trips.</li>\n<li>Prior approval whenever possible. Only <strong>standard or compact</strong> models; the extra cost of sports or luxury cars is not paid.</li>\n<li>Personal mileage is not paid by the company. The <strong>full collision insurance</strong> bought from the rental company is reimbursed.</li>\n<li>Responsibilities: a. promptly report accidents with damage or injuries to the rental company, the local police, the consultant's supervisor, and the company's <strong>security control center</strong>; b. return the car to the rental company or its representative.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reporting:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Travel expenses reported <strong>within 30 days</strong> after the trip.</li>\n<li>Original receipts except meals. Not required for expenses that usually have none, such as taxi or bus.</li>\n<li>a. Unusual expenses (secretarial service, office equipment rental): explain and justify them each time.</li>\n<li>b. Dry cleaning and laundry: only on trips of <strong>more than 4 days</strong> or in unusual circumstances that are explained.</li>\n<li>c. Meals and lodging: actual expenses that are reasonable and necessary.</li>\n<li>d. Calls to company facilities: collect and over tie-lines when they exist.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Caps, losses, and deviations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maximum lodging per night in the headquarters area, taxes included: blank. Cap on three daily meals: blank.</li>\n<li>The company is not responsible for loss of cash or for loss of or damage to personal property while traveling.</li>\n<li>Deviations from the policy are approved in specific cases with unusual circumstances. They must be documented and approved by the appropriate management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the receipt thresholds do not match. The form asks for a receipt from <strong>$5.00</strong>; the expense and travel policies use <strong>$10.00 per trip</strong>; the reporting section exempts taxi and bus without setting an amount.</p>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the daily commuting distance is measured from the “assigned work location” (expense policy) or from the “company facility” (travel policy). Both use 50 miles.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> chapter 4 presents it as the contract signed after negotiating with the best candidate, with supplementary agreements. Its forms show the client the time and expense records that will be required. Key points: independent contractor, inventions and writings belong to the client, confidentiality, and free termination.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e8\">Key Terms (appendices 1A–4C)</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Misconduct</td>\n<td>Willful or wanton disregard of the interests of the employer or coworkers, or serious or repeated negligence (1A).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gross misconduct</td>\n<td>Work-related crime with conviction or confession, or flagrant and wanton disregard of the rights of the employer or coworkers (1A).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Willful misconduct</td>\n<td>Basis for dismissal in 1A. Includes violating any of the 37 rules.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Progressive discipline</td>\n<td>Increasing sanctions according to seriousness and mitigating factors: from verbal counseling to the last warning (1B).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preponderance of evidence</td>\n<td>Level of proof the disciplinary investigation must reach (1A).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fundamental fairness</td>\n<td>Principle guiding the 17 questions before sanctioning (1A).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>No-call no-show</em></td>\n<td>Missing work without notice. Cause for immediate dismissal (1B).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope of work</td>\n<td>Tasks, days, hours, and total hours of the service (3A).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ST / OT</td>\n<td>Straight time / overtime hours.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General orders</td>\n<td>Performance standards for all officers (3B).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special orders</td>\n<td>Duties of each specific assignment (3B).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special instructions</td>\n<td>Memoranda for special events, such as inspections or tours (3B).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property pass</td>\n<td>Signed authorization to remove company property (3B).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjustment of compensation</td>\n<td>Reduction of the charge for noncompliance; provisional deduction of 50 dollars per occurrence, per day, and per incident (3C).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Short-Notice Supplemental Service</em></td>\n<td>Special reinforcement rate when there is no 4-hour notice (3C).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lease-purchase</td>\n<td>Way of providing the patrol equipment in 3C.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rekeying</td>\n<td>Paid by the Contractor if it loses or duplicates the master keys (3C).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent contractor</td>\n<td>The consultant's status in 4C: not an employee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Subject Inventions</em></td>\n<td>Consultant's inventions tied to the work, which become the company's (4C).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Facility clearance</td>\n<td>Requirement to keep classified material outside the company (4C).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organizational conflict of interest</td>\n<td>Disclosed immediately and in writing to the Contracting Officer (4C).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>KSAs</td>\n<td>Knowledge, skills, and abilities on the résumé (4B).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e9\">Appendix 7A — Supervisor&#x27;s Checklist (pp. 265–266)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a checklist for supervisors with behaviors and symptoms that may indicate substance abuse. It accompanies chapter 7 (workplace substance abuse).</p>\n<p><strong>Rule of use (most likely to be asked):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>That some indicators appear <strong>does not necessarily mean</strong> the person has a substance abuse problem.</li>\n<li>One must look for <strong>groups or patterns</strong> of behaviors and symptoms (<em>clusters</em>), not isolated indicators.</li>\n<li>The list is only an <strong>aid</strong> for detecting possible abuse; it is not for diagnosing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The book divides the indicators into four blocks (17 + 21 + 13 + 17 = 68; own count).</p>\n<h4>1. Tardiness and Absenteeism — 17</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Frequent breaks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Long meals</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Tardiness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Arriving late and leaving early</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Being absent from the area or office</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Going to the restroom an abnormal number of times</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Unexplained absences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Absences due to accidents on and off the job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Missing work before and after payday</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Missing work before and after holidays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Missing work on Mondays and Fridays</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Using up vacation as soon as it is earned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Missing work during periods of heavy workload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Reporting sick after being denied vacation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Asking for vacation to be extended</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Asking for sick leave to be extended</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Extending sick leave again and again</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>2. Performance — 21</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Repeated procrastination</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Repeated delay in finishing assigned tasks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Irresponsibility in completing tasks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Faulty decision making</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Increased accident rate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Increased errors of judgment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Unnecessary waste of materials and scrap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Unnecessary damage to equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Excessive time to do assigned tasks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Difficulty remembering instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>General lack of interest in the work or product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Difficulty handling difficult assignments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Difficulty remembering earlier mistakes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Alternating periods of high and low productivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Missed deadlines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Errors due to poor judgment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Customer complaints</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18</td>\n<td>Inappropriate conduct with other people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19</td>\n<td>General carelessness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>Sloppy work habits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>21</td>\n<td>Asset losses</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>3. Interpersonal Relationships — 13</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Inappropriate emotional outbursts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Mood swings at the beginning or end of the day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Overreaction to criticism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Always blaming others</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Making inappropriate statements or comments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Rambling, incoherent speech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Withdrawing from coworkers and friends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Physical volatility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Exaggerated sense of self-importance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Inflexible and unreasonable attitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Excessive time on the telephone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Failing to keep commitments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Failing to show up for appointments</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>4. Appearance and Mood — 17</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Indicator</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Inappropriate clothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Neglected personal hygiene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Body odor, unkempt hair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Little interest in appearance in general</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Glassy or red eyes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Slurred speech</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Staggered gait</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Episodes of heavy sweating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Wearing sunglasses at inappropriate times</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Withdrawn attitude</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>Unusually deep sadness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>Inappropriate laughter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>Suspiciousness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>Paranoia</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>Extreme sensitivity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>16</td>\n<td>Unusual irritability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17</td>\n<td>Preoccupation with death and illness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> the supervisor observes work behavior (attendance, performance, dealings, appearance), not diagnoses. Only a pattern of several indicators justifies acting: it moves to the intervention in Appendix 7B and can ground the “reasonable suspicion” of 7C.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e10\">Appendix 7B — Intervention Checklist (p. 267)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a brief list of what supervisors and managers should do, never do, and always do when intervening with an employee who may have a substance problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Principle:</strong> intervention serves to <strong>correct, not punish</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Recommended Steps (9, in the book's order)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Observe and document.</li>\n<li>Confront the problem employee <strong>in private</strong>.</li>\n<li>Discuss his performance and conduct.</li>\n<li>Reaffirm expectations.</li>\n<li>Offer reasonable accommodations.</li>\n<li>Set goals.</li>\n<li>Document the results.</li>\n<li>Communicate with executive management.</li>\n<li>Follow up.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Never vs. Always</h4>\n<p>The two columns are independent lists: there is no row-by-row correspondence.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Should never — 7</th>\n<th>Should always — 8</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Diagnose a personal problem</td>\n<td>Monitor performance and conduct</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Take responsibility for others' personal problems or matters</td>\n<td>Document specifics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Generalize</td>\n<td>Follow the organization's policy and procedures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Moralize</td>\n<td>Talk with the employee in private</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Cover up</td>\n<td>Interview him with a witness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Self-treat the problem</td>\n<td>Offer professional help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Enter into a confidential or protective relationship</td>\n<td>Let him know you care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Let the employee make a choice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> apparent internal contradiction between “talk with the employee in private” and “interview him with a witness.” The book does not explain how they are reconciled or who the witness should be (pending).</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> the “never” list (do not cover up, do not protect, do not carry the problem) is the practical way to avoid the denial, enabling, and codependency described in chapter 7. The supervisor focuses on performance, documents, and refers to professional help, such as the employee assistance program (EAP).</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e11\">Appendix 7C — Sample Substance Abuse Policy (pp. 268–272)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a model substance abuse policy for a fictitious company, “XYZ Company.” It covers prohibitions, drug and alcohol testing, rehabilitation, costs, and definitions.</p>\n<h4>Scope</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>XYZ declares itself a drug-free workplace.</li>\n<li>It does not permit its employees to be impaired by drugs or alcohol on company time or property.</li>\n<li>Failing to follow the policy’s rules, procedures, requirements <strong>or spirit</strong> leads to corrective action.</li>\n<li>Possible corrective actions, depending on the circumstances:<ul>\n<li>termination;</li>\n<li>suspension;</li>\n<li>warning;</li>\n<li>probation;</li>\n<li>any lesser sanction;</li>\n<li>other action, at the company’s discretion, proportionate to the problem.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Use or possession at work</h4>\n<p>Prohibited on company property, at the job, or when doing company business:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>using or possessing alcoholic beverages or illegal drugs;</li>\n<li>unlawfully manufacturing, distributing, dispensing, possessing, offering, or using a controlled substance;</li>\n<li>possessing drug paraphernalia or empty alcohol containers on company time or property.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Only exception:</strong> alcohol served at company-sponsored events (for example, a year-end party). There, responsible, moderate consumption does not violate the policy.</p>\n<h4>Impairment</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Reporting to work, or performing company duties or business, while impaired by alcohol or drugs is prohibited.</li>\n<li>If an employee is believed to be impaired, in addition to other measures, the employee may be suspended, sent home, or reassigned for safety reasons while the situation is evaluated.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Off-duty use</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Alcohol</strong> off duty and off premises: prohibited if it<ol>\n<li>causes impairment at work;</li>\n<li>harms attendance or performance; or</li>\n<li>otherwise harms the company’s image.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Illegal drugs:</strong> prohibited in all circumstances, on or off duty and on or off the premises.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Legal drugs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Using over-the-counter or prescription medications as directed by the doctor or manufacturer is not prohibited.</li>\n<li><strong>Abuse</strong> of legal drugs is treated the same as use of illegal drugs.</li>\n<li>If proper use may affect the ability to work safely and effectively, the employee must notify their supervisor <strong>in advance</strong>. That way, steps can be taken to protect safety and productivity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Drug convictions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Anyone convicted of any drug offense <strong>that occurred in the workplace</strong> must notify their supervisor <strong>within five days</strong> of the conviction.</li>\n<li>XYZ may be required to report to the government agencies with which it has contracts.</li>\n<li>Link to section 7.8: the Drug-Free Workplace Act of 1988 (US) requires companies with federal contracts and grants over $25,000 to certify that they have drug-free workplace policies.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Job applicants</h4>\n<p>XYZ will not knowingly hire an applicant who currently <strong>abuses</strong> alcohol or legal drugs, or who currently <strong>uses</strong> illegal drugs. Nuance: for alcohol and legal drugs, abuse is what counts; for illegal drugs, use alone is enough.</p>\n<h4>Right of inspection</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>XYZ may inspect employees’ personal belongings on its premises at any time, with or without notice:<ul>\n<li>vehicles;</li>\n<li>lunch containers;</li>\n<li>bags;</li>\n<li>boxes;</li>\n<li>packages;</li>\n<li>desks;</li>\n<li>lockers;</li>\n<li>other personal belongings.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Purpose: to enforce this policy or for other safety and security reasons.</li>\n<li>The premises include all employee parking areas and parking lots designated by the company.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Drug and alcohol testing policy</h4>\n<p>Any employee or applicant may be required to take a breath and/or urine test, for drugs or alcohol, in <strong>three</strong> circumstances:</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Circumstance</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Key rules</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Preemployment</td>\n<td>All applicants for the facilities or job categories that management determines from time to time</td>\n<td>Anyone who fails is <strong>ineligible for at least one year</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasonable suspicion</td>\n<td>Any employee</td>\n<td>Raised by a supervisor or higher-level manager and concurred in by the highest-ranking manager available at the affected facility or department</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Universal</td>\n<td>All employees of the facilities or departments that management designates from time to time</td>\n<td>Randomization done by the testing provider using its own systems; the provider notifies XYZ whom to test; may be done without notice at any time of the year</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Definition of reasonable suspicion:</strong> reasonable suspicion by a supervisor or higher-level manager, concurred in by the highest-ranking manager available, that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the employee’s faculties are impaired at work; or</li>\n<li>the employee has used or possessed illegal drugs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Factors that may support it</strong> (open list, <em>not limited to</em>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Direct observation, or reports from coworkers or others reasonably believed to be reliable.</li>\n<li>Possession of drugs or alcohol on the premises, or use at work, before work, or during breaks, such that the employee is impaired on the premises.</li>\n<li>Conduct, speech, or other physical signs consistent with impairment.</li>\n<li>A pattern of abnormal or erratic behavior with no other satisfactory explanation.</li>\n<li>Unexplained accidents, work injuries, or property damage.</li>\n<li>A combination of some of these factors and/or others, in management’s judgment.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Management’s determination is <strong>discretionary and final</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Consequence:</strong> refusing the test, failing to cooperate, or testing positive for illegal drugs or alcohol leads to <strong>termination</strong>. Exception: as provided in the rehabilitation section.</p>\n<h4>Testing process</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scope:</strong> urine and/or breath analysis, as XYZ and the provider decide.</li>\n<li><strong>Substances it may detect</strong> (open list):<ol>\n<li>marijuana;</li>\n<li>cocaine;</li>\n<li>opiates;</li>\n<li>amphetamines;</li>\n<li>phencyclidine (PCP).</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>XYZ may expand or reduce the list at any time, with or without notice.</li>\n<li>It may request separate specimens if several tests are performed.</li>\n<li>Testing levels and standards are set by XYZ and the provider.</li>\n<li><strong>Confirmation:</strong> every initial positive is confirmed with a <strong>second test</strong>, in accordance with applicable law.</li>\n<li><strong>Specimen:</strong><ul>\n<li>It is collected at a facility designated by XYZ.</li>\n<li>Applicants and selected employees (universal or reasonable cause testing) must report at the exact time and place indicated.</li>\n<li>An employee tested for suspected impairment is <strong>taken to the testing site by a supervisor</strong> or other designated person.</li>\n<li>The employee must sign the consent and provide the information requested. If they do not, they may be terminated or lose the opportunity for employment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Injured employee:</strong> if seriously injured and unable to provide a specimen at the time of the accident, the employee must authorize access to hospital reports and other documents. These are used to determine whether controlled substances or alcohol were in the employee’s system.</li>\n<li><strong>Notification of results:</strong> employees and applicants are notified of positive results and may explain them. If they do not respond in time, the result may stand as an <strong>uncontested positive verification</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Rehabilitation</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Purpose and responsibility:</strong><ul>\n<li>XYZ recognizes drug dependency and alcoholism as <strong>health problems</strong>.</li>\n<li>At management’s sole discretion and case by case, it will try to support an employee who becomes dependent.</li>\n<li>It will help the employee identify rehabilitation services, referral agencies, or other resources.</li>\n<li>But it is the employee’s responsibility to ensure that their problem does not affect their performance or endanger others.</li>\n<li>Everyone is encouraged to seek help before a personal problem becomes a work problem.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation and treatment:</strong><ul>\n<li>As an alternative to discipline or termination, a chemical dependency evaluation may be permitted.</li>\n<li>It is offered case by case, at management’s sole discretion.</li>\n<li>If the evaluation recommends it, enrolling in and successfully completing an approved treatment may be offered <strong>only once</strong>. It is an alternative to discipline and a condition of continued employment.</li>\n<li><strong>It does not apply to applicants.</strong></li>\n<li>Return to work and any special conditions are decided case by case. XYZ’s interest in safety and operational efficiency is weighed.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Costs</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Who pays</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mandatory drug or alcohol tests</td>\n<td>XYZ</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatment</td>\n<td>The employee, to the extent not covered by their health insurance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Definitions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Impairment:</strong> a condition caused by any drug, alcohol, or a combination of them, that affects the employee in a physically or mentally detectable way.<ul>\n<li>Its symptoms are not limited to misconduct or obvious impairment (slurred speech, difficulty keeping balance, smell of alcohol).</li>\n<li>It may be determined by any supervisor or manager, a medical professional, a scientific test such as urinalysis, or sometimes a layperson.</li>\n<li>In some cases, with no objective or subjective indicator at all, mere use may constitute impairment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Illegal drug:</strong> any drug that (a) cannot be obtained legally, or (b) can be obtained legally but was not obtained or is not used legally.<ul>\n<li>It includes prescription drugs obtained illegally or used for purposes other than those prescribed.</li>\n<li>It includes prescription drugs shared with a coworker, <strong>in any circumstance</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Legal drug:</strong> a prescription or over-the-counter medication, legally obtained and used for the purpose for which it was prescribed or manufactured.</li>\n<li><strong>Drug paraphernalia:</strong> objects, tools, and devices normally used to prepare, store, or administer illegal drugs. Examples (open list):<ul>\n<li>rolling papers;</li>\n<li>roach clips;</li>\n<li>glass pipes;</li>\n<li>water pipes and bongs;</li>\n<li>vials;</li>\n<li>straws and spoons;</li>\n<li>in some cases, hypodermic syringes.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Serious injury:</strong> any work injury that stops work and requires medical attention of any kind.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reasonable suspicion vs. reasonable cause:</strong> the policy defines “reasonable suspicion,” but then uses “reasonable cause” as a synonym. The defined term is <em>reasonable suspicion</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fewer testing situations than the chapter:</strong> the general guidance in section 7.8 gives six testing moments. For all workers: preemployment, reasonable suspicion, post-accident/injury, return to duty, and follow up. Random testing, only for safety-sensitive positions.</li>\n<li>Model 7C regulates only three situations. Its random “universal” test covers all employees in the designated areas, not just safety-sensitive positions.</li>\n<li>In addition, it defines “serious injury” but does not provide for post-accident testing: it only requires authorizing access to hospital reports.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> 7C is the practical template for chapter 7: it turns prohibitions, testing, and rehabilitation into written rules. Figures to remember: conviction notice within five days, one year of ineligibility, five substances, a second confirmation test, and treatment offered only once.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e12\">Appendix 8A — Sample Policy for Workplace Violence (pp. 293–294)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a model workplace violence (WPV) policy for the fictitious company XYZ. It puts into practice what section 8.4 requires of the policy of the workplace violence prevention and intervention (WVPI) program.</p>\n<h4>Statement and removal of the offender</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>XYZ is committed to the safety of its personnel.</li>\n<li>It will not tolerate violence by anyone, on company property, against employees, visitors, guests, or other persons.</li>\n<li>Anyone who violates the policy on XYZ property must be removed from the premises as quickly as safety permits.</li>\n<li>They must stay off the premises until the investigation of the incident is complete.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Conduct that violates the policy (7)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Causing physical injury to another person.</li>\n<li>Making threats.</li>\n<li>Displaying aggressive or hostile behavior.</li>\n<li>Intentionally damaging property.</li>\n<li>Possessing a weapon on company property.</li>\n<li>Committing acts related to sexual harassment or domestic violence.</li>\n<li>Making false or misleading reports about what others do.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Response if the investigation confirms the violation</h4>\n<p>A “decisive and appropriate” response, which may include (open list):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>suspension and/or termination of any business relationship;</li>\n<li>reassignment of duties;</li>\n<li>suspension or termination of employment;</li>\n<li>and/or requesting the arrest and criminal prosecution of those involved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Prevention prevails over procedure</h4>\n<p>No existing policy, practice, or procedure may prevent decisions made to avoid:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a threat being carried out;</li>\n<li>a violent act occurring; or</li>\n<li>a life-threatening situation developing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Duty to report</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>All personnel must notify the company of any threat, real or perceived, that they have witnessed or received.</li>\n<li>Also of those they are told another person witnessed or received.</li>\n<li>Channels (3):<ol>\n<li>employee hotline;</li>\n<li>their superior;</li>\n<li>the designated management representative (DMR).</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Every report is handled with discretion, investigated, and the relevant parties are notified.</li>\n<li><strong>No retaliation</strong> against anyone who reports in good faith.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Protective or restraining orders</h4>\n<p>Anyone who requests or obtains a protective or restraining order that lists company locations as protected areas must give the DMR:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>a copy of the petition and of the statements used to request it;</li>\n<li>a copy of any temporary order granted;</li>\n<li>a copy of any order that becomes permanent.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Reason:</strong> XYZ must provide a safe workplace and protect its employees from threats. It cannot do so effectively without knowing who is under an order from a court, or another legal entity, to stay away from its locations.</li>\n<li>XYZ recognizes that this information is sensitive. It has procedures to receive it, store it, and act on it while respecting the privacy of the employee who reports.</li>\n<li>Those procedures are in a separate document.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>DMR details (blank fields in the model)</h4>\n<p>Name · Title · Phone · Email · Office mail.</p>\n<h4>Contrast with section 8.4 (what a WPV policy must include)</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The policy must… (ch. 8.4)</th>\n<th>Does 8A cover it?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Define unacceptable conduct</td>\n<td>Yes: 7 behaviors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Regulate or prohibit weapons, to the extent permitted by law</td>\n<td>Prohibits possessing weapons, without the legal qualifier (see Exam note)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Require prompt reporting of any concerning behavior or circumstance</td>\n<td>Yes: duty to report real or perceived threats (sets no deadline)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Offer several ways to report</td>\n<td>Yes: 3 channels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guarantee that reports are treated seriously and with discretion</td>\n<td>Yes: discretion and investigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commit to no retaliation for good faith reports</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Require that protective or restraining orders be reported</td>\n<td>Yes: copies to the DMR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sanction violations, including false or misleading reports</td>\n<td>Yes: a false report is a violation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid the term “zero tolerance”</td>\n<td>Does not use the literal term (see Exam note)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>“Zero tolerance”:</strong> section 8.4 asks to avoid the term <em>zero tolerance</em> because it reduces reporting and safety. Model 8A does not use that label, but it opens by saying violence “will not be tolerated.” On a question, the correct answer is to avoid the expression “zero tolerance.”</li>\n<li><strong>Weapons:</strong> section 8.4 says to regulate or prohibit weapons to the extent permitted by applicable law. 8A prohibits them without that qualifier.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> 8A is the policy template of chapter 8. It defines behaviors, provides several channels, guarantees no retaliation, centralizes protective orders with the DMR, and puts prevention ahead of any procedure.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e13\">Appendix 8B — Workplace Violence Plan Scenario (pp. 295–296)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> an example of how to apply the WPV plan to a specific threat: a potentially violent, terminated former employee re-entering the building. Chapter 8 cites it when discussing training with periodic drills and exercises with outside police and emergency services.</p>\n<h4>Setup</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The security threat and risk assessment identified this threat as a <strong>priority</strong>.</li>\n<li>The stakeholders working group (SWG) is tasked with applying mitigations that manage the threat and its risk well.</li>\n<li>According to chapter 8, the SWG ends up forming the permanent threat management team (TMT). The TMT is also called the threat assessment team (TAT) or incident management team (IMT).</li>\n<li><strong>Best protection method</strong> against this threat: <strong>access control</strong> to the premises.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Diffusion of benefits</h4>\n<p>Robust access and entry control also prevents and mitigates other external WPV threats. The right-hand column is our own classification using the aggressor typology of section 8.2.</p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Appendix example</th>\n<th>Aggressor type (section 8.2)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Criminal planning a robbery</td>\n<td>Type 1: no legitimate relationship, with criminal intent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delivery person planning an assault</td>\n<td>Type 2: legitimate relationship (customer, patient, visitor, service provider)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Customer planning to intimidate employees</td>\n<td>Type 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Person in an employee’s circle planning to harass them</td>\n<td>Type 4: personal relationship, real or perceived, with an employee</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Terminated former employee (the scenario’s threat)</td>\n<td>Type 3: current or former employee, including contractors</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Assessment: comprehensive security survey</h4>\n<p>The current access and entry control system is evaluated, specifically against the return of a terminated former employee. The building’s physical, electronic, and design elements are reviewed against unauthorized entry. The book groups what must be reviewed into four blocks.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Physical, electronic, and design elements (8)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Signage.</li>\n<li>Fencing.</li>\n<li>Exterior lighting.</li>\n<li>Door and window alarms.</li>\n<li>Video surveillance system.</li>\n<li>Entry intercom with audio and video.</li>\n<li>Reception that monitors the entry control system, with controls to open doors remotely.</li>\n<li>Protective barriers at reception and a duress/panic alarm.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>2. Access control policy and procedure (5)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>List of terminated former employees, with photos, at reception.</li>\n<li>Policy prohibiting terminated employees from entering the property.</li>\n<li>Collection of keys and IDs from terminated employees.</li>\n<li>Deactivation of access credentials and passwords.</li>\n<li>Response procedure when a terminated employee is seen on or near the property.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>3. Security personnel (3)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>List of terminated employees available to security, with photos and vehicle information.</li>\n<li>Patrol of the building exterior, including parking lots.</li>\n<li>Response capability: radio and telephone communication with the TMT and with police, fire, and ambulance services.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>4. Security and crime prevention awareness, education, and training for directly involved employees (3)</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Under no circumstances is a terminated employee allowed to enter the property and/or the building.</li>\n<li>Immediately report the presence of a terminated employee on or near the property.</li>\n<li>Train reception staff in their response role and in the use of the available security measures.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the scenario calls the terminated former employee an “external threat.” But in the typology of section 8.2, a current or former employee is a <strong>Type 3</strong> aggressor. They are “external” only because they no longer have a right of access. If asked about the aggressor type, the answer is Type 3.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> against the return of terminated employees, the best mitigation is layered access control. Layers: physical and electronic barriers, exit policy (keys, ID, credentials), security personnel, and training. It shows the chain of chapter 8: risk assessment, an SWG that mitigates, and a TMT that responds.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e14\">Appendix: Standards in Security (pp. 297–300)</h3><p><strong>What it is:</strong> a general appendix on security standards. It explains what they are, their types, their benefits, management system standards, and the continual improvement cycle.</p>\n<h4>Definition and origin</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Standard:</strong> a set of criteria, guidelines, and best practices that improves the quality and reliability of products, services, or processes.</li>\n<li>Thanks to standards, many parts, processes, and systems work regardless of who creates or runs them, who uses them, and where.</li>\n<li>They arise to meet a market need or fill a gap in existing standards.</li>\n<li>They are coordinated by accredited standards developing organizations (SDOs). These bring together stakeholders from the private and public sectors to identify best practices.</li>\n<li>They are the basis of innovation and competitiveness, and a common language that supports national and international trade.</li>\n<li><strong>Four development principles:</strong> consensus, openness, due process, and transparency.</li>\n<li>In security, they help nations, communities, societies, organizations, and individuals protect assets and gain resilience against natural and human threats.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Types of standards</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Obligation</th>\n<th>What the book says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Voluntary</td>\n<td>Not binding by themselves, but widely adopted</td>\n<td>Those of the International Organization for Standardization (ISO) and of national standards institutes. Some have been integrated into national regulatory frameworks. In the US they are also set by trade or professional associations, such as ASIS International</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Statutory or regulatory</td>\n<td>Binding by law</td>\n<td>Enforced by formal authorities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mixed</td>\n<td>Blurred boundary</td>\n<td>Voluntary standards incorporated into laws or regulations: fire, construction, and life safety. Or standards that are technically voluntary but mandatory in practice (see below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Voluntary but mandatory in practice:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Insurers may condition the availability and cost of casualty insurance on the use of approved materials or standardized practices.</li>\n<li>Contracts may require standards as a requirement. The adoption of standards is increasingly client driven.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Areas of ISO standards relevant to asset protection (5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>facility management;</li>\n<li>health, environment and safety;</li>\n<li>risk management;</li>\n<li>security and resilience;</li>\n<li>information technology (IT) and information security.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Issuing bodies cited</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Body</th>\n<th>What it standardizes (according to the book)</th>\n<th>Nature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO</td>\n<td>The areas above; management systems</td>\n<td>Voluntary, widely adopted; some integrated into national regulations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National standards institutes</td>\n<td>Not detailed</td>\n<td>Voluntary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>Security management; has a broad library of standards and guidelines</td>\n<td>Voluntary (professional association)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>National Fire Protection Association (NFPA)</td>\n<td>Not detailed</td>\n<td>Not all voluntary: some are incorporated into regulations, such as building codes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Underwriters Laboratories (UL)</td>\n<td>Various standards on security equipment: locks, alarms, and access control systems</td>\n<td>Cited among the voluntary ones</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illuminating Engineering Society</td>\n<td>Lighting standards and practices</td>\n<td>Trade or professional association</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Electronic Industries Association</td>\n<td>Electronic components and products</td>\n<td>Trade or professional association</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Standards cited by designation</h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Designation</th>\n<th>Issuer</th>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO 9001</td>\n<td>ISO</td>\n<td>Not indicated</td>\n<td>Quality management systems. Together with ISO 14001, the most prominent management system standard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ISO 14001</td>\n<td>ISO</td>\n<td>Not indicated</td>\n<td>Environmental management systems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASIS ORM.1-2017</td>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>2017</td>\n<td><em>Security and Resilience in Organizations and their Supply Chains – Requirements with Guidance</em>: security and resilience in organizations and their supply chains, requirements with guidance. It is the source of figure A.1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Workplace Violence and Active Assailant – Prevention, Intervention, Response Standard</em></td>\n<td>ASIS International</td>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td>Workplace violence and active assailant: prevention, intervention, and response. <strong>It does not appear in this appendix</strong>: it is the reference standard for chapter 8 (references 8.11)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Benefits of standards for the security professional (9)</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>Codify best practices and processes, and share lessons learned.</li>\n<li>Provide tools to assess threats, risks, vulnerabilities, criticalities, and impacts.</li>\n<li>Define measurement methods.</li>\n<li>Document equipment performance requirements, to ensure effectiveness and safety.</li>\n<li>Set design requirements so that devices, systems, and infrastructure withstand threats.</li>\n<li>Define effective methods to identify people.</li>\n<li>Increase knowledge and competence.</li>\n<li>Improve information sharing between jurisdictions and interoperability.</li>\n<li>Provide consistency in services.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Management system standards</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>They help improve how the organization delivers services and runs processes. They are accepted and used in many fields.</li>\n<li>The most prominent: ISO 9001 (quality) and ISO 14001 (environment).</li>\n<li>The approach calls for analyzing the requirements of the organization and of stakeholders, and defining the processes that lead to success.</li>\n<li>They provide a framework for <strong>continual improvement</strong>. They increase the likelihood of achieving strategic, operational, tactical, and reputational objectives, and strengthen resilience.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Plan-Do-Check-Act (PDCA) cycle</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the <strong>operating principle</strong> of management system standards.</li>\n<li>It is a structured problem-solving method focused on continual improvement.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Appendix text</th>\n<th>Label in figure A.1</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Plan</td>\n<td>Identify and analyze the organization’s problems and assets, and rank them by importance</td>\n<td>Define and analyze a problem and its context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Do</td>\n<td>Prioritize the next steps, develop a detailed action plan, and execute it</td>\n<td>Devise a solution, develop a detailed action plan, and execute it systematically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check</td>\n<td>Examine whether the solutions produce results consistent with the plan</td>\n<td>Confirm results against the plan; identify deviations and problems</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Act</td>\n<td>If the solutions resolve the problems, standardize them across the organization, review the list of problems, and define new ones. Here the cycle starts over</td>\n<td>Standardize the solution; review and define the next problems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Figure A.1</strong> (caption: ASIS ORM.1-2017; label: <em>Plan-Do-Check-Act Model</em>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Input: stakeholders and interested parties with their <strong>requirements and expectations</strong>.</li>\n<li>Center: <strong>organizational resilience management systems</strong>, driven by the PDCA cycle.</li>\n<li>Output: stakeholders with <strong>managed risk</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"ojo\"><strong>Exam note:</strong> the header of these pages says “Investigations — Maximizing the Investigation,” and that of the index, “Physical Security.” It appears to be text reused from other volumes of the <em>Protection of Assets</em> series. It is a layout error; the content on standards is still valid.</p>\n<p><strong>Takeaway:</strong> standards are tools for the security professional. You need to distinguish voluntary, regulatory, and mixed standards, and master the PDCA cycle of management systems. In this book, the policy and program of chapter 8 rest on the 2020 ASIS standard on workplace violence and active assailants.</p><h3 id=\"personnel-A-e15\">Key terms (appendices 7A–Standards)</h3><div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>English</th>\n<th>What it means here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cluster of indicators</td>\n<td><em>cluster</em></td>\n<td>Several symptoms or behaviors together; only then do they provide guidance (7A)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intervention</td>\n<td><em>intervention</em></td>\n<td>Supervisor action to correct, not punish (7B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasonable accommodation</td>\n<td><em>reasonable accommodation</em></td>\n<td>Fifth step of the intervention (7B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drug-free workplace</td>\n<td><em>drug-free workplace</em></td>\n<td>Scope statement of the policy (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Impairment</td>\n<td><em>impairment</em></td>\n<td>Detectable physical or mental effect of drugs or alcohol; sometimes mere use is enough (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reasonable suspicion</td>\n<td><em>reasonable suspicion</em></td>\n<td>By a supervisor or higher-level manager, concurred in by the highest-ranking manager available (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Preemployment testing</td>\n<td><em>preemployment testing</em></td>\n<td>For applicants; anyone who fails is ineligible for at least one year (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Universal testing</td>\n<td><em>universal testing</em></td>\n<td>Random selection by the provider, without notice, in designated areas (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Confirmation test</td>\n<td><em>confirmation</em></td>\n<td>Second test after an initial positive (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uncontested positive verification</td>\n<td><em>uncontested positive verification</em></td>\n<td>Effect of failing to respond in time to the positive notice (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Illegal drug</td>\n<td><em>illegal drug</em></td>\n<td>Not legally obtainable, or obtained or used illegally; includes shared prescriptions (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legal drug</td>\n<td><em>legal drug</em></td>\n<td>Prescription or over-the-counter, legally obtained and used for its purpose (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drug paraphernalia</td>\n<td><em>drug paraphernalia</em></td>\n<td>Objects to prepare, store, or administer illegal drugs (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serious injury</td>\n<td><em>serious injury</em></td>\n<td>Work injury that stops work and requires medical attention (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chemical dependency</td>\n<td><em>chemical dependency</em></td>\n<td>Evaluated as an alternative to discipline; treatment offered once (7C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Designated management representative (DMR)</td>\n<td><em>designated management representative</em></td>\n<td>Receives reports and copies of protective orders (8A)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protective or restraining order</td>\n<td><em>protective / restraining order</em></td>\n<td>If it covers company locations, copies go to the DMR (8A)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good faith report</td>\n<td><em>good faith report</em></td>\n<td>Protected against retaliation (8A)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stakeholders working group (SWG)</td>\n<td><em>stakeholders working group</em></td>\n<td>Applies the mitigations; basis of the future TMT (8B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Threat management team (TMT)</td>\n<td><em>threat management team</em></td>\n<td>Also TAT or IMT; security communicates with it (8B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diffusion of benefits</td>\n<td><em>diffusion of benefits</em></td>\n<td>One measure against one threat also protects against others (8B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security survey</td>\n<td><em>security survey</em></td>\n<td>Comprehensive review of the property and building (8B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duress/panic alarm</td>\n<td><em>duress/panic alarm</em></td>\n<td>Reception protection element (8B)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td><em>standard</em></td>\n<td>Criteria, guidelines, and best practices for quality and reliability (Standards)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standards developing organization (SDO)</td>\n<td><em>standards developing organization</em></td>\n<td>Coordinates standards with public and private stakeholders (Standards)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Voluntary / statutory or regulatory / mixed standard</td>\n<td><em>voluntary / statutory or regulatory / mixed</em></td>\n<td>The three types of obligation (Standards)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Management system standard</td>\n<td><em>management system standard</em></td>\n<td>Continual improvement framework; examples ISO 9001 and ISO 14001 (Standards)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PDCA cycle</td>\n<td><em>Plan-Do-Check-Act</em></td>\n<td>Operating principle of management systems (Standards)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>","toc":[{"id":"e0","t":"Appendix 1A — Sample Disciplinary Policy"},{"id":"e1","t":"Appendix 1B — Company Fact Sheet on Employee Discipline"},{"id":"e2","t":"Appendix 3A — Sample Scope of Work"},{"id":"e3","t":"Appendix 3B — Sample General and Special Orders"},{"id":"e4","t":"Appendix 3C — Sample Operating Agreement for Security Service"},{"id":"e5","t":"Appendix 4A — Application for Consulting Assignment"},{"id":"e6","t":"Appendix 4B — Résumé"},{"id":"e7","t":"Appendix 4C — Professional Consulting Services Agreement"},{"id":"e8","t":"Key Terms"},{"id":"e9","t":"Appendix 7A — Supervisor's Checklist"},{"id":"e10","t":"Appendix 7B — Intervention Checklist"},{"id":"e11","t":"Appendix 7C — Sample Substance Abuse Policy"},{"id":"e12","t":"Appendix 8A — Sample Policy for Workplace Violence"},{"id":"e13","t":"Appendix 8B — Workplace Violence Plan Scenario"},{"id":"e14","t":"Appendix: Standards in Security"},{"id":"e15","t":"Key terms"}],"ojo":[{"s":"Appendix 1A — Sample Disciplinary Policy","h":"section D declares any violation of the 37 rules <em>willful</em> misconduct. But C(3) excludes ordinary isolated negligence and good-faith errors. For example, rule 18 (“any damage… by act or omission”) clashes with that exclusion."},{"s":"Appendix 3A — Sample Scope of Work","h":"the last shift of post 2 says “Saturday through Sunday” but adds up to 56.0 h. Two nights would give 16 h; 56 h is 7 nights. The totals (303.5 h and 32 shifts) only add up with 7 nights: it seems to mean “Monday through Sunday.”"},{"s":"Appendix 3A — Sample Scope of Work","h":"the chapter 3 analysis example speaks of about 308 hours per week (about 16,000 per year). This appendix adds up to 303.5. The book does not say they are the same example: do not mix figures."},{"s":"Appendix 3C — Sample Operating Agreement for Security Service","h":"steps 1 (“0 to 6 months”) and 2 (“6 to 12 months”) overlap at month 6."},{"s":"Appendix 3C — Sample Operating Agreement for Security Service","h":"clause C.1 and the chapter 3 example set the lease-purchase at <strong>24 months</strong>. Schedule 3 says <strong>12 months</strong>."},{"s":"Appendix 3C — Sample Operating Agreement for Security Service","h":"if the rates are read in the order of the steps, they do not rise with seniority: they drop from step 2 to 3, and the supervisor rate drops from 1 to 2. Step 3 would bill $13.35 with a wage of $12.05. The book does not explain the correspondence and states that the figures are illustrative."},{"s":"Appendix 4C — Professional Consulting Services Agreement","h":"in this form the company is called “Contractor”; in the main contract it is “Company.”"},{"s":"Appendix 4C — Professional Consulting Services Agreement","h":"the receipt thresholds do not match. The form asks for a receipt from <strong>$5.00</strong>; the expense and travel policies use <strong>$10.00 per trip</strong>; the reporting section exempts taxi and bus without setting an amount."},{"s":"Appendix 4C — Professional Consulting Services Agreement","h":"the daily commuting distance is measured from the “assigned work location” (expense policy) or from the “company facility” (travel policy). Both use 50 miles."},{"s":"Appendix 7B — Intervention Checklist","h":"apparent internal contradiction between “talk with the employee in private” and “interview him with a witness.” The book does not explain how they are reconciled or who the witness should be (pending)."},{"s":"Appendix 7C — Sample Substance Abuse Policy","h":""},{"s":"Appendix 8A — Sample Policy for Workplace Violence","h":""},{"s":"Appendix 8B — Workplace Violence Plan Scenario","h":"the scenario calls the terminated former employee an “external threat.” But in the typology of section 8.2, a current or former employee is a <strong>Type 3</strong> aggressor. They are “external” only because they no longer have a right of access. If asked about the aggressor type, the answer is Type 3."},{"s":"Appendix: Standards in Security","h":"the header of these pages says “Investigations — Maximizing the Investigation,” and that of the index, “Physical Security.” It appears to be text reused from other volumes of the <em>Protection of Assets</em> series. It is a layout error; the content on standards is still valid."}]}};
